\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 US Starbucks workers\u8217\'20\'19 strike expands to 11 states as Christmas approaches\b0\par\par\uc2 Julian Roberts-Grmela \par \uc2 650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 December 2024\par 20:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Walkouts have caused nearly 50 stores nationwide to close in five days, says Starbucks Workers United union\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/20/starbucks-strike-hundreds-stores-christmas-eve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five-day strike from the Starbucks workers\u8217\'20\'19 union}}}\uc2  has grown to include locations in at least 11 states.\par \par \uc2 As of Sunday, the strike had caused \u8220\'20\'1Calmost 50 stores nationwide\u8221\'20\'1D to close after it began, according to a statement provided by the union.\par \par \uc2 The strike started with baristas walking off the job in Los Angeles, Chicago and Seattle on Friday. It has since expanded into Denver; Columbus, Ohio; and Philadelphia.\par \par \uc2 Other locations are: New York City; Boston; Texas\u8217\'20\'19s Dallas-Forth Worth area; and Portland, Oregon, according to a written statement provided by the union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWalkouts are expected to continue surging through Christmas Eve,\u8221\'20\'1D Starbucks Workers United said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe final days before Christmas are traditionally one of \uc2 Starbucks\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 busiest customer traffic times of the year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union told CNN that the strike could reach \u8220\'20\'1Chundreds\u8221\'20\'1D of stores by Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 In a statement provided to the Guardian, a \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson, Phil Gee, said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe few disruptions we have experienced this week have had no significant impact to our store operations.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COnly a small handful of our US stores have been impacted,\u8221\'20\'1D Gee said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe respect our partners\u8217\'20\'19 right to engage in lawful strike activity, and we appreciate the thousands of partners across the country who are continuing to support each other and deliver the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  experience for our customers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  operates with more than 200,000 employees at more than 10,000 stores, according to CNN. Starbucks Workers United represents more than 10,500 workers at more than 500 stores, according to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sbworkersunited.org/#stores"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 their website}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a statement on X, the union wrote on Thursday: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  baristas are going on five days of escalating [unfair labor practice] strikes in response to the company backtracking on our promised path forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The union wrote that it hoped to reach an agreement on contract negotiations before the new year \u8211\'20\'13 but said that \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  management had \u8220\'20\'1Cyet to present workers with a serious economic proposal\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The union added: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Starbucks\uc2  proposed no immediate wage increase for union baristas, and a guarantee of only 1.5% wage increases in future years,\u8221\'20\'1D the union added.\par \par \uc2 Lynn Fox, the president of Starbucks Workers United, said in a written statement: \u8220\'20\'1CWe were ready to bring the foundational framework home this year, but \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  wasn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter all \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has said about how they value partners throughout the system, we refuse to accept zero immediate investment in baristas\u8217\'20\'19 wages and no resolution of the hundreds of outstanding unfair labor practices,\u8221\'20\'1D Fox said. \u8220\'20\'1CUnion baristas know their value, and they\u8217\'20\'19re not going to accept a proposal that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t treat them as true partners.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In his written statement to the Guardian, Gee said: \u8220\'20\'1CWorkers United delegates prematurely ended our bargaining session this week. It is disappointing they didn\u8217\'20\'19t return to the table given the progress we\u8217\'20\'19ve made to date.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But the \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  spokesperson said that proposals from the union and received by management \u8220\'20\'1Ccall for an immediate increase in the minimum wage of hourly partners by 64% and by 77% over the life of a three-year contract\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is not sustainable,\u8221\'20\'1D Gee said.\par \par \uc2 Nonetheless, Gee also said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe are ready to continue negotiations to reach agreements. We need the union to return to the table.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Starbucks Workers United issued a statement on behalf of Fatmeh Alhadjaboodi, a bargaining delegate and five-year barista, saying: \u8220\'20\'1CNobody wants to strike. It\u8217\'20\'19s a last resort, but \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  has broken its promise to thousands of baristas and left us with no choice.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn a year when \uc2 Starbucks\uc2  invested so many millions in top executive talent, it has failed to present the baristas who make its company run with a viable economic proposal and resolve the pending unfair labor practices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241223ekcn002ux\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
