\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 Why does \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  still get hired?\b0\par\par\uc2 Peter O'Toole \par \uc2 1,114 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2024\par 19:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The famed consulting firm is expected to pay a $600m settlement over its role in the US opioid crisis. But the company\u8217\'20\'19s problems don\u8217\'20\'19t start there\par \par \uc2 In late 2005, I was in a Tokyo meeting room with some top \uc2 GE\uc2  executives. As the company\u8217\'20\'19s corporate director of public relations at the time, I was helping sort through a corporate initiative we were planning to announce with some important Japanese customers. A few minutes later, a handful of people I\u8217\'20\'19d never met came into the room. Most were not from Japan; all of them, I was told, were from \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 , perhaps the world\u8217\'20\'19s most famous management consulting firm. Following the 15-minute meeting they had probably flown in from all over the world to attend, they left.\par \par \uc2 The firm \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 , of course, remains, dispensing advice to thousands of companies like \uc2 GE\uc2  and to governments and institutions in more than 65 countries. But as the company {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/21/mckinsey-opioid-crisis-epidemic-government-settlement"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 approaches}}}\uc2  a $600m settlement with the \uc2 US government\uc2  over its role helping opioid makers boost sales and drag out the painkiller epidemic, you might ask: why does \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  still get hired?\par \par \uc2 There are reasons why it shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t.\par \par \uc2 In 2018, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  agreed to repay {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politicsweb.co.za/politics/pityana-commends-mckinsey-for-paying-back-the-mone"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $74m in fees}}}\uc2  to the government of South Africa after a judicial inquiry found evidence of contract abnormalities surrounding its work. This came soon after \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  was forced to pay back more than $66m for its work alongside a contract-looting partner who later fled the country. And just last week it was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.justice.gov/opa/pr/mckinsey-company-africa-pay-over-122m-connection-bribery-south-african-government-officials"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 confirmed}}}\uc2  that the firm\u8217\'20\'19s South African subsidiary will pay more than $122m \u8220\'20\'1Cto resolve an investigation by the Justice Department into a scheme\u8221\'20\'1D to bribe South African government officials between 2012 and 2016.\par \par \uc2 Chasing a Mongolian railroads contract in 2010, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  ignored a US state department corruption {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://2009-2017.state.gov/e/eb/rls/othr/ics/2010/138766.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warning}}}\uc2  and signed an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/the-country-that-exiled-mckinsey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agreement}}}\uc2  with the government even as the government rainmaker who connected \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  to the contract signed his own contract with the same government entity. By 2015, Mongolian anti-corruption investigators were snooping and \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  was eventually barred from doing business there.\par \par \uc2 Since 2019, the firm\u8217\'20\'19s bankruptcy advisory business has paid millions in fines, or relinquished fees, for \u8220\'20\'1Cdisclosure deficiencies\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The firm can also give questionable advice.\par \par \uc2 Under former \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  partner Jeff Skilling, \uc2 Enron\uc2  paid the firm $10m a year, during which the firm endorsed \uc2 Enron\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s suspect accounting tactics that would ultimately torpedo the business. According to a \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  employee quoted in Duff McDonald\u8217\'20\'19s book The Firm: The Story of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  and Its Secret Influence on American Business, \uc2 Enron\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cwas a great business that just got out of hand\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In April 2017, after three years studying the escalating inmate brawls and assaults by guards that endangered the viability of New York City\u8217\'20\'19s Rikers Island jail complex, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  sent a confidential final report to the city corrections commissioner. \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  had tested its new anti-violence strategy, the report said, and violence was down by more than half in the units where \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  had recommended new tactics.\par \par \uc2 What the report didn\u8217\'20\'19t say was that jail officials and \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  consultants had fixed the game, moving inmates they believed were less violent into the units ahead of the period of study. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nydailynews.com/new-york/ny-mckinsey-rikers-violence-data-20190910-3mwj7vmocba35cqhv4wto2sqpa-story.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The reality}}}\uc2  was the opposite of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s report: violence {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/new-york-city-paid-mckinsey-millions-to-stem-jail-violence-instead-violence-soared"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 actually rose}}}\uc2  by nearly 50% since the firm had been working at Rikers. The city government dumped \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 , but not before paying it more than $27m.\par \par \uc2 Then there\u8217\'20\'19s the Department of Justice case, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.industrydocuments.ucsf.edu/opioids/collections/mckinsey-documents/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found}}}\uc2  the firm\u8217\'20\'19s invisible hand reached into virtually every opioid manufacturer and distributor. While \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  was advising the opioid manufacturers and distributors, the lawsuit noted, it was also advising the \uc2 US Food and Drug Administration\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s drug-approval department.\par \par \uc2 So why does \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  still get hired? Because it sells what business wants.\par \par \uc2 Its presence offers the plausible deniability that layoffs were just a business decision, one recommended by a respected, external party\par \par \uc2 According to \uc2 McDonald\uc2 , in The Firm, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Ccertainly made the world a more efficient, rational and objective place than it might otherwise have been\u8221\'20\'1D. Eighty-five per cent of \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work is repeat business, which would seem to show that the company provides useful counsel.\par \par \uc2 But \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  also shields weak leaders from accountability. After a preventable shop floor accident or before big layoffs \u8211\'20\'13 known as \u8220\'20\'1Cefficiency consulting\u8221\'20\'1D, \uc2 McDonald\uc2  notes \u8211\'20\'13 a \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  report can shield a company from an angry plaintiff or its soon-to-be former workers. Its presence offers the plausible deniability that layoffs were just a business decision, one recommended by a respected, external party. In the Rikers Island case, \uc2 McKinsey\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s contract let city administrators say they had tried their best, since they had only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/new-york-city-paid-mckinsey-millions-to-stem-jail-violence-instead-violence-soared"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 requested proposals}}}\uc2  from consultants on a pre-approved list from the previous administration.\par \par \uc2 The durability of firms like \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  points to festering weakness in business\u8217\'20\'19s upper reaches: today\u8217\'20\'19s leaders either don\u8217\'20\'19t have the skills to meet the accelerating world around them, can\u8217\'20\'19t effect change within their own organizations, or are too afraid to make a mistake and fail.\par \par \uc2 But if they succeed? Well, that\u8217\'20\'19s what was supposed to happen, just as what we heard in Tokyo was preordained.\par \par \uc2 I later asked a colleague from a \uc2 GE\uc2  business who also attended the Tokyo meeting what they had heard. Our own ideas, they replied: \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  had surveyed our own people and customers for ideas, then tried selling them as their own.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s unlikely that \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  will change. After all, the firm\u8217\'20\'19s partners ousted former CEO \uc2 Kevin Sneader\uc2  not for being too weak on compliance failures, but for being too tough. \u8220\'20\'1CMr Sneader\u8217\'20\'19s letter to employees about the [opioid] settlement was blunt in criticizing the firm\u8217\'20\'19s behavior,\u8221\'20\'1D the Wall Street Journal {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.wsj.com/articles/mckinsey-partners-vote-out-leader-after-opioid-settlement-other-crises-11614187437?mod=hp_lead_pos3"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 noted}}}\uc2 , and \u8220\'20\'1C[s]ome partners felt that language was too strong.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s business leaders should look within themselves and determine why they don\u8217\'20\'19t trust their own people to make tough decisions. They need to find out why they are afraid to fail on their own terms. Making yourself accountable can be more painful than hiring a consultant to be accountable for you, but it\u8217\'20\'19s likely to be more rewarding.\par \par \uc2 Nearly 20 years after that meeting in Tokyo, I am also a consultant. And while my small firm didn\u8217\'20\'19t earn the record $16bn that \uc2 McKinsey\uc2  did last year, we also didn\u8217\'20\'19t get kicked out of Mongolia.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Peter O\u8217\'20\'19Toole is principal of Objective Lab and on the adjunct faculty at \uc2 Georgetown University\uc2 . He was a speechwriter in the Clinton administration and a senior executive at \uc2 GE\uc2  and \uc2 Pfizer\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241218ekch002tc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
