\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 The legal battle over abortion-by-mail in the US has begun \u8211\'20\'13 and the stakes are high\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,059 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 December 2024\par 12:22 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This fight will test whether pro-choice states can protect providers who mail abortion pills to anti-choice states\par \par \uc2 The legal battle over the interstate mailing of abortion pills has begun. On Friday, the Texas attorney general, \uc2 Ken Paxton\uc2 , sued Dr Margaret Carpenter, a New York-based OB-GYN and reproductive justice activist, over what he alleges was Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s choice to mail abortion pills from New York to a 20-year-old pregnant woman in Texas.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/sites/default/files/images/press/Dr%20Carpenter%20Filed%20Petition.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lawsuit}}}\uc2 , filed in a Texas state court but almost certainly the beginning of a federal legal battle, marks the first formal legal challenge by an anti-abortion attorney general against a Democratic-controlled state\u8217\'20\'19s shield laws, which protect abortion providers from out-of-state liability, and is slated to test how far pro-choice states can go to protect providers within their state borders \u8211\'20\'13 and how much force anti-choice states can give to their abortion bans beyond theirs.\par \par \uc2 The alleged facts go something like this: sometime last summer, a young Texas woman from the Dallas suburbs discovered that she was pregnant, and contacted one of Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s advocacy organizations seeking access to abortion medication. Carpenter is part of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theactgroup.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Abortion Coalition for Telemedicine}}}\uc2 , or Act, a national network of doctors located in pro-choice states that dispense abortion medication through the mail; she has also worked with the abortion access advocacy groups {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.heyjane.com/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hey Jane}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aidaccess.org/en/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AidAccess}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Through the group, Carpenter prescribed the woman the pills, which she took as directed. The woman\u8217\'20\'19s abortion was discovered by authorities after the patient, concerned about heavy bleeding, asked her boyfriend to take her to the hospital. The Texas attorney general\u8217\'20\'19s complaint makes repeated, disconcerting reference to the fact that the boyfriend had evidently not been told about the pregnancy and abortion before this, suggesting that he was entitled to the information or had somehow been wronged.\par \par \uc2 Doctors like Carpenter have become a central part of the public health and civil rights response to Dobbs. They have stepped in to provide American women with the safety and dignity that their states seek to deny them. An estimated 8,000 women in ban states access pills by mail every single month, getting prescriptions from doctors like Carpenter in safe states and from providers located abroad.\par \par \uc2 The ability to access the pills this way has meant that the abortions being conducted in these states, outside of clinical settings, are overwhelmingly safe. The pills, that is, keep women from seeking out the much more dangerous illegal surgical abortions, whose incompetent or careless administration by black market providers were the source of large numbers of deaths in the pre-Roe era. Doctors like Carpenter, then, do not merely give their patients control over their own bodies and destinies; in giving them a safe, reliable way to end their pregnancies in ban states, they may well be saving these women\u8217\'20\'19s lives.\par \par \uc2 It is this safe, effective and accessible form of self-managed abortion that the state of Texas is trying to end. Now, Paxton\u8217\'20\'19s office is claiming that Carpenter, though she never set foot in the state of Texas, violated Texas state law. Further, Paxton believes that Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law can reach all the way to New York: the suit aims to make Texas\u8217\'20\'19s law shape the conduct of people outside its borders, curtailing both Carpenter\u8217\'20\'19s medical practice and her freedom of expression.\par \par \uc2 Texas\u8217\'20\'19s suit will quickly force a confrontation over one prominent obstacle: New York\u8217\'20\'19s abortion shield law. In the wake of the Dobbs decision overturning Roe v Wade, many Democratic-controlled state legislatures, anticipating an influx of abortion seekers from out of state and lawsuits seeking to prevent women from accessing care in places where it is legal, passed laws seeking to protect abortion providers in their states from lawsuits and prosecutions brought by Republican state governments.\par \par \uc2 Known as shield laws, these bills prevent any state resources from being used to cooperate with abortion lawsuits and prosecutions. That means that, by law, the state of New York cannot cooperate with Paxton\u8217\'20\'19s lawsuit, and they can\u8217\'20\'19t force Carpenter to do so, either.\par \par \uc2 This conflict \u8211\'20\'13 between New York\u8217\'20\'19s attempt to protect abortion and Texas\u8217\'20\'19s increasingly expansive attempts to eradicate it \u8211\'20\'13 is the conflict that will probably arrive in federal court. When it gets there, it will raise some fraught constitutional questions. If Dobbs was based partly on a states\u8217\'20\'19 rights argument, about the freedom of states such as Texas to ban abortion \u8211\'20\'13 as \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  said \u8211\'20\'13 then do pro-choice states also have a similar right to robustly enforce their own preferred abortion policy? Or do states like Texas, with their anti-choice agendas, get to have a longer arm, extending their own policy preferences beyond their borders and compelling other states to help enforce them?\par \par \uc2 The looming conflict over whether states such as New York have a right to protect their abortion providers has reminded some historians of the constitutional conflict that arose over slavery in the 19th century: when enslaved people escaped the south, and made their way to free states, slave states cried foul, and tried to compel the free northern states to kidnap and return the fugitives to enslavement.\par \par \uc2 The federal government ultimately agreed, resolving the issue with the Fugitive Slave Act of 1850, which forced free states to help slave states enforce slavery by returning escapees to captivity. But in attempting to solve a legal problem, the Fugitive Slave Act created a political one: free states resented the imposition of the slave states\u8217\'20\'19 will on their own policy and functioning, and their anger over being forced to participate in a legal system they found morally abhorrent eventually contributed to the outbreak of the civil war.\par \par \uc2 In questions of people\u8217\'20\'19s essential freedoms, it is not sustainable for the country to be divided between freedom in some states and unfreedom in others. The two world views \u8211\'20\'13 between liberty and restriction, equality and hierarchy enforced by law \u8211\'20\'13 cannot abide one another. The country must choose. Now, as Texas seeks to force the issue, it looks like it will eventually be the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  that decides.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241217ekch001p5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
