\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Ro Khanna: \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  killing was \u8216\'20\'18horrific\u8217\'20\'19 but people \u8216\'20\'18aren\u8217\'20\'19t getting care they need\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Ramon Antonio Vargas \par \uc2 612 \uc2 words\par \uc2 9 December 2024\par 14:36 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Congressperson shares sympathy in UnitedHealthcare CEO\u8217\'20\'19s death but says \u8216\'20\'18outpouring \u8230\'20\'26 has not surprised me\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Progressive congressperson Ro Khanna has sympathy for the murdered UnitedHealthcare CEO, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/brian-thompson-shooting"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Brian Thompson }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 yet at the same time is not surprised that the killing reignited a national dialogue about inequities in the US healthcare system, he said in an interview on Sunday.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was horrific,\u8221\'20\'1D the California Democrat said on ABC This Week with respect to the slaying of Thompson, whose survivors include his widow and two sons ages 16 and 19. \u8220\'20\'1CI mean, this is a father we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about \u8211\'20\'13 of two children, and \u8230\'20\'26 there is no justification for violence.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBut the outpouring afterwards has not surprised me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khanna {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/rep-ro-khanna-us-moving-medicare-cure-inequities/story?id=116564621"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told }}}\uc2  the show\u8217\'20\'19s host, Martha Raddatz, that he agreed with fellow liberal and US senator Bernie Sanders when he wrote recently on social media: \u8220\'20\'1CWe waste hundreds of billions a year on health care administrative expenses that make insurance CEOs and wealthy stockholders incredibly rich while 85 million Americans go uninsured or underinsured. Health care is a human right. We need Medicare for all.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAfter years, Sanders is winning this debate,\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/08/bernie-sanders-ro-khanna-medical-debt-bill"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Khanna }}}\uc2  said, referring to the Vermont senator\u8217\'20\'19s support for a single-payer national health insurance system seen in other wealthy democracies.\par \par \uc2 While police have stopped short of offering a possible motive behind Thompson\u8217\'20\'19s 4 December shooting death, the apparent targeted nature of the attack \u8211\'20\'13 as well as shell casings found at the scene of the killing displaying the words \u8220\'20\'1Cdelay\u8221\'20\'1D, \u8220\'20\'1Cdeny\u8221\'20\'1D and possibly \u8220\'20\'1Cdepose\u8221\'20\'1D have suggested it was maybe linked to the largely privatized US healthcare industry\u8217\'20\'19s routine denial of payments to many Americans.\par \par \uc2 Healthcare debt has emerged as a leading cause of bankruptcy in the US while for-profit health insurers such as UnitedHealthcare are among the country\u8217\'20\'19s richest companies. Thompson, 50, who lived near UnitedHealthcare\u8217\'20\'19s headquarters in Minnesota, commanded a salary of $10m annually before a gunman wearing a mask shot him dead outside a hotel in Manhattan as he prepared to attend a meeting with investors of his company.\par \par \uc2 Many greeted news of Thompson\u8217\'20\'19s death not with sympathy but with mockery. A widely shared example of the sentiment was a social media post from Columbia School of Social Work\u8217\'20\'19s Anthony Zenkus, which read: \u8220\'20\'1CToday, we mourn the death of United Healthcare CEO \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2 , gunned down\u8230\'20\'26. wait, I\u8217\'20\'19m sorry \u8211\'20\'13 today we mourn the deaths of the 68,000 Americans who die needlessly each year so that insurance company execs like \uc2 Brian Thompson\uc2  can become multimillionaires.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khanna on Sunday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://abcnews.go.com/Politics/week-transcript-12-8-24-rep-ro-khanna/story?id=116563335"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 said }}}\uc2  his status as a member of the US House has not immunized him from absurd insurance battles.\par \par \uc2 Though he acknowledged it paled in comparison to people with cancer, heart disease and diabetes being denied coverage while they battle for their lives, Khanna said: \u8220\'20\'1CI, as a congressperson had UnitedHealthcare deny a prescription for a nasal \u8211\'20\'13 a $100 pump spray, and I couldn\u8217\'20\'19t get them to reverse this. So imagine what ordinary people are dealing with.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Khanna said some modest steps that the US could take to begin addressing injustices in the country\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare include capping out-of-pocket costs while also requiring the private insurers relied on by many Americans \u8220\'20\'1Cto cover anything\u8221\'20\'1D that Medicare would.\par \par \uc2 Medicare is the public US health insurance program for those older than 65 and people who are disabled.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to understand people with cancer, with heart disease, with diabetes, with insurance aren\u8217\'20\'19t getting the care that they need. They\u8217\'20\'19re getting stuck with huge medical bills.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241209ekc90015q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
