\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Investors up bets on \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  interest rate cut after US jobs data \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Jasper Jolly \par \uc2 4,690 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2024\par 16:13 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Live, rolling coverage of business, economics and financial markets as American private sector adds 227,000 jobs in November\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Investors increase bets on interest rate cut from \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  after jobs data\par \par \uc2 Investors have increased their bets on the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  cutting interest rates this month after jobs data came in roughly in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 Futures markets put an 89% chance on the Fed cutting rates by 25 basis points at its next meeting on 18 December after the payrolls data, compared to a 68% chance earlier in the session, Reuters reported.\par \par \uc2 Adam Hetts, global head of multi-asset at Janus Henderson Investors, said:\par \par \uc2 A strong November non-farm payrolls at 227,000 included some reversals from October and was further offset by a tick up in the unemployment rate to 4.24%. This big rebound from a distorted October read is actually quite balanced, should relieve some economic concerns, and keep the 18 December rate cut expectations on track.\par \par \uc2 Zooming out a bit from today, the trend of a slowly slowing labour market continues to sit in the sweet spot as far as rate cuts are concerned.\par \par \uc2 Samuel Tombs\uc2 , chief US economist at Pantheon Macroeconomics, said:\par \par \uc2 November\u8217\'20\'19s labor market data give the FOMC the green light to ease policy again this month.\par \par \uc2 With the cost of external finance for businesses still high, Indeed\u8217\'20\'19s measure of job openings at a four-year low and catch-up growth in health and education payrolls now fizzling out, we expect monthly growth in payrolls to average about 100K in 2025, steering the FOMC to reduce the funds rate by 25bp at alternate meetings despite the risk of tariff-fuelled inflation.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s it from the business live blog this week.You can continue to follow our live coverage from around the world:\par \par \uc2 In our coverage of the Middle East crisis, thousands flee Homs in central Syria as rebel forces push on\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/dec/06/middle-east-crisis-thousands-people-flee-homs-central-syria-rebel-forces-israel-gaza-latest-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle East crisis live: thousands flee Homs in central Syria as rebel forces push on }}}\par \par \uc2 In the US, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  defends Pete Hegseth as he announces more administration picks\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/dec/06/trump-cabinet-picks-pete-hegseth-us-politics-latest"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump defends Pete Hegseth as he announces more administration picks \u8211\'20\'13 live }}}\par \par \uc2 In the UK, a minister declines to endorse \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s claim about civil servants being comfortable with \u8216\'20\'18decline\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/live/2024/dec/06/keir-starmer-labour-housebuilding-uk-politics-latest-news"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Minister declines to endorse Keir Starmer\u8217\'20\'19s claim about civil servants being comfortable with \u8216\'20\'18decline\u8217\'20\'19 \u8211\'20\'13 UK politics live }}}\par \par \uc2 Thanks for reading, and please do join us next week. JJ\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.14pm GMT\par \par \uc2 BAE Systems\uc2  has signed contracts worth $2.5bn (\u163\'00\'A32bn) with Sweden and Denmark for new CV90 combat vehicles \u8211\'20\'13 with some to go to Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 Denmark will get 115 vehicles, while Sweden will buy 50. BAE, a member of the FTSE 100, said that the agreement also includes \u8220\'20\'1Cfurther vehicles for Ukraine financed by the two governments\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The vehicles are made in Sweden by BAE\u8217\'20\'19s H\u228\'00\'E4gglunds subsidiary.\par \par \uc2 Major general Peter Boysen, chief of the Royal Danish Army, said:\par \par \uc2 The infantry fighting vehicle is an essential component of the heavy brigade we are currently building. The 115 new vehicles will significantly enhance Denmark\u8217\'20\'19s contribution to collective security and international operations. With the 44 existing vehicles, we will have a total of 159 vehicles, providing us with substantial strength \u8211\'20\'13 also from an international perspective.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.55pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  complained constantly during the US presidential election campaign that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  had driven the economy into the ground, but the latest jobs numbers suggest that it is actually in fairly rude health.\par \par \uc2 Trump will take control on 20 January once more with questions being asked about whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  should actually even continue its path of cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Fed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/07/federal-reserve-interest-rates-lowered"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 last month lowered its key lending rate }}}\uc2  for the second time in this cycle, but there is not much sign that the US economy is in desperate need of support.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2024/11/archive-zip/giv-4559Y9Icg67Adyre/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive }}}\uc2  A graph showing that the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  has cut interest rates twice since it finished its post-pandemic hiking cycle.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Hyett, investment manager at Wealth Club, an investment platform, said:\par \par \uc2 Healthy US job numbers were widely expected, but they still come as a bit of a relief after last month\u8217\'20\'19s shock slump. The overall impression is that the US economy continues to go from strength-to-strength.\par \par \uc2 The question investors will be asking themselves is, with the US economy booming does the Fed really need to keep cutting interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The incoming Trump administration is set to do everything it can to spur domestic economic activity, and with tariffs potentially pushing up the cost of imports the combination of surging demand and restricted supply could trigger a resurgence in inflation. With labour markets seemingly doing well enough on their own interest rate cuts look increasingly unnecessary.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.41pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Michael Brown, senior research strategist at Pepperstone, a foreign exchange broker, said:\par \par \uc2 The November US labour market report showed a rebound from the dismal, weather-affected October report, as the overall employment situation remains resilient.\par \par \uc2 Headline nonfarm payrolls rose by 227k in November, a touch above consensus expectations, but well within the forecast range. At the same time, the prior 2 months of data was revised higher, by a net 56k, taking the 3-month average of job gains to +173k.\par \par \uc2 Taking a step back, my base case remains that the [Federal Reserve] will still deliver a 25 basis point [0.25 percentage point] cut later this month, continuing to normalise the monetary policy stance, as the labour market also continues to normalise. Naturally, the committee will seek not to over-react to a single data point, particularly when incoming figures remain skewed by a number of one-off factors, though the modest rise in unemployment will embolden some of the committee\u8217\'20\'19s doves for now.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US economy added 227,000 jobs in November\par \par \uc2 The US economy added 227,000 private-sector jobs in November, above the 200,000 expected by economists.\par \par \uc2 It was a stronger-than-expected bounceback from the 36,000 jobs added in October, according to the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 . That October reading was revised up from the 12,000 initial reading.\par \par \uc2 The American unemployment rate rose from 4.1% to 4.2%, an increase in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.24pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The problem with analysing this month\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payrolls figures will be working out how much of the upward movement \u8211\'20\'13 presuming there is upward movement \u8211\'20\'13 is reversion to normal, or whether it is showing genuine economic strength.\par \par \uc2 Oscar Munoz, chief US macro strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2 , an investment bank, said:\par \par \uc2 We do not think that a surge in November job gains implies a sudden resurgence in hiring, but only the normalization from temporary shocks in the data.\par \par \uc2 It would be more unbiased to analyse the recent payrolls performance by looking at the October-November data together.\par \par \uc2 Bob Savage, head of markets strategy and insights at BNY Mellon, an investment bank, said he expected 220,000 were added. The focus will be on revisions and unemployment holding at 4.1%, but also on hourly earnings growth.\par \par \uc2 That is expected at 0.3%, down from 0.4%. The mix of jobs added and the participation rate \u8211\'20\'13 the proportion of working-age adults in work \u8211\'20\'13 will be key for the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plans to lower interest rates further.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.11pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US non-farm payrolls: economists forecast 200,000 jobs added\par \par \uc2 It has been a fairly quiet day on financial markets, but all financial traders\u8217\'20\'19 eyes will turn to the week\u8217\'20\'19s biggest data release: US non-farm payrolls.\par \par \uc2 Economists expect a major bounceback in the jobs figures \u8211\'20\'13 which show how many private sector jobs were added in the previous month \u8211\'20\'13 after a shock decline in October.\par \par \uc2 That reading, at 12,000, dropped far below consensus expectations. Hurricanes and a big \uc2 Boeing\uc2  strike hindered hiring, but even so the figure was a fraction of the 113,000 expectation.\par \par \uc2 It would be a second big shock if November\u8217\'20\'19s figures were not much better. Economists polled by Reuters expect 200,000 new jobs were added for the month.\par \par \uc2 Economists at \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , an investment bank, estimate nonfarm payrolls rose by 235k in November, above the consensus. The three-month average of job creation is 104,000 \u8211\'20\'13 generally a reasonably healthy rate.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.56pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Retailers saw a surge in sales on Black Friday week \u8211\'20\'13 both in stores and online \u8211\'20\'13 raising hopes that festive spending will grow this year.\par \par \uc2 Sales at established stores rose 19% with strong growth at fashion and homewares stores, according to advisory firm \uc2 BDO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s high street sales tracker which mainly involves small and medium-sized retailers.\par \par \uc2 Sales in stores rose more than 14% and online sales were up more than 23% as cash- strapped shoppers sought out bargain presents and treats for themselves during the US-inspired discount event.\par \par \uc2 Fashion performed strongly \u8211\'20\'13 up 18% as cold weather hit after a difficult mild October while homeware sales were up 28% as families prepared for guests.\par \par \uc2 However, the numbers are flattered by comparison to last year when spending tailed off\par \par \uc2 after Black Friday week \u8211\'20\'13 which fell a week earlier in the calendar. It may not be clear until mid-December whether households have felt secure enough to bump up festive spending after a lacklustre few years.\par \par \uc2 Recent figures from credit and debit card operator Barclaycard suggests consumers\u8217\'20\'19 confidence in their ability to spend on non-essential items reached its highest level since February in the month to mid November.\par \par \uc2 However it found supermarket spend decreased 1.8%, as two thirds or cardholders said they were looking for ways to reduce the cost of their weekly shop helped by easing inflation. Spending on travel and holidays was up but spending on clothing down amid the mild October and then stormy start to November which held back trips to the high street.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.53pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Steelmaking unions have called for the UK\u8217\'20\'19s two remaining blast furnaces in Scunthorpe to remain open in a meeting with Jingye, the Chinese owner of British Steel.\par \par \uc2 Community, GMB and \uc2 Unite\uc2 , the three main unions representing steel workers, said on Friday that a meeting was \u8220\'20\'1Cconstructive\u8221\'20\'1D as they presented a report prepared by consultants \uc2 Syndex\uc2 . The report contained a \u8220\'20\'1Cstrong recommendation to operate two blast furnaces at Scunthorpe to facilitate a smooth and low-risk transition\u8221\'20\'1D to electric arc furnaces.\par \par \uc2 They said:\par \par \uc2 If these talks are to lead to a successful outcome, the \uc2 UK government\uc2  must make good on its promises to steelworkers by supporting the huge investments required to successfully transition to greener steelmaking.\par \par \uc2 The Labour government\u8217\'20\'19s talks with Jingye have proven to be difficult, with sources with knowledge of the situation suggesting that Jingye is driving a hard bargain.\par \par \uc2 The government is even considering nationalisation as one option \u8211\'20\'13 although it is not seen as the preferred route.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 At least 11 picks for strategic positions after Trump returns to the White House in January have either achieved billionaire status themselves, have billionaire spouses or are within touching distance of that threshold.\par \par \uc2 The net result will be the wealthiest administration in US history \u8211\'20\'13 worth a total of $340bn at the start of this week, before Trump further boosted its monetary value by trying to appoint at least three more billionaires.\par \par \uc2 One of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s latest appointments was podcaster and former \uc2 PayPal\uc2  chief operating officer \uc2 David Sacks\uc2 . Sacks will be \u8220\'20\'1Cartificial intelligence and crypto tsar\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Sacks, a venture capitalist and Silicon Valley insider, hosted big spenders at his San Francisco mansion in June to support the Trump campaign, with tickets ranging up to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/newsletters/2024-05-31/trump-conviction-doesn-t-matter-to-elon-musk-david-sacks-tech-backers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $300,000 a head }}}\uc2 . The event reportedly raked in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/06/06/us/politics/trump-sacks-silicon-valley-donors.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than $12m }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 You can read the full story on the wealth of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s cabinet here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/dec/06/trump-us-cabinet-billionaires"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump assembling US cabinet of billionaires worth combined $340bn }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 A clutch of bidders have expressed interest in buying \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 , after its initial owners essentially said they will walk away with nothing.\par \par \uc2 However, any buyer \u8211\'20\'13 including {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/dec/06/aviva-direct-line-thames-water-suez-covalis-us-non-farm-payrolls-nonfarm-jobs-numbers-ftse-100-business-live?page=with:block-67529a828f08065f386f1f21#block-67529a828f08065f386f1f21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the newly public Covalis bid }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 will have to negotiate a complex regulatory and political process in order to take ownership. And workers at the sprawling utility will have their say.\par \par \uc2 Gary Carter, national officer at GMB, which represents water workers, said:\par \par \uc2 Any plan to takeover Thames and break it up would be a disaster for consumers and workers. These bids won\u8217\'20\'19t stop the leaks nor pollution \u8211\'20\'13 they will only line the pockets of those who want to break it up.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  needs considered, long-term investment to repair its infrastructure, stop the leaks and provide stability; not the plundering of its assets to make a quick buck.\par \par \uc2 The government has to stop this vulture auction and take control of Thames in the interest of the public.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  official calls for lower interest rates\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  should be cutting interest rates by more, a member of the rate-setting monetary policy committee member has said.\par \par \uc2 Swati Dhingra, an external member of the committee, said high interest rates had hurt consumption and business investment, and damaged the supply capacity of Britain\u8217\'20\'19s economy, according to Reuters. In an interview on Friday with Bloomberg TV, she said:\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why I think we should be easing policy more.\par \par \uc2 Dhingra said that the UK has limited direct exposure to US tariffs, but added that the indirect exposure may be greater. Tariffs could lead to a return of supply chain disruption that we\u8217\'20\'19ve seen in recent years, she said.\par \par \uc2 She estimated that the neutral rate \u8211\'20\'13 the rate at which monetary policy is neither restrictive nor expansionary, was between 2.5% and 3.5%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 Quiz shares slump by 40% as it warns it could run out of cash\par \par \uc2 Glasgow-based {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2019/dec/04/quiz-fashion-brand-reports-loss-high-street-sales-slump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 clothing retailer Quiz }}}\uc2  has said it could run out of cash early next year after a \u8220\'20\'1Cmarked decline\u8221\'20\'1D in trade both online and in stores in October and November.\par \par \uc2 The company\u8217\'20\'19s share price has fallen by 44% to 2.35p. It was only valued at \u163\'00\'A36.6m on Thursday evening.\par \par \uc2 The business, which has more than 70 stores in the UK and Ireland as well as concessions in New Look and outlets in the Middle East, said it had appointed\par \par \uc2 advisers to \u8220\'20\'1Cconsider appropriate options\u8221\'20\'1D from both a financing and strategic point of view.\par \par \uc2 In a statement issued to the stock market, Quiz said that changes in the budget had combined with poor autumn trading to put pressure on its balance sheet.\par \par \uc2 The company continues to proactively manage its cost base and identify opportunities to improve performance and profits. However, ongoing improvements being made in these areas will be offset by the recent proposed changes to the National Living Wage and Employer\u8217\'20\'19s National Insurance arrangements, resulting in circa \u163\'00\'A31.7m per annum of\par \par \uc2 additional costs from April 2025.\par \par \uc2 Quiz said it was managing its working capital carefully but currently had just \u163\'00\'A31.2m\par \par \uc2 of headroom within its \u163\'00\'A34m borrowing facilities.\par \par \uc2 Tarak Ramzan\uc2 , the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2017/jul/20/quiz-flotation-set-to-value-fashion-retail-chain-at-200m"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 company\u8217\'20\'19s founder and largest shareholder }}}\uc2 , has offered to provide a\par \par \uc2 \u163\'00\'A31m loan to assist the company but this is subject to approval from Quiz\u8217\'20\'19s other lenders.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 Guardian Media Group\uc2  agrees sale of Observer to Tortoise Media\par \par \uc2 The sale of the Observer, the world\u8217\'20\'19s oldest Sunday newspaper, to Tortoise Media has been agreed in principle.\par \par \uc2 The announcement by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/the-scott-trust/2015/jul/26/the-scott-trust-board"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Scott Trust }}}\uc2 , the ultimate owner of the Guardian and Observer, came as it also revealed that it would invest in Tortoise to become a key shareholder and take a seat on both the editorial and commercial boards of the media company.\par \par \uc2 The Trust said the new ownership model would \u8220\'20\'1Cprotect the Observer\u8217\'20\'19s future, championing the voice of liberal values and investing in exceptional journalism while building its digital offering\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe knew we needed the right combination of resources and commitment to build a new platform for the Observer,\u8221\'20\'1D said the Scott Trust chair, Ole Jacob Sunde.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt required an ally to be sufficiently funded, long-term in nature and respect editorial independence and liberal values. I believe we have found this in Tortoise Media. We are looking forward to being part of the next phase in the Observer\u8217\'20\'19s journey.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Observer staff are due to meet managers today.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/dec/06/sale-of-observer-to-tortoise-media-agreed-in-principle"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sale of the Observer to Tortoise Media agreed in principle }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 The eurozone economy grew by 0.4% in the third quarter of 2024, according to a third estimate of GDP.\par \par \uc2 That was an acceleration compared with the 0.2% growth in the second quarter, according to \uc2 EU statistics office Eurostat\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, despite the acceleration, the eurozone economy only grew by 0.9% compared with the same quarter of the previous year \u8211\'20\'13 a relatively meagre growth rate. Growth has been held back in particular by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/nov/15/germany-shrinking-gdp-economy-growth-france-eu-commission"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Germany, where economic growth lagged }}}\uc2  at 0.1% in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 Ireland (+3.5%) recorded the highest increase of GDP compared to the previous quarter \u8211\'20\'13 although Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s economic figures are heavily caveated because of the infuence of tech companies\u8217\'20\'19 offshore holding companies. It was followed by Denmark and Lithuania (both +1.2%). The highest decreases were observed in Hungary (-0.7%) and Latvia (-0.2%).\par \par \uc2 Household spending was a big contributor, increasing by 0.7% in the euro area.\par \par \uc2 However, exports decreased by 1.5% in the euro area in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.16am GMT\par \par \uc2 The UK may not retaliate if \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  imposes tariffs on British goods imports, the business secretary has said.\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Reynolds said that the UK could be relatively sheltered from Trump\u8217\'20\'19s ire because the US runs a goods trade surplus, exporting more goods to Britain than it imports the other way.\par \par \uc2 But if Trump does impose tariffs on all countries, the UK would not automatically respond, Reynolds said in an interview with the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/68e5de84-163d-4488-821a-def2d556a41c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Financial Times }}}\uc2 . He said:\par \par \uc2 In this country there\u8217\'20\'19s no political constituency for protectionism.\par \par \uc2 Increasing costs of goods or food for your constituents is not attractive.\par \par \uc2 Reynolds also cautioned that the prospects for a trade deal between the US and UK could be tricky, given the \u8220\'20\'1Cvery different regulatory regimes for agriculture and food\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.39am GMT\par \par \uc2 British trains able to run normally once underway says National Rail\par \par \uc2 National Rail has given an update on the rail disruption: it appears to be limited mainly to trains setting off for their first journey, although there could be knock-on delays through the day.\par \par \uc2 Its update said:\par \par \uc2 This issue is mainly affecting trains on some routes leaving the depot to start their service. However, trains can run normally once they are underway.\par \par \uc2 Short-notice cancellations and alterations are expected due to the knock-on effect on the timetables. Please check before you travel, allow extra time for your journey and monitor live departure boards.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.25am GMT\par \par \uc2 Mike Ashley\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Frasers Group\uc2  is in the market for another retailer: this time it\u8217\'20\'19s Norwegian sporting goods retailer \uc2 XXL ASA.\par \par \uc2 Frasers\uc2  said that it is launching a hostile takeover of the brand because it disagreed with the company\u8217\'20\'19s plan to issue new shares, which would dilute \uc2 Frasers\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 existing shareholding.\par \par \uc2 Frasers\uc2 , the bulk of whose earnings come via Sports Direct, is XXL\u8217\'20\'19s second largest shareholder, with about 32.5% of the voting rights and about 25.8% of the issued share capital.\par \par \uc2 It said that it opposed a share issue by XXL which would have sought to raise 600m Norwegian krone (\u163\'00\'A342m). It said in a statement to Oslo\u8217\'20\'19s stock market:\par \par \uc2 We believe that the proposed alternative rights Issue is wrong, its legality is questionable and its implementation will be extremely detrimental to both \uc2 Frasers\uc2  and the other minority holders of XXL shares, who will be unfairly and significantly diluted by the commission shares to be issued under the terms of the Alternative Rights Issue.\par \par \uc2 In addition, we do not believe that shareholders, especially minority shareholders, should be asked to provide further funding to XXL when it has not articulated any clear plan to address and resolve the root causes of its persistent problems.\par \par \uc2 Michael Murray\uc2 , \uc2 Frasers\uc2  chief executive (and Ashley\u8217\'20\'19s son-in-law), said:\par \par \uc2 Our strategic vision and industry experience position us uniquely to help XXL navigate its current challenges. We are committed to ensuring that XXL reaches its full potential.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.38am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK house prices hit new record high of \u163\'00\'A3298,083 says Halifax\par \par \uc2 The average price of a house in the UK has hit a record high as homeowners experience a fifth successive month of increases in the value of their properties, Britain\u8217\'20\'19s biggest mortgage lender has said.\par \par \uc2 Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s monthly house price index found that the cost of an average home stood at \u163\'00\'A3298,083 in November, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/nov/07/property-prices-record-highs-budget-slow"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 up almost \u163\'00\'A35,000 }}}\uc2  on the previous record set in October.\par \par \uc2 Prior to the latest rise, the record average house price was set in June 2022, at 293,507.\par \par \uc2 House prices increased by 1.3% in November, the biggest increase this year and the fifth consecutive monthly rise.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis property prices are up 4.8%, the highest rate of increase since November 2022.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Bryden, the head of mortgages at Halifax, said:\par \par \uc2 Latest figures continue to show improving levels of demand for mortgages, as an easing in mortgage rates boost buyer confidence. However, despite these positive trends, many potential buyers and movers still face significant affordability challenges and buyer confidence may be tested against a changeable economic backdrop.\par \par \uc2 As we move towards the end of the year and into 2025, positive employment figures and anticipated decreases in interest rates are expected to continue supporting demand. This should underpin further house price growth, albeit at a modest pace as borrowing costs remain above the average of a few years ago.\par \par \uc2 You can read the full story here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/06/average-uk-house-price-hits-record-says-halifax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Average UK house price hits record \u163\'00\'A3298,083, says Halifax }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30am GMT\par \par \uc2 Here is the full statement from Suez on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/dec/06/aviva-direct-line-thames-water-suez-covalis-us-non-farm-payrolls-nonfarm-jobs-numbers-ftse-100-business-live?page=with:block-67529a828f08065f386f1f21#block-67529a828f08065f386f1f21"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bid by Covalis for Thames Water }}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 In exclusive partnership with Covalis, \uc2 Suez\uc2  is submitting a non-binding offer to advise and assist \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  by leveraging \uc2 Suez\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s expertise in technical advisory and organizational optimization.\par \par \uc2 At this stage, \uc2 Suez\uc2  scope of work is limited to advisory mission to ensure the project\u8217\'20\'19s success and address the specific challenges faced by \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Suez\uc2  attaches great importance to support \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  in its operational recovery and long-term sustainability in alignment with regulatory expectations.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.13am GMT\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 in London has edged down in the opening trades by five points \u8211\'20\'13 less than 0.1% \u8211\'20\'13 to 8,344.\par \par \uc2 Direct Line Group\uc2  has jumped by 7.6% to \u163\'00\'A32.53 \u8211\'20\'13 although it remains well off the \u163\'00\'A32.75 offer price, suggesting investors may have questions over whether the deal will be completed.\par \par \uc2 Here are the opening snaps from across Europe\u8217\'20\'19s major stock indices:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 EUROPE\u8217\'20\'19S STOXX 600 FLAT\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 FRANCE\u8217\'20\'19S CAC 40 UP 0.1%; SPAIN\u8217\'20\'19S IBEX DOWN 0.2%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 EURO STOXX INDEX DOWN FLAT; EURO ZONE BLUE CHIPS DOWN 0.1%\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 GERMANY\u8217\'20\'19S DAX UP 0.1%\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Trains across Great Britain could be disrupted by nationwide communications fault\par \par \uc2 Trains across Great Britain may be disrupted this morning because of a nationwide fault with the radio systems used by drivers to communicate with signaller, National Rail has reported.\par \par \uc2 National Rail, which is run by train operating companies, said that all National Rail routes may be affected, in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nationalrail.co.uk/service-disruptions/onboard-communications-20241206/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 statement on its website }}}\uc2 . It said it was investigating the problem.\par \par \uc2 It said:\par \par \uc2 There is a nationwide fault with the communication system used between train drivers and signallers. As a result, services across the National Rail network may be subject to disruption this morning.\par \par \uc2 Trains across the network are having to start their journeys later because of this fault and some may also be subject to cancellations or alterations. Please check before you travel, allow extra time for your journey and monitor live departure boards.\par \par \uc2 The lines affected included London\u8217\'20\'19s Elizabeth line \u8211\'20\'13 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s most popular line \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 ScotRail\uc2 , and services across England.\par \par \uc2 National Rail said the problem was with the GSMR system, or the Global System for Mobile Communications-Railway. The technology is designed to deliver \u8220\'20\'1Cdigital, secure and dependable communications between drivers and signallers\u8221\'20\'1D including within tunnels and deep cuttings, according to \uc2 Network Rail\uc2 , which runs the UK\u8217\'20\'19s rail tracks.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 Aviva agrees \u163\'00\'A33.6bn Direct Line takeover; Suez lined up to manage \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  in bid\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome back to our love coverage of business, economics and financial markets ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/2024/dec/04/journalists-strike-proposed-sale-observer-tortoise-media-guardian"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 after a two-day break }}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 FTSE 100 insurer Aviva has agreed to buy rival Direct Line in a \u163\'00\'A33.6bn cash and shares deal after a sweetened offer.\par \par \uc2 Direct Line\u8217\'20\'19s board said that it would be minded to accept the offer of \u163\'00\'A32.75 per share, up from Aviva\u8217\'20\'19s first bid of \u163\'00\'A32.50 per share that valued its FTSE 250 rival at \u163\'00\'A33.3bn.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/dec/01/direct-line-takeover-target-aviva-bid-adam-winslow-ceo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Direct Line had argued }}}\uc2  that it could turn its performance around, after slumping in recent years. It rejected the first offer, saying it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/27/direct-line-rejects-aviva-takeover-offer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 substantially undervalued the company }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The latest offer is a 73% premium to the closing price before the first bid was announced, the companies said in a joint stock market statement on Friday morning. The companies said:\par \par \uc2 The Direct Line board believes that, in addition to the attractive headline value per share, the combination would provide the opportunity to deliver significant synergies, creating substantial additional value for both sets of shareholders.\par \par \uc2 Aviva has until 5pm on \u8211\'20\'13 ah \u8211\'20\'13 Christmas day to make a firm offer or walk away.\par \par \uc2 Another \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  bidder emerges\par \par \uc2 French utility \uc2 Suez\uc2  could reportedly be brought in to manage the struggling \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  as part of a new \u163\'00\'A35bn bid by an infrastructure investor.\par \par \uc2 Covalis Capital has submitted a bid to \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 , the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  reported, as it looks for new owners and tries to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/13/thames-water-gains-key-support-from-creditors-for-funding-lifeline"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 agree an emergency debt package }}}\uc2  to avoid temporary nationalisation.\par \par \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  provides water and sewage services to 16 million customers across London and the Thames Valley in south-east England. It has been on the verge of collapse for several months, labouring under its \u163\'00\'A319bn debt pile.\par \par \uc2 The investor plans to break up \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  and list the remainder on the stock market, while giving the \uc2 UK government\uc2  a seat on the board, according to people cited by the FT. It reported:\par \par \uc2 Covalis would provide about \u163\'00\'A31bn up front on agreement of the deal, the people added. The London-based investor would then raise another \u163\'00\'A34bn from asset sales, refinancing and the listing, which is expected in two to three years\u8217\'20\'19 time.\par \par \uc2 Under the deal \uc2 Suez\uc2  would only function as a service provider. \uc2 Suez\uc2  said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 At this stage, \uc2 Suez\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s scope of work is limited to [an] advisory mission to ensure the project\u8217\'20\'19s success and address the specific challenges faced by \uc2 Thames Water\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The FT reported that other potential bidders for \uc2 Thames Water\uc2  could be Hong Kong-based firm \uc2 CK Infrastructure Holdings\uc2 , the owner of \uc2 Northumbrian Water\uc2 , and Castle Water, which is owned by Conservative party treasurer Graham Edwards.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Eurozone GDP growth third estimate (third quarter; previous: 0.2% quarter-on-quarter; consensus: 0.4%)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1:30pm GMT: US non-farm payrolls (November; prev.: 12,000 jobs; cons.: 200,000)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.29am GMT\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241206ekc60012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
