\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 How did transgender children in the US become so politicized?\b0\par\par\uc2 Moira Donegan \par \uc2 1,044 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 December 2024\par 16:20 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  is likely to uphold a flimsy and bad-faith Tennessee law banning gender-affirming care for minors\par \par \uc2 The politicization of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/transgender"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 transgender }}}\uc2  children in the US is one of the most astounding coups of propaganda and organized animus in recent history. Rarely has so much attention and rage been directed at such a minuscule number of people, and more rarely, still, have those people been the most vulnerable and blameless among us: kids and teens.\par \par \uc2 The first state to pass a ban on transition-related care for minors was Arkansas, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.kff.org/other/dashboard/gender-affirming-care-policy-tracker/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 in April 2021 }}}\uc2  ; less than four years later, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.hrc.org/resources/attacks-on-gender-affirming-care-by-state-map"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 more than half of states }}}\uc2  have such a ban on the books. In 2016, North Carolina lost an estimated $3.76bn in revenue following boycotts after they passed a law banning trans people, including transgender students, from using appropriate restrooms in public facilities; now, 14 states have such bathroom bans on the books, and the boycotts have receded.\par \par \uc2 These changes in public attitudes towards trans youth \u8211\'20\'13 from a broad if imperfect sentiment of tolerance to a widespread and politically weaponized attitude of hostility toward a small minority of kids \u8211\'20\'13 did not emerge by accident. It was the product of a deliberate, conscious effort to radicalize large swaths of the United States, and significant chunks of state policy, into a hostility towards a few children.\par \par \uc2 That effort seems set to bear fruit now, at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/us-supreme-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US supreme court }}}\uc2 , in US v Skrmetti, a lawsuit brought by the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  and the Biden Department of Justice challenging {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://legiscan.com/TN/text/HB0001/id/2756066"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tennessee\u8217\'20\'19s HB1 }}}\uc2 , a sweeping ban on transition-related care for minors that was passed in 2023. The law prohibits any puberty blockers or hormones from being prescribed for the purposes of gender transition, but it does not prohibit these medications from being prescribed for any non-transition-related purpose. A minor can be prescribed puberty blockers, for instance, if their doctor believes they are experiencing early onset, or \u8220\'20\'1Cprecocious\u8221\'20\'1D, puberty; they cannot be prescribed puberty blockers to delay the onset of a puberty that may change their bodies in ways they do not desire for gender identity-related reasons.\par \par \uc2 That means, too, that a child assigned male at birth could access, say, testosterone treatment, but a child assigned female at birth could not. In oral arguments on Wednesday, solicitor general \uc2 Elizabeth Prelogar\uc2  and Chase Strangio of the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 the first trans attorney to argue before the supreme court \u8211\'20\'13 explained that this was a straightforward case of sex discrimination, and hence needed to be subjected to a heightened standard of judicial review under the 14th amendment\u8217\'20\'19s equal protection clause.\par \par \uc2 It will not be. A majority of the court\u8217\'20\'19s conservatives seemed poised to uphold the ban on transgender healthcare, though for a variety of different reasons. \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  made his usual mealy-mouthed paean to states\u8217\'20\'19 rights, an argument he always makes in questions of federally guaranteed equality provisions, but not before extolling the hypothetical suffering of teenagers who may access gender-affirming care but then later come to regret it. (One wonders if there are any choices from his own adolescence that \uc2 Brett Kavanaugh\uc2  has come to regret.) \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2  and chief justice \uc2 John Roberts\uc2 , meanwhile, both advanced the idea that the physiological differences between male and female bodies could moot the equal protection clause\u8217\'20\'19s reach, giving states broad leeway to regulate medicine in ways that would uphold gender hierarchy.\par \par \uc2 For his part, \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  also seemed interested in the idea that states might have a right to effect gender discrimination via their regulation of medicine. He repeatedly cited the 1974 case {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.oyez.org/cases/1973/73-640"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Geduldig v Aiello, }}}\uc2  in which the supreme court ruled that states could discriminate on the basis of pregnancy, and that pregnancy discrimination was not sex discrimination \u8211\'20\'13 because even though only female people become pregnant, not all of them are pregnant all of the time. (At the time, Congress found the outcome in Geduldig so egregious that it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://socialchangenyu.com/review/constitutionality-of-pregnancy-discrimination-the-lingering-effects-of-geduldig-and-suggestions-for-forcing-its-reversal-the/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed a law }}}\uc2  clarifying that pregnancy discrimination does count as sex discrimination for the purposes of federal civil rights law, and the precedent was largely mooted, but Alito\u8217\'20\'19s controlling opinion in Dobbs has revived it.)\par \par \uc2 But Alito, true to form, did not confine his opining to the notion that discrimination against trans people does not count as sex-based discrimination: he went on to suggest that trans people are not quite real, peppering Strangio, in a scene that seemed intended to humiliate the trans attorney, with questions about whether trans identity was truly an \u8220\'20\'1Cimmutable\u8221\'20\'1D characteristic. For his part, Strangio responded with a dignity and respect that Alito\u8217\'20\'19s line of questioning did not merit.\par \par \uc2 It was not the only low moment. James Matthew Rice, the Tennessee solicitor general who defended the ban in court, repeatedly compared gender affirming care with suicide, as well as to lobotomies and eugenics. During his time, justices \uc2 Ketanji Brown Jackson\uc2  and \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2 , with occasional assists from \uc2 Elena Kagan\uc2 , tried to chase Rice down on the inconsistencies in his own argument.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee claimed, after all, that the law did not discriminate on the basis of patients\u8217\'20\'19 sex, but rather on the basis of the purpose of their treatment; when the liberal justices pointed out that this was a distinction without a difference, because the purpose of the treatment was dependent on the patients\u8217\'20\'19 sex, Rice simply repeated his assertion that there was a difference, there, somewhere. Jackson, in particular, worked to get Rice to explain his position for some time. He declined to.\par \par \uc2 To call the Tennessee ban sex-neutral is laughable, almost insulting. The statute itself makes gender conformity its explicit justification in its text, saying that it aims to prohibit \u8220\'20\'1Csex inconsistent treatment\u8221\'20\'1D, or anything that \u8220\'20\'1Cmight encourage minors to become disdainful of their sex\u8221\'20\'1D. The law has long included sex role stereotyping within the purview of sex discrimination; Tennessee has sought to enforce sex roles, and sexed embodiment, with the force of the state. There is no good faith reading of the law that would allow it to withstand the scrutiny that the 14th amendment requires. But luckily for Tennessee, this is not a good faith court.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Moira Donegan is a Guardian US columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241206ekc6001mk\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
