\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US grocery workers hit by rising prices: \u8216\'20\'18We\u8217\'20\'19re at the bottom of the food chain\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,587 \uc2 words\par \uc2 27 November 2024\par 18:05 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Food store employees grapple with fewer hours and inflation \u8211\'20\'13 and sound alarm at merger of two largest chainsTell us: what purchases are you making ahead of potential tariffs\par \par \uc2 Grocery prices have surged in recent years, rising by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/07/10/nx-s1-5033145/inflation-food-grocery-prices-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 almost 27% }}}\uc2  since the months before the pandemic. Workers inside grocery stores have been hit particularly hard.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re often the people down at the bottom of the food chain when it comes to wages,\u8221\'20\'1D said Conor Watson, a meat cutter at a \uc2 Kroger\uc2 -owned Fred Meyer store in Ellensburg, Washington. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd we\u8217\'20\'19re very, very impacted with these rising prices.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Witnessing inflation\u8217\'20\'19s rapid rise on shelves was \u8220\'20\'1Cincredibly frustrating\u8221\'20\'1D, he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe were, every week, going around and changing our price tags and increasing the prices on items \u8211\'20\'13 and seeing our wages do nothing.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/iag/tgs/iag445.htm#workforce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 3.2 million }}}\uc2  Americans work in grocery stores in the US. While the median hourly wage for non supervisor employees has increased from $14.91 in 2020 to $17.88 in 2024, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/spotlight/2024/scanning-grocery-store-productivity-statistics/home.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 average weekly hours }}}\uc2  dropped from 30 hours in 2020 to 28.5 last year \u8211\'20\'13 a record low \u8211\'20\'13 as grocery chains cut back workers\u8217\'20\'19 hours.\par \par \uc2 Grocery workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages are \u8220\'20\'1Cdefinitely not keeping up\u8221\'20\'1D with the \u8220\'20\'1Cprice gouging\u8221\'20\'1D committed by grocery chains, said Kyong Barry, a front-end manager at an \uc2 Albertsons\uc2 -owned \uc2 Safeway\uc2  in Auburn, Washington.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re all struggling,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s really hard. Your hours get cut. Your workload goes up because you have more stuff you have to do. The math doesn\u8217\'20\'19t jive, and even our customers are having issues trying to get groceries.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAll everyone wants\u8221\'20\'1D is to make a living, said Barry. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not asking for millions and millions of dollars. We\u8217\'20\'19re asking for a good, livable wage where you can enjoy raising your family, being able to pay your bills, being able to eat and not eat stuff that are always on sale because you can\u8217\'20\'19t afford good nutritional food.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The math doesn\u8217\'20\'19t jive\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The price of many staples have climbed far beyond the average rate of food inflation. According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://drive.google.com/file/d/11kpGfdQDhoBm56Mmbi1TTUxxnvfJK7yi/view?usp=drive_link"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 data }}}\uc2  compiled by \uc2 Food & Water Watch\uc2 , beef prices rose 44.8% between December 2019 and last month, while egg prices rose 119.5%, ham prices 53.5%, and whole chicken prices rose37%.\par \par \uc2 As grocery workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages struggled to keep up, the food and grocery corporations employing them have generated billions of dollars in profit. \u8220\'20\'1CYou just have to look at the numbers to see that corporations in the food industry: they\u8217\'20\'19re making windfall profits above and beyond what they need to cover any kind of rising costs,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Amanda Starbuck\uc2 , research director at \uc2 Food & Water Watch\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe last thing that we need is to give them more control and more of a monopoly, especially once you get to a certain point where they can afford to take losses to keep competition down. That\u8217\'20\'19s when it becomes almost impossible to fight back,\u8221\'20\'1D said Watson, the \uc2 Kroger\uc2  worker.\par \par \uc2 And yet, \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 two of the largest grocery chains in the US \u8211\'20\'13 have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/26/kroger-albertsons-grocery-merger-blocked-ftc"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 proposed a merger }}}\uc2 . Their plan is being opposed by the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW)\uc2 , which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ufcw.org/who-we-represent/grocery/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represents }}}\uc2  835,000 grocery workers across the US and Canada. Challenges against the merger are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cpr.org/2024/10/25/kroger-albertsons-merger-trial-closing-arguments/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 currently pending }}}\uc2  in state and federal courts.\par \par \uc2 Kroger\uc2  and \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  have already {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://sherwood.news/business/kroger-albertsons-merger-deal-spending/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spent over $1bn }}}\uc2  on the merger. During a hearing on the merger, one \uc2 Kroger\uc2  executive {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-08-27/kroger-hiked-milk-egg-prices-above-inflation-merger-judge-told"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 admitted }}}\uc2  that the company had increased prices on milk and eggs by more than was necessary to account for inflation.\par \par \uc2 The company refuted claims the testimony admitted price gouging and claimed the merger was \u8220\'20\'1Cpro-union\u8221\'20\'1D as the company has added 100,000 unionized jobs since 2012.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Kroger\uc2  committed to build on this work by investing $1bn to raise associate wages and comprehensive benefits and ensuring zero layoffs or store closures related to the merger,\u8221\'20\'1D said a \uc2 Kroger\uc2  spokesperson.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAdditionally, \uc2 Kroger\uc2 , \uc2 Albertsons\uc2  and C&S have committed to honoring all current collective bargaining agreements. The only winners if this merger is blocked will be larger, non-unionized retailers who will continue to fight union growth.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That same greed that is driving prices going up is also driving the diminished standards in the stores\par \par \uc2 Joe Mizrahi\par \par \uc2 But opponents have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/dec/12/us-supermarket-merger-kroger-albertsons"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 argued }}}\uc2  the merger will result in less competition, store closures, job losses and higher prices for consumers, and noted promises made by the company in favor of the merger are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.economicliberties.us/press-release/empty-promises-big-paydays-kroger-and-albertsons-ceos-fail-to-justify-largest-supermarket-merger-in-history/#:~:text=%E2%80%9CWhile%20Kroger's%20CEO%20made%20lofty,the%20American%20Economic%20Liberties%20Project."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 not legally enforceable }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of this inflation is driven by greed. And I think it\u8217\'20\'19s not a stretch to say that a lot of that is because of the consolidation in the grocery industry,\u8221\'20\'1D said Joe Mizrahi, secretary treasurer at UFCW Local 21 in Seattle. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t have the check of having enough competition. So obviously a merger would make that problem even worse for the consumer and in our estimation, drive prices up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Inside grocery stores, many customers are feeling \u8220\'20\'1Clike there\u8217\'20\'19s a race to the bottom\u8221\'20\'1D, with \u8220\'20\'1Clonger checkout lines, more self checkouts, fewer people in the aisles\u8221\'20\'1D, added Mizrahi. \u8220\'20\'1CI do think that actually unionization is one of the biggest backstops we have against that. That same greed that is driving prices going up is also driving the diminished standards in the stores.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Prioritizing profits\par \par \uc2 Amid widespread corporate consolidation in the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/ng-interactive/2021/jul/14/food-monopoly-meals-profits-data-investigation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 food  }}}\uc2  and grocery retail industry, alternative and specialty grocery chains have gained popularity in recent years. But despite marketing and branding efforts painting many of these firms as progressive and ethical, workers describe similar issues \u8211\'20\'13 low pay, under-staffing, poor working conditions and union opposition \u8211\'20\'13 as their colleagues at larger retailers.\par \par \uc2 For the past several years, workers at Trader Joe\u8217\'20\'19s have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/sep/24/trader-joes-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushed }}}\uc2  for a first union contract. The \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) has fielded numerous unfair labor practice charges against the chain, and its opposition to unions. Grocery workers at the Oregon supermarket chain New Seasons have also {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nslu.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faced similar }}}\uc2  opposition.\par \par \uc2 In May, workers at a \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  store in Norman, Oklahoma, became the first store in the company to successfully {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.coworker.org/petitions/natural-grocers-uphold-your-commitments-to-crew-and-community"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 unionize }}}\uc2  with \uc2 UFCW\uc2 . \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  is an organic supermarket {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.naturalgrocers.com/our-story"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 chain }}}\uc2  with 168 stores across 21 states.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of lip service, and talking about, the kind of company they want to be,\u8221\'20\'1D said a \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  employee who requested to remain anonymous for fear of retaliation. \u8220\'20\'1CBut when it actually came down to having a contract that would hold the company account, having a unionized store, that was so out of left field and scary for them that, they\u8217\'20\'19ve been very frantic. They were so shocked when it passed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In reality, they are a company, much like other publicly traded companies, that prioritizes profits and pleasing shareholders over fair treatment\par \par \uc2 Ben Renfrow\par \par \uc2 Workers claimed the company aggressively opposed the unionization efforts, hiring union avoidance consultants and holding captive audience anti-union meetings in a storage closet at the store, though the company denied this. They cited stagnant pay, high healthcare costs, and unfair policy changes and enforcement as driving factors to unionize.\par \par \uc2 A subsequent unionizing effort in Tucson, Arizona, came up short, although challenged ballots that could be determinative and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/28-RC-345142"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 objections }}}\uc2  to the election are still under review at the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 William Gamble had worked at \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  since 2014, and was one of several workers fired before the union election was held. Workers allege this was prompted by their mobilization efforts.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe had almost no income coming in for two months with the kids, terrifying,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe applied for [nutrition program] {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fns.usda.gov/wic"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 WIC }}}\uc2  and food stamps. We came very close to losing our home.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey bankrupted all of us. We were living check to check, and they just threw us out \u8211\'20\'13 after some of us [had] almost a decade of service to the company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Several unfair labor practice charges were {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/search/case/natural%20grocers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2  by workers against the company that are also currently under review, including allegations of firing workers in retaliation. The union is petitioning to demand those workers be reinstated.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere was so much pressure from them, and so much kind of emotional manipulation. People felt scared,\u8221\'20\'1D said Ben Renfrow, a former employee at the Oklahoma store involved in the union effort. \u8220\'20\'1CThey want to be able to maintain the image of being an ethical alternative grocery store and profit from that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn reality, they are a company, much like other publicly traded companies, that prioritizes profits and pleasing shareholders over fair treatment.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  denied all allegations in the unfair labor practice charges, including allegations of firing workers in retaliation for organizing, and of anti-union bias, but did not disclose how much money the company spent in response to the union drives.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe suggestion that \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  engages in favoritism and anti-union bias couldn\u8217\'20\'19t be further from the truth and we unequivocally deny these allegations,\u8221\'20\'1D said a \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  spokesperson, Katie Macarelli. \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  is a success because it treats employees fairly. All claims of unfair labor practices by \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  are baseless.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn Norman, Oklahoma, a thin majority of crew members voted to organize, and \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  is currently bargaining a contract; whereas, in Tucson, the employees voted to reject unionization after a free and fair election. In both instances, \uc2 Natural Grocers\uc2  continued to treat all employees in those stores with the same dignity and respect it treats all employees in the organization.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241127ekbr001ur\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
