\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Philadelphia workers at \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  file for first union election\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 620 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 November 2024\par 15:21 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has aggressively opposed efforts and is challenging the federal labor watchdog that ruled against its actions\par \par \uc2 Workers at a \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  store in Philadelphia, Pennsylvania, have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/case/04-RC-355267"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 filed }}}\uc2  to hold a union election, the first union filing at the supermarket chain since \uc2 Amazon{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2017/06/16/business/dealbook/amazon-whole-foods.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 acquired }}}\uc2  it in 2017.\par \par \uc2 The union drive comes as \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has aggressively opposed unionization efforts and is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/legal/government/amazon-spacex-challenges-nlrb-may-be-thrown-out-appeals-court-2024-11-18/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 challenging }}}\uc2  the constitutionality of the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ), the federal labor watchdog that has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nlrb.gov/news-outreach/news-story/board-rules-captive-audience-meetings-unlawful"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ruled against }}}\uc2  the company\u8217\'20\'19s anti-union actions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis company makes billions of dollars in profits every year,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Ben Lovett\uc2 , a \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  store employee in Philadelphia who along with 300 colleagues is hoping to join the \uc2 United Food and Commercial Workers (UFCW)\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CAnd my coworkers and I are not paid anywhere near a living wage for living in Philadelphia. I\u8217\'20\'19ve worked two jobs. Many of my coworkers do two jobs, even three jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Workers at the store had organized around safety issues at the beginning of the Covid-19 pandemic, said Lovett, but about a year ago workers began getting together to discuss unionizing in response to issues such as low pay, high workloads and intense productivity demands he compared to those reported in \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouses.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ve seen the cost of living going up. We\u8217\'20\'19ve seen \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  raising the price of groceries. We cannot really afford to buy our groceries at \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2 . I don\u8217\'20\'19t really know any of my coworkers who buy their groceries at the store that we work at,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CWe want other \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  and \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers to see us going up against one of the largest companies in the world. This is a scary thing to do, but we want to show other workers it is possible.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Piper, another worker at the \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  store for about three years, said the union effort has come as workers at the store feel overworked and constantly under pressure from management to be doing more.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know myself and a lot of my coworkers are always coming to work, we always try our best, and it feels like we\u8217\'20\'19re being discouraged by leadership. It\u8217\'20\'19s never enough,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CI loved during Covid when we were essential workers and now it\u8217\'20\'19s oh, we just work in grocery stores again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Ed Dupree, an employee in the produce department for eight years, said working conditions had worsened over his time at the store.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve been working here for a while, and it\u8217\'20\'19s just been seeing the degradation of the workplace, seeing a lot of my co-workers new and old struggling,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 A date for the union election has yet to be set, but \uc2 UFCW\uc2  Local 1776 president Wendell Young IV said the union expects a quick decision and date to be set by the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn this country, due to a conservative watering down and defanging of laws that were designed to protect workers, often employers are pretty harsh on those workers that want to organize,\u8221\'20\'1D said Young. He said \uc2 Amazon\uc2  had \u8220\'20\'1Cdone a lot to create the environment for these folks to demand better and want to have a union,\u8221\'20\'1D and that the union was \u8220\'20\'1Creally proud\u8221\'20\'1D of the workers who were standing up for their rights.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 Whole Foods\uc2  said in an email: \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Whole Foods Market\uc2  recognizes the rights of our team members to make an informed decision on whether union representation is right for them. We agree with the overwhelming majority of our team members who value our open door policy and our ability to quickly respond to the needs of our workforce.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241126ekbq001mn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
