\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 California voters reject $18 minimum wage ballot measure\b0\par\par\uc2 Guardian staff and agencies \par \uc2 345 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 November 2024\par 17:15 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 State narrowly votes against measure that would have benefited 2 million workers\par \par \uc2 Voters in California have rejected a ballot measure that would have made the state minimum wage the highest in the US by 2026, at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/minimum-wage-california-ballot-measure-c430b0821884b1f53d62f5cf4178143c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $18 an hour }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The current minimum wage rates are $16 an hour for most workers and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/california-fast-food-minimum-wage-a04c2e559b09cbcd26dd5702e0755a83"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $20 in the fast-food sector }}}\uc2 . The state\u8217\'20\'19s healthcare sector will eventually see its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/california-health-care-minimum-wage-delay-ba84849b7c0a8e96ed9d7da0c126395d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 minimum wage reach $25 an hour }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But voters narrowly rejected a measure to lift salaries for a broader swath of workers. The Associated Press called the vote on Tuesday evening, with 49.2% of state voters voting \u8220\'20\'1Cyes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Proponents estimated that the measure would have benefited 2 million workers, including hotel and grocery employees.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CProposition 32\u8217\'20\'19s failure to pass is disappointing for all Californians who believe that everyone who works should earn enough to support their families,\u8221\'20\'1D said Kathy Finn, president of \uc2 UFCW\uc2  770, a southern California union representing nearly 30,000 workers in various sectors.\par \par \uc2 Opponents of the measure had argued it would have increased costs, led to higher taxes and pushed businesses to cut jobs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith the economy and costs top of mind for many voters this election, that message appears to have resonated,\u8221\'20\'1D said Jennifer Barrera, the president and CEO of the California chamber of commerce.\par \par \uc2 Polling by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/projects/election-results-2024/california/?r=0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Associated Press }}}\uc2  and Norc at the \uc2 University of Chicago\uc2  suggest that California voters saw the economy as the most important issue facing the country, ahead of immigration, the climate and abortion access.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, California became the first state to pass a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/general-news-5fa6dea5b7f14fff8836400785d92d71"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $15 hourly minimum wage }}}\uc2  under a law signed by Jerry Brown, the governor at the time. About 40 cities and counties already have minimum wages higher than the statewide rate, and six of them require minimums above $18 an hour as of this year.\par \par \uc2 Hawaii passed a law in 2022 to gradually increase the statewide minimum wage to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/hawaii-david-ige-minimum-wage-tax-refunds-7d6aa36ae50a023115e2c219e38ab034"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $18 an hour }}}\uc2 , but it does not take effect until 2028.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241120ekbk002s1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
FF
