\par\par \uc2 Politics\par \b \uc2 Federal workers brace for Trump with morale \u8216\'20\'18as low as it\u8217\'20\'19s ever been\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,003 \uc2 words\par \uc2 18 November 2024\par 17:27 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 President-elect has expressed desire for mass firings of civil service workers to be replaced with political appointees\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s return to office sets the stage for harassment, intimidation and \u8220\'20\'1Cold-fashioned corruption\u8221\'20\'1D to spread throughout the \uc2 US federal government\uc2 , a top union leader has warned.\par \par \uc2 The president-elect and his allies have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/biden-2024-government-regulations-democrats-6badc3b424b9eff3ba51e0ec35a8d824"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 expressed support }}}\uc2  for the mass firing of civil service workers and abolishing certain government agencies upon his return to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Officials at the heart of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s first administration have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.axios.com/2022/07/22/trump-2025-radical-plan-second-term"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 spoken }}}\uc2  of purging thousands of federal workers by using controversial powers to reshape the bureaucracy. Now, inside dozens of government agencies, staff are bracing for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s second administration.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty bleak,\u8221\'20\'1D said Steve Lenkart, executive director of the National Federation of Federal Employees, which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nffe.org/about/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 represents 110,000 employees  }}}\uc2  at various agencies around the US. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s pretty grim.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The former president attacked federal employees on the campaign trail, claiming they were \u8220\'20\'1Cdestroying this country\u8221\'20\'1D in an August {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=G80iLTctFuY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 podcast interview }}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re crooked people, they\u8217\'20\'19re dishonest people,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re going to be held accountable.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump has signaled his support for reviving the Schedule F executive order he issued in the final days of his last administration, under which swaths of the federal workforce could be stripped of their civil servant protections and redefined as political appointees. Advocates of the policy claimed to have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.govexec.com/workforce/2022/07/trump-endorsed-plan-purge-civil-service-rogue-bureaucrats/375028/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 identified }}}\uc2  50,000 employees that could be fired.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s probably been 150 years since we had something like this happen,\u8221\'20\'1D Lenkart said, pointing to the Pendleton Act, which in 1883 mandated that federal employees be hired based on merit, subject to competitive exams, and protected from political influences.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne hundred and fifty years of hard lessons learned are all erased,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CAnd now you\u8217\'20\'19ve taken away that ability for federal employees to report bad things they see; you take away the ability of an employee to resist an illegal order, or an order that\u8217\'20\'19s a political overreach of some kind, or simply a threat of old-fashioned corruption, stealing stuff, helping someone gain a market or consolidate their personal wealth.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s old-fashioned corruption that is going to come back, too, because of people looking to make money off of this authority that federal employees have.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Schedule F allows an unlimited number of political appointees to be hired into the federal government, as direct hires with no expiration date based on presidential terms, Lenkart said.\par \par \uc2 The success of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/nov/04/what-is-project-2025-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025 }}}\uc2 , the rightwing plan for Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidency, \u8220\'20\'1Crevolves around\u8221\'20\'1D the implementation of Schedule F, he added: reclassifying roles typically filled by career government employees as political, \u8220\'20\'1Cand having the ability to, you know, threaten, harass, intimidate or fire them\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Emboldened by a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/ng-interactive/2024/jul/01/supreme-court-decision-trump-immunity-ruling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supreme court decision }}}\uc2  earlier this year that former presidents are entitled to some degree of immunity from criminal prosecution, Trump is \u8220\'20\'1Cgoing to do some pretty amazing things that we\u8217\'20\'19ve never seen before, and I think it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be pretty horrible\u8221\'20\'1D, said Lenkart.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.afge.org/article/union-membership-in-federal-sector-went-up-in-2023/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 More than 1 million federal employees }}}\uc2  in the US are unionized, according to the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2 , {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/08/15/nx-s1-5052728/federal-labor-unions-trump-project-f-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 about half }}}\uc2  of the 2.1 million-strong civilian workforce in the federal government.\par \par \uc2 During his first administration, Trump chipped away at labor rights inside the federal government, shortening the time frame for collective bargaining, directing agencies not to bargain over certain issues, limiting the use of official work time for union business by federal employees and scaling back due process protections for employees.\par \par \uc2 At federal agencies, unions expect such measures to return. Inside the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2  (\uc2 NLRB\uc2 ) \u8211\'20\'13 the government agency tasked with enforcing federal labor laws and protecting the rights of workers across the US \u8211\'20\'13 the workforce is worried.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMorale is about as low as it\u8217\'20\'19s ever been,\u8221\'20\'1D said Michael Bilik, executive vice-president of the NLRB Union, which represents attorneys, investigators and administrative professionals in the agency\u8217\'20\'19s field offices. It is already grappling with underfunding, staffing shortages and growing delays in processing cases, he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m hearing questions, especially from new employees, like: \u8216\'20\'18Should I be looking for another job?\u8217\'20\'19 \u8216\'20\'18Am I going to be the first to go since I was the last one hired?\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D Bilik said. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think people fully understand the impact this could have, especially on smaller agencies like ours. It will further erode our day-to-day work resolving labor disputes, which benefits no one.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Limiting official time for union matters will \u8220\'20\'1Cstrain labor relations\u8221\'20\'1D within the \uc2 NLRB\uc2 ,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1COur work will suffer, our union will be weakened, and the agency\u8217\'20\'19s ability to function will be compromised if our staff and union are undermined.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re in a very precarious position right now. Our backlog is skyrocketing, and we urgently need funding and personnel \u8211\'20\'13 not punitive restrictions on our basic ability to operate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 AFGE\uc2  Local 1003, which represents non-supervisory \uc2 Environmental Protection Agency\uc2  employees in Texas and surrounding states, is braced for a tough four years. During the first Trump administration, the union at the \uc2 EPA\uc2  was operating under an expired contract, and it only {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.epa.gov/newsreleases/us-epa-signs-new-bargaining-agreement-afge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached }}}\uc2  a new one this summer.\par \par \uc2 Under Trump, managers used a directive \u8220\'20\'1Cwhich supplanted our expired contract at that time and caused a whole bunch of chaos, problems and headaches for the unions at the agency\u8221\'20\'1D, Justin Chen, president of \uc2 AFGE\uc2  Local 1003, said. \u8220\'20\'1CWe couldn\u8217\'20\'19t represent employees and grievances while on duty time. Essentially, they took away our offices and significantly shrunk our rights as the representation for the bargaining unit employees.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Inside Trump World, there has been talk of replacing federal workers with \u8220\'20\'1Cpatriots\u8221\'20\'1D who live in communities nationwide. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re already out in these communities,\u8221\'20\'1D said Chen. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re just regular folks doing our jobs out over here as best as we can. We\u8217\'20\'19ve often taken these positions because we have a love of our country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241118ekbi001s0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
