\par\par \uc2 Opinion\par \b \uc2 How a small group of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers took on big business and challenged traditional unions\b0\par\par\uc2 Kenan Malik \par \uc2 1,127 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 November 2024\par 15:42 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 A documentary about unionising the company\u8217\'20\'19s Staten Island warehouse reveals new ways of negotiating with oppressive bosses\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18The union wants to protect workers. The employer wants to protect workers. How do I choose between them?\u8221\'20\'1D So asks one young worker in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://union-db.webflow.io/#about"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Union, a documentary }}}\uc2  about the battle to unionise an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  warehouse on Staten Island, New York. It is a telling comment on the confusion today about what it means to defend working-class interests and the difficulties in trying to build working-class organisations.\par \par \uc2 Directed by Brett Story and Stephen Maing, two of the most engaging and innovatory documentary film-makers today, Union opens with a huge cargo ship piled high with containers, sailing slowly into view. The film then cuts to a line of people, half asleep in the early hours of the morning, waiting to be transported to an \uc2 Amazon\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cfulfilment centre\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 a vast warehouse stuffed full of commodities, both goods and humans. It cuts again to a shot of the \uc2 Blue Origin\uc2  rocket carrying \uc2 Amazon\uc2  owner \uc2 Jeff Bezos\uc2  and a few friends and fellow billionaires into space. It is a visual metaphor for the disparity of power that lies at the heart of the story.\par \par \uc2 Union follows a small group of \uc2 Amazon\uc2  workers and ex-workers between the summer of 2021 and the spring of the following year as they try to establish the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.amazonlaborunion.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Amazon Labour Union }}}\uc2  (ALU). The central figure in the story is Chris Smalls, a former worker at the Staten Island warehouse who was sacked after leading a protest against \uc2 Amazon\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failure to protect workers from Covid. He is charismatic and passionate, someone as comfortable in front of a camera as in a campaign meeting.\par \par \uc2 Union is, though, no hagiography. Filmed in v\u233\'00\'E9rit\u233\'00\'E9 style, with no narrator or talking heads, it is as much a portrait of the difficulties and conflicts that attend attempts to forge solidarity, as it is of the ALU. It is to the credit of Story, Maing and Smalls himself that the film shows Smalls not just as a hugely inspiring leader \u8211\'20\'13 which he is \u8211\'20\'13 but also as someone others often find exasperating and who leaves some feeling unheard. It is not simply a feelgood David and Goliath drama but an exploration of the messy reality of building solidarity, the disorderliness of democratic decision-making, the frustrations that come with challenging overwhelming odds.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  is a company with seemingly limitless resources and a long history of often {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2020/10/24/how-amazon-prevents-unions-by-surveilling-employee-activism.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devious manoeuvres }}}\uc2  aimed at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/16/technology/amazon-unions-virginia.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 crushing unions }}}\uc2 . Its tactics were all on display on Staten Island: a deluge of anti-union propaganda; constant surveillance; threats to, even sackings of, those who push for a union; the use of police to harass campaigners. That \uc2 Amazon\uc2  would rather pay millions of dollars to lawyers and union busters than provide even half-decent wages and conditions to its employees tells us much about how people and profits are valued in today\u8217\'20\'19s world. \uc2 Amazon\uc2  may be a particularly shoddy employer but it is not unique. From \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2 , from \uc2 Tesla\uc2  to \uc2 Walmart\uc2 , the same calculations apply in every dystopian workplace.\par \par \uc2 That \uc2 Amazon\uc2  would rather pay millions to lawyers\u8230\'20\'26 than provide half-decent wages says much about how profit and people are valued\par \par \uc2 Yet, despite the odds, the ALU triumphed, winning sufficient support among the warehouse workers to force \uc2 Amazon\uc2  to recognise the union in April 2022. The triumph, though, was bittersweet, revealing not just the fortitude of the campaigners but also the enormous capacity of big business to resist them. Disdaining the ALU\u8217\'20\'19s victory, \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has refused for more than two years to negotiate with the union, using its lawyers to drag out the process.\par \par \uc2 The experience of being working class is significantly different today than it would have been even half a century ago. Unionisation has plummeted (just {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/pdf/union2.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 one in 10 American workers are in a union }}}\uc2 , half the figure of that in 1983) and many people have no generational experience of being part of a labour movement. Class is perceived less as a collective identity than as a cultural identifier.\par \par \uc2 As they have lost members and power, unions themselves have transformed, their leaders preferring to cultivate political influence than to organise industrial action. \u8220\'20\'1CUnions have renounced class warfare,\u8221\'20\'1D the late sociologist and activist Stanley Aronowitz observed in his book {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.versobooks.com/en-gb/products/2386-the-death-and-life-of-american-labor?srsltid=AfmBOoonAgY3-F3R1BtQ8FRByZOeuNZij58TyDtm4XJyjEvMjuA6E4Lz"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Death and Life of American Labor }}}\uc2 , while corporations \u8220\'20\'1Cpursue it with a vengeance \u8211\'20\'13 against the workers unions are supposed to represent and defend\u8221\'20\'1D. The consequence is a disconnect with workers, many questioning the very purpose of a union.\par \par \uc2 There is equally a disconnect with, and a sense of betrayal by, political parties, from the Democrats in America to social democratic parties in Europe, which once were seen as representing working-class interests, but have long since abandoned such a commitment. That sense of betrayal, combined with a lack of any alternatives, has pushed sections of the working class towards politicians and parties that are among the most hostile to working-class interests, from the far right in Europe to Donald Trump in America.\par \par \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s presidential triumph illuminates both the failure of the old and the cynicism of the new. \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , Trump\u8217\'20\'19s new cheerleader, whom the president-elect has tasked with cutting government bureaucracy and spending, is more rabidly anti-union than even Bezos. He has refused to countenance unions at \uc2 Tesla\uc2 , threatening to remove stock options from any employee who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2021/03/25/business/musk-labor-board.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 went on strike }}}\uc2 , an act the courts have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/elon-musk-uaw-twitter-nlrb-tesla-5fab2a42b9543f7702deed80d79c7d02"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed legal }}}\uc2 , and sacked workers for union activity and for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2024/01/03/business/spacex-elon-musk-nlrb-workers.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticising his policies }}}\uc2 . Trump h {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.rollingstone.com/politics/politics-news/trump-elon-musk-administration-role-1235083514/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as praised Musk\u8217\'20\'19s willingness }}}\uc2  to sack striking workers. He has also previously claimed that US workers\u8217\'20\'19 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2015/11/12/politics/donald-trump-explains-wages-too-high-comment/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 wages are too high }}}\uc2 . Musk has joined Bezos in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/10/starbucks-trader-joes-spacex-challenge-labor-board"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 bringing a legal challenge }}}\uc2  to the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 , the federal agency that regulates collective bargaining.\par \par \uc2 Why should working-class people give their support to parties and politicians so hostile to working-class interests? For the same reason that a worker in an anonymous, alienating, algorithm-driven factory run by a company that pays execrable wages, monitors their every activity and maintains order though fear and intimidation, cannot decide whether the company or the union might better protect her. So disenchanted have many become with the traditional organisations that claimed to safeguard working-class interests, so enraged with their failures and betrayals, that they feel it more rational to look elsewhere for answers.\par \par \uc2 In such a world, an organisation such as the ALU, that shows the practical possibilities of building solidarity, of challenging corporations and of defending working-class interests without tumbling into bigotry or divisiveness, becomes more important than ever.\par \par \uc2 \u8226\'20\'22 Kenan Malik is an Observer columnist\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241120ekbh0033z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
