\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Eric Adams aide under \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation was key player at fundraiser with reported \u8216\'20\'18straw\u8217\'20\'19 donations\b0\par\par\uc2 George Joseph, Alyssa Katz, Yoav Gonen and Katie Honan \par \uc2 1,644 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2024\par 12:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Revealed: Video footage casts doubt on compliance with campaign finance laws and past characterizations of the event by the Adams campaign\par \par \uc2 Winnie Greco, a longtime Eric Adams aide now under \uc2 FBI\uc2  investigation, was a key player at a secretive fundraiser that netted the future New York mayor what appears to be illegal campaign cash, according to interviews with purported donors to the event and video footage unearthed by the Guardian and the City.\par \par \uc2 The City, a local independent newsroom, and the news site Documented {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/08/18/eric-adams-straw-donors-new-world-mall/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 previously reported }}}\uc2  on several suspicious donations stemming from the fundraiser, which was hosted in August 2021 at the home of a Queens mall operator. One listed donor to the fundraiser, a low-wage employee at that mall, admitted to being reimbursed in cash after donating to Adams at the behest of their employer \u8211\'20\'13 an act that would constitute an illegal \u8220\'20\'1Cstraw\u8221\'20\'1D donation. Three other purported donors denied giving to Adams, including a woman who was listed in the campaign\u8217\'20\'19s financial disclosures as a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliveryman\u8221\'20\'1D at a restaurant in the mall that she never worked for and that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t do delivery.\par \par \uc2 At the time of the original reporting, the role of Greco at the mall operator\u8217\'20\'19s event was not publicly known. The newly unearthed footage shows the aide escorting Adams in and out of the event, stage-managing photo ops for the candidate, and hugging and laughing with family members of the evening\u8217\'20\'19s host, Lian Wu Shao, the chairman of Queens\u8217\'20\'19 New World Mall.\par \par \uc2 Greco, a prolific fundraising liaison for Adams, has also played a central role in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/08/18/eric-adams-straw-donors-new-world-mall/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 at least }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/feb/01/eric-adams-donations-reimbursed-2025-campaign-new-york-mayor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 two }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/23/eric-adams-weihong-hu-fundraiser-new-york-city-mayor"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 other Adams fundraisers }}}\uc2  in which purported donors said their donations were forged or resulted in illegal cash reimbursements, as the Guardian, the City, and Documented have previously reported.\par \par \uc2 Last February, following a series of stories on Greco by the three outlets, the \uc2 FBI\uc2  raided two of Greco\u8217\'20\'19s homes and Shao\u8217\'20\'19s mall.\par \par \uc2 Steven Brill, an attorney representing Greco, declined to answer questions about his client\u8217\'20\'19s fundraising activities.\par \par \uc2 Adams\u8217\'20\'19s campaign attorney, Vito Pitta, did not respond to questions about Greco\u8217\'20\'19s role in the event or the alleged straw donations, but said the campaign was reviewing the details of the fundraiser.\par \par \uc2 The footage of the fundraiser at Shao\u8217\'20\'19s home also casts doubt on the Adams campaign\u8217\'20\'19s past characterizations of the event and his team\u8217\'20\'19s compliance with campaign finance laws.\par \par \uc2 Last year in response to questions about the suspicious donations, Adams\u8217\'20\'19s campaign insisted in an email to the City that Shao\u8217\'20\'19s fundraiser was bankrolled by the people who attended the event that evening, and that \u8220\'20\'1Cmany\u8221\'20\'1D of these attendees were employees of a local grocery chain, partially owned by Shao\u8217\'20\'19s family.\par \par \uc2 Campaign finance records show that just under 200 small donors gave to Shao\u8217\'20\'19s fundraiser on the day of the event, including almost 40 cashiers and other employees from Shao\u8217\'20\'19s grocery chain, who each gave exactly $249.\par \par \uc2 But the footage shows that only a few dozen guests, not about 200, attended the fundraiser. And most of them, wearing dress shirts and dresses and raising glasses of expensive red wine to toast Shao, appear to be his relatives and business associates, not his low-wage workers.\par \par \uc2 In addition, at least seven of the people listed in the campaign\u8217\'20\'19s financial disclosures as having contributed to the barbecue that day said they never attended \u8211\'20\'13 or did not recall attending \u8211\'20\'13 the fundraising event, which took place at the Shao family\u8217\'20\'19s $4m mansion outside New York City, where a select crowd enjoyed catered sushi and lobster.\par \par \uc2 The discrepancy between the footage of the intimate event and the Adams campaign\u8217\'20\'19s description of it as a large, grassroots fundraiser raises questions about whether his staff knowingly accepted campaign cash that wealthy interests had bundled together and falsely attributed to an army of small donors in order to score additional taxpayer dollars for the campaign.\par \par \uc2 New York City\u8217\'20\'19s campaign finance board runs a public matching fund program that provides candidates with eight-to-one matches for donations of $250 or less, and nearly all of the donations from Shao\u8217\'20\'19s fundraiser were for exactly $249 and $250. Thanks to the small nature of most of these donations, his campaign team was able to apply for more than $300,000 in taxpayer-funded matching funds.\par \par \uc2 The revelations about Shao\u8217\'20\'19s fundraiser come on the heels of September\u8217\'20\'19s explosive federal indictment which {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/26/eric-adams-bribes-turkey"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 accused }}}\uc2  Adams\u8217\'20\'19s 2021 mayoral campaign of similar conduct: knowingly soliciting and accepting illegal donations from wealthy interests \u8211\'20\'13 in that case, donors with ties to the Turkish government \u8211\'20\'13 who passed their money through small \u8220\'20\'1Cstraw\u8221\'20\'1D donors in order to score Adams additional {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.brennancenter.org/our-work/research-reports/public-campaign-financing-and-indictment-nyc-mayor-eric-adams"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public matching funds }}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Adams, who {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/02/eric-adams-corruption-scandals"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 faces charges }}}\uc2  of wire fraud, bribery and soliciting campaign donations from foreign nationals, has pleaded not guilty.\par \par \uc2 After the indictment, which also referred to Greco without naming her, the longtime Adams aide resigned from her government post as Adams\u8217\'20\'19s Asian affairs director.\par \par \uc2 She is currently being investigated as part of a separate federal inquiry by prosecutors in New York\u8217\'20\'19s eastern district, whose investigation resulted in the recent raids on her two homes and Shao\u8217\'20\'19s mall.\par \par \uc2 As of now Greco has not been publicly charged with any crime.\par \par \uc2 Low-w\par \par \uc2 age\par \par \uc2 e\par \par \uc2 mployees s\par \par \uc2 ay m\par \par \uc2 all m\par \par \uc2 anagement p\par \par \uc2 ushed t\par \par \uc2 hem to d\par \par \uc2 onate\par \par \uc2 New York City campaign finance rules attempt to curb the influence of business interests in politics, like those of the Shao family, by limiting how much individuals can give to candidates who participate in the city\u8217\'20\'19s public matching funds program.\par \par \uc2 By January of 2021, Shao, chairman of Queens\u8217\'20\'19 New World Mall and the local grocery chain, which is called J-Mart, had already donated $2,000 to Adams, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nyccfb.info/candidate-services/limits-thresholds/2025/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the maximum }}}\uc2  he was allowed to give at that time.\par \par \uc2 But in the months after Shao made his big individual donation, a swarm of his subordinates suddenly started donating in droves, despite the fact that few of them had ever shown an interest in local elections before.\par \par \uc2 On 18 April of that year, for example, Shao\u8217\'20\'19s son, William, a co-owner of J-Mart, gave $249 to the Adams campaign. The same day, the campaign reported receiving the same amount from 30 J-Mart and New World Mall employees, including more than a dozen cashiers and servers, none of whom were registered to vote at the time.\par \par \uc2 That bundle of purported small donations put nearly $8,000 into Adams\u8217\'20\'19s campaign coffers, and allowed his campaign team to ask authorities for more than $37,000 in taxpayer-funded matching funds.\par \par \uc2 Campaign finance board records show regulators repeatedly asked Adams\u8217\'20\'19s team to explain who, if anyone, had bundled this haul together, which came from a fundraising event held at a restaurant at the mall, partially owned by Shao\u8217\'20\'19s wife.\par \par \uc2 Adams\u8217\'20\'19s team did not disclose whether any intermediaries were behind the cluster of donations ahead of the election in response to regulators\u8217\'20\'19 inquiries.\par \par \uc2 Several workers at Shao\u8217\'20\'19s mall interviewed for this story, however, said that they were directed to make the donations by members of mall management. One purported donor, who works at the New World Mall\u8217\'20\'19s food court, said he gave $249 on 18 April after somebody from the mall\u8217\'20\'19s office approached him and asked him to donate. Another New World Mall employee, who made a campaign donation a few months later, said that \u8220\'20\'1Cmall office people\u8221\'20\'1D handed him and his colleagues contribution forms and collected checks from them at the office.\par \par \uc2 Some employees insisted they donated of their own volition.\par \par \uc2 Standing outside his apartment door, Yuzan Qui, a former bookkeeper employed by J-Mart, said that on 18 June 2021, he donated $249 to Adams, rather than the more customary donation of $250, because that was the exact amount that he had left in his bank account.\par \par \uc2 But the same day, 17 of his colleagues, also employees of the Shao family\u8217\'20\'19s mall or grocery chain, each gave the same amount: $249. The donations were also reported to have come from a fundraiser at the restaurant in the mall, partially owned by Shao\u8217\'20\'19s wife. The small donations from that June fundraiser, which added up to nearly $5,000, allowed the Adams campaign to ask for almost $40,000 more in additional public matching funds.\par \par \uc2 Campaign finance regulators again asked Adams\u8217\'20\'19s team to identify who, if anyone, had bundled together these donations. Adams\u8217\'20\'19s staff again failed to provide them with an answer ahead of the election \u8211\'20\'13 a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.thecity.nyc/2023/11/02/adams-donations-fbi-turkey-straw-donors/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 routine failure }}}\uc2  by his campaign.\par \par \uc2 At least one New World Mall employee suggested the donation drive at the Shao family\u8217\'20\'19s mall was not entirely voluntary.\par \par \uc2 That employee, the self-described straw donor, said that they only gave after they were approached at work by a higher-up at the mall. The higher-up knew the employee\u8217\'20\'19s boss, a vendor at the mall, so the employee said they allowed the higher up to use their identity for a campaign contribution, then reimbursed them in cash.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t really know how the donation process works,\u8221\'20\'1D the employee said, who spoke on the condition of anonymity for fear of losing their job. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I gave the person my identity, and that was it \u8211\'20\'13 to use my identity, and they pay the money.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Federal prosecutors from the eastern district of New York declined to comment on whether they are now investigating the family of Lian Wu Shao following their raid of his mall in Queens.\par \par \uc2 Shao did not respond to multiple phone calls and emails seeking comment ahead of this story.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Additional reporting by Bianca Pallaro\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241113ekbd001jm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
