\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 Hachette employees protest and quit over launch of conservative imprint\b0\par\par\uc2 Lucy Knight \par \uc2 691 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2024\par 17:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Basic Liberty, a publishing imprint launched in the wake of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election win, will be helmed by Thomas Spence, an editor associated with Project 2025 thinktank \uc2 The Heritage Foundation\par \par \uc2 Staff are protesting against a new US imprint of global publisher \uc2 Hachette Book Group\uc2  (HBG) specialising in conservative books, launched in the wake of Donald Trump\u8217\'20\'19s election win.\par \par \uc2 A letter from an anonymous group of HBG employees has been published on social media, criticising the launch of Basic Liberty. It also expresses concern at the hiring of executive editor Thomas Spence. Spence is the former president of conservative publisher Regnery and a visiting fellow at \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , the rightwing thinktank that coordinated the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/project-2025"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025 }}}\uc2  initiative, which sets out plans to reshape the \uc2 US government\uc2  and strip minorities of legal protections.\par \par \uc2 Announced two days after the presidential election, Basic Liberty is one of two new branches of Hachette US\u8217\'20\'19s Basic Books, alongside Basic Venture, which will focus on business and economics. Basic Liberty is \u8220\'20\'1Cdedicated to publishing serious works of cultural, social, and political analysis by conservative writers\u8221\'20\'1D with a focus \u8220\'20\'1Con topics of enduring interest rather than transitory political concerns\u8221\'20\'1D, according to HBG\u8217\'20\'19s website.\par \par \uc2 After the announcement, the anonymous letter was posted to the \uc2 Instagram\uc2  account xoxopublishinggg, stating employees\u8217\'20\'19 \u8220\'20\'1Cfirm disapprobation of \uc2 the Heritage Foundation\uc2 , Project 2025, and any conservative movement or thought that strips away sacred rights and the humanity of people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe condemn HBG\u8217\'20\'19s decision to put profit before its own people,\u8221\'20\'1D the letter goes on to say. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are calling on HBG to recognise the responsibility it has as one of the world\u8217\'20\'19s leading publishers, to act with empathy and compassion for all people, and to re-evaluate its decision to move forward with the creation of Basic Liberty and the hiring of Thomas Spence.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Those behind the letter have not been named, and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  is the only place it has been published. It has not been sent directly to anyone at HBG, and the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  has been unable to confirm the identities of the letter\u8217\'20\'19s authors. However, HBG has confirmed that Alex DiFrancesco, a US-based editor at Jessica Kingsley Publishers (JKP), which is part of Hachette UK, has resigned over the same issue.\par \par \uc2 JKP\u8217\'20\'19s focus is on underrepresented voices, publishing books on subjects including neurodiversity, mental health and gender diversity. In {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/Alex_JKPGender/status/1854835064141414402"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a statement posted on X }}}\uc2 , DiFrancesco described the books JKP publishes as being \u8220\'20\'1Cof great importance towards the project of making the world better, more understanding, and more compassionate.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI have felt, in these two and a half years, that not only our division, but the wider company stood behind such work\u8221\'20\'1D, they added. \u8220\'20\'1CAfter the announcement about the formation of Basic Books\u8217\'20\'19 Liberty imprint, I can no longer say I feel this way.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 DiFrancesco, who is transgender, said that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe placement of Thomas Spence in a prominent role in the company feels like a direct threat not only to my work and livelihood, but to my person, and to many other minority workers within the company \u8230\'20\'26 I can only hope that others will make choices like the one I am currently making instead of rolling over to the forces that would destroy our nation, our neighbours, and ourselves.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Approached for comment, a representative from HBG said \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Hachette Book Group\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s mission is to reach a broad spectrum of readers by making it easier for everyone to discover new worlds of ideas, learning, entertainment and opportunity. We publish books from all sides of the political debate \u8230\'20\'26 Basic Books continues to build on HBG\u8217\'20\'19s legacy of reaching readers of all backgrounds and beliefs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time HBG employees have taken issue with the company. In 2020, Hachette US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2020/mar/06/woody-allen-memoir-hachette-books"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropped plans to publish Woody Allen\u8217\'20\'19s memoir }}}\uc2  Apropos of Nothing after staff at the New York office staged a walkout, because of the allegations of sexual abuse made against the film-maker. Allen\u8217\'20\'19s memoir was instead published by independent publisher \uc2 Skyhorse\uc2  books, which is now the parent company of Regnery.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241112ekbc002s5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
