\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 OBR\uc2  moving to \u8216\'20\'18trust but verify\u8217\'20\'19 relationship with Treasury after \u8216\'20\'18black hole\u8217\'20\'19 row \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 9,292 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 November 2024\par 16:58 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial newsTreasury may have broken law by failing to reveal \u163\'00\'A39.5bn spend in Tory budget, MPs told\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.42pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 MPs have been told that Treasury officials may have broken the law when they failed to alert the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/office-for-budget-responsibility"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility}}}\uc2  about \u163\'00\'A39.5bn of spending that should have appeared in the March Tory budget.\par \par \uc2 Richard Hughes, the head of the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , told MPs that the system in which the \uc2 OBR\uc2  and the Treasury shared information ahead of the budget had \u8220\'20\'1Cvery clearly broke down.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 That, he said, resulted in officials failing to disclose \u163\'00\'A39.5bn of spending pressures which the Treasury knew about, which \u8220\'20\'1Cunder the law and under the Act they should have\u8221\'20\'1D, Hughes said.\par \par \uc2 As a result, he said, the \uc2 OBR\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cmoving from a system of trust, to a system of trust but verify\u8221\'20\'1D in its dealings with the Treasury.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief also said the watchdog had expected the markets to react to the budget by pushing up UK bond yields, as indeed happened, as investors were being asked to buy more UK debt.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/05/treasury-may-have-broken-law-failing-reveal-budget-plan-mps-told"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Treasury may have broken law by failing to reveal \u163\'00\'A39.5bn spend in Tory budget, MPs told}}}\par \par \uc2 In other news\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 UK borrowing costs have jumped, after an auction of British debt attracted {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?page=with:block-672a1ccd8f081ed2bfa20b39#block-672a1ccd8f081ed2bfa20b39"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the lowest demand in almost a year}}}\uc2 .\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672a31af8f0841bfc64a7f02#block-672a31af8f0841bfc64a7f02"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The US dollar has weakened}}}\uc2  against other currencies as the US heads to the polls, lifting the pound back over $1.30 for the first time in nearly a week.\par \par \uc2 Growth in the UK services sector has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672a1e138f08b5f06d4ee9e8#block-672a1e138f08b5f06d4ee9e8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowed}}}\uc2 , while in the US it has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-672a36c68f08ec080b93fdc8#block-672a36c68f08ec080b93fdc8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  hit its highest in two years.}}}\par \par \uc2 A planned strike by tube drivers that threatened to halt all {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk/london-underground"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 London Underground}}}\uc2  services on Thursday has been called off, their union announced.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/nov/05/tube-drivers-call-off-planned-strikes-after-improved-offer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tube drivers call off planned strikes after \u8216\'20\'18improved offer\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Shares in pharmaceuticals giant \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  have dropped over 8%, following a report that dozens of senior executives at its China unit could be implicated in the largest insurance fraud case in the country\u8217\'20\'19s pharma sector in years.\par \par \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s tumble has weighed on the London stock market, where the FTSE 100 index has just closed 11.85 points lower at 8172, a drop of 0.14%.\par \par \uc2 Shares are rallying in New York, though, where the S&P 500 index is up almost 1%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.27pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Planned strikes by \uc2 London Underground\uc2  drivers over pay are suspended\par \par \uc2 A planned strike by tube drivers that threatened to halt all \uc2 London Underground\uc2  services on Thursday has been called off, the \uc2 Aslef union\uc2  has announced.\par \par \uc2 Talks between \uc2 Aslef\uc2  officials and \uc2 Transport for London\uc2  management on Tuesday led to a \u8220\'20\'1Csignificantly improved offer\u8221\'20\'1D, according to the union.\par \par \uc2 Aslef\uc2  had called two 24-hour walkouts in the capital, for Thursday and Tuesday next week.\par \par \uc2 Finn Brennan, \uc2 Aslef\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s full-time organiser on \uc2 London Underground\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CFollowing fresh talks, and an improved offer, \uc2 ASLEF\uc2  has agreed to suspend our planned industrial action on \uc2 London Underground\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDetails of the offer will be discussed with our reps at a meeting on Thursday. We are pleased that this progress has been made and that strike action has been averted at this time. We are not able to reveal any further details until after that meeting with our reps on Thursday.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Just to clarify that post, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s shares are down about 8.5% now (I\u8217\'20\'19ve fixed a typo in the initial version).\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/AZN?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #AZN}}}\uc2  A rare sight for this FTSE100 giant:\par \par \uc2 *\uc2 ASTRAZENECA\uc2  SHARES HALTED FOR BREACH OF TOLERANCE LEVEL\par \par \uc2 Shares have crashed -8%\par \par \uc2 Apparently a probe by China into the company\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Baron Investments (@baroninvestment) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/baroninvestment/status/1853817266229424590?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere in the City, shares in pharmaceuticals giant \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  have fallen by over 8% [CORRECTED] after a report that dozens of senior executives at its China unit could be implicated in an insurance fraud case.\par \par \uc2 Financial media company Yicai is reporting that the investigation by Chinese authorities has now expanded to include the public security bureau, supervisory commission and other relevant bodies.\par \par \uc2 Last week, \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  revealed that its China president \uc2 Leon Wang\uc2  is under investigation, and pledged to cooperate with Chinese authorities.\par \par \uc2 In early September, Chinese police reportedly detained five current and former {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/astrazeneca"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AstraZeneca}}}\uc2  employees as part of an investigation into possible breaches related to data privacy and importing unlicensed medications.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/china-detains-five-astrazeneca-staff-in-investigation-over-data-privacy-and-import-breaches"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 China detains five AstraZeneca staff over \u8216\'20\'18data privacy and import breaches\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.44pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK gilt yields rising after weak debt auction\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing costs are rising, after today\u8217\'20\'19s debt auction drew fewer bids than normal ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?page=with:block-672a1ccd8f081ed2bfa20b39#block-672a1ccd8f081ed2bfa20b39"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see 1.26pm}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 The yield (or interest rate) on five-year \uc2 UK government\uc2  bonds has risen to 4.42% today, which would be the highest closing level since November 2023.\par \par \uc2 10-year bond yields have also risen towards the one-year highs seen after last week\u8217\'20\'19s budget, at 4.51%.\par \par \uc2 This has pushed the gap between UK and German borrowing cost to its highest in a year (that also reflects forecasts that eurozone interest rates could fall faster than in the UK).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.45pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 US service sector growth at two-year high\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US services sector grew at the fastest rate in over two years in October.\par \par \uc2 The closely watched Services Sector PMI report from the Institute of Supply Management shows that economic activity in the services sector expanded for the fourth consecutive month in October.\par \par \uc2 The Services \uc2 PMI\uc2  has risen to 56%, which is the highest reading since July 2022 and indicates the sector expanded for the 50th time in 53 months.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s up from 54.9% in September, indicating growth in the sector picked up. Perhaps a sign that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  will leave an economy in pretty good shape to his successor\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/04/america-economy-election-voters"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 If the US is heading for a soft landing, why do people feel so hard up?}}}\par \par \uc2 Firms reported a slowdown in new order growth, but firms also took on more staff last month.\par \par \uc2 Fourteen industries reported growth in October, up two from the 12 industries reporting growth in September.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe latest Services ISM\u174\'00\'AE report indicated that economic activity in the services sector expanded for the fourth consecutive month in October. The Services \uc2 PMI\uc2 \u174\'00\'AE registered 56.0, which is the highest reading since July 2022 and was above expectations. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/guQNvJN6MR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/guQNvJN6MR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Dr Thomas Kevin Swift (@DrTKSwift) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/DrTKSwift/status/1853818231183843809?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Some companies reported that recent hurricanes have affected supplier deliveries, but also lifted demand in the equipment sales and rental businesses.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.31pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.03pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Carson Group\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief market strategist, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.linkedin.com/in/ryandetrick/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ryan Detrick}}}\uc2 , argues that a divided \uc2 US government\uc2  (where one party controls the White House, and the other Congress) could be the best result for the markets.\par \par \uc2 The resulting gridlock could prevent more radical policies from getting through, as agreement between Congress and the President is needed to spend more money.\par \par \uc2 Detrick says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe election is finally here and emotions are running high. Elections matter, but let\u8217\'20\'19s not forget that an economy that continues to surprise to the upside, record earnings, and a dovish Fed likely matters more for this bull market than who is in the White House.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThings are close, we know that. But we also know this year will be the thirteenth year in a row that saw the S&P 500 close higher amid a split Congress. Gridlock can be a good thing and should we see this once again, this could be what investors should be rooting for.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Dollar dips, but Bitcoin rallies\par \par \uc2 The US dollar is losing a little ground; it\u8217\'20\'19s now down about 0.3% against a basket of other major currencies.\par \par \uc2 This has lifted the pound back over the $1.30 mark for the first time in nearly a week.\par \par \uc2 Bitcoin is rallying, though \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s up over 4% today and just traded over the $70,000 mark.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.51pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Q: Is the \uc2 OBR\uc2  too pessimistic in its forecast about the impact of planning reforms?\par \par \uc2 Mike Brewer of \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2  says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cdidn\u8217\'20\'19t do the government any favours\u8221\'20\'1D in its growth forecasts.\par \par \uc2 But that\u8217\'20\'19s because we don\u8217\'20\'19t yet have full details about the government\u8217\'20\'19s growth plans.\par \par \uc2 About a third of the government\u8217\'20\'19s investment spending is going on fixing hospitals, schools and prisons \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cvitally needed\u8221\'20\'1D but won\u8217\'20\'19t immediately lift growth.\par \par \uc2 Brewer adds:\par \par \uc2 As the government says more about its growth strategy, I expect the \uc2 OBR\uc2  will respond to that, by revising up its trend rate of growth assumption.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Andy King of Flint Global, who is a former member of the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , has explained to MPs how the Treasury can attempt to game the system when drawing up budget measures.\par \par \uc2 King says:\par \par \uc2 Every time the \uc2 OBR\uc2  sets out a methodology\u8230\'20\'26 the Treasury can then \u8216\'20\'18teach to the test\u8217\'20\'19. You\u8217\'20\'19ve seen that in the last couple of years.\par \par \uc2 He cites the example of personal tax measures \u8211\'20\'13 in his time at the \uc2 OBR\uc2 , the watchdog assumed they didn\u8217\'20\'19t affect the supply side of the economy (because \u8220\'20\'1Cthe income gain and the substitution incentive\u8221\'20\'1D offset each other), just the demand side.\par \par \uc2 But now the \uc2 OBR\uc2  does factor in an impact on the supply side, and \u8220\'20\'1Call of a sudden there\u8217\'20\'19s a raft of measures in that space, because they score\u8221\'20\'1D on the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s forecasting table.\par \par \uc2 King explains that he used to run the costings process at the \uc2 OBR\uc2 , totalling up the fiscal cost of measures.\par \par \uc2 The Treasury is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery adept\u8221\'20\'1D at spotting what scores in the forecasts, and adjusting policy accordingly, he adds.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.46pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens higher on election day\par \par \uc2 Over in New York, stocks have opened a little higher as traders await the results of the US election.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average, which tracks 30 large US companies, rose 40.9 points, or 0.10%, at the open to 41835.49.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index gained 9.7 points, or 0.17%, to 5722.43?, while the tech-focused Nasdaq Composite picked up 70.7 points, or 0.39%, to 18250.707 at the opening bell.\par \par \uc2 The outcome of the US election will impact financial markets around the globe in two ways, says \uc2 Kathleen Brooks\uc2 , research director at XTB:\par \par \uc2 Short term: Global financial markets could be hit by volatility if there is a surprise outcome. As we have mentioned, we think that financial markets are pricing for a Trump victory. Thus, a win for Harris could lead to a short term sell off in the dollar, gold and potentially in US stocks. This could also boost global equities, as Harris is seen to be less tempted to slap tariffs on imports. Chinese stocks could rally sharply, along with key European and UK firms.\par \par \uc2 Longer term: A win for Trump, could see the dollar pop higher, but we think a win for the former President could have a long-term impact on global financial markets due his plans for tariffs. A US system of import tariffs would reorder global trade flows and hurt global growth.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Yuan Yang returns to the problems with the UK\u8217\'20\'19s employment data:\par \par \uc2 Q: Interest rate forecasts are very dependent on estimates of the output gap, so how confident are you about the quality of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s labour market data?\par \par \uc2 Paul Johnson of the IFS (who is caught smiling during the question) agrees that the current labour market statistics are \u8220\'20\'1Cnot ideal\u8221\'20\'1D. But they\u8217\'20\'19re only part of the data available.\par \par \uc2 Unemployment is still relatively low, he says, and vacancies are still high by historic standards (although falling).\par \par \uc2 So it seems likely that there isn\u8217\'20\'19t a big output gap to be filled, Johnson argues, even if the precise numbers are uncertain.\par \par \uc2 Q: How unusual is the UK\u8217\'20\'19s levels of economic inactivity compared to other countries, and how can it be aleviated?\par \par \uc2 Johnson says the UK is \u8220\'20\'1Cvery unusual\u8221\'20\'1D in terms of the number of people out of work due to sickness or disability, as those numbers have grown in a way they haven\u8217\'20\'19t in almost any other country post-Covid.\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s not sure why, though, or what can be done about it.\par \par \uc2 There is an \u8220\'20\'1Curgent need\u8221\'20\'1D to address the rise in mental health issues among younger people, he adds.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.21pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Q: Gilt yields rose by 10 basis points on Friday after the budget \u8211\'20\'13 is that a sign that the government\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal room for manouvrer is limited?\par \par \uc2 KPMG\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Yael Selfin\uc2  explains that the important question is what a government is borrowing for \u8211\'20\'13 if it is going to generate much stronger growth, it could pay for itself.\par \par \uc2 Compared to other countries, the UK doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the highest ratio of debt as a share of its economy, Selfin points out, and it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t face sharply higher borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 She says:\par \par \uc2 The confidence of the markets is very important in terms of the premium you\u8217\'20\'19re paying on top of the base rate, but that base rate is also very important \u8211\'20\'13 we have higher rates than we had previously.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.12pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Harriett Baldwin\uc2  MP asks about the \u163\'00\'A3350bn increase of public spending outlined in the budget, which the \uc2 OBR\uc2  suspects will raise gilt yields and interest rates by a quarter of one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 Do any economists disagree with the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s forecast?\par \par \uc2 Yael Selfin\uc2 , vice chair and chief economist at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , reckons it will have larger impact, meaning short-term interest rates will be half a point higher than would otherwise be the case, not just a quarter.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.17pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.07pm GMT\par \par \uc2 MPs quiz economists on the budget\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Committee are now holding a second hearing on the budget, with some leading UK economists.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ll hear from Mike Brewer, interim chief executive of \uc2 Resolution Foundation\uc2 ; Paul Johnson, director of \uc2 the Institute for Fiscal Studies\uc2 ; \uc2 Yael Selfin\uc2 , vice chair and chief economist at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 ; and Andy King, specialist partner at Flint Global.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm GMT\par \par \uc2 UK service sector growth slowed in run-up to the Budget\par \par \uc2 New economic data today has shown that the UK services sector slowed last month.\par \par \uc2 Data firm S&P Global reports that its UK service \uc2 PMI\uc2 , which measures activity in the sector, dipped to 52.0 in October, down from 52.4 in September.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the weakest reading since last November; S&P Global says it shows \u8220\'20\'1Cweaker growth momentum in October\u8221\'20\'1D, it says, with the \uc2 PMI\uc2  still above the 50-point mark.\par \par \uc2 New orders growth slowed, despite a solid rise in new work from abroad, and firms said they cut their workforces for the first time this year.\par \par \uc2 Some service firms said heightened business uncertainty ahead of the Autumn Budget was a factor delaying spending decisions among clients. Some firms also noted that geopolitical tensions had weighed on willingness-to-spend.\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, explains:\par \par \uc2 The wait for clarity on government policy ahead of the Autumn Budget was widely reported to have weighed on business confidence and spending. Broader geopolitical concerns and forthcoming US elections also added to a sense of wait-and-see on business investment decisions in October. At the same time, cost of living pressures remained a constraint on household spending.\par \par \uc2 With service providers grappling with softer new order growth and less upbeat business activity expectations for the year ahead, the latest survey pointed to a decline in staffing numbers for the first time since December 2023. A number of firms also noted budget constraints due to elevated salary pressures.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.54pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.26pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Back in the financial markets, a sale of \uc2 UK government\uc2  debt has attracted the lowest interest from investors this year.\par \par \uc2 The sale was still over-subscribed, but by less than usual.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-11-05/uk-10-year-bond-auction-attracts-least-demand-since-december?srnd=homepage-uk&sref=Xl91GI8N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-11-05/uk-10-year-bond-auction-attracts-least-demand-since-december?srnd=homepage-uk&sref=Xl91GI8N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  has the details}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 UK bonds attracted the weakest demand in almost a year at an auction Tuesday, a sign of lingering investor anxiety over last week\u8217\'20\'19s fiscally expansive budget and the US presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Bids for 10-year gilts totaled 2.81 times the \u163\'00\'A33.75bn ($4.9bn) on offer, the lowest ratio for a sale of that maturity since December. Last month, a similar auction attracted demand equivalent to 3.25 times the offer.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.48pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Did Treasury break law over March budget spending plans?\par \par \uc2 Treasury committee chair \uc2 Meg Hillier\uc2  takes the \uc2 OBR\uc2  back to its claim that the Treasury did not give you all the information should have done under the Act ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6729fce98f08cfd6372166da#block-6729fce98f08cfd6372166da"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see 11.27am}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 Q: In your view, did think the Treasury broke the law when preparing the spending forecasts it passed to you in preparation for the March budget?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chair Richard Hughes replies that \u8220\'20\'1Cthey didn\u8217\'20\'19t provide all the information that we require to do our forecast\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 This was a \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterial amount of information\u8221\'20\'1D which would have led to a \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterially different forecast\u8221\'20\'1D had the \uc2 OBR\uc2  had it.\par \par \uc2 Hughes adds that he thinks every public servant does their best to obey the law, and respect its spirit and letter.\par \par \uc2 But there was a \u8220\'20\'1Csystemic failure\u8221\'20\'1D in putting together the March forecasts, and potentially a \u8220\'20\'1Cmisunderstanding of expectations on both sides about what needed to be provided\u8221\'20\'1D, Hughes suggests.\par \par \uc2 Q: So what went wrong? Did the Treasury break the law?\par \par \uc2 Hughes suggests there may have been a \u8220\'20\'1Cmisunderstanding\u8221\'20\'1D about how the law should be interpreted, regarding sharing information about departmental spending.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.57pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.32pm GMT\par \par \uc2 Q: Was the \uc2 OBR\uc2  asked to do any work around the changes to inheritance tax relief for farmers; did you model any different changes?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  member Tom Josephs says the watchdog can only cost policies that have been announced by the government. Obviously, if you raised the thresholds you\u8217\'20\'19d raise less money, he adds.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/nov/04/nfu-rejects-treasury-claim-that-72-of-farms-wont-pay-inheritance-tax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NFU rejects Treasury claim that 72% of farms won\u8217\'20\'19t pay inheritance tax}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.35pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.20pm GMT\par \par \uc2 The committee then turns to \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s new fiscal rules.\par \par \uc2 Q: Is the new fiscal rule more, or less, responsible than the previous one?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief Richard Hughes says one difference is the change to measuring public sector net financial liabilities, \u8220\'20\'1Cknown in the trade as persnuffle\u8221\'20\'1D, Hughes grins, rather than net debt.\par \par \uc2 A second change is the timescale.\par \par \uc2 Initially, Reeves\u8217\'20\'19s debt rule requires that debt is falling as a share of the economy by 2029-30 (five year\u8217\'20\'19s time, the same as the old rule). That will change when 2029-30 becomes the third year of the forecast period. From that point, debt should then fall by the third year of the rolling forecast period.\par \par \uc2 The other fiscal rule is to show the current budget must be in surplus in 2029-30 (and then by the third year ahead).\par \par \uc2 Hughes says \u8220\'20\'1Cboth targets are more likely to be binding on more meaningful parts of our forecast than some previous fiscal targets have been\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 David Miles adds that the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s forecast shows that the number of people in employment will be 50,000 lower than would otherwise be the case, due to the rise in employers\u8217\'20\'19 NICs contributions.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a \u8220\'20\'1Cmaterial number, it\u8217\'20\'19s not trivial\u8221\'20\'1D, but a small proportion of the overall workforce of around 32 to 33m people.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.09pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.54am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2 's Miles: Raising employers' NICs contributions will hit real wages\par \par \uc2 Liberal Democrat MP Bobby Dean then asks the \uc2 OBR\uc2  about the impact of raising employers\u8217\'20\'19 national insurance contributions.\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  member professor David Miles says the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s analysis shows that in the long run, the majority of the impact is that real wages will be lower than they would otherwise of been.\par \par \uc2 [reminder: \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  announced that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2024/oct/15/what-are-employer-national-insurance-contributions-rachel-reeves-tax-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the main rate of employers\u8217\'20\'19 National Insurance contributions}}}\uc2  will increase from 13.8% to 15% from April 2025, and lowered the threshold at which an employer has to pay on a worker\u8217\'20\'19s salary from \u163\'00\'A39,100 a year to \u163\'00\'A35,000.]\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/oct/30/bosses-reeves-tax-rises-employers-national-insurance-contributions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18That\u8217\'20\'19s two people we won\u8217\'20\'19t hire now\u8217\'20\'19: bosses react to Reeves\u8217\'20\'19s tax rises}}}\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OBR\uc2  estimates that three-quarters of the impact of raising employers\u8217\'20\'19 NICS payments falls on workers, with a quarter showing up in lower profits.\par \par \uc2 Miles says:\par \par \uc2 Nobody escapes completely, but more of it falls on workers in terms of lower real wages than in lower profits.\par \par \uc2 The impact in the short-run is a bit different, though, as employers aren\u8217\'20\'19t going to hit workers with immediate pay cuts. Instead, it\u8217\'20\'19s likely that pay rises will be lower in the next few years, to cushion the impact.\par \par \uc2 enltrDavid Miles discusses at the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CommonsTreasury?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @CommonsTreasury}}}\uc2  hearing how corporate profits are expected to continue falling as a share of GDP in the near term, before rising steadily from 2026 as firms rebuild margins and pass on more of the cost of the employer NICs rise. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/d7u7TpTZqy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/d7u7TpTZqy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  (@OBR_UK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/OBR_UK/status/1853757804726653353?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.40am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR's Hughes: failure to disclose spending pressures in March 'is regrettable'\par \par \uc2 Richard Hughes adds that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  relies on the Treasury being forthcoming on their best estimates of the costs of government policies and the risks around them.\par \par \uc2 In this case [the March forecasts], that does not seem to have happened.\par \par \uc2 Conservative MP \uc2 John Glen\uc2  asks about the political row over the \u8216\'20\'18black hole\u8217\'20\'19:\par \par \uc2 Q: Isn\u8217\'20\'19t it highly regrettable that you\u8217\'20\'19ve been sucked into this political debate about a black hole; can that be avoided in future?\par \par \uc2 Hughes says \u8220\'20\'1Cwhat happened\u8221\'20\'1D is regrettable.\par \par \uc2 There was a serious overspend against our forecasts, we\u8217\'20\'19ve needed to make serious adjustments to that, governments had to make serious adjustments to policy.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OBR\uc2  doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have the luxury of standing on the sidelines on these things, Hughes points out \u8211\'20\'13 it has to draw up forecasts for departmental spending. If there are errors, or undisclosed information, it has to correct for that and explain itself.\par \par \uc2 Q: Ministers relied on officials to provide whatever information the \uc2 OBR\uc2  requested \u8211\'20\'13 so how can information not be disclosed, given the integrity of Treasury officials?\par \par \uc2 Hughes replies that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  relies on the Treasury to disclose the information it has at the time about departmental spending, adding:\par \par \uc2 That information was not shared with us at the time of the March budget, and it was information that was available to the Treasury at the time.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief: We're moving to a system of 'trust but verify' with the Treasury\par \par \uc2 The Treasury committee then turns to the \u8216\'20\'18black hole\u8217\'20\'19 which Labour claims it found in the public finances when it took office.\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief Richard Hughes explains that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  is changing the way it interacts with the Treasury when drawing up its economic and fiscal forecasts.\par \par \uc2 He tells MPs:\par \par \uc2 We are moving from a system of trust, to a system of trust but verify.\par \par \uc2 That will see the \uc2 OBR\uc2  ask more questions about the pressures on departmental budgets, and what decisions on, for example, pay, will mean for those budgets.\par \par \uc2 That, Hughes says, should mean that the \u8220\'20\'1Cfailure of oversight that very clearly happened in March doesn\u8217\'20\'19t happen again.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hughes explains:\par \par \uc2 It will involve greater scrutiny on our part of departmental spending limits, and the preparations of departments\u8217\'20\'19 budgets, to make sure that those kind of pressures do get disclosed to us, and we have a conversation about how they will be managed and offset, and what it means for departmental overspending or underspending.\par \par \uc2 Hughes outlines how the \uc2 OBR\uc2  had built up a way of working with the Treasury over the years, which was a \u8220\'20\'1Chigh trust relationship with the Treasury\u8221\'20\'1D that expenditure pressures on departments were being well managed and managed within the spending total allocated to departments.\par \par \uc2 He admits:\par \par \uc2 That system very clearly broke down.\par \par \uc2 Hughes explains that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  was not aware of the extra pressures on government departments when, in July, chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  told MPs there was a \u163\'00\'A322bn black hole in the public finances.\par \par \uc2 After that happened, Hughes says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  asked the Treasury what they were aware of when the fiscal watchdog put its March forecasts together.\par \par \uc2 That uncovered \u8220\'20\'1C\u163\'00\'A39.5bn of net [spending] pressure which they did not declare to us, which under the law and under the Act they should have\u8221\'20\'1D, Hughes says.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.58pm GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.08am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2 : cutting waiting lists won't end labour shortages\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Dr Jeevun Sandher then asks about the impact of the UK\u8217\'20\'19s health crisis on the economy.\par \par \uc2 Q: Is it fair to say that potential output is limited by the state of our \uc2 NHS\uc2 , with the highest waiting lists in history?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief Richard Hughes says hundreds of thousands of people are out of work due to health reasons.\par \par \uc2 But most people on the waiting lists are not those people, Hughes adds: they\u8217\'20\'19re people who are older, who weren\u8217\'20\'19t working, or are still working even though they need healthcare.\par \par \uc2 So fixing the lengthy waiting lists might have some impact on the labour force.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.42am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.04am GMT\par \par \uc2 Q: How confident are you in the picture you have on the UK\u8217\'20\'19s jobs market, as the \uc2 Office for National Statistics\uc2  had problems getting responses to its labour market survey, forcing the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  to use alternative sources?\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNot very\u8221\'20\'1D, sighs professor David Miles.\par \par \uc2 The reliability of the ONS\u8217\'20\'19s labour force surveys has fallen a lot, he says, due to the drop in response rates. Fixing that is taking time.\par \par \uc2 Miles adds that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  has other surveys of the jobs market too.\par \par \uc2 And he\u8217\'20\'19s fairly confident that the labour market is \u8220\'20\'1Crelatively tight\u8221\'20\'1D, given vacancy levels are still high (although falling).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Yuan Yang asks about the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s assumption that there is very little spare capacity in the UK economy.\par \par \uc2 Q: Isn\u8217\'20\'19t that pessimistic, given the scarring effect of the pandemic on the economy, the shrinking workforce since 2020? If that scarring is caused by a health crisis, can the government fix it?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chair Richard Hughes responds, saying there\u8217\'20\'19s a difference between untapped potential and spare capacity.\par \par \uc2 With unemployment at a low rate, around 4%, and high vacancies, that implies a small output gap, he says.\par \par \uc2 Many of the people who have left the workforce are out of work due to health reasons, Hughes points out, and will need help to get back into the labour force. That will take time, and those people may not return to full-time jobs.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.58am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.47am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief: We expected gilt market to be surprised by the budget\par \par \uc2 Liberal Democrat MP Chris Coghlan turns the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s attention to the rise in government borrowing costs after the budget.\par \par \uc2 Q: How worried are you about the increase in gilt yields \u8211\'20\'13 they rose by 10 basis points on Friday, compared to 40 points after the 2022 mini-budget?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief Richard Hughes says the fiscal regulator had expected the budget to be \u8220\'20\'1Ca surprise to the markets\u8221\'20\'1D, due to the volume of government debt (gilts) that are to be issued.\par \par \uc2 He tells MPs:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo a large extent the gilt market response was just a response to higher volumes \u8211\'20\'13 if there are more gilts on the market that drives down the price.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 [Reminder: the yield (or interest rate) on a government bond measures the cost of borrowing, and rises when prices fall].\par \par \uc2 Hughes adds that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe front-loading of the expenditure\u8221\'20\'1D in \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s budget was also a surprise.\par \par \uc2 The volume that the market was being asked to take down this year and next was more than they expected.\par \par \uc2 He explains that the \uc2 OBR\uc2  had expected the gilt market to be a bit surprised \u8211\'20\'13 that\u8217\'20\'19s why the \uc2 OBR\uc2  raised its forecasts for UK interest rates by a quarter of one percentage point.\par \par \uc2 enltrWe expect Bank Rate to fall from 5% now to 3\u189\'00\'BD% by the end of the forecast. We have raised Bank Rate and gilt rates \u188\'00\'BC percentage point above pre-measures market data, as the fiscal easing in this Budget was unlikely to have been fully anticipated when we finalised our forecast. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GQ1ntgmETH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/GQ1ntgmETH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  (@OBR_UK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/OBR_UK/status/1853743901028323639?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Hughes adds that gilt yields have now settled broadly in line with its forecasts, tho \u8220\'20\'1Cmaybe a little bit above, but not significantly\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Today, UK 10-year gilts are yielding 4.48%, up from 4.31% the day before the Budget, but below the 4.53% they hit after the fiscal event.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/31/uks-borrowing-costs-rise-as-reevess-budget-prompts-fears-of-slower-interest-rate-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK\u8217\'20\'19s borrowing costs rise as Reeves\u8217\'20\'19s budget prompts fears of slower interest rate cuts}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.51am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 Harriett Baldwin\uc2 , Conservative MP,\par \par \uc2 Q: Your report says that the economic and fiscal outlook is broadly unchanged since March, while the budget creates a \u163\'00\'A3350bn increase in public spending \u8211\'20\'13 is that responsible?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  chief Richard Hughes says there is a \u8220\'20\'1Cloosening\u8221\'20\'1D of fiscal policy in last week\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 About half the tax rises announced last week are \u8220\'20\'1Crelatively certain and reliable\u8221\'20\'1D, such as the rise in employers\u8217\'20\'19 national insurance contributions, Hughes says.\par \par \uc2 The other half, focused on raising taxes on a small number of people, are less certain, though.\par \par \uc2 Q: What is the impact on interest rates, and how sensitive are we to further shocks?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  member Tom Josephs says a 1% rise in interest rates would add about \u163\'00\'A316bn to borrowing, while it would only take a 0.3% increase to wipe out the government\u8217\'20\'19s headroom to hit its fiscal targets (tho there are different estimates within the \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s work).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.36am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.27am GMT\par \par \uc2 Labour MP Rachel Blake asks the \uc2 OBR\uc2  about the impact that the government\u8217\'20\'19s proposed planning reforms will have on growth\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Q: has the \uc2 OBR\uc2  factored in the \u8220\'20\'1Cdeliverability\u8221\'20\'1D and the market\u8217\'20\'19s response to those reforms?\par \par \uc2 David Miles says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  hasn\u8217\'20\'19t made a direct estimate of the impact on growth, as it is waiting for more detail on the plans.\par \par \uc2 The chance are, he adds, that the plans will create some upside on residential construction (housebuilding).\par \par \uc2 Miles also reminds MPs that previous governments have also promised to tweak the planning system to improve housebuilding, but found it hard to \u8220\'20\'1Cmove the dial\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s hard to know quite how effective measures will be.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.22am GMT\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Committee turn to household spending and saving\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Q: Savings patterns changed after Covid \u8211\'20\'13 what do you think will happen with savings levels going forward, if people feel insecure?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 OBR\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s David Miles says the household savings ratio was very high in Covid as people couldn\u8217\'20\'19t go out and spend. That\u8217\'20\'19s not surprising \u8211\'20\'13 but what is surprising, he adds, is that savings rates haven\u8217\'20\'19t fallen back to pre-Covid levels, as expected.\par \par \uc2 One minor factor is the rise in interest rates, making saving more lucrative.\par \par \uc2 A stronger factor, though, is \u8220\'20\'1Cheightened uncertainty\u8221\'20\'1D after the shock of Covid, and rising energy prices.\par \par \uc2 Professor Miles says:\par \par \uc2 Our best guess\u8230\'20\'26. is that the savings rate does drop a little bit from its [current] level, but it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get back as low as it was pre-Covid.\par \par \uc2 And that could have an impact on demand in the economy, given the importance of consumer spending.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.18am GMT\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2 : Budget gives temporary growth boost\par \par \uc2 The Treasury Committee kicks off its hearing with the \uc2 OBR\uc2  by asking about its forecasts for GDP growth, which are \u8220\'20\'1Ctypically more optimistic\u8221\'20\'1D than the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 Richard Hughes says the \uc2 OBR\uc2  has revised its forecast for growth this year up to 1.1%, after the \u8220\'20\'1Crelatively disappointing\u8221\'20\'1D growth of almost 0% last year.\par \par \uc2 He tells MPs:\par \par \uc2 We do think that the combination of the momentum coming into this year plus the boost given to GDP by government policies boosts that growth rate up to 2% going into next year.\par \par \uc2 But, Hughes then cautions that this is mostly a \u8220\'20\'1Ctemporary boost to output\u8221\'20\'1D, due to the government increasing spending by more than taxation in this budget.\par \par \uc2 That raises GDP by half a percentage point at its peak, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a temporary effect, with growth expected to dip back to 1.5% at the end of the forecast horizon (to 2029-30). That, Hughes points out, is below its estimated long-run potential growth of the UK, of around 1.66% per year.\par \par \uc2 enltrRichard Hughes explains to the Treasury Committee that the economy stagnated last year but is expected to grow by 1.1% this year.\par \par \uc2 Growth is set to rise to 2% in 2025, before returning to slightly below the economy\u8217\'20\'19s estimated potential growth rate of 1?% over the rest of the\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/denQGVrXb5"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/denQGVrXb5}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  (@OBR_UK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/OBR_UK/status/1853741123249836245?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Conservative MP \uc2 John Glen\uc2  probes the \uc2 OBR\uc2  on this\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Q: Your assessment over the forecast period is that growth will be 0.7% lower than expected at the March budget. The government, though, would argue that their supply-side reforms would have a positive effect on growth \u8211\'20\'13 why have you not factored that in?\par \par \uc2 OBR\uc2  member David Miles says the substantial investment in public investment will lift growth, but that takes time to come though \u8211\'20\'13 so most of the impact on growth will arrive beyond the five-year horizon.\par \par \uc2 But on the downside, Miles says the increase in employers\u8217\'20\'19 national insurance contributions will dampen labour market growth, which hurts the UK\u8217\'20\'19s productivity in the long term.\par \par \uc2 Miles adds that productivity growth has been \u8220\'20\'1Cabsymal\u8221\'20\'1D in the last 12 years, for various reasons (such as Covid, and the invasion of Ukraine). The \uc2 OBR\uc2  takes the view that the next 10 years will be more positive \u8211\'20\'13 and roughly halfway between the dismal last decade and the brighter 40 years before that.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.59am GMT\par \par \uc2 MPs prepare to question \uc2 OBR\uc2  about the budget\par \par \uc2 Over in parliament, MPs on the Treasury committee are taking evidence from the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2  about last week\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19ll hearing from Richard Hughes, Chair of the \uc2 OBR\uc2 , along with professor David Miles and Tom Josephs, who are both members of the Budget Responsibility Committee.\par \par \uc2 The committee says its scrutiny is \u8220\'20\'1Clikely to examine whether the Chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s new fiscal rules are right for the health of the UK economy and changes to spending, taxation and debt\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 You can watch it live here:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.53am GMT\par \par \uc2 The US dollar has dipped very slightly this morning, and is down by 0.12% against other major currencies.\par \par \uc2 Matt Britzman, senior equity analyst at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is a \u8216\'20\'18wait and see mood\u8217\'20\'19 on the markets as uncertainty weighs about the outcome of what appears to be a deadlocked US Presidential election.\par \par \uc2 Campaigning is converging on the crucial swing state of Pennsylvania and the extent to which voters can be galvanised to join queues at polling booths could make all the difference. The dollar has been fluctuating but is still lower on the week, as \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19 chances have appeared to have improved. A pro-tariff Trump presidency could see the dollar strengthen amid concerns higher inflation will prompt the Fed to keep interest rates higher.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.37am GMT\par \par \uc2 Pimco on the economic impact of Trump vs. Harris\par \par \uc2 Looking back at the US election, bond trading giant Pimco suggests that the potential economic impact of the candidates\u8217\'20\'19 policies could be less than the market assumes.\par \par \uc2 Libby Cantrill\uc2 , Pimco\u8217\'20\'19s head of U.S. public policy, points to four areas where the choice between the Republican and Democratic candidate won\u8217\'20\'19t make a difference:\par \par \uc2 Specifically, 1) deficits will be higher under either \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  or \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and the differences between the two are not likely to be that much different due to the narrow Congressional majorities that either will likely have to contend with);\par \par \uc2 2) both candidates will have to deal with taxes in 2025 due to the expiring tax cuts under the Trump Tax Cuts and Jobs Act, and in either case, we are likely to see most, if not all of the tax cuts be extended;\par \par \uc2 3) both candidates will continue to be hawkish on China ; and\par \par \uc2 4) the Fed will remain independent due to institutional guardrails and no current vacancies (and despite some jawboning that we can expect if Trump wins in particular).\par \par \uc2 But there are (clearly!) also large differences between the two candidates.\par \par \uc2 And on economic issues, Cantrill identifies three:\par \par \uc2 1) tariffs, which are likely to stay high under Harris on China but will likely go higher under Trump and other countries are likely to be subject to increased tariff pressure as well\par \par \uc2 2) immigration, although we would note that for Trump to do some of what he is said he will do would largely require Congress, and:\par \par \uc2 3) regulation, where we would expect Trump to have a lighter touch (we would note that reversing regulation is hard, although there would undoubtedly be more regulatory certainty under Trump from the absence of new regulation as well as rolling back Biden executive orders).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.48am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.17am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK car sales fall despite rise in electric vehicle registrations\par \par \uc2 Just in: Car sales in the UK fell last month, despite a pick-up in registrations of electric vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Industry group the SMMT reports that businesses, fleets and private buyers registered 9,241 fewer vehicles last month than in October 2023, bringing total new registrations down to 144,288.\par \par \uc2 Sales of diesel-powered cars fell by 20%, with petrol down 14% year-on-year.\par \par \uc2 But\u8230\'20\'26. sales of battery electric vehicles (BEVs) rose by 24.5% year-on-year, helped by \u8220\'20\'1Ca raft of new models driving the strongest growth this year\u8221\'20\'1D. That lifted Bev\u8217\'20\'19s share of the market to 20.7%.\par \par \uc2 enltr??Battery electric the only powertrain to see uptake rise as manufacturers continue to subsidise transition with billions in unsustainable discounting. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/njGDUQCh41"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/njGDUQCh41}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BhoV1ONluu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BhoV1ONluu}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 SMMT (@SMMT) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SMMT/status/1853724710485238021?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The SMMT warns, though, that manufacturers are subsidising the transition to electric cars with \u8220\'20\'1Cbillions in unsustainable discounting\u8221\'20\'1D, adding:\par \par \uc2 One in five BEV models now retailing for less than the average petrol or diesel but consumer support still needed for fast and fair transition.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.24am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.09am GMT\par \par \uc2 Online fast fashion chain \uc2 ASOS\uc2  has tumbled deeper in the red, as it tries to implement its turnaround plan.\par \par \uc2 ASOS\uc2  has reported a pre-tax loss of \u163\'00\'A3379m for the year to 3rd September, up from a \u163\'00\'A3296m loss in the previous year.\par \par \uc2 The company says it hit its targets in its \u8220\'20\'1CBack to Fashion\u8221\'20\'1D push, having completed its stock clearance process and embedded a new commercial model.\par \par \uc2 Group revenues fell 18% in the year, as \uc2 ASOS\uc2  focused on cutting its clothing inventory in half, through \u8220\'20\'1Cdisciplined stock management\u8221\'20\'1D and writing off around \u163\'00\'A3100m of products.\par \par \uc2 ASOS\uc2  insists it is focused on delivering \u8220\'20\'1Csustainable, profitable growth\u8221\'20\'1D, and points out that sales of new ranges were up 24% last year.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/nils-pratley-on-finance/2023/nov/01/asos-reputation-online-revolution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CEO }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/nils-pratley-on-finance/2023/nov/01/asos-reputation-online-revolution"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jos\u233\'00\'E9 Antonio Ramos Calamonte}}}\uc2  says \uc2 ASOS\uc2  is now in the strongest position it has been in years, with \u8220\'20\'1Cthe right level of newness to excite customers\u8221\'20\'1D, and improved operational efficiency.\par \par \uc2 Calamonte adds:\par \par \uc2 With these solid foundations in place, we can focus on delivering experiences that delight our 20 million customers. There is much work to do, but we have already seen our efforts rewarded with new product sales increasing 24% YoY over the last three months. I am energised by the progress we have made so far and excited for the next phase of our journey.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 ASIS\uc2  have dropped by 6.5% this morning, though, hitting 345p, the lowest since the end of August. Back in March 2021 they were worth almost \u163\'00\'A360.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.55am GMT\par \par \uc2 Associated British Foods\uc2 , the owner of discount clothing chain \uc2 Primark\uc2 , has posted a 43% rise in pre-tax profits for the last year.\par \par \uc2 ABF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO, \uc2 George Weston\uc2 , says \uc2 Primark\uc2  achieved \u8220\'20\'1Cgood sales growth this year\u8221\'20\'1D (up 6%, or 1.2% on a like-for-like basis) and managed to increase its profit margin (it rose to 11.7% in the last year, up from 8.2% in 2023).\par \par \uc2 Primark\uc2  grew its sales by 2% in the UK and Ireland, and also expanded in its \u8220\'20\'1Ckey growth markets\u8221\'20\'1D, including the US, France, Spain, Italy and Central and Eastern Europe.\par \par \uc2 Weston has told Reuters this morning that \uc2 Primark\uc2  is expecting strong trading this Christmas, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe think that Christmas is going to be good.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ABF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s grocery sales grew 4%, despite a \u8220\'20\'1Cmixed\u8221\'20\'1D performance by Ovaltine, while operating profits at its sugar business jumped by 52%\par \par \uc2 But revenues at \uc2 ABF\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s agriculture revenue decreased by 9%.\par \par \uc2 It says:\par \par \uc2 Lower sales in our compound feed businesses reflected reduced commodity prices and continued soft demand in the UK and China.\par \par \uc2 Market conditions in the UK remained challenging due to reduced herd sizes and excess feed production capacity and in China the market was depressed by the economic environment and low farm profitability.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.57am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.43am GMT\par \par \uc2 The results of today\u8217\'20\'19s Congressional elections will have a massive influence on the next president\u8217\'20\'19s ability to get their policy goals into law.\par \par \uc2 A divided government (where one party controls the White House, and the other controls Congress) means less can be done.\par \par \uc2 Brian Gardner, chief Washington policy strategist at investment bank \uc2 Stifel\uc2 , predicts that Republicans will win enough seats to retake the Senate.\par \par \uc2 He tells clients:\par \par \uc2 Democrats currently hold a 51-49 advantage in the Senate. Thirty-four seats are being contested this year and Democrats hold 23 of those seats while Republicans hold 11 which gives the GOP a numerical advantage in the ability to gain seats.\par \par \uc2 With the retirement of Sen. Joe Manchin (D-WV), Republicans are likely to gain that seat and are well positioned to flip Sen. \uc2 Jon Tester\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s seat (D-MT). This would give the GOP 51 seats and the majority. Ohio is the GOP\u8217\'20\'19s next best pick-up opportunity and while there are several other close races (Michigan, Pennsylvania, and Wisconsin). We think Democrats will likely (but not definitely) retain those seats.\par \par \uc2 But, the race for the House of Representatives is \u8220\'20\'1Ceven tougher to forecast than the presidential race\u8221\'20\'1D, Gardner says, adding:\par \par \uc2 Regardless of which party wins the House, we think the majority will be less than 10 seats which will make governing challenging (again)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.34am GMT\par \par \uc2 Michael Brown\uc2 , senior research strategist at brokerage Pepperstone, has predicted the likely impact on the markets for a \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , or \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 , victory.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 A TRUMP victory would likely see markets focus on a theme of reflation, with tax cuts likely to provide a fillip to growth, and the imposition of tariffs giving rise to a potential resurgence in inflationary pressures. In terms of initial market reactions, one would expect:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 US dollar upside, particularly against those currencies which could find themselves subject to tariffs, such as the MXN [Mexican peso], \uc2 CNH\uc2  [the Chinese Yuan] and EUR [the euro]\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Treasury [\uc2 US government\uc2  debt] downside, most notably at the long-end of the curve, as growth and inflation expectations rerate higher\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Upside for equities, broadly speaking, amid expectations for a lower regulatory burden, with the Energy and Defence sectors likely to outperform\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, a HARRIS victory would represent a theme of continuity. In terms of initial market reactions, one would likely see:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 US dollar weakness, as Trump-linked hedges are unwound, and trade-sensitive currencies breathe a sigh of relief\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Treasury downside, albeit not to the same degree as in a Trump win, on bets that fiscal policy will become more expansionary\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Knee-jerk equity downside, on concerns over potential for tighter regulation, though any dips are likely to be bought in short order, while sectors such as \uc2 Clean Energy\uc2  and Technology could outperform\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.12am GMT\par \par \uc2 European stock markets have opened cautiously, with investors eying events in the US.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s FTSE 100 share index is down 4 points in early trading, at 8179 points. Defensive stocks are in demand, with \uc2 Severn Trent\uc2  (+2.4%) and \uc2 United Utilities\uc2  (+2.5%) among the top Footsie risers.\par \par \uc2 At the other end of the table, \uc2 Schroders\uc2  are down 11% after reporting that its client funds saw a net outflow of \u163\'00\'A32.3bn in July-September. \uc2 Schroders\uc2  says it was hit by \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinued market volatility in China\u8221\'20\'1D in the last quarter.\par \par \uc2 France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC and Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX indices rose 0.1% at the open, while Spain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 IBEX\uc2  dipped by 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  and Three: we see a path to final clearance\par \par \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  and Three have welcomed {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-vote-end-strike-markets-us-election-harris-trump-dollar-bonds-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6729ca0a8f0842b343aea036#block-6729ca0a8f0842b343aea036"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the CMA\u8217\'20\'19s new Remedies Working Paper on their merger.}}}\par \par \uc2 In a statement, the two (soon to become one?) mobile operators say they believe the Paper \u8220\'20\'1Cprovides a path to final clearance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 They say:\par \par \uc2 The Parties welcome the CMA\u8217\'20\'19s recognition that the significant improvements in network quality delivered by their Joint Network Plan will \u8220\'20\'1Cboost competition between mobile network operators in the long term and benefit millions of people who rely on mobile services.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The merger is a once-in-a-generation opportunity to transform the UK\u8217\'20\'19s digital infrastructure \u8211\'20\'13 which lags significantly behind its European peers \u8211\'20\'13 and for more than 50 million UK customers to benefit from a vastly better mobile experience.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.49am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.45am GMT\par \par \uc2 UK regulator indicates \u163\'00\'A315bn \uc2 Vodafone\uc2 -Three merger likely to proceed\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, the \u163\'00\'A315bn merger between \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  and Hutchison\u8217\'20\'19s Three UK has moved closer to being cleared.\par \par \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s competition authority has declared today that the merger could proceed \u8220\'20\'1Cif appropriate remedies are implemented,\u8221\'20\'1D including upgrading the merged company\u8217\'20\'19s network to speed up the roll-out of 5G services.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 The CMA has today set out a Remedies Working Paper to seek views on the effectiveness of a proposed remedy package.\par \par \uc2 It provisionally finds that a legally binding commitment to undertake the network integration and investment programme proposed by \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  and Three would significantly improve the quality of the merged company\u8217\'20\'19s mobile network, boosting competition between mobile network operators in the long term and benefiting millions of people who rely on mobile services.\par \par \uc2 Previously, the CMA had sounded more concerned about the deal \u8211\'20\'13 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/sep/13/vodafone-three-merger-tens-of-millions-could-face-higher-bills-says-uk-watchdog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 saying it could hurt competition and push up bills}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Back in July, \uc2 Vodafone\uc2  CEO \uc2 Margherita Della Valle\uc2  told the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  that the Labour government will fail to achieve its promise of nationwide access to 5G if the deal was blocked.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/28/vodafone-says-labour-must-let-it-merge-with-three-uk-to-deliver-nationwide-5g"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Vodafone says Labour must let it merge with Three UK to deliver nationwide 5G}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.50am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.31am GMT\par \par \uc2 The first results in the US presidential race are in, and it\u8217\'20\'19s a tie!\par \par \uc2 Fittingly (given the closeness of the polls) \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  and \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  have won three votes each in Dixville \uc2 Notch\uc2 , a tiny New Hampshire town which traditionally kicks off voting on election day.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://edition.cnn.com/2024/11/05/politics/dixville-notch-new-hampshire-2024-results/index.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 According to CNN}}}\uc2 , four Republicans and two undeclared voters participated in the poll \u8211\'20\'13 in which all eligible voters gather at the now-dormant Balsams Hotel in Dixville \uc2 Notch\uc2  to cast their secret ballots once polls open at midnight.\par \par \uc2 enltrAnd the first ballots actually cast on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ElectionDay?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ElectionDay}}}\uc2  in person have been cast in Dixville \uc2 Notch\uc2 , New Hampshire.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a precinct of 6 people.\par \par \uc2 4 registered GOP, 2 undeclared, CNN reports.\par \par \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  and \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  tie with 3 votes each. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zeGHfqpoDU"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/zeGHfqpoDU}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/NIilzC7CiV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/NIilzC7CiV}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andrew Havranek ?? (@Andrew_Havranek) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Andrew_Havranek/status/1853666975831957756?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.32am GMT\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.23am GMT\par \par \uc2 Investors brace for uncertainty over US election\par \par \uc2 With Americans heading to the polls today, the financial markets are in a state of high excitement over the race to the White House.\par \par \uc2 Market volatility has been picking up in recent days, as investors have tried to assess the likely winner of today\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election, and how congressional elections may play out too.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, the US dollar dipped to a two-week low, after a poll showed \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  with a surprise three-point lead over \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in Iowa. The greenback is flat this morning.\par \par \uc2 Stocks fell on Wall Street last night too, with the Dow Jones industrial average losing 257 points or 0.6% to 41,794 points.\par \par \uc2 enltr???? StockMarket close lower ahead of residential election results\par \par \uc2 ?? Latest poll from NBC News shows a \u8220\'20\'1Cdeadlocked race\u8221\'20\'1D between former President \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  & Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\par \par \uc2 ???? Markets on Monday {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Dowjones?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Dowjones}}}\uc2  -0.6% {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Nasdaq?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Nasdaq}}}\uc2  -0.33%\par \par \uc2 S&P500 -0.28%\par \par \uc2 Dow Jones dragged by\par \par \uc2 ??\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Nigel D'Souza (@Nigel__DSouza) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Nigel__DSouza/status/1853605744660410709?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 November 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 As the polls have been so tight, traders are concerned we will not know the result for several days or even longer.\par \par \uc2 Erik Knutzen\uc2  and Jeff Blazek, co-CIOs of multi-asset strategies at \uc2 Neuberger Berman\uc2 , told clients that the uncertainty could last days, or even weeks:\par \par \uc2 Uncertainty around control of Congress, particularly the House, could also extend longer than uncertainty over who is in the White House. And a contested result could add still more days or weeks of market volatility.\par \par \uc2 We have been anticipating a pick-up in volatility as the election nears, and this week is likely to see a peak in that volatility, lasting until we have an unambiguous outcome. This is a key reason why we have been counselling against taking substantial active positions\u8212\'20\'14particularly active positions tilted to one or other election result.\par \par \uc2 The exception would be in assets that can help to diversify portfolios and dampen their volatility, such as gold and other commodities.\par \par \uc2 Anxiety over the election has already contributed to \uc2 US government\uc2  bonds suffering their worst month in two years in October.\par \par \uc2 Betting markets have narrowed significantly on the eve of Tuesday\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election, eroding Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lead over Harris as Americans cast their votes.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/nov/04/presidential-election-betting-odds-polling"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s lead over Harris in betting market erodes as platforms tighten odds}}}\par \par \uc2 Last night, Trump and Harris have held their final election rallies.\par \par \uc2 Harris ended with a message of unity, telling supporters in Philadelphia that her campaign was ending \u8220\'20\'1Cwith energy, with joy,\u8221\'20\'1D adding:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInstead of stewing on an enemies list I will work on my to-do list.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump has just wrapped up in Michigan, where he insulted President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , former House Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , representative Adam Schiff, California governor \uc2 Gavin Newsom\uc2  and Harris, and also promised action on immigration, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe day I take office the migrant invasion ends.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 You can track all the action in our US election liveblog:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2024/nov/05/us-election-2024-live-updates-donald-trump-vs-kamala-harris-results-presidential-elections-day-latest-news-polls-update"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US election 2024 live updates: Trump launches insults at final rally as Harris ends campaign promising to \u8216\'20\'18get to work\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.21am GMT\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO: Much work ahead to return \uc2 Boeing\uc2  to excellence\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  has said he was pleased the plane-maker\u8217\'20\'19s union had ratified the latest pay deal.\par \par \uc2 In a message to staff, Ortberg said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the past few months have been difficult for all of us, we are all part of the same team.\par \par \uc2 There is much work ahead to return to the excellence that made \uc2 Boeing\uc2  an iconic company.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.20am GMT\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2  strike ends as workers accept new contract\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s management, and airline operators across the world, will be letting out a large sign of relief today, after its factory workers agreed to end their strike after 53 days.\par \par \uc2 West Coast factory workers have accepted a pay deal, ending {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/13/boeing-factory-workers-vote-to-strike-threatening-aircraft-deliveries-gold-hits-record-high-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the walkout that began in mid-September}}}\uc2  and halted much of the company\u8217\'20\'19s jet production.\par \par \uc2 Boeing\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s union said members voted 59% in favor of the new contract, which offers a 43.65% compounded wage increase over four years \u8211\'20\'13 13%, 9%, 9%, and 7%.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/05/boeing-workers-end-strike-walkout-seattle-portland-california"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Boeing workers end bitter seven-week strike after clinching new pay deal }}}\par \par \uc2 Jon Holden, the union\u8217\'20\'19s lead negotiator, told members:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a victory. We can hold our heads high.\par \par \uc2 Now it\u8217\'20\'19s our job to get back to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Holden has also said that the new contract can \u8220\'20\'1Ccreate a new foundation\u8221\'20\'1D, to help \uc2 Boeing\uc2  return to building the highest quality and safest airplanes in the world.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWorking people know what it\u8217\'20\'19s like when a company overreaches and takes away more than is fair. Through this strike and the resulting victory, frontline workers at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  have done their part to begin rebalancing the scales in favor of the middle class \u8211\'20\'13 and in doing so, we hope to inspire other workers in our industry and beyond to continue standing up for justice at work.\par \par \uc2 Through this victory and the strike that made it possible, IAM members have taken a stand for respect and fair wages in the workplace. Our members perform high quality and flight critical work for the airplanes we build and deserve a return on their labor investment that provides for the quality of life worthy of that labor.\par \par \uc2 The breakthrough eases the pressure on new \uc2 Boeing\uc2  CEO \uc2 Kelly Ortberg\uc2  after two previous offers were voted down in recent weeks.\par \par \uc2 Airlines will also be relieved, after being hit by delays to \uc2 Boeing\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s shipments.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, \uc2 Ryanair\uc2  said continued problems at \uc2 Boeing\uc2  meant it wouldcut its growth plans for next year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/nov/04/ryanair-profits-lower-and-boeing-737-michael-oleary"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ryanair profits plunge amid lower fares and Boeing delays}}}\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am GMT: UK new car sales for October\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am GMT: UK Service sector \uc2 PMI\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am GMT: Treasury committee hearing with Richard Hughes, chair of the \uc2 Office for Budget Responsibility\uc2 , on its economic and fiscal forecast.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 2pm GMT: Treasury committee hearing with independent economists about the budget\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241105ekb500132\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
