\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump\u8217\'20\'19s mass deportation plan would be \u8216\'20\'18economic disaster\u8217\'20\'19 for US\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore \par \uc2 1,196 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2024\par 13:53 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Loss of migrant workers would trigger productivity losses and a new round of inflationary pricing pressureUS elections 2024 \u8211\'20\'13 live updates\par \par \uc2 If elected, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  plans to carry out \u8220\'20\'1Cthe largest deportation operation in American history\u8221\'20\'1D. After pushback on Joe Biden\u8217\'20\'19s border policies, \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  has embraced border restrictions and the need to maintain limits on asylum seekers. But neither candidate captures the realities of US immigration.\par \par \uc2 US consumers are accustomed to cheap goods and services, and the economic rationale for large-scale immigration has been largely avoided. In a country that relies on a mobile, low-cost workforce, the loss of migrant workers would trigger productivity losses and a new round of inflationary pricing pressure.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be an economic disaster for America and Americans,\u8221\'20\'1D says Zeke Hernandez, an economics professor at the \uc2 Wharton School of the University of Pennsylvania\uc2 , of Trump\u8217\'20\'19s deportations threat. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not just the immigrants would be harmed, but we, the people of America, would be economically harmed.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Baby boomers are retiring, and with fewer immigrants, the workforce will struggle to sustain economic output: US employers will need to hire 240,000 people a month for the next five years just to replace those who are stepping out, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.protectedincome.org/news/labor-day-peak-65-trades-hit-hardest/#:~:text=Washington%2C%20D.C.%2C%20August%2027%2C,of%20Peak%20Baby%20Boomer%20retirements."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to one recent study.}}}\par \par \uc2 Hernandez, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://us.macmillan.com/author/zekehernandez"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 author of a recent book, The Truth About Immigration}}}\uc2 , argues that immigrants contribute talent, investment, innovation, consumption and tax revenue. \u8220\'20\'1CIf you lose those things, there are fewer jobs, the economy contracts and becomes less diversified.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Of the undocumented migrants, between 8 and 9 million are in the workforce doing essential jobs that Americans disproportionately don\u8217\'20\'19t want to do or work in sectors where there aren\u8217\'20\'19t enough workers.\par \par \uc2 Typically, that\u8217\'20\'19s farm work (one third of the labor force), construction work (about one quarter) and about half of the labor force in skilled work like drywalling, plumbing and insulation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUndocumented immigrants make up a huge proportion of household services, manufacturing work, kitchen staff in restaurants. Americans simply do not do those jobs, or there are not enough to go around. But if you lose those key \u8216\'20\'18bottleneck\u8217\'20\'19 workers, the native workforce also can\u8217\'20\'19t do their jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D says Hernandez.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cmsny.org/publications/2024-mass-deportation-program-devastate-american-families-101024/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A study by the Center for Migration Studies}}}\uc2  estimates undocumented workers contribute $97bn in federal, state and local taxes, their removal from the workforce would have a substantial impact on local economies, including pushing nearly 10 million US citizens into economic hardship.\par \par \uc2 Families would also be profoundly impacted. About 5.8m US households are home to at least one undocumented resident and mass deportations would break up nearly 5m US families. The cost of bringing up US-born children whose caregivers are removed has been put at $116bn.\par \par \uc2 Taxpayers would have to foot the bill. Apprehending and deporting just 1 million of an estimated 11 million-12 million undocumented migrants in the US could cost taxpayers about $20bn, or $19,599 per person, according to a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cbsnews.com/news/trump-plan-deport-immigrants-cost/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CBS News analysis}}}\uc2  of federal data \u8211\'20\'13 and take far longer than the term of a four-year Trump administration.\par \par \uc2 Business leaders have been fairly quiet on Trump\u8217\'20\'19s plans \u8211\'20\'13 possibly fearing retribution \u8211\'20\'13 but some lobby groups have begun to tally the costs of mass deportation. The construction sector employs an estimated 1.5 million undocumented workers, or 13% of its total workforce \u8211\'20\'13 a larger share than any other, according to data the \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Construction firms, already facing labor shortages, are warning that the loss of immigrant workers would push new home prices higher. The \uc2 National Association of Home Builders\uc2  considers foreign-born workers, regardless of legal status, \u8220\'20\'1Ca vital and flexible source of labor\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The CEO of the \uc2 NAHB\uc2 , Jim Tobin, told NBC that their loss would be \u8220\'20\'1Cdetrimental to the construction industry and our labor supply and exacerbate our housing affordability problems\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Business Roundtable notes that \u8220\'20\'1Cimmigrants have always been a key part of America\u8217\'20\'19s innovative spirit. A vast majority of economists and business leaders agree that immigration is a net positive for the US economy\u8221\'20\'1D but says \u8220\'20\'1Cthe system for welcoming these highly valuable workers is broken\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.chloeneast.com/uploads/8/9/9/7/8997263/ehlmv_draft.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 A 2023 study of previous efforts at mass deportation,}}}\uc2  such as President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Secure Communities program from 2008 to 2014 that resulted in the deportation of almost half a million people, found that any benefits from reduced job competition that US-born workers face were counteracted by a decline in labor demand due to an increase in labor costs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPolice-based enforcement policies aimed at reducing the number of undocumented immigrants should consider the potential negative spillover effects on the labor market outcomes of immigrants who remain in the US and on US-born workers,\u8221\'20\'1D a \uc2 University of Denver\uc2  study concluded.\par \par \uc2 The same effect was seen during the Trump and Biden administrations when the Covid pandemic caused about a million fewer immigrants to enter the US leading to labor shortages and reduced output, and contributing to inflationary pressure.\par \par \uc2 The post-pandemic spike in immigration contributed to inflation coming down, according to Hernandez. \u8220\'20\'1CImmigration allowed business to hire again and raise output to what the market demands, so prices normalized,\u8221\'20\'1D he says.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless opponents of immigration have been \u8220\'20\'1Cvery effective at flooding the zone with false or dubious claims\u8221\'20\'1D, said Hernandez.\par \par \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s policies, which Harris is now walking back, have created complications for the Latino communities in the US, resulting in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.documentcloud.org/documents/25177909-nbc-news-telemundo-cnbc-latino-voter-poll-topline-results-september-17-23-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  declining support}}}\uc2  for Democrats and a frantic late-campaign effort to shore up their votes. In 2012, Latino support for Obama was at 71%. Eight years later Biden won 62%. A recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2024/10/13/us/elections/times-siena-poll-hispanic-likely-electorate.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Times/Siena poll}}}\uc2  found Harris with 56%.\par \par \uc2 According to Ana Valdez, president and CEO of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://email.pollackgroup.com/c/eJwUyj2S6yAMAODTmNKDfoxRQfGa3AMkSDxxQoaHs9ff2f6zlAk2M1cT7CgQmTy7R_K4mRFkIvVSyNjvXjUThLJBYHFHQo8MHnYIW0ReY94aUgUQCkVsX9h_-nlmfd5Hvz6r9pc702POz_-F_i14W_B25nm8u_V3H_pnSx95Ht-69nFf8OZGqvaTh62Per76qAv7-5WHHfm9al-vp5uJsDaJrLGZL2IEbEUlVIlNma24mWIQ3Qs1wWChlNggNNB9A6lVCLz7JvwNAAD___7oT1A"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Latino Donor Collaborative}}}\uc2 , says the negative myths around Latino immigration are just \u8220\'20\'1Cpure rhetoric \u8230\'20\'26 Trump knows that most of the workforce the US needs to continue growing comes from Latinos.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Valdez cites {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://blog.dol.gov/2021/09/15/hispanics-in-the-labor-force-5-facts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 labor department statistics }}}\uc2  that show Latino workers in the labor force have grown from 10.7 million in 1990 to 29 million in 2020, and are projected to reach 36 million in 2030. In 2030, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  projects they will account for one out of every five workers in the labor force, at 21.2%, or or 78% of net new workers by 2030.\par \par \uc2 Without those immigrant workers, the change in the standard of living for the middle class would be much more dramatic, Valdez says. \u8220\'20\'1CThe reason we have avocados or chicken to choose from every day is because of immigration.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Valdez, who worked in the Clinton administration, cites figure that show US Latinos generate $3.6tn in GDP and says the political discourse around the issue by both parties has equated all Latinos to undocumented immigrants and does not reflect the economic, data-backed reality.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Trump and Harris want to win the Latino vote, and if the winner wants to keep our support once they\u8217\'20\'19re in office, they need to change their perspective and messaging to Latinos and recognize the full scope of our economic contributions,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CNot doing so is reckless for their campaigns and the US economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241030ekau001md\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
