\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Jane Street Capital: the trader paying its interns more than Keir Starmer\b0\par\par\uc2 Kalyeena Makortoff Banking correspondent \par \uc2 757 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2024\par 08:04 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 30\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Secretive firm that has grown to rival \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  is revealed to reward lowliest recruits with $250,000 salary\par \par \uc2 Sitting down for his internship interview at Jane Street Capital in the summer of 2013, the future crypto-billionaire \uc2 Sam Bankman-Fried\uc2  was presented with a stack of poker chips and playing cards. But this was no ordinary game.\par \par \uc2 Jane Street traders, renowned for their financial trading prowess, unveiled an evolving list of quirky rules, peppered with intriguing side bets, as they started to vet the potential recruit.\par \par \uc2 The aim? To test whether Bankman-Fried had the problem-solving chops to join the secretive, fast-growing and high-speed trading firm that would eventually rival Wall Street behemoths such as \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The future \uc2 FTX\uc2  boss bagged the internship, was hired full-time and was reportedly paid $300,000 (\u163\'00\'A3230,000) in his first year.\par \par \uc2 Jane Street, which shuns the limelight, made headlines this week when it emerged that it was advertising New York internships that came with a $250,000 salary. At that rate, the firm\u8217\'20\'19s next crop of interns \u8211\'20\'13 particularly those involved in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.janestreet.com/join-jane-street/position/7450341002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quantitative research}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.janestreet.com/join-jane-street/position/7629383002/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 engineering}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 will be paid more than Keir Starmer, who is paid about \u163\'00\'A3167,000 ($218,000), and the head of the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2 , Jerome Powell, who takes home $203,500.\par \par \uc2 That is also while enjoying breakfast and lunch buffets, mentorship meetings and daily lectures on anything from coding to mock trades and, of course, poker.\par \par \uc2 But those same highly paid interns do not need a background in markets or finance. Instead, \uc2 Jane Street\uc2  is offering the big bucks to graduates with experience in data science, machine learning and coding in order to propel future profits.\par \par \uc2 But what is Jane Street and how is it able to offer such big cheques to its least experienced recruits?\par \par \uc2 Jane Street\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s beginnings date back to 2000, when it was founded by an ex-\uc2 IBM\uc2  developer and a small group of traders in New York. In the decades that followed, it quietly became one of the biggest financial firms that most people have never heard of.\par \par \uc2 It emerged as part of a new generation of quantitative trading outfits, meaning its traders use mathematics and statistics to determine how to bet on certain assets or financial markets. The company proudly declares itself to be \u8220\'20\'1Ca firm of puzzle solvers on and off the clock\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It has particularly become known for its expertise in exchange trading funds (ETFs), which are investment funds made up of a portfolio of assets which are sliced up into shares that can be bought and sold on the market to investors.\par \par \uc2 That is on top of becoming a major player in equities, bonds and options markets, which have helped push its trading volumes to new heights.\par \par \uc2 Jane Street\uc2  is notoriously secretive about its operations. \u8220\'20\'1CThe first time a New York Times piece appeared about them, there was like a nuclear meltdown,\u8221\'20\'1D one former employee told the author Michael Lewis for Going Infinite, his book on the rise and fall of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/nov/02/sam-bankman-guilty-ftx-alameda"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bankman-Fried}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Part of that may stem from its flat leadership structure, with the firm led by a group of senior managers rather than a single chief executive. Its senior staff are also the equity holders, though only one of its founders, Rob Granieri, remains at the firm.\par \par \uc2 It has also managed to foster a cult-like dedication from its 2,600-plus employees, who work across its five global offices in New York, Amsterdam, Hong Kong, Singapore and London. The last employs about 636 staff.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/54671865-4c7f-4692-a879-867ef68f0bde"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Documents leaked to the Financial Times}}}\uc2  this year showed that \uc2 Jane Street\uc2  estimates that it accounted for 10.4% of all stock trading in North America last year and more than 2% of all trading in more than 20 countries.\par \par \uc2 It reportedly made a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/7bdd0bc1-5284-4d52-93aa-ddfb609b0bd6"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 12-month profit of around $7.4bn}}}\uc2 , meaning it is catching up with well-known titans in US banking including \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2 , which reported $8.5bn in profits for 2023.\par \par \uc2 It has helped \uc2 Jane Street\uc2  to continue to pay its workforce handsomely. The firm offered pay and benefits worth $2.4bn last year, an average of about $900,000 for each employee.\par \par \uc2 Between its culture and pay, it seems to have found a winning combination, reportedly keeping staff turnover at just 6% over the last two years.\par \par \uc2 Jane Street turned down the Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s request for an interview and declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241020ekak001gt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
