\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Trump would weaken child labor protections in second term, report warns\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 1,011 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 October 2024\par 15:33 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Project 2025, a conservative playbook for a second Trump term, advocates for rolling back child labor protections, report also cites\par \par \uc2 State governments across the US are taking steps to eliminate protections for minors as rates of child labor violations, injuries and chronic school absenteeism rise, according to a report released on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://governingforimpact.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/10/GFI-EPI-CLC-Child-Labor-FLSA-Report_FINAL-1.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 report}}}\uc2  by Governing for Impact, the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2 , and Child Labor Coalition proposes actions the Biden-Harris administration can take in response to a recent surge in child labor violations around the country and a trend of some states passing legislation that rolls back state-level child labor protections.\par \par \uc2 Its authors also warn that moves to weaken child protections will probably escalate under a second Donald Trump presidency.\par \par \uc2 Injury rates among workers under the age of 18 nearly doubled from 2011 to 2020. All child labor violations almost increased by four times between 2015 and 2022, and hazard occupation violations more than doubled during the same period.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, legislators in more than 30 states have moved to weaken child labor protections since 2021, often citing claims of labor shortages and backed by industry groups in these pushes. The report noted these rollbacks at the state level have often either conflicted with federal law or sowed confusion among employers over which are applicable to them, leading to more child labor violations.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/may/26/what-is-project-2025-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Project 2025}}}\uc2 , a conservative guide for a second Trump administration, which aligns closely with Trump\u8217\'20\'19s policies and was authored and promoted by numerous former Trump officials and allies, advocates for rolling back child labor protections, the report cites.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome young adults show an interest in inherently dangerous jobs,\u8221\'20\'1D Project 2025 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://static.project2025.org/2025_MandateForLeadership_CHAPTER-18.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 claims}}}\uc2 . \u8220\'20\'1CWith parental consent and proper training, certain young adults should be allowed to learn and work in more dangerous occupations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf Trump wins, we know what to expect. He will try to weaken and loosen child labor protections. He did that in his first term,\u8221\'20\'1D said \uc2 Reid Maki\uc2 , director of child labor advocacy and coordinator at the Child Labor Coalition. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ll look for weak points, or use some false arguments to try to weaken protections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He noted the Trump administration attempted several rollbacks of child labor protections, including an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nelp.org/insights-research/trump-administration-moves-roll-back-child-labor-rules-expose-teen-workers-hazardous-jobs/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 effort}}}\uc2  to weaken hazardous occupation rules for child workers in nursing homes and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2019/jul/18/epa-chlorpyrifos-ban-children-brain-damage-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rolling back a ban}}}\uc2  on the pesticide chlorpyrifos, despite its ties to childhood brain damage, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.huffpost.com/entry/trumps-epa-could-expose-child-workers-to-dangerous-pesticides_n_5a565e90e4b0a300f9054405"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pushing}}}\uc2  to allow child workers to apply dangerous pesticides.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need to look for ways to make work safer for kids and not expose them to unnecessary hazards,\u8221\'20\'1D added Maki. \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re particularly concerned about the idea that tobacco has never been added to the list of hazardous occupations, and we know that kids in the US are farming tobacco, are harvesting tobacco, and they\u8217\'20\'19re getting sick while doing it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In light of these ongoing policy efforts to further roll back current child labor protections, the report argues the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  should strengthen current child labor protections to address the recent increases in child labor violations across the US.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CEven with full-throated enforcement of these regulations, it\u8217\'20\'19s not enough to sort of protect kids from what\u8217\'20\'19s going on now in the economy,\u8221\'20\'1D said Reed Shaw, policy counsel at Governing for Impact and co-author of the report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn recent years, states have been dropping their protections below the federal minimum, which serves to confuse employers and employees about what their actual protections are, so a revised federal regulation that is more clear for loopholes and exemptions would just serve as more of a bright line rule for employers,\u8221\'20\'1D added Shaw. \u8220\'20\'1CThe legal authority is there, the empirical data is there. And so it\u8217\'20\'19s time for the Department of Labor to implement some of these changes to protect workers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The report argued the wage and hour division at the US labor department should expand the list of hazardous occupations, increase protections for child workers in hazardous agricultural jobs for the first time in nearly 60 years, and issue regulations prohibiting employers from scheduling certain child workers for overnight shifts and requiring rest breaks and one day off a week for others.\par \par \uc2 The report also argues the student-learner and apprenticeship exemptions should be closed and that additional funding and personnel need to be provided to the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  to address these child labor issues.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  declined to enact several recommendations from the \uc2 National Institute for Occupational Safety and Health\uc2  in 2007 for administrative reasons, but the report argues the department should reassess and enact the rest of those recommendations.\par \par \uc2 These include creating new hazardous occupation orders, revising existing hazardous occupations, closing exemptions to certain hazardous occupations.\par \par \uc2 The report also noted regulations to protect child workers have not been updated substantially since 2010, while most are far older.\par \par \uc2 Work hours should also be revised to protect child workers given chronic school absenteeism and increases in injuries and child labor violations, it argues, recommending the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2  wage and hour division begin a notice and comment rule-making process to expand breaks for 14- and 15-year-old workers, prohibit overnight hours for 16- and 17-year-old workers, and issue a ban on overnight shifts for agricultural workers under the age of 16.\par \par \uc2 An effort to enact child labor restrictions in the agricultural sector was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/business-a-lobbying/112762-obama-administration-scraps-child-labor-restrictions-for-farms/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 abandoned}}}\uc2  in 2012 after an aggressive industry lobby campaign against it.\par \par \uc2 Nina Mast, policy and economic analyst at the \uc2 Economic Policy Institute\uc2  and a co-author of the report, said: \u8220\'20\'1CAt the time when we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing violations on the rise, and we\u8217\'20\'19re simultaneously seeing states go back on their commitment to raising standards to be above federal minimums, I think it\u8217\'20\'19s really urgent that we address our federal standards and raise them for children across the country who may be working in hazardous environments or in an environment that is not appropriate for someone of their age.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241016ekag001mf\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
