\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18We need \u8217\'20\'19em worse than they need us\u8217\'20\'19: how Haitian workers feed the US\b0\par\par\uc2 Ayanna J Legros \par \uc2 1,626 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 October 2024\par 12:45 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Laborers from the Caribbean nation pick berries and process Thanksgiving turkeys across rural America\par \par \uc2 On a foggy morning in June 2021, I left my Durham, North Carolina, home to travel two and a half hours to rural Whiteville, North Carolina, population 5,000-ish. I headed there to meet some of the town\u8217\'20\'19s newest, albeit temporary, residents: 200 Haitian migrants employed as blueberry pickers.\par \par \uc2 These farm workers put food on our tables \u8211\'20\'13 and on family tables back in Haiti. But they\u8217\'20\'19re a less visible work force in our food supply chain, toiling largely out of sight on farms in places like Columbus county, with its miles of fields. They are doubly invisible among US guest workers, who overwhelmingly hail from Mexico.\par \par \uc2 But Haitian migrants also come to the US and locations across the hemisphere to work in food production or other service industries. Their numbers have increased after the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/ng-interactive/2015/jan/16/the-haiti-earthquake-five-years-on-then-and-now"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 devastating 2010 earthquake}}}\uc2 , and many have been able to use temporary protected status (TPS) to stay and work in the US due to conditions that make it hard to return home.\par \par \uc2 Others brave unsafe border crossings into the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.americamagazine.org/politics-society/2024/02/27/haiti-dominican-republic-sugarcane-247382"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Dominican Republic\u8217\'20\'19s sugarcane fields}}}\uc2  for abusively low wages. Some {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2023/jan/05/turks-and-caicos-haitian-migrants-immigration"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 board rickety boats to voyage into Turks and Caicos\u8217\'20\'19s}}}\uc2  shark-filled waters to serve tourists in luxury resorts. Many {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cdr.lib.unc.edu/downloads/xk81jk39j?locale=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 endure human trafficking into Maryland to pick tomatoes}}}\uc2  or {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.texastribune.org/2021/09/20/border-patrol-texas-del-rio/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 risk getting whipped}}}\uc2  by border patrol agents as they walk across the arid US-Mexico border. And as anthropologists Vincent Joos and Laura Wagner once pointed out, there\u8217\'20\'19s a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://indyweek.com/food-and-drink/immigrants-packing-butterball-turkey-threat/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 good chance your Thanksgiving turkey was processed by Haitian workers}}}\uc2  in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 During the pandemic\u8217\'20\'19s peak, I volunteered to do Krey\u242\'00\'F2l translation so the Whiteville workers could get what was then a scarce commodity, the Covid vaccine. Because non-Spanish-speaking migrant farm workers were often an afterthought (then and now), there were few Covid-specific health resources in the Krey\u242\'00\'F2l language Haitians speak.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m a scholar of Haitian migration, and much of my writing and research is inspired by my own family history. My {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://amsterdamnews.com/news/2023/02/16/new-york-citys-newest-centenarian-lamercie-oge-petit/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 paternal grandmother}}}\uc2  arrived in the US in 1963, and my father joined her in New York a few years later. I grew up knowing about urban Haitian enclaves all over the US, from Queens to Miami\u8217\'20\'19s Little Haiti. However, few people have documented or even know about Haitian life in the rural US, especially in the south.\par \par \uc2 Acting as an intermediary between English-speaking nurses and the Haitian migrants, I saw a different part of the Haitian diaspora in small-town America. By now, most of us have heard that thousands of Haitians have migrated to Springfield, Ohio, to escape volatility at home. In Springfield, they boosted an ailing midwestern factory sector only to become {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/sep/14/racist-history-trump-pet-eating-immigrant"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 targets of racist rhetoric}}}\uc2  from the Trump-Vance ticket and others.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s become popular to spread false narratives about Haitians, to deride our community as one of criminals and resource vampires. Those unfair charges ignore the history of how western powers plundered Haiti, undermined its existence and fostered systemic crisis.\par \par \uc2 Haiti\u8217\'20\'19s role in world food systems dates to the 1700s when French colonizers called it the \u8220\'20\'1CPearl of the Antilles\u8221\'20\'1D. During the transatlantic slave trade, it ranked among the world\u8217\'20\'19s top sugar and coffee producers. When the Haitian Revolution brought the end of slavery on the island and its leaders declared their independence, \uc2 Napoleon Bonaparte\uc2  obsessed over the loss of his empire\u8217\'20\'19s crown jewel. France later forced the young country to reimburse former enslavers for losing their once-captive workforce \u8211\'20\'13 an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2010/aug/15/france-haiti-independence-debt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 indemnity of 150m francs }}}\uc2  with added interest over time. For decades, the US and other countries refused to recognize Haiti, the world\u8217\'20\'19s first independent Black nation. And we wonder why Haiti is the poorest country in the hemisphere, after centuries of extraction, a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2022/05/20/world/haiti-wall-street-us-banks.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wall Street-fomented US occupation}}}\uc2  and a succession of coup d\u8217\'20\'19\u233\'00\'E9tats.\par \par \uc2 The workers I met in Whiteville were far from \u8220\'20\'1Ctakers\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Cillegals\u8221\'20\'1D. They held H-2A visas that permit approved businesses to hire foreign labor when US workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t available or interested in open jobs. Bruce McLean Jr, an extension agent in Columbus county, believes that anywhere from 20% to 25% of all blueberry pickers in south-eastern North Carolina are Haitian.\par \par \uc2 The Whiteville Haitians picked blueberries by hand from mid-June to August. Blueberry pickers often make money by the bucket or box; according to the \uc2 US Department of Labor\uc2 , the going wage should be {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://seasonaljobs.dol.gov/jobs/H-300-24053-738126"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 50 cents a pound for seasonal labor}}}\uc2 , assuming workers actually make that. In October 2023, the labor department {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.dol.gov/newsroom/releases/whd/whd20231023"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fined a Sampson county, North Carolina, farm contractor}}}\uc2  for stiffing blueberry workers out of their last weeks of wages, taking their passports and even charging them fees to work in the H-2A program.\par \par \uc2 The six-week season is intense, with long workdays in blistering heat and rain. Beyond the general physical exertion and working in an unfamiliar place, workers are also exposed to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/apr/18/fruits-vegetables-most-pesticide-risk"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pesticides that linger on the berries}}}\uc2  or soil. They are sometimes shuttled from farm to farm because it\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/sections/thesalt/2016/07/13/484015376/for-pickers-blueberries-mean-easier-labor-but-more-upheaval"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Calways blueberry season somewhere\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 . This group of workers would be moving in 10 days, and that made it all the more important to vaccinate them before their next stop.\par \par \uc2 At Covid\u8217\'20\'19s height, their very status as farm workers put them at high risk. I understood this instantly when I knocked on doors to find a bathroom. One woman allowed me in, and I saw substandard housing in a mildewy old motel. Although the group in Whiteville was working in the open air, they shared cramped housing without appropriate safety measures. Days or weeks of lodging in a room with four twin beds and one shower made privacy and social distancing impossible.\par \par \uc2 But there was no time to be sick. Relatives back home \u8211\'20\'13 in places like L\u8217\'20\'19Est\u232\'00\'E8re, known for its luscious rice fields, or La Gon\u226\'00\'E2ve island north-west of Port-au-Prince \u8211\'20\'13 were sending frequent WhatsApp messages asking for financial help. Cellphones are a lifeline for these workers, connecting them to family or online church services. The likelihood of attending Sunday mass locally was slim without their own transportation. And when you\u8217\'20\'19re a migrant berry picker, there\u8217\'20\'19s almost always more work to do.\par \par \uc2 The Springfield, Ohio, of North Carolina\par \par \uc2 Seventy miles away, Haitian labor flocked to Mount Olive, an agricultural town known for its pickle company of the same name (and also being the site of a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://labornotes.org/2024/09/farmworker-union-hold-new-election-two-years-after-controversial-vote"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 long struggle to organize farm workers}}}\uc2  for better conditions).\par \par \uc2 There are no good estimates of how many Haitians have come to Mount Olive. Advocates usually say that about 3,000 to 4,000 Haitians arrived after 2010. Pastor Occene Louis of the First Haitian Baptist church thinks that as many as 10,000 have passed through Mount Olive, coming from Brazil or Chile and then moving to other North Carolina cities. A large enough number stayed in Mount Olive that he said the town doesn\u8217\'20\'19t have enough housing; he knows of households with a dozen residents.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the US census sheds the best, though still imperfect, light on Mount Olive migration patterns. In 2000, Black residents of Mount Olive made up 12% of the population. Twenty years later, they constituted 49%.\par \par \uc2 The town\u8217\'20\'19s now-deceased longtime mayor, Ray McDonald, acknowledged the essential role that Haitians play in the local economy in the film {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://vimeopro.com/toddtaylor/todd-taylor-unc-chapel-hill/video/114367940"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Home Away from Haiti.}}}\par \par \uc2 He said: \u8220\'20\'1CWe need \u8217\'20\'19em worse than they need us, as most of the locals will simply not do that work. It is very hard work. It is dirty work. Again, if they went away, a lot of our major production facilities would have to struggle.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In poultry processing, Haitians drive delivery trucks, butcher carcasses and trim skin from the meat. It\u8217\'20\'19s not only dirty work; it\u8217\'20\'19s dangerous. A town resident told me one laborer lost his hand.\par \par \uc2 This \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bbc.com/news/av/world-us-canada-17039167"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 beneficial Haitian invasion}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D met with various responses. Some longstanding residents were \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ourstate.com/mount-olive-welcomed-haitian-immigrants-created-community/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 big-hearted\u8221\'20\'1D and welcoming}}}\uc2 . Others sold their homes and left.\par \par \uc2 Former resident Paulette Bekolo lived in Mount Olive for several years starting in 2011 and saw the growing pains. She found herself advocating for basic rights for Haitian and other workers. Bekolo met with some poultry plants\u8217\'20\'19 human resources departments because \u8220\'20\'1Cthe working conditions were awful. Some of them were denied basic toilet breaks. They were humiliated peeing on them[selves].\u8221\'20\'1D She organized classes in English as a second language, first aid and immigrant rights to help her compatriots fit in.\par \par \uc2 Haitians have left an undeniable mark on the town. Haitian supermarkets have popped up and sell lalo (jute leaves) for stew. While attending a church service was largely out of reach for the Whiteville seasonal workers, Mount Olive\u8217\'20\'19s more established community has seven churches vying for their attendance and souls. Some offer services and Bible study in Krey\u242\'00\'F2l.\par \par \uc2 In Whiteville, the only business near the moldy motel where Haitians resided was a farm advertising its strawberries, likely picked by other migrants. Women cooked over open fires in a parking lot \u8211\'20\'13 a sign of just how transient this migrant community was. The chef there asked me, \u8220\'20\'1C Cherie, ki sa ou vle? \u8221\'20\'1D (Honey, what do you want?) as she grabbed the vegetable oil.\par \par \uc2 In Mount Olive, I was served a kitchen-prepared plate of macaroni, rice, beans and meat, and was invited to sit with church congregants. The smells of simmering onions and vegetables in both towns reminded me of my grandmother\u8217\'20\'19s food. I was at home in another part of the country, being fed by Haitian women who help feed us all.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241011ekab002jt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
