\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 UK borrowing costs hit three-month high amid \u8216\'20\'18buyers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\u8217\'20\'19 fears; US inflation dips to 2.4% as jobless claims jump \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 5,913 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 October 2024\par 15:10 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Yield on 10-year \uc2 UK government\uc2  debt hits highest since early July, while inflation has eased in the USSeptember inflation weakest in three years as price growth continued decline\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time for a recap.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-6707afd58f08a4688dc2221c#block-6707afd58f08a4688dc2221c"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK benchmark borrowing costs have hit a three-month high today}}}\uc2 , as City traders brace for a possible increase in borrowing in this month\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 Chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  is also considering raising capital gains tax as high as 39%, in a scramble to raise funds for crumbling public services.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/oct/10/rachel-reeves-capital-gains-tax-rise-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rachel Reeves considers raising capital gains tax to 39%}}}\par \par \uc2 Analysts have calculated that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670778868f083b19333a9112#block-670778868f083b19333a9112"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  tax rises worth \u163\'00\'A325bn woud be neede}}}\uc2  d to avoid further austerity.\par \par \uc2 Inflation across the US has weakened to 2.4%, its slowest pace in more than three years.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/10/september-inflation-declines"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September inflation weakest in three years as price growth continued decline}}}\par \par \uc2 UK surveyors have reported that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670769a38f08f65f2b701514#block-670769a38f08f65f2b701514"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 national house prices are rising again}}}\uc2 , helped by hopes of lower interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Renters, though, are facing higher tenancy costs as some landlords sell up.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?page=with:block-6707940f8f08a4688dc220ae#block-6707940f8f08a4688dc220ae"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK lenders have reported }}}\uc2  that the default rates on secured loans to households increased in the third quarter of this year, and are expected to increase again in the final quarter\par \par \uc2 Shares in pharmaceuticals group \uc2 GSK\uc2  have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-670781d38f087a7bbbc8db9b#block-670781d38f087a7bbbc8db9b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 rallied}}}\uc2  after it agreed to settle US litigation over its heartburn treatment Zantac.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/10/uk-property-market-strengthens-renters-squeeze-credit-conditions-nick-read-post-office-us-inflation-business-live-news?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-67078ff28f087a7bbbc8dbf7#block-67078ff28f087a7bbbc8dbf7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 TSB bank has been fined \u163\'00\'A311m}}}\uc2  for mistreating customers.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Over at the UK\u8217\'20\'19s Horizon inquiry, the chief executive of the \uc2 Post Office\uc2  has said previous leaders may not have been \u8220\'20\'1Cheld to account\u8221\'20\'1D for being aware of problems with the flawed IT system.\par \par \uc2 Speaking about the system which sparked the wrongful prosecutions of hundreds of postmasters, Nick Read said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think one of the themes that has emerged amongst colleagues still working within the organisation is that many of the leaders historically who have appeared before this inquiry appear not to have been held to account, if in indeed they were aware of and understood other issues associated with Horizon in the past.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Read also said he was \u8220\'20\'1Csurprised\u8221\'20\'1D at the \u8220\'20\'1Cscale\u8221\'20\'1D of interest from the police, after learning that 33 investigations into branches were being carried out in June this year.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI think there was some surprise at the scale of requests in terms of the 22 law enforcement agencies and the 33 requests.\par \par \uc2 I think we were of the opinion that it was in the ones and twos in terms of requests for information... the primary discomfort was the size and the number of the requests that were coming forward.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Read told the inquiry that no employee is \u8220\'20\'1Cabove the law\u8221\'20\'1D\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/09/post-office-boss-nick-read-horizon-inquiry-no-employee-above-law"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Post Office boss Nick Read tells inquiry no employee is above the law}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  considers raising capital gains tax to 39%\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  is considering raising capital gains tax as high as 39% in the budget, the \uc2 Guardian\uc2  can reveal, amid a scramble to raise funds for crumbling public services.\par \par \uc2 Treasury modelling being reviewed by the chancellor and seen by this newspaper shows officials are testing a range of 33% to 39% for capital gains tax (CGT). The wealth tax is paid by about 350,000 people and is levied on the sale of assets including second homes and shares but at significantly lower rates than wages.\par \par \uc2 Whitehall sources say there is growing concern about the limited options for tax rises to fill a hole the \uc2 Institute for Fiscal Studies (IFS)\uc2  thinktank says is as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/10/labour-needs-25bn-a-year-in-tax-rises-to-rebuild-public-services-warns-ifs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 big as \u163\'00\'A325bn}}}\uc2 , ahead of the budget on 30 October.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSome very big tax decisions are being left until very late in the day,\u8221\'20\'1D one senior source claimed. Another said the Treasury\u8217\'20\'19s tax-raising plans were in \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplete disarray\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/oct/10/rachel-reeves-capital-gains-tax-rise-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the full story}}}\uc2  by my colleague Anna Isaac :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/oct/10/rachel-reeves-capital-gains-tax-rise-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rachel Reeves considers raising capital gains tax to 39%}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Stocks have dipped a little on Wall Street after today\u8217\'20\'19s inflation report.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average has slipped by 75 points, or 0.18%, in early trading to 42,437 points, while the broader S&P 500 share index is down 0.23%.\par \par \uc2 Bret Kenwell, US investment analyst at investment platform \uc2 eToro\uc2 , says investors are rethinking whether the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  is likely to cut US interest rates by as much as half a percent at its next meeting:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe latest CPI figures are hardly a disaster, but after a far stronger-than-expected jobs report last week, many are questioning the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision to cut by 50 basis points last month. The two reports have all but taken another 50 basis point cut off the table next month, while some could argue that it rules out a rate cut of any kind in November.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBoth bulls and bears can find things they like in this report. While year-over-year headline CPI continues to move lower, core CPI inched higher. If investors are looking for a silver lining, it\u8217\'20\'19s this: Despite this morning\u8217\'20\'19s disappointing jobless claims data, worries over the labour market eased with last week\u8217\'20\'19s strong jobs report. While lower inflation is the goal, falling off a cliff may cause some concern about the economy.\par \par \uc2 One report doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make a trend, but the US economy appears to be on solid footing. We\u8217\'20\'19ll turn our attention to the retail sales report and to earnings to get better insights on the health of the consumer.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 US inflation: What the experts say\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some snap reaction from financial experts to today\u8217\'20\'19s US inflation report.\par \par \uc2 Neil Birrell\uc2 , chief investment officer at Premier Miton Investors, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s hard to know if it\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs data or CPI that is more important overall, but today it\u8217\'20\'19s undoubtedly the CPI as it came in a little higher than expected, particularly core inflation. However, this shouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be enough to worry markets or indeed the Fed.\par \par \uc2 Although there\u8217\'20\'19s more data to be released before the next Fed meeting, this will probably firm up views that a 0.25% cut is appropriate. As we know, one rogue number can get people worried or excited in equal measure, but there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing to do that today.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s Mahmoud Alkudsi, senior market analyst at ADSS :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CInflation appears to still be cooling off from the elevated levels seen earlier in the year, with September\u8217\'20\'19s year-on-year CPI coming slightly above the forecasted 2.3%. This has continued to the disinflationary pattern we have seen in the last several months, although not at the pace the markets had predicted. This pattern comes contrary to the unexpectedly strong job market data reported earlier in the month. These developments may give the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  conflicting views when considering the pace of its interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 Slowing inflation has fueled expectations of a monetary policy pivot from the data-driven \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 . It appears that interest rate cuts could potentially fulfill the inflationary element of the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s dual mandate, whilst possibly risking the overstimulation of the jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Patrick O\u8217\'20\'19Donnell, senior investment strategist at Omnis Investment, suggests the report could weigh on markets today:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA higher print than consensus forecasts on headline inflation today. Hot on the heels of the employment report last week, this will further pressure bond yields higher in the short-term. The read-through for equity markets is also negative at the margin.\par \par \uc2 Core CPI is still relatively elevated right now but we expect rental prices to continue to drag this measure down into 2025. For setting policy, the FOMC will remain more focused on growth data and the labour market, and we expect significant revisions to the report last week. Geopolitical developments and earnings season are going to be the key market drivers right now.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobless claims highest in a year\par \par \uc2 The number of Americans filing for for unemployment benefits last week has jumped to their highest level in a year.\par \par \uc2 The Labor Department has reported that applications for jobless claims jumped by 33,000 to 258,000 last week.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the most since early August 2023 and well above the 229,000 analysts were expecting.\par \par \uc2 Rising jobless claims could be a sign of softness in the labor market, but this may also be due to the stormy weather that has hit the US:\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial jobless claims jumped last week, likely due to impact of Hurricane Helene. Both Florida and North Carolina experienced a large increase. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/DKBrd71cfH"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/DKBrd71cfH}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Kathy Jones (@KathyJones) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/KathyJones/status/1844360213064974733?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s CPI report brings \u8220\'20\'1Cmore good news on inflation\u8221\'20\'1D, reports Heather Long of the \uc2 Washington Post\uc2 , even though the headline reading was a little higher than expected:\par \par \uc2 enltrJUST IN: More good news on inflation. US inflation was 2.4% (y/y) in September. That\u8217\'20\'19s close to the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s 2% target and it\u8217\'20\'19s the lowest since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 For the month of September alone, inflation rose just 0.2%. Rent continues to be the key driver of inflation.\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/yien1yLgE1"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/yien1yLgE1}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1844355851206918278?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrInflation continues to trend down. 2.4% (y/y) in September is the lowest since February 2021.\par \par \uc2 The forecast was for 2.3%. Inflation came in a bit higher due mainly to food prices (grocery +0.4% in Sept. and restaurant +0.3%). That's definitely worth watching, but the general\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/SFydqvfgHC"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/SFydqvfgHC}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1844358513641795734?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 US energy costs fell last month, the inflation report shows.\par \par \uc2 Gasoline prices dropped by 4.1% during September, and were 15.3% lower than a year ago. That could please the White House ahead of next month\u8217\'20\'19s elections, given the criticism they have faced in 2022 when prices surged.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2022/mar/20/joe-biden-cursed-presidency-gas-prices-covid-inflation-russia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18cursed presidency\u8217\'20\'19: gas prices are latest headache as midterms loom}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Most of the 0.2% increase in US consumer prices during September was due to more expensive food and housing.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s inflation report says:\par \par \uc2 The index for shelter rose 0.2% in September, and the index for food increased 0.4%. Together, these two indexes contributed over 75 percent of the monthly all items increase.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 US inflation drops to 2.4%\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: US inflation fell last month to its lowest in over three and a half years, but was higher than expected.\par \par \uc2 The annual US CPI rate has dipped to 2.4% in September, the smallest 12-month increase in consumer prices since February 2021, in the final set of inflation data before next month\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s down from 2.5% in August. Economists had forecast a larger drop to 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  also reports that core inflation (excluding food and energy) rose by 3.3% over the last year.\par \par \uc2 The energy index decreased 6.8 percent for the 12 months ending September. The food index increased 2.3 percent over the last year.\par \par \uc2 On a monthly basis, prices rose by 0.2% in September alone.\par \par \uc2 enltr????US September CPI Inflation Higher Than Expectations!\par \par \uc2 Year-over-year growth:\par \par \uc2 ?? CPI 2.4% (est. 2.3%, prev. 2.5%)\par \par \uc2 ?? Core CPI 3.3% (est. 3.2%, prev. 3.2%)\par \par \uc2 Month-over-month growth:\par \par \uc2 ? CPI 0.2% (est. 0.1%, prev. 0.2%)\par \par \uc2 ? Core CPI 0.3% (est. 0.2%, prev. 0.3%)\par \par \uc2 ??More Details:\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/IcejPemBSO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/IcejPemBSO}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 MacroMicro (@MacroMicroMe) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MacroMicroMe/status/1844356446865305978?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.14pm BST\par \par \uc2 Swedish homeware retailer \uc2 IKEA\uc2  has reported a 6.8% drop in UK sales in the last year, after cutting prices.\par \par \uc2 The UK arm of the Swedish homeware giant said the fall was partly driven by price reductions to keep attracting customers hit hard by the higher cost of living. It also suffered from the decline in demand for bigger-ticket items from shoppers.\par \par \uc2 Jesper Brodin\uc2 , CEO of parent company \uc2 Ingka Group\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn all our markets we experienced a slowdown of the economy and a slowdown of the home furnishing industry, almost simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe never experienced anything like that since 2008, to be honest.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 After seeing a decline in store visits and sold quantities, \uc2 IKEA\uc2  decided to cut prices, which boosted footfall and the amount of products sold, Brodin explains.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Pharmaceuticals firm Invidior has cut its sales forecast for the year, sending its shares slliding over 15%.\par \par \uc2 Invidior says that its opioid addiction treatment SUBLOCADE is selling more poorly thann expected this year, partly due to faster than expected initial adoption of a competitive product.\par \par \uc2 The company, which moved its stock market listing from London to the US this year, also blamed \u8220\'20\'1Cgreater variability in the timing of funding among Criminal Justice System customers\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltr {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24INDV&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $INDV}}}\uc2  lowers guidance, comments seemingly bullish for Breaburn's Brixadi ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24CAMX&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $CAMX}}}\uc2  ) & another LAI competitor\par \par \uc2 Indivior\uc2  Provides Preliminary Q3 2024 Results; Updates FY 2024 Guidance; Group Continues to Expect SUBLOCADE Peak Net Revenue of >$1.5 Billion {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/3xauVBEefE"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/3xauVBEefE}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bertrand Delsuc (@BertrandBio) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BertrandBio/status/1844335986622464395?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 BCA Research\uc2  upgrades UK gilts\par \par \uc2 Despite fears of a possible \u8216\'20\'18buyers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\u8217\'20\'19 on UK debt, Chester Ntonifor, \uc2 BCA Research\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Global Fixed Income Strategist, is recommending that clients buy more.\par \par \uc2 Ntonifor has advised investors to upgrade UK gilts to overweight, and downgraded European credit to underweight.\par \par \uc2 Chester explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cin our view, non-US bond yields will remain under pressure since their central banks will cut interest rates by more than is currently priced, as we suggested last week. This is becoming more apparent in the UK, which we upgraded to overweight today\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Quite simply, economic surprises in the UK are collapsing relative to those in the US. Elsewhere, we remain underweight JGBs, and maintain overweight positions in Canadian, European, and Kiwi government bonds.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.57am BST\par \par \uc2 UK 10-year bond yields hit three-month high\par \par \uc2 UK government\uc2  borrowing costs have hit a three-month high today, as bond traders ponder whether \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  will announce an increase in borrowing in this month\u8217\'20\'19s budget.\par \par \uc2 The yield (or rate of return) on UK 10-year debt has risen to 4.231% today, the highest since 3 July.\par \par \uc2 Bond yields move inversely to prices (when one rises, the other falls); today\u8217\'20\'19s moves suggest investors are seeking a higher interest rate for holding UK debt.\par \par \uc2 The move comes after \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Ben Nabarro warned there was a risk of a \u8220\'20\'1Cbuyers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\u8221\'20\'1D (a run on government bonds) if chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  announced a rapid increase in investment spending.\par \par \uc2 Reeves has been mulling whether to change the way the national debt is measured to account for the value of assets such as roads, schools and hospitals. That could give the chancellor room to borrow as much as \u163\'00\'A350bn more than currently planned without breaking fiscal rules.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, Nabarrro explained:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf the rules are changed and there is a material risk, or the possibility is entertained that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/rachel-reeves"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rachel Reeves}}}\uc2  could invest something like \u163\'00\'A350bn next year, then I think it\u8217\'20\'19s a conceivable risk [of a buyers\u8217\'20\'19 strike].\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think it\u8217\'20\'19s inherent in changing the fiscal rules at all. But it does require us to put some guardrails around that fiscal headroom and making clear it\u8217\'20\'19ll only be spent in part; that it\u8217\'20\'19ll be increased over time, and is policed by institutions. If that is the case then I think the risk is very low.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltr???? Gilts wider on this headline.\par \par \uc2 - Story more caveated.\par \par \uc2 - \uc2 Citi\uc2  note that such a risk is \u8221\'20\'1Da material concern, but not now. If the rules are changed & the possibility is entertained that Reeves could invest ~\u163\'00\'A350bln next year, then it\u8217\'20\'19s a conceivable risk.\u8221\'20\'1D {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Fnp8j2rJEv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Fnp8j2rJEv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Anthony Barton (@ABartonMacro) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ABartonMacro/status/1844287778194063557?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Other advanced economies\u8217\'20\'19 bond yields are also rising today, including the US and Germany, but not quite as quickly as the UK.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Inflation in Ireland falls to 0.7%, lowest since March 2021\par \par \uc2 Inflation in Ireland has dropped to just 0.7%, a three and a half-year low.\par \par \uc2 Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s Central Statistics Office has reported that annual inflation fell to its lowest since March 2021, down from 1.7% in the year to August.\par \par \uc2 The decline in annual inflation was driven by cheaper clothing and footwear (where prices fell by 7.5% over the year), and a 2.6% drop in housing, water, electricity, gas & other fuels.\par \par \uc2 In September alone, consumer prices fell by 0.9%, including a drop in the cost of transport (-5.2%) and recreation & culture (-2.2%).\par \par \uc2 On an EU-harmonised basis, Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s annual inflation rate was 0% in September (meaning the cost of living was unchanged over the year).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.09am BST\par \par \uc2 The Bank of England\uc2  has also found that demand for mortgages was unchanged in the July-September quarter.\par \par \uc2 Demand for secured lending for house purchase is expected to increase in October-December, lenders report.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/bankofengland?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @bankofengland}}}\uc2  - Credit Conditions Survey lending snapshot in Q3 2024 showed a steady market; secured lending for house purchases remaining unchanged in Q3. Yet for those remortgaging lending decreased, homeowners bidding their time in the hope of better rates later on in the\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/kHQueKxgNr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/kHQueKxgNr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1844308281516249219?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The money markets are quite confident that the \uc2 BoE\uc2  will lower interest rates again at its next meeting, in early November (a rate cut is seen as a 75% chance this morning)\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.58am BST\par \par \uc2 UK lenders see mortgage default rates rising\par \par \uc2 More UK households have defaulted on their mortgages in the last quarter, new \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  data shows, and the situation is likely to worsen in the run-up to Christmas.\par \par \uc2 UK lenders have reported that the default rates on secured loans to households increased in the third quarter of this year, and are expected to increase again in the final quarter (October-December).\par \par \uc2 This is the seventh quarter in a row in which lenders have reported a rise in default rates on secured loans, as this chart shows:\par \par \uc2 However, default rates for total unsecured lending slightly decreased in the last quarter, helped by a drop in defaults on credit cards.\par \par \uc2 Karim Haji, global and UK head of financial services at \uc2 KPMG\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThese latest figures suggest that many households are still struggling in the current environment.\par \par \uc2 Unsecured lending demand, while stable, remained elevated compared to the first quarter of the year. A fall in default rates for unsecured lending is an encouraging sign and reflects the cautious approach to credit being taken by households.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.51am BST\par \par \uc2 Unions are calling on ministers to undo \u8220\'20\'1Cyears of damage to the housing sector\u8221\'20\'1D by the previous Conservative government and honour its pledge to tackle the housing emergency by introducing a form of rent cap.\par \par \uc2 More {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/oct/10/ministers-urged-to-introduce-rent-caps-to-tackle-housing-crisis-in-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/oct/10/ministers-urged-to-introduce-rent-caps-to-tackle-housing-crisis-in-england"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Ministers urged to introduce rent caps to tackle housing crisis in England}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.41am BST\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2  fined \u163\'00\'A310.9m over treatment of customers in financial difficulty\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2  bank has been fined almost \u163\'00\'A311m for failing to treat struggling customers fairly when they fall behind on their loans.\par \par \uc2 The Financial Conduct Authority has fined \uc2 TSB\uc2  \u163\'00\'A310,910,500 for failing to ensure customers who were in arrears were treated fairly.\par \par \uc2 The watchdog has found that \uc2 TSB\uc2  risked agreeing unaffordable payment arrangements with customers in difficulty or charging them inappropriate fees, because it lacked suitable systems and controls to secure fair outcomes.\par \par \uc2 The bank has paid almost \u163\'00\'A3100m in redress to the 232,849 mortgage, overdraft, credit card and loan customers affected.\par \par \uc2 Therese Chambers, joint executive director of enforcement and market oversight at the \uc2 FCA\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18If you get into difficulty, you hope for \u8211\'20\'13 and we expect \u8211\'20\'13 fair treatment so a stressful situation isn\u8217\'20\'19t made worse.\par \par \uc2 TSB\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s woeful systems and controls exposed its customers to risk of harm and meant it missed opportunity after opportunity to do the right thing. While it did take action, it took us instigating a review before it acted effectively to address all the issues.\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 : EDF seeks \u163\'00\'A34bn to fund Hinkley Nuclear Plant construction\par \par \uc2 The cost of building a new UK nuclear power plant at Hinkley Point is weighing on French energy firm EDF.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  are reporting that EDF is holding talks with investors over funding for the Hinkley Point C nuclear power plant.\par \par \uc2 With the costs ballooning, EDF is seeking to raise as much as \u163\'00\'A34bn through a bespoke financial instrument which would give investors a stake in the Hinkley project, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  say.\par \par \uc2 Back in January, EDF warned that the plan was running behind schedule and could cost up to \u163\'00\'A335bn in 2015 prices.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/jan/23/hinkley-point-c-could-be-delayed-to-2031-and-cost-up-to-35bn-says-edf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hinkley Point C could be delayed to 2031 and cost up to \u163\'00\'A335bn, says EDF}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.30am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Unilever\uc2  completes sale of Russian business\par \par \uc2 Unilever\uc2  has exited Russia, more than two and a half years after the invasion of Ukraine.\par \par \uc2 The consumer good giant has today completed the sale of its Russian assets to Arnest Group, which makes perfume, cosmetics, and household products.\par \par \uc2 The sale includes all of \uc2 Unilever\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s business in Russia and its four factories in the country, plus its business in Belarus.\par \par \uc2 Hein Schumacher\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 Unilever\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COver the past year, we have been carefully preparing the Unilever Russia business for a potential sale. This work has been very complex, and has involved separating IT platforms and supply chains, as well as migrating brands to Cyrillic.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe completion of the sale ends Unilever Russia\u8217\'20\'19s presence in the country.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Unilever\uc2 , whose brands include Marmite, Dove and Domestos, had been criticised for nor quitting Russia following the invasion of February 2022.\par \par \uc2 Last year, it was named as an international sponsor of war by the Ukrainian government after a law forced large companies operating in Russia to contribute directly to its war effort.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jul/03/unilever-named-international-sponsor-of-war-by-ukraine"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Unilever named \u8216\'20\'18international sponsor of war\u8217\'20\'19 by Ukraine}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Analysts at Jefferies predict \uc2 GSK\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stock should \u8220\'20\'1Cuptick\u8221\'20\'1D by around 10%, now that most of the uncertainty around Zantac has been removed.\par \par \uc2 Jefferies had previously estimated that settling the litigation around Zantac would cost \uc2 GSK\uc2  between $2bn and $3.5bn, so last night\u8217\'20\'19s settlement is at the bottom end of that forecast.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.37am BST\par \par \uc2 GSK\uc2  shares jump after reaching Zantac settlement\par \par \uc2 Shares in pharmaceuticals group \uc2 GSK\uc2  have jumped over 6.5% in early trading after agreeing to settle US litigation over its heartburn treatment Zantac.\par \par \uc2 GSK\uc2  said last night that it has struck agreements with 10 plaintiff law firms who represent about 93%, roughly 80,000, of the US state court product liability cases pending against it over Zantac. The company will pay up to $2.2bn to settle the claims.\par \par \uc2 Relief that the litigation is largely resolved has pushed up \uc2 GSK\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s share price to \u163\'00\'A315.52, a three-week high.\par \par \uc2 That lifts its value by around \u163\'00\'A33.7bn, from \u163\'00\'A360.6bn to \u163\'00\'A364.4bn.\par \par \uc2 Derren Nathan, head of equity research at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPharma giant \uc2 GSK\uc2  has taken a giant leap towards drawing a line under the long-running legal battle concerning alleged cancer to heartburn remedy Zantac.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s agreed to settle around 93% of cases pending in the US State Courts for a payment of up to $2.2bn as well as an additional $70mn in a separate but related action. In sterling terms, it\u8217\'20\'19s expecting to recognise a \u163\'00\'A31.8bn charge for the settlements, which also covers the remaining 7% of outstanding claims, with some analysts seeing scope for the final quantum to come in a little lower.\par \par \uc2 This is a significantly better outcome than initially expected, with some estimates standing at as much as $45bn just a couple of years ago. Since then, \uc2 GSK\uc2  and other drugmakers implicated in the case, \uc2 Sanofi\uc2  and \uc2 Boehringer Ingelheim\uc2 , have seen several key rulings go in their favour. \uc2 GSK\uc2  continues to accept no liability.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/09/gsk-strikes-22bn-deal-to-resolve-legal-cases-in-us-over-heartburn-treatment-zantac"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 GSK strikes $2.2bn deal to resolve legal cases in US over heartburn treatment Zantac}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Tributes paid after Indian tycoon \uc2 Ratan Tata\uc2  dies\par \par \uc2 Tributes have been pouring in for Indian business tycoon and former \uc2 Tata Group\uc2  chairman \uc2 Ratan Tata\uc2  after his death yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Tata\uc2 , who acquired several UK companies during his career including tea firm Tetley, Anglo-Dutch steelmaker Corus, and car brands British \uc2 Jaguar\uc2  and Land Rover, died aged 86 yesterday.\par \par \uc2 Indian prime minister \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  led the tributes, calling \uc2 Tata\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Ca visionary business leader, a compassionate soul and an extraordinary human being\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2  CEO \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 He leaves an extraordinary business and philanthropic legacy and was instrumental in mentoring and developing the modern business leadership in India. He deeply cared about making India better.\par \par \uc2 enltrMy last meeting with Ratan \uc2 Tata\uc2  at \uc2 Google\uc2 , we talked about the progress of \uc2 Waymo\uc2  and his vision was inspiring to hear. He leaves an extraordinary business and philanthropic legacy and was instrumental in mentoring and developing the modern business leadership in India. He deeply\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Sundar Pichai\uc2  (@sundarpichai) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/sundarpichai/status/1844089576257949978?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 9, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 During his long career, Ratan \uc2 Tata\uc2  expanded and grew his family conglomerate in a bold bold international expansion.\par \par \uc2 Harsh Vardhan Goenka\uc2 , chair of conglomerate \uc2 RPG Enterprises\uc2 , dubbed \uc2 Tata\uc2  a \u8220\'20\'1Ctitan\u8221\'20\'1D who had left \u8220\'20\'1Can indelible mark on the world of business and beyond\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 enltrThe clock has stopped ticking. The Titan passes away. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/RatanTata?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #RatanTata}}}\uc2  was a beacon of integrity, ethical leadership and philanthropy, who has imprinted an indelible mark on the world of business and beyond. He will forever soar high in our memories. R.I.P {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/foYsathgmt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/foYsathgmt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Harsh Goenka\uc2  (@hvgoenka) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hvgoenka/status/1844073874268487750?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 9, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 N. Chandrasekaran\uc2 , the current chairman of \uc2 Tata Sons\uc2 , says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMr. \uc2 Tata\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s dedication to philanthropy and the development of society has touched the lives of millions. From education to health care, his initiatives have left a deep-rooted mark that will benefit generations to come.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tata\uc2  was the largest international donor to US university Cornell, whose interim president \uc2 Michael I. Kotlikoff\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRatan \uc2 Tata\uc2  has left an extraordinary legacy in India, across the world and at Cornell, which he cared about deeply.\par \par \uc2 Ratan\u8217\'20\'19s quiet demeanor and humility belied his international profile. His generosity and concern for others enabled research and scholarship that improved the education and health of millions of people in India and beyond, and extended Cornell\u8217\'20\'19s global impact.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Tata\uc2  was also an animal lover, who ordered that stray dogs be allowed to lounge in the lobby of the \uc2 Tata\uc2  headquarters in Mumbai.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/09/indian-tycoon-ratan-tata-dies-at-age-86"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Indian tycoon Ratan Tata dies at age 86}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.31am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Everyone will be better off under the new Government, the Business Secretary has said, rejecting the suggestion pensioners would have to \u8220\'20\'1Chide behind the sofa\u8221\'20\'1D when the Budget was announced.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether it was fair to say pensioners would have to \u8220\'20\'1Chide behind the sofa wrapped in a blanket\u8221\'20\'1D on October 30, Jonathan Reynolds told Sky News:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a Government that is going to make everybody better off.\par \par \uc2 Specifically for pensioners, we already have the commitment to the triple lock, that\u8217\'20\'19s a guarantee that pensioners will be better off this year, next year, the year after that.\par \par \uc2 That is a significant pledge from this new Government, so people should be reassured from commitments like that.\par \par \uc2 Yes, it is a challenging situation, but we are serious on delivering on our objectives, which is an economy that works better for everyone, better growth, better investment and to make sure everyone benefits from that.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  has also been warned that she risks a \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 -style meltdown if the City responded badly to substantially higher borrowing in the budget later this month.\par \par \uc2 Ben Nabarro, chief UK economist at \uc2 Citi\uc2 , said there was a risk of a \u8220\'20\'1Cbuyer\u8217\'20\'19s strike\u8221\'20\'1D(a run on government bonds) unless Reeves made it clear any increase in investment spending would be gradual.\par \par \uc2 Nabarro (whose economic forecasts underpin the \uc2 IFS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s latest projections), said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere is material concern in the gilt market about an unconstrained dash for investment out there\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 International investors are not really giving the gilt market the benefit of the doubt.\par \par \uc2 enltrIFS says Reeves has to raise taxes by \u163\'00\'A325b at the budget\par \par \uc2 Spending has to increase by \u163\'00\'A330b to avoid austerity\par \par \uc2 And \uc2 Citi\uc2  warns gilts risk a \u8220\'20\'1Cbuyers\u8217\'20\'19 strike\u8221\'20\'1D fiscal rules are relaxed too far to borrow tens of billions for investment\par \par \uc2 Via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/tomelleryrees?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @tomelleryrees}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5g6GDGrD5n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/5g6GDGrD5n}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alex Wickham (@alexwickham) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/alexwickham/status/1844262649573540341?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.53am BST\par \par \uc2 IFS: Labour needs \u163\'00\'A325bn a year in tax rises to avoid austerity\par \par \uc2 Chancellor \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  needs to make \u163\'00\'A325bn of tax rises to avoid breaking Labour\u8217\'20\'19s pledge not to return to austerity, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ifs.org.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Institute for Fiscal Studies}}}\uc2  is warning this morning.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 IFS\uc2  has calculated that taxes would need to rise substantially even if Reeves changes the UK\u8217\'20\'19s debt rules to allow extra investment spending.\par \par \uc2 enltrNEW: The decisions made at this month\u8217\'20\'19s Budget could shape domestic policy for the whole parliament to come.\par \par \uc2 Our IFS Green Budget report launching today, funded by {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NuffieldFound?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @NuffieldFound}}}\uc2  and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Citi?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @citi}}}\uc2 , examines the fiscal situation and options for the Chancellor: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/ev5r12WLqY"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/ev5r12WLqY}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Institute for Fiscal Studies\uc2  (@TheIFS) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TheIFS/status/1844263848506740944?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 In the run-up to the election, Labour outlined plans for \u163\'00\'A39bn of tax increases, but the \uc2 IFS\uc2  said that if Reeves wanted to increase spending on public services in line with national income she would need to raise a further \u163\'00\'A316bn to meet her rule that day-to-day spending should be covered by tax receipts.\par \par \uc2 The thinktank says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGiven the pledges she has made not to raise the main rates of income tax and corporation tax, or to increase national insurance or VAT at all, she might struggle to implement a tax rise on that scale.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt would be bigger than the net tax rises implemented in July 1997 and October 2010 (both around \u163\'00\'A313bn-\u163\'00\'A314bn). In which case she might have to live with day-to-day spending on many public services falling as a fraction of national income.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/10/labour-needs-25bn-a-year-in-tax-rises-to-rebuild-public-services-warns-ifs"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labour needs \u163\'00\'A325bn a year in tax rises to rebuild public services, warns IFS}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.45am BST\par \par \uc2 Rents expected to keep rising\par \par \uc2 The shortage of rental properties means most surveyors expect rents to keep rising, as this chart shows:\par \par \uc2 The government\u8217\'20\'19s renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights bill will ban {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/2023/apr/08/bidding-wars-inside-the-super-charged-fight-for-rental-properties"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 competitive bidding}}}\uc2  in the housing market, by preventing property owners accepting more rent than they have asked for.\par \par \uc2 enltrWe are changing renting for the better.?\par \par \uc2 Here are just some the of changes the Bill will introduce: ??\par \par \uc2 ???Ending no-fault evictions??\par \par \uc2 ?? Banning bidding wars??\par \par \uc2 ???Improving safety??\par \par \uc2 ???Giving the right to request a pet?? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/RentersRights?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #RentersRights}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/zdl4Ahniyl"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/zdl4Ahniyl}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Ministry of Housing, Communities & Local Gov (@mhclg) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/mhclg/status/1843974715204911515?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 9, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 RICS\uc2  president Tina Paillet says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile the Renter\u8217\'20\'19s Rights Bill aims to improve standards and offer better protections for tenants, we must ensure that these reforms do not discourage responsible landlords from remaining in the market.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMost importantly, the planned changes in the private rental sector fall short of tackling the core issue: increasing supply and making housing more affordable for tenants.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrFor those renting, stock levels continued to reduce in September 2024 as some landlords hoping to avoid any potential CGT increase look for a quick completion before budget day. Should the Gov follow through with a delayed start date, expect more stock to hit the shelves and\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MEGkFyNvSt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MEGkFyNvSt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1844264030044815400?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 10, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK property market strengthens, but renters face more pressure\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 The UK housing market is continuing to pick up, the nation\u8217\'20\'19s surveyors have reported, but renters are being squeezed by a fall in properties available to them.\par \par \uc2 The latest \uc2 Royal Institution of Chartered Surveyors\uc2  (\uc2 RICS\uc2 ) Residential Market Survey has shown that UK house price growth turned positive across the country for the first time in almost two years.\par \par \uc2 More surveyors reported rising house prices in their area than falling prices in September, which ends a run of negative or flat returns for this indicator stretching back to October 2022.\par \par \uc2 RICS\uc2  reports that most parts of the UK are experiencing rising house prices, although the West Midlands, South West and East Anglia are lagging behind.\par \par \uc2 With borrowing costs expected to keep falling, prices are expected to rise across the UK in the year ahead.\par \par \uc2 Surveyors also reported that demand for homes, sales, and new listings all returned to growth last month.\par \par \uc2 RICS\uc2  head of market analytics, Tarrant Parsons, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe latest survey results once again convey a brighter picture for housing market activity, with the recent easing in mortgage interest rates continuing to support a recovery in buyer demand.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CCritical for the outlook, a further unwinding in monetary policy is anticipated over the months ahead, which should create a more favourable backdrop for the market moving forward. In keeping with this idea, forward-looking sentiment data from the survey points to sales volumes gaining impetus, both in the near-term and over the next twelve months.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But renting a house in the UK is becoming harder, as demand continues to grow and outstrip supply.\par \par \uc2 RICS\uc2  reports that demand from tenants increased again last month, while there was a drop in the number of properties listed for rent \u8211\'20\'13 possibly because landlords are fearful of capital gains tax (CGT) changes in the budget this month.\par \par \uc2 The group explains::\par \par \uc2 This trend is further influenced by some landlords listing their properties for sale before potential CGT rises. Unfortunately for renters, the continuing squeeze on supply will likely mean further rent rises and difficulties finding property.\par \par \uc2 The struggle to rent property has already led to a rise in rough sleeping last year. Homelessness charities warned yesterday that rough sleeping will head back towards record levels unless the government addresses a looming \u163\'00\'A31bn shortfall in frontline funding.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/oct/09/rough-sleeping-homelessness-labour-rachel-reeves"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rough sleeping will soar unless Labour fills \u163\'00\'A31bn shortfall, charities say}}}\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s credit conditions survey\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: \uc2 Post Office\uc2  chief executive, Nick Read, to give evidence at the Horizon IT inquiry for the second day\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US inflation report for September\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.52am BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241010ekaa000uo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
