\par\par \uc2 Books\par \b \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18I\u8217\'20\'19ve dealt with anti-hillbilly bigotry all my life\u8217\'20\'19: Barbara Kingsolver on JD Vance, the real Appalachia and why Demon Copperhead was such a hit\b0\par\par\uc2 Rachel Cooke \par \uc2 3,127 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 October 2024\par 07:00 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As the Pulitzer-winning author\u8217\'20\'19s little-known debut \u8211\'20\'13 about the 1983 Arizona miners\u8217\'20\'19 strike \u8211\'20\'13 is published in the UK, she discusses the roots of division in the US, her wild childhood, and putting the story straight about where she\u8217\'20\'19s from\par \par \uc2 When ecstatic fans tell Barbara Kingsolver they\u8217\'20\'19ve read every last one of her books, she always smiles inwardly. \u8220\'20\'1CI bet you haven\u8217\'20\'19t,\u8221\'20\'1D she thinks, knowing it was a nonfiction account of an Arizona miners\u8217\'20\'19 strike in 1983 that set her on the road to the bestseller list.\par \par \uc2 Admittedly, this recherch\u233\'00\'E9 volume has never been out of print in the US. But its long-term home is on the specialist list of an academic publisher, where it nestles beside other, even chewier books about labour relations. \u8220\'20\'1CIt was hard to place,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CEvery editor who read it said: wow, this is interesting. But we couldn\u8217\'20\'19t sell it.\u8221\'20\'1D Only after her first novel, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/the-bean-trees-9780349114170/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Bean Trees}}}\uc2 , was published in 1988 did her agent send it out again, at which point an offshoot of Cornell University Press stepped in. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the book nobody knows about,\u8221\'20\'1D she concludes, a statement only a writer who has since won just about every literary prize going could make without sounding utterly depressed (she is smiling broadly).\par \par \uc2 So, yes, she\u8217\'20\'19s surprised \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m amazed!\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/holding-the-line-9780571392070/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Holding the Line: Women in the Great Arizona Mine Strike}}}\uc2  is about to be published in the UK for the first time, though the credit belongs to her editor at Faber, Louisa Joyner, who having unaccountably dug it up, approached her about a British edition. \u8220\'20\'1CI hadn\u8217\'20\'19t looked at it in decades, and I was a little nervous to reread it,\u8221\'20\'1D she says. \u8220\'20\'1CBut I\u8217\'20\'19m very proud of it, and I hope it\u8217\'20\'19s OK for me to say that. I feel like this is maybe the place where I found my narrative voice.\u8221\'20\'1D Isn\u8217\'20\'19t her twentysomething self a bit of a stranger to her at this distance? \u8220\'20\'1CNo, I think I was [already] so much the person I am today. I look back at the writer I was 40 years ago, and fundamentally, nothing has changed.\par \par \uc2 My whole life until Demon Copperhead I was snubbed because I\u8217\'20\'19m rural, I\u8217\'20\'19m from this place that\u8217\'20\'19s considered backward\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI grew up in working-class, rural Kentucky, and in those days \u8211\'20\'13 hard to imagine now \u8211\'20\'13 almost no one was a registered Republican. Southern Appalachia was formed by the collision between labour and big capital. It wasn\u8217\'20\'19t mining country, but it was tobacco country, and everyone had a sense of \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s us or the big companies\u8221\'20\'1D. Of course you don\u8217\'20\'19t think about these things when you\u8217\'20\'19re young; you just absorb your milieu. But as I got older, I always felt the story I wanted to hear was the workers\u8217\'20\'19 story. In a cafeteria, I want to go into the kitchen, and talk to those people \u8211\'20\'13 and they\u8217\'20\'19re often the women.\u8221\'20\'1D Her point is that she knew all about unions and how vital they can be long before she arrived in Clifton, Arizona, in her \uc2 Nissan\uc2  pickup with only a tape recorder and a few high-minded journalistic ideals for company.\par \par \uc2 If Holding the Line belongs, in a variety of ways, to another era, it speaks to today nonetheless; the politics of the US in 2024 are, she believes, inextricably linked to the story it tells, a turning point in terms of American industrial relations. (And it will resonate with some British readers, too, in the year of the 40th anniversary of the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/miners-strike-1984-85"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 miners\u8217\'20\'19 strike}}}\uc2 .) But Kingsolver had little idea of this at the time \u8211\'20\'13 at least, in the beginning. She was working in Tucson as a scientific writer, and cutting her teeth as a freelance journalist in her spare time. Her mission on this occasion was simply to report for several news outlets on the strike that was taking place in a constellation of remote copper mining towns in the south of the state. She wasn\u8217\'20\'19t, in other words, supposed to stay out there for very long.\par \par \uc2 She soon found, however, that she couldn\u8217\'20\'19t look away; the drive to Clifton, the town where she was mostly based, was three-and-a-half hours from Tucson, and in the end she did it so often, she wore the tread from her tyres. The strike had begun as a straightforward dispute between a mining company, \uc2 Phelps Dodge\uc2 , and its unionised workforce; copper prices had fallen, \uc2 Phelps Dodge\uc2  was losing money, people were being laid off. When, during contract negotiations, the company insisted it required wages to be frozen, thousands of miners elected to strike.\par \par \uc2 Most commentators believed the dispute would be conventional and relatively brief, but within a month, things had shifted dramatically: squads of armoured men with teargas and automatic weapons were storming tiny, bucolic main streets; people were being jailed for nothing more than calling a neighbour a scab. It lasted from June 1983 until December 1984. By the time it was over, the trade union that represented the workers had been decertified, and half a century of organised labour in Arizona was at an end.\par \par \uc2 Appalachian people felt betrayed by JD Vance\u8217\'20\'19s book a long time before he became a Republican politician\par \par \uc2 Kingsolver is speaking to me from her home in Virginia: a farm in a hollow surrounded by deciduous forests, to which she moved with her husband and two daughters in 2004. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.instagram.com/barbara.kingsolver/?hl=en"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 On Instagram}}}\uc2 , she often posts photos of her abundant homegrown crops, sometimes before mammoth pickling and bottling sessions in her kitchen: tomatoes, cardoons, padr\u243\'00\'F3n peppers, sweet potatoes. I can\u8217\'20\'19t see any of these things on my screen now; only a bookcase is on offer. But rightly or wrongly, I feel I know all about her particular contentment: happy, productive hours at her desk followed by equally happy, productive hours outdoors. We\u8217\'20\'19re very far indeed from dusty Arizona, where her 28-year-old self once spent her days carefully interviewing the women who kept the show on the road during the strike (they staffed the picket lines when the men travelled to other states in search of a wage), sitting with them on the \u8220\'20\'1Csqueaky porch-swings of [their] slant-frame houses\u8221\'20\'1D. These days, after all, her characters belong only to her imagination, no Dictaphone required.\par \par \uc2 In 1983, she struggled to maintain the dispassion she believed was required of a good journalist. It was impossible not to take sides, and having won their trust, the people of Clifton began to refer to her fondly as the \u8220\'20\'1Cgal\u8221\'20\'1D who was writing a book about them. Back in Tucson, her interview tapes began to pile up in what she describes as an \u8220\'20\'1Cimpugning\u8221\'20\'1D way; in the end, she wrote Holding the Line because she saw \u8220\'20\'1Cno other decent option\u8221\'20\'1D. Only later did it occur to her that the story had historic implications: \u8220\'20\'1CThis was the moment when the forces of capital teamed with governments to crush labour. A lot of it turned [in the US] on the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://lhrp.georgetown.edu/collections/the-1981-patco-strike/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 air traffic controller strike [of 1981]}}}\uc2 . Reagan broke that strike overnight. He fired 11,000 unionised controllers in a single morning and then \u8211\'20\'13 this is the really shocking part \u8211\'20\'13 he banned them from working in the federal service ever again. It felt, in a weird way, like a military coup. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t understand that the president had the power to destroy so many lives at once, and it terrified [workers]. Your working life \u8211\'20\'13 your professional life \u8211\'20\'13 could end by edict.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Has she been back to Clifton recently? \u8220\'20\'1CI did for years, but [not recently], no. The women in my book mostly moved to other places. Ajo, Clifton and Morenci were classic mining towns, and once those closed, there was nothing else in them. They\u8217\'20\'19re something like ghost towns now, I\u8217\'20\'19d say.\u8221\'20\'1D But she hardly needs to take a long drive to grasp how these things play out. In Virginia, where coal mining was once king, she has seen with her own eyes what happens when stable jobs disappear.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe mines kept out other factories and mills that might have come in. They owned everything: the land, the courthouse, the politics. When coal left this region, it left an enormous void, which created a kind of hopelessness. I love Appalachian culture. It\u8217\'20\'19s community-based. It\u8217\'20\'19s very self-sufficient. But you have this hopelessness that [makes people] vulnerable to politicians who say: I\u8217\'20\'19m on your side. When someone comes along who says: I see you, I hear you, and what I especially hear is that the government has abandoned you, because look at your schools, your hospitals, your unemployment rates\u8230\'20\'26 Populist politicians have tapped into this sense that we\u8217\'20\'19re on our own, that government can\u8217\'20\'19t help us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This brings us inevitably to JD Vance, the Republican vice-presidential candidate who rose to prominence on the back of his hit 2016 memoir, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2016/dec/07/hillbilly-elegy-by-jd-vance-review"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hillbilly Elegy}}}\uc2 , in which he describes the values of his family, who came originally from rural Kentucky, and the socioeconomic travails of Middletown, Ohio, to which his mother\u8217\'20\'19s parents moved after the second world war. When the book was first published, it enthralled many urban liberals, who took it at face value, glad to be instructed about a world they didn\u8217\'20\'19t know and possibly feared. But what did Kingsolver think? Did she hate it? And if so, how did she feel about the attention \u8211\'20\'13 even the acclaim \u8211\'20\'13 it received?\par \par \uc2 Drawing breath, she seems to grow a little taller in her seat. \u8220\'20\'1CI can tell you that Appalachian people felt betrayed by that book a long time before he became a Republican politician. I\u8217\'20\'19ll begin by saying: anyone is entitled to write a memoir. That\u8217\'20\'19s his story, fine. But for him to say that his story explains all of us \u8211\'20\'13 I say, no, I resent that, because it\u8217\'20\'19s very condescending. There\u8217\'20\'19s this subtext all the way through it that suggests we\u8217\'20\'19re in a boat that\u8217\'20\'19s sinking because we\u8217\'20\'19re lazy, unambitious and uncreative, which I resent.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The positive responses to the book, she believes, were born of the fact that it simply confirmed well-worn stereotypes. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve dealt with this condescension, this anti-hillbilly bigotry for a lot of my life. I didn\u8217\'20\'19t realise it was a problem until I left Kentucky and went to college [she went to university in Indiana] and people made fun of my accent, and said things like: \u8216\'20\'18Look at you, you\u8217\'20\'19re wearing shoes, ha ha!\u8217\'20\'19\u8221\'20\'1D She pauses. \u8220\'20\'1CYou know, it\u8217\'20\'19s more insidious than that. Even as a writer, I feel like my whole life until {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2022/oct/16/demon-copperhead-by-barbara-kingsolver-review-appalachian-saga-in-the-spirit-of-dickens"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Demon Copperhead}}}\uc2  [her Pulitzer prize-winning 2022 novel, which is set in modern Appalachia] I was snubbed because I\u8217\'20\'19m rural, I\u8217\'20\'19m from this place that\u8217\'20\'19s considered backward. I\u8217\'20\'19m quite used to it. But it [Vance\u8217\'20\'19s book] really made a lot of us angry that this became the explanation for us.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her neighbours, she says, saw through him immediately: \u8220\'20\'1CThe hollowness, the fact that he isn\u8217\'20\'19t really one of us.\u8221\'20\'1D And perhaps this only increased her determination to write a book like Demon Copperhead, which tackles head on the agonising effects of the opioid crisis in Appalachia: \u8220\'20\'1CWe have to talk about history. This was done to us. This region has been treated as a kind of internal colony exploited by the outside. It was just so personal for him [Vance]. There was no analysis, and no compassion. It was just: if I can survive, anyone can survive.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 How does she think Vance is working out for \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , who picked him as his running mate? She is scornful. \u8220\'20\'1CThe bottom line is: Trump doesn\u8217\'20\'19t want a vice-president. I don\u8217\'20\'19t think it was a thoroughly considered decision. He\u8217\'20\'19s entirely about himself. He\u8217\'20\'19s not even interested in the presidency. He\u8217\'20\'19s only interested in winning.\u8221\'20\'1D Is she anxious about the election? \u8220\'20\'1COf course I\u8217\'20\'19m nervous. But I\u8217\'20\'19ll tell you this: I\u8217\'20\'19m a lot less nervous than I was. I have immense respect for Joe Biden, he has done so much. But the image of an old man at the helm\u8230\'20\'26 I have enormous respect for his decision to turn over the keys to a younger generation. Almost all my friends are younger than me. I have millennial daughters. They\u8217\'20\'19re so enthusiastic about a [potential] new administration, and how it will represent them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It goes without saying that Kingsolver\u8217\'20\'19s career was stellar long before Demon Copperhead was published. Novels such as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2013/may/11/book-club-barbara-kingsolver-poisonwood"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Poisonwood Bible}}}\uc2 , about a family of Baptist missionaries in Congo, and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2009/nov/08/the-lacuna-barbara-kingsolver"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Lacuna}}}\uc2 , which pieces together the story of a man whose friends include Frida Kahlo and Diego Rivera, were acclaimed, prizewinning bestsellers. But even she seems to feel that Demon Copperhead, her most recent book, a retelling of Charles Dickens\u8217\'20\'19s David Copperfield that won her {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2023/jun/14/barbara-kingsolver-wins-the-womens-prize-for-fiction-for-second-time"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a second Women\u8217\'20\'19s prize for fiction}}}\uc2  as well a Pulitzer, is special \u8211\'20\'13 not least because it has changed perceptions of Appalachia.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI know that it has,\u8221\'20\'1D she says, \u8220\'20\'1Cbecause I hear that from people every day. It broke the \u8211\'20\'13 I don\u8217\'20\'19t know what to call it \u8211\'20\'13 grassroots ceiling.\u8221\'20\'1D Starting out, she tells me, there were male writers at the top of the pile, women\u8217\'20\'19s literature in a special category, and then, far below both of these, rural writers \u8211\'20\'13 where they remain to this day. \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19re just not respected, I\u8217\'20\'19ve always known that. I\u8217\'20\'19ve felt it, in interviews, time and time again. But I long ago gave up the expectation of that kind of approval, I guess I\u8217\'20\'19m just a rebel \u8211\'20\'13 and this book [ Demon Copperhead ] broke through that. It won the big prizes, and it got all the attention, and it stayed on the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  bestseller list for over a year. And this seems important to me because it looks straight at the bigotry rural people face.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Identity politics has helped people like her, she says: it has chipped away at the parameters of what subjects are deemed acceptable. For readers, Demon Copperhead is a bridge over the urban-rural divide; a \u8220\'20\'1Cwindow on a world\u8221\'20\'1D in which doctors are in such short supply that women commonly have to drive to another state to have their babies, as happened to Kingsolver\u8217\'20\'19s own daughter.\par \par \uc2 She had wanted to write such a book \u8211\'20\'13 her great Appalachian novel \u8211\'20\'13 for a long time, but the project seemed daunting, maybe impossible. On a book tour in England in 2018, however, she stayed at Bleak House in Broadstairs, where Dickens wrote David Copperfield (it\u8217\'20\'19s now a B&B), and everything became clear. Alone, late at night, in the writer\u8217\'20\'19s former study, she felt \u8220\'20\'1Cthe presence of his outrage\u8221\'20\'1D, and his voice urging her to \u8220\'20\'1Clet the child tell the story\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 On the flight home, she reread his great tale of an orphan, the book of his own that he loved best of all, and saw how she could rework it to write about the \u8220\'20\'1Clost boys\u8221\'20\'1D of Appalachia, where 40% of children have parents who have either died as a result of opioid addiction, are incapacitated by one, or are in prison. Mr Creakle\u8217\'20\'19s boarding school would become a tobacco farm, and the blacking factory, a meth lab; Uriah Heep would be transformed into a soccer coach called U-Haul, and David\u8217\'20\'19s friend Steerforth into Demon\u8217\'20\'19s pal, Fast Forward.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s almost outrageous, I tell her: a woman from Kentucky taking on Dickens. She laughs. Her career as a writer, she says, still amazes her \u8211\'20\'13 \u8220\'20\'1CI get up surprised every day that I do this for a living\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 perhaps because her path towards it wasn\u8217\'20\'19t entirely straightforward. The daughter of a doctor, she spent part of her childhood in the Democratic Republic of the Congo, an experience she later drew on for The Poisonwood Bible ; at university, she began by studying classical piano on a music scholarship before \u8211\'20\'13 on the realisation that she didn\u8217\'20\'19t want to spend her life \u8220\'20\'1Cplaying Blue Moon in a hotel lobby\u8221\'20\'1D \u8211\'20\'13 switching to biology. \u8220\'20\'1CI had a wild childhood. I was always catching bugs and lizards and learning the names of things, so science was natural for me. But it also seemed practical. I was lucky to get to college; a lot of my friends had babies by the time they were 17. I had this sense, almost like having been shot into space, of needing to make good use of my time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She professes herself \u8211\'20\'13 somewhat unconvincingly, I would say \u8211\'20\'13 to be \u8220\'20\'1Ca hermit\u8221\'20\'1D nowadays, her longstanding introversion intact, even if she has mastered her shyness. \u8220\'20\'1CI live in a beautiful place, and I want to stay here for ever. My family jokes about it, they keep a record of how long it is since I have left the hollow\u8230\'20\'26\u8221\'20\'1D Sometimes, though, there\u8217\'20\'19s nothing for it. \u8220\'20\'1CI have to move in the world to know what I want to write about. The currency of my fiction is human interaction, but that does not exist without a sense of place \u8211\'20\'13 my characters are not just people doing people things among, you know, objects made by people. There are trees overhead. A river comes roaring through the town. In Holding the Line, you smell the honeysuckle. So much of the world is not made by people, and paying attention to that is important to me.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Her books are so replete, somehow: so bright and so big. Does she ever worry that they will go out of fashion? That the internet is making novels smaller: more colourless and inward-looking? \u8220\'20\'1CAre we discussing Sally Rooney?\u8221\'20\'1D she asks, with sudden and unexpected waspishness. But no, she isn\u8217\'20\'19t worried at all. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve seen minimalism come and go,\u8221\'20\'1D she tells me, in the moments before she disappears (I picture a scrubbed kitchen table, laden with immaculate veg). \u8220\'20\'1CI mean, take {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/books/2018/apr/08/the-overstory-richard-powers-review"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Overstory}}}\uc2  [a Booker prize shortlisted novel] by Richard Powers. There\u8217\'20\'19s a book about trees, and people loved it!\u8221\'20\'1D She nods her head, a silver curl flashing. \u8220\'20\'1CGive them something bigger than a conversation in a room and they\u8217\'20\'19re going to eat it up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Holding the Line: Women in the Great Arizona Mine Strike\par \par \uc2 is published by Faber (\u163\'00\'A316.99). To support the Guardian and Observer order your copy at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://guardianbookshop.com/holding-the-line-9780571392070/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 guardianbookshop.com}}}\uc2 . Delivery charges may apply\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241009eka6002nb\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
