\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 This swing county saw a job boom due to Biden. Yet, many union members still back Trump\b0\par\par\uc2 Chris McGreal in Saginaw, Michigan \par \uc2 1,629 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 October 2024\par 14:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Factory closures and little improvement in working people\u8217\'20\'19s lives in Saginaw a \u8216\'20\'18death by a thousand cuts\u8217\'20\'19 that still hurts\par \par \uc2 Evan Allardyce worked as an electrician at one of the \uc2 General Motors\uc2  factories that once dotted Michigan\u8217\'20\'19s Saginaw county and now stand as decaying markers to thousands of jobs lost to corporate agendas. He struggled to find work after the plant closures and was forced to travel across the country for contract jobs.\par \par \uc2 So Allardyce, now a leader of a Saginaw branch of the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest electricians union, understands blue-collar anger over the free trade agreements that allowed car makers and other industries to move hundreds of thousands of well-paying jobs into Mexico and Canada since the 1990s. He sees how the resulting economic decline and rising poverty in the industrial heartlands helped elect \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  in 2016.\par \par \uc2 What Allardyce doesn\u8217\'20\'19t understand is why so many of his own members in the International Brotherhood of Electrical Workers (IBEW) still support Trump when they have not known anything but full employment and good pay that is likely to continue for years to come, thanks to booming construction work in Saginaw. For that, he says, they can thank \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s huge spending on infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Yet, to Allardyce\u8217\'20\'19s despair, many members of his branch will again vote for Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s still over 50% Democrat in this local [union branch] but 20 years it was way higher than that. Something\u8217\'20\'19s happened in the last little bit. There\u8217\'20\'19s a lot of reasons but part of it is generational. Those of us who\u8217\'20\'19ve seen the downtimes, we\u8217\'20\'19ve been beaten up a little bit. But the younger generation have only seen really great work times, have only seen busy, busy work,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThanks to Biden that will go on for years but they don\u8217\'20\'19t see that and they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear that credit should go to Biden. I think the younger ones feel they can make a decision more outside of the box of what\u8217\'20\'19s good for their pay cheque. Trump is targeting people to get them fired up about the immigration issue or whatever and it works.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It is political lore in many parts of the US that Trump was elected eight years ago on the back of anger in Rust belt states at the impact of the North America Free Trade Agreement (Nafta) signed into law in 1993 by \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  even if it was largely negotiated by his Republican predecessors. Millions of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.citizen.org/wp-content/uploads/nafta_factsheet_deficit_jobs_wages_feb_2018_final.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jobs were lost}}}\uc2  in the following decades as factories moved out of the US and the car industry took advantage of cheaper labour abroad.\par \par \uc2 In 2016, Trump won support in states with a high proportion of blue-collar worker who were key to his election by railing against Nafta as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe worst trade deal ever\u8221\'20\'1D. \uc2 General Motors\uc2  shut a dozen factories around Saginaw and moved most of that work to Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Trump {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/03/donald-trump-michigan-rally-car-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 was back in Saginaw}}}\uc2  on Thursday because it\u8217\'20\'19s a bellwether county in a battleground state, and he was pushing the same message as eight years ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAmericans have watched as our country has been stripped of our jobs. By the way, this state, more than any other, you lost 60% of your automobile business over the years,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUnder my plan American workers will no longer be worried about losing their jobs to foreign nations. Instead, foreign nations will be worried about losing their jobs to America. We\u8217\'20\'19re bringing them all back.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Trump made similar promises in 2016 and while he did renegotiate some of the terms of Nafta, the car factories did not return.\par \par \uc2 Carly Hammond, a \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  member and Saginaw organiser for the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest union confederation, the \uc2 AFL-CIO\uc2 , spends a lot of time talking to members about how they\u8217\'20\'19re going to vote and trying to steer them toward Harris. She thinks it\u8217\'20\'19s a mistake to see Nafta as the original sin that drove blue-collar workers to support Trump.\par \par \uc2 There is, said Hammond, a deep disillusion with politicians rooted in years of failure to improve working people\u8217\'20\'19s lives and Trump continues to garner support by marketing himself as separate from that.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think there is an original sin. The hurt and the pain is in the repetition. It\u8217\'20\'19s death by a thousand cuts. It\u8217\'20\'19s the lying became so mainstream, and just the over-promising and the under-delivering became policies. What people saw in Trump, and what they still see in Trump, is a counter-influence to the over-polished politician who does nothing but lie to you and pretend to be your friend,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s the \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  voters in unions that I see. I think most of them are still in the same place. The trends that I see with labour people who are Trump supporters is a tendency to be very upset with the status quo, which everyone should be. People are going to stick with Trump until they see and they feel like things are getting better for them.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hammond, whose grandfather worked at \uc2 General Motors\uc2  in Saginaw while both her parents are anti-union Republicans, said many union members continue to see the Democrats as representing corporate interests over those of American workers. She identifies President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s bailout of the banks following the 2008 financial crash, while millions of Americans lost their homes to foreclosure, as one of the deepest wounds that soured a lot of Democratic working-class voters on the party.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA lot of it is our own party betrayed the working class. When \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  got elected in 2008 on hope and change, the first thing he did was bail out the banks which was exactly the opposite,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe legitimate concerns that a lot of Trump voters in the unions have about the Democratic party are mostly regarding who\u8217\'20\'19s bought more by corporations.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For some union members there are also more immediate concerns at work. There is strong support for Trump among members of the \uc2 United Auto Workers\uc2  branch who work at a Saginaw factory making vehicle steering systems who fear that the Democrats\u8217\'20\'19 promotion of electric cars will put them out of work.\par \par \uc2 The impact of the blue-collar vote may not be what it was eight years ago. Trump does better with white voters who do not have a college degree, a demographic that accounts for a high proportion of union membership.\par \par \uc2 Historically, about two-thirds of union members in Michigan do not have a degree. But white people without a college degree {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.npr.org/2024/09/06/nx-s1-5101765/swing-states-demographics-electorate"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 account for}}}\uc2  a shrinking proportion of voters in the state, falling to about 55% at this year\u8217\'20\'19s election.\par \par \uc2 Still, union members matter in a state like Michigan which Trump won by fewer than 11,000 votes in 2016 and then lost by a narrow margin four years later.\par \par \uc2 Union members in \uc2 Teamsters\uc2  for Trump T-shirts were lined up behind the former president at Thursday\u8217\'20\'19s rally in Saginaw. The \uc2 Teamsters\uc2 , one of the US\u8217\'20\'19s largest unions, has declined to endorse a candidate, a blow to Harris, after a ballot of its membership showed a clear majority backed Trump.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy pro-worker policies are one of the major reasons why I\u8217\'20\'19ve been overwhelmingly endorsed by the rank-and-file membership,\u8221\'20\'1D Trump told the rally.\par \par \uc2 The IBEW has endorsed Harris in part because of the huge infrastructure spending that helped bring jobs to Saginaw, including a vast semiconductor factory. Allardyce said its construction will provide work to hundred of electricians.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI completely give the credit to the Biden administration. We could be talking about years\u8217\'20\'19 worth of work here, which is an unheard of conversation to have,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a message Allardyce pushes to his members at union meetings.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe IBEW and trade unions have traditionally got on the backs of Democrats. Even from members who tell us they\u8217\'20\'19re going to support them, they say: \u8216\'20\'18But what have they done for us?\u8217\'20\'19 But now we finally have an administration that\u8217\'20\'19s done something for us and they\u8217\'20\'19re still not happy,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Rex Christian, an IBEW official responsible for membership recruitment in Saginaw county, said Trump supporters in the union branch mostly remain unpersuaded.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI feel like a lot of times they\u8217\'20\'19re maybe embarrassed or ashamed, ashamed because they know how we feel. But you\u8217\'20\'19re not going to persuade anybody if they\u8217\'20\'19ve got their mind made up,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CYou can try to give them facts, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t want to hear it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Hammond will vote for Harris but does not think the vice-president breaks the cycle and will struggle to win many over union members from Trump. She points to the economy, which many Americans say is their primary concern.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou can blame Covid or external factors but inflation rose and corporations are doing better than ever. The stock prices keep growing yet people are still struggling. People see there\u8217\'20\'19s still no change,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t want to make it sound like there\u8217\'20\'19s no difference between the candidates because there is and I\u8217\'20\'19m a \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  supporter. But Harris, as she raises more money, has walked back her promises. She didn\u8217\'20\'19t put a whole lot out there to begin with but as the weeks go by the messages are getting different. The ads are changing. It\u8217\'20\'19s Harris\u8217\'20\'19s race to lose and, every week that goes by, Harris is going to lose more.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241005eka5001b9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
