\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Dollar strengthens after US beats forecasts with 254,000 new jobs in September \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 8,335 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 October 2024\par 15:19 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Rolling coverage of the latest economic and financial newsUS adds over 250,000 jobs in September, defying fears of slowdown\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up, after a lively week.\par \par \uc2 American employers added 254,000 jobs last month in the penultimate jobs report before the US election, defying fears of a slowdown in the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Job creation unexpectedly accelerated in September, while the headline unemployment rate slipped to 4.1% from 4.2% in August.\par \par \uc2 Economists had forecast a non-farm payrolls reading of around 132,500 for September, after a cooler summer of employment growth.\par \par \uc2 Hiring instead rose sharply from recent months. In August {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/06/august-us-jobs-report-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 employers added 159,000 jobs}}}\uc2 , and in July, they added 144,000.\par \par \uc2 Both estimates for July and August were revised higher by the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  on Friday \u8211\'20\'13 adding 72,000 more jobs than previously reported \u8211\'20\'13 highlighting the strength of the labor market.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/us-jobs-report-september"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US adds over 250,000 jobs in September, defying fears of slowdown}}}\par \par \uc2 The strong jobs report lifted shares on Wall Street, and sent the dollar flying to a seven week high, dragging down the pound.\par \par \uc2 Sterling is now firmly on track for its worst week since February 2023, having dropped by around 2% or two and a half-cents this week.\par \par \uc2 It had rallied, before the jobs report, after the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief economist warned against cutting interest rates \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo far or too fast\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  said there should be a gradual reduction in interest rates to make sure inflation remains near the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s 2% target, a day after the governor, Andrew Bailey, said the central bank could move \u8220\'20\'1Cmore aggressively\u8221\'20\'1D to lower borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/bank-of-england-chief-economist-urges-caution-over-interest-rate-cuts"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England chief economist urges caution over interest rate cuts}}}\par \par \uc2 In a boost for the UK\u8217\'20\'19s Labour government, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/04/pound-worst-week-bank-of-england-us-dollar-jobs-report-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66ffac7d8f0818b7c81cc491#block-66ffac7d8f0818b7c81cc491"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the UK construction sector grew at its fastest pace in two-and-a-half years in September}}}\uc2 , as housebuilding strengthened.\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s the rest of today\u8217\'20\'19s business news:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/society/2024/oct/04/denise-coates-bet365-biggest-beneficiary-charity-tax"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Denise Coates\u8217\'20\'19 charity may have saved Bet365 more in tax than it has given to good causes}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/news/2024/oct/04/west-ham-owner-wealthy-leaving-uk-non-dom-crackdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 West Ham owner says non-dom crackdown is driving wealthy from the UK}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/euston-station-advertising-screens-network-rail"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Euston station\u8217\'20\'19s big advertising screens turned off after overcrowding fears}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/04/carmakers-ramp-up-pressure-on-chancellor-for-ev-sales-subsidies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Carmakers ramp up pressure on chancellor for EV sales subsidies }}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/wetherspoons-boss-tim-martin-calls-academics-smaller-beer-glass-proposal-slightly-daft"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Wetherspoon\u8217\'20\'19s boss calls academics\u8217\'20\'19 smaller beer glass proposal \u8216\'20\'18slightly daft\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/eu-chinese-ev-tariffs-split-vote-germany-car-firms"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 EU leaders back extra Chinese EV tariffs despite split vote}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/barratt-takeover-of-redrow-given-go-ahead-by-uk-regulator"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Barratt\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A32.5bn takeover of Redrow given go-ahead by UK regulator}}}\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/04/keir-starmer-clean-energy-plans-new-technologies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Starmer pledges to avoid rerun of 1980s deindustrialisation with clean energy plans}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report for September suggests the labor market remains strong, says the White House\u8217\'20\'19s council of economic advisers, here:\par \par \uc2 enltrToday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report for September suggests the labor market remains strong. Payrolls rose 254,000 and the unemployment rate fell to 4.1%, both surpassing market expectations. 1/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188113541480909?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrRevisions added a cumulative 72,000 jobs, and the three-month pace of payroll gains was 186,000 in September, up sharply from last month\u8217\'20\'19s 140,000. Private payroll gains were 223,000 in September and 145,000 on a three-month basis. 2/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/JoegGfakLR"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/JoegGfakLR}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188115139580283?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrWhile job gains have cooled since last year, payroll employment growth is still comfortably in the range needed to maintain a low unemployment rate. 3/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188116712448133?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrEmployment grew substantially in health care and social assistance, leisure and hospitality (especially restaurants), and construction. The diffusion index of employment growth across industries showed widespread gains across private sector industries in September. 5/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YX5pmJ02z4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/YX5pmJ02z4}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188119963062441?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrWhile manufacturing employment is up a strong 729,000 since January 2021, it fell 7,000 last month. 6/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188122689544197?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrDespite record high construction of manufacturing facilities, factory employment has been flat in recent months. But, taken together, manufacturing and non-residential construction jobs are up 1.3 million since January 2021. 7/\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188125319348668?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe unemployment rate declined for a second month in a row to 4.1%, down 0.2 p.p.t. since July. While the unemployment rate has increased since last year, it remains historically low: it has only been lower 19% of months since 1948. 8/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/7uaI3fDT73"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/7uaI3fDT73}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188127278104587?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrThe unemployment rate fell by 0.4 p.p.t. for both Black workers (to 5.7%) and Hispanic workers (to 5.1%) and fell by 0.2 p.p.t for White workers (to 3.6%). 9/ {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/HyEXLUkSzb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/HyEXLUkSzb}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Council of Economic Advisers (@WhiteHouseCEA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/WhiteHouseCEA/status/1842188129597350048?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 The dollar is trading at a seven-week high after the jobs report, as traders conclude that a second \u8216\'20\'18jumbo\u8217\'20\'19 interest rate cut next month is now very unlikely.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index has reached 102.65, the highest since August 16, and is on track for its best weekly percentage gain since September 2022.\par \par \uc2 enltrBoth the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24DXY&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $DXY}}}\uc2  Dollar Index and S&P 500 are up today after the employment run. As you can see in the 20-day rolling historical correlation, that's unusual.\par \par \uc2 On both the 'risk' and 'rate forecast' dimension, these are on opposite ends of the spectrum. Which will stall? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/T9QnXfjwF7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/T9QnXfjwF7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 John Kicklighter (@JohnKicklighter) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JohnKicklighter/status/1842202966402519514?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 Last month\u8217\'20\'19s surging in US jobs suggests the Fed needs to tread carefully, say analysts at ING.\par \par \uc2 In a research note, they explain:\par \par \uc2 The US jobs report was incredibly strong on every front possible \u8211\'20\'13 job creation, unemployment, wages and hours worked. Nonetheless, caution lingers given the lack of corroborating data.\par \par \uc2 While the inflation backdrop is allowing the Fed to start moving monetary policy back to neutral, we think it will be in 25bp incremements, not the 50bp we saw in September.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, the government has welcomed the pick-up in construction sector activity last month ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/04/pound-worst-week-bank-of-england-us-dollar-jobs-report-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66ffac7d8f0818b7c81cc491#block-66ffac7d8f0818b7c81cc491"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 Chief Secretary to the Treasury Darren Jones says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe are focused on restoring economic stability and rebuilding Britain. This boost in business activity is clearly a positive sign, but there is more to be done as we drive towards growth as our number one mission.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why the Budget on 30 October will be about fixing the foundations of our economy so we can begin to deliver on the promise of change.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The surge in homebuilding activity last month may be thanks to the government\u8217\'20\'19s housing drive.\par \par \uc2 As {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bnnbloomberg.ca/investing/2024/10/04/uk-construction-sector-surges-in-pre-budget-boost-for-starmer/#:~:text=The%20PMI%20index%20is%20now,to%20fix%20Britain's%20housing%20crisis."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bloomberg puts it}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 A gauge of housebuilding rose to its highest since March 2022, boosted by government plans to build 1.5 million new homes over the next five years to fix Britain\u8217\'20\'19s housing crisis.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 : Jobs report is good news for American families\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has welcomed today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report, saying:\par \par \uc2 Today, we received good news for American workers and families with more than 250,000 new jobs in September and unemployment back down at 4.1%. With today\u8217\'20\'19s report, we\u8217\'20\'19ve created 16 million jobs, unemployment remains low, and wages are growing faster than prices.\par \par \uc2 Under my Administration, unemployment has been the lowest in 50 years, a record 19 million new businesses have been created, and inflation and interest rates are falling.\par \par \uc2 And we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing the power of collective bargaining to lift up workers\u8217\'20\'19 wages\u8212\'20\'14including the progress made by dockworkers on record wages with carriers, and port operators and the reopening of East Coast and Gulf ports.\par \par \uc2 Make no mistake: We have more to do to lower costs and expand opportunity. Congress should pass our plan to build millions of new homes, expand prescription drug price caps, empower workers and protect the right to organize, and cut taxes for hardworking families.\par \par \uc2 Congressional Republicans have a different plan\u8212\'20\'14more giant tax cuts for billionaires and big corporations, ending the Affordable Care Act, undermining workers by cutting overtime and making it harder to organize, and imposing a national sales tax that would raise costs by nearly $4,000 per year. While they put billionaires first, we\u8217\'20\'19ll keep fighting to grow the middle class.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 Financial stocks are among the top risers on the Dow Jones industrial average, with \uc2 JP Morgan Chase\uc2  up 2.2%, \uc2 American Express\uc2  rising 1.8% and \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  up 1%.\par \par \uc2 High interest rates have been good for bank profits (despite leading to a rise in bad debts), and today\u8217\'20\'19s strong jobs report suggests they may not fall as quickly as expected.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  (+1.8%) and \uc2 Caterpillar\uc2  (+1.55%) are also among the risers.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street rallies after strong jobs report\par \par \uc2 Stocks have jumped on Wall Street at the start of trading, as investors hail today\u8217\'20\'19s stronger-than-expected US jobs report.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones industrial average gained 233 points at the open, up 0.55% to 42,244 points.\par \par \uc2 The broader S&P 500 index gained 0.75%, while the tech-focused Nasdaq is up 1.2%.\par \par \uc2 Susannah Streeter, head of money and markets at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says hopes are rising for a \u8220\'20\'1Cgoldilocks scenario\u8221\'20\'1D for the US economy, explaining:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe US jobs market appears to have been infused with confidence in September, with new hirings shooting past expectations and the unemployment rate edging down. Fed policymakers had been worried about the potential for the jobs market to rapidly cool off, which is why they opted for a super-size rate cut last month. These latest numbers will help assuage those worries and add to a picture of a resilient US economy, which appears to be heading for a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 Financial markets are pricing in the chances of a cut in November at 93%, but are putting bets on a more usual 0.25% reduction. As expectations have retreated for a bigger rate cut, it\u8217\'20\'19s helped push the dollar to a seven-week high, and Wall Street looks set to end the week on an upbeat note.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s strong jobs report should calm fears about the state of the US jobs market, says Janet Mui, head of market analysis at wealth manager \uc2 RBC Brewin Dolphin\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSeptember\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll report, particularly the fall in the unemployment rate, should remove any near-term concerns on the state of the US labour market.\par \par \uc2 The combination of strong job creation and wage growth (both nominal and adjusted for inflation) is supportive to US consumption. With inflation heading in the right direction and ongoing expansion in the US economy, this should increase confidence that a soft-landing is coming to realisation, barring any shock.\par \par \uc2 While low inflation will allow the Fed to keep cutting rates, this strong set of results should reduce the likelihood of another outsized rate cut by the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  in November.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 The surging dollar has now dragged the pound down to a new three-week low of $1.3070.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 The chances of the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  making another jumbo cut to interest rates next month have collapsed.\par \par \uc2 According to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 CME Fedwatch}}}\uc2 , the odds of a half-point cut by the Fed in November are just 10%, down from over 30% before September\u8217\'20\'19s strong jobs report was released.\par \par \uc2 A smaller, quarter-point cut is now 90% likely.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report beats forecasts: What the experts say\par \par \uc2 Neil Birrell\uc2 , chief investment officer at Premier Miton Investors, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat was quite some jump in US payrolls in September, almost off the scale relative to expectations, and earnings were up more than anticipated as well. This is without question a strong jobs report.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUS employment data has been the focal point for bond and equity markets for the last two months and that will continue to be the case as everyone tries to second guess Fed policy. As it stands, a 0.5% cut must now be off the cards at their next meeting, although we do have one more jobs report before then \u8211\'20\'13 guess what we\u8217\'20\'19ll all be looking at!\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Richard Flynn, managing director at \uc2 Charles Schwab UK\uc2 , agrees that the jobs report is stronger than expected:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figures have exceeded expectations, indicating a high level of demand in the labour market, reversing a recent trend. At the last Fed meeting, \uc2 Jerome Powell\uc2  emphasised that the slowdown in job growth was the key factor behind the decision to kick off the easing cycle with a larger-than-normal rate cut. Powell indicated that the \u8220\'20\'1Cbalance of risks\u8221\'20\'1D to the US economy has shifted\u8212\'20\'14implying that supporting the job market has taken precedence over fighting inflation.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs figures suggest the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s action is working well to support its full-employment mandate. With high inflation largely in the rearview mirror, this could be less good news for markets, as it may slow the pace of future rate cuts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management, says such a strong jobs report means the Fed can\u8217\'20\'19t consider another large cut to interest rates at its next meeting:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDid the Fed even need to cut rates in September, let alone cut by 50bps?\par \par \uc2 The monster upside surprise suggests that the labor market may actually be a picture of strength, not weakness, and it completely dismisses the idea that the Fed could even contemplate another 50bps cut in November.\par \par \uc2 As jobless claims, the Challenger survey, and the multitude of strong economic data have been suggesting, the U.S economy is still robust. And with Fed easing now underway, recession risk has collapsed. Markets will need to keep a closer eye on inflation as, now, there are policy risks on both sides of the economy.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wage growth across the US economy was also a little stronger than expected.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report shows that average hourly earnings rose by 0.4% in September, after gaining 0.5% in August. Wages increased 4.0% year-on-year after climbing 3.9% in August.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street economists had expected a 0.3% monthly rise in hourly earnings, and a 3.8% annual increase.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Where were the 254,000 new jobs created last month?\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  reports that:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in foodservices and drinkingplaces rose by 69,000 in September.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Healthcare added 45,000 jobs in September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment rose in homehealthcareservices (+13,000), hospitals (+12,000), and nursingandresidentialcarefacilities (+9,000).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in government rose by 31,000, by 16,000 in localgovernment, and by 13,000 in stategovernment.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Employment in socialassistance increased by 27,000, primarily in individualandfamilyservices (+21,000).\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Constructionemployment continued to trend up in September (+25,000)\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Nonresidentialspecialtytradecontractors added 17,000 jobs.\par \par \uc2 Employment showed little change over the month in other major industries, including mining, quarrying, and oil and gas extraction; manufacturing; wholesale trade; retail trade; transportation and warehousing; information; financial activities; professional and business services; and other services, the \uc2 BLS\uc2  adds.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 At 254,000, the increase in total nonfarm payroll employment last month was higher than the average monthly gain of 203,000 over the previous year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s latest household survey has more details about the US labor market.\par \par \uc2 It shows that the unemployment rate for adult men decreased in September to 3.7%. The jobless rates for other groups were little changed.\par \par \uc2 The number of Americans who have been out of work for less than 5 weeks decreased by 322,000 to 2.1 million in September.\par \par \uc2 But the number of long-term unemployed (those jobless for 27 weeks or more) was little changed over the month at 1.6 million, up from 1.3 million a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dollar jumps after strong US jobs report\par \par \uc2 The dollar is soaring, after September\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report smashed forecasts.\par \par \uc2 The dollar index has jumped by almost 0.6%, putting it on track for its biggest one-day jump in four months.\par \par \uc2 This is crushing the pound\u8217\'20\'19s attempts to rally \u8211\'20\'13 sterling is now down 0.2% today at $1.31, cementing the chances that this week will be the pound\u8217\'20\'19s worst in over a year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.33pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report beats forecast\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Hiring across the US accelerated last month, with many more jobs created than expected.\par \par \uc2 The US non-farm payroll rose by 254,000 in September, new data from the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  shows.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s better than expected, and will be welcomed in the White House as a sign that the US economy is not weakening.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 BLS\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 Employment continued to trend up in food services and drinking places, health care, government, social assistance, and construction.\par \par \uc2 The US unemployment rate has fallen, to 4.1% from 4.2% the previous month.\par \par \uc2 August\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll has been revised up too, from +142,000 to +159,000.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s a wide range of forecasts for today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll, points out \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Valerie Tytel :\par \par \uc2 enltrNFP cons. 150k today, the range of forecasts is wide 70k-220k due to hard to predict seasonal adj. Revisions might also catch the markets eye - the number has been revised down every month this year except for one. One more NFP before Nov Fed, next week CPI and Fed minutes. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bkL0mMB2L0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/bkL0mMB2L0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Valerie Tytel (@ValerieTytel) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ValerieTytel/status/1842094490523222514?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 The countdown to the most crucial set of US data during October is nearly over, says Achilleas Georgolopoulos, investment analyst at brokerage XM.\par \par \uc2 At 12.30 GMT the non-farm payrolls figure is expected to show a 140k increase, with forecasts ranging from 70k to 220k. Both the unemployment rate and the average hourly earnings growth will probably remain unchanged at 4.2% and 3.8%, respectively.\par \par \uc2 Up to now, data prints have been mixed, with the ISM manufacturing survey disappointing but both the weekly jobless claims and the \uc2 ISM\uc2  services survey raising the probability of an upside surprise today. A stronger set of prints later today, especially if the non-payrolls figure surpasses the 200k level, could force the most dovish Fed members to tone down their rhetoric for the November 7 meeting.\par \par \uc2 Such an outcome could really dent the current sizeable 35% probability for a 50bps rate move in November and further boost the US dollar. It has been a rather strong week for the greenback on the back of the reduced Fed rate cut expectations and the developments in the Middle East, with the dollar index being on course for its best week since mid-March.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s nearly time for the final major piece of economic news of the week, the US non-farm payroll.\par \par \uc2 September\u8217\'20\'19s NFP \u8211\'20\'13 due at 8.30am East Coast time or 1.30pm in the UK \u8211\'20\'13 will give an insight into the health of the US jobs market \u8211\'20\'13 crucial both for the upcoming presidential election, and the path of US interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Economists predict that around 140,000 new jobs were created in September, according to a Reuters poll.\par \par \uc2 That would be very slightly lower than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/06/august-us-jobs-report-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August\u8217\'20\'19s 142,000, when there was a hiring slowdown}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 A surprisingly strong jobs report, or a jump in wage growth, might shake investors\u8217\'20\'19 confidence that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  will cut interest rates speedily this year.\par \par \uc2 Currently, a quarter-point cut in November is a 70% chance, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  according to CME Fedwatch}}}\uc2 , with a 30% possibility of a second half-point cut in a row.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the construction sector, the merger of housebuilders \uc2 Barratt\uc2  and \uc2 Redrow\uc2  has been given the green light by competition authorities.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  has said it has decided to accept undertakings made by the two companies, so the merger will therefore not be referred for a phase 2 investigation.\par \par \uc2 The CMA had been concerned that a Barratt development in Whitchurch, Shropshire, was close to \uc2 Redrow\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s development in Nantwich, Cheshire, possibly creating a local competition issue.\par \par \uc2 David Thomas\uc2 , CEO of \uc2 Barratt\uc2  says :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday is a significant milestone for Barratt Redrow, as we come together as one organisation. With this combination, we have created an exceptional housebuilder in terms of quality, service and sustainability, able to accelerate the delivery of the homes this country needs.\par \par \uc2 Together, we offer a broader range of homes and price points for our customers who we will continue to put at the heart of everything we do. Our focus now is on integrating our businesses as efficiently and effectively as we can to deliver the expected benefits of the Combination.\par \par \uc2 We will leverage the best of both companies to deliver significant benefits to our people, our customers and our supply chain partners, and ensuring that Barratt Redrow is set up to deliver long term value to all of its stakeholders.\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.32am BST\par \par \uc2 The pound is continuing to bounce back this morning.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s now up almost half a cent at $1.317, recovering around a third of yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s selloff.\par \par \uc2 This is thanks to \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s hawkish words about interest rates, and also the pick-up in construction sector activity.\par \par \uc2 Samer Hasn, senior market analyst at brokerage {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://u7061146.ct.sendgrid.net/ls/click?upn=u001.gqh-2BaxUzlo7XKIuSly0rC9L-2B1E-2Bp7Vs1dle6Bjxz3Ss-3DGV6e_pB8nDhcf0CbOCfG8oA3pUo18GXt-2F-2Bo-2BhB-2FK21dTqIPda5y-2BwiQGrnLPy99hKY-2Bsmv-2FtSBWheVfixegLmwZtfYDgDr4BFCz9JlBrZ4yjUk-2FTGiU8rq5C5HuRB5OMiF02Cb2wmXVCI08gts8PDgN1dtOHb1Pi1BMINUGR-2FnMXlEWXx1YV1tcnZJ7IQxg53o0-2FeKBjA20UocT8wU7VVcaModLzoF-2FRhioaCk2e-2BQDjAjZRU-2FKBWIWaQiNWM1EgQiRMbFalKd7iCkjJfetdW38qUmbgXOHabzQYrMOxsHG2rvC7QS3i1j4I2eE0ayP5TG-2FPdr7RcOIAzOYyNzLqqRLiWmzesiptUDLdHeO-2FGZzZFgX0-3D"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 XS.com}}}\uc2  , says:\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected construction data, with activity growing at the fastest pace in two and a half years, boosted the pound\u8217\'20\'19s gains today.\par \par \uc2 Escalating geopolitical tensions are likely to keep the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  wary of cutting interest rates too quickly as upward risks to inflation mount.\par \par \uc2 But, the pound is still on track for its worst week in over a year\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.27am BST\par \par \uc2 Senior Post Office executives treated the company board as a \u8220\'20\'1Cconfessional\u8221\'20\'1D, burdening them with huge amounts of paperwork in an attempt to dodge their responsibilities for delivering on the roles, the inquiry into the Horizon IT scandal has heard.\par \par \uc2 Rachel Scarrabelotti, group company secretary for the \uc2 Post Office\uc2  since April 2022, said that respect was lost for the board through the actions of senior executives presenting long papers on operational matters that were not part of their oversight responsibilities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey treated the board as a confessional,\u8221\'20\'1D she said, giving testimony at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/post-office-horizon-scandal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  the public inquiry into the Post Office and the Horizon IT scandal}}}\uc2  this morning.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey would bring all the information they could to the board and tell the board everything hoping it would somehow alleviate them of their responsibility.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said that the issues and presentations should properly have been handled by the \uc2 Post Office\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s executives instead.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt blurs the lines of accountability,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. \u8220\'20\'1CRespect has been perhaps lost for the board as they are associated with operational matters that are for the executive. Ultimately, the board doesn\u8217\'20\'19t get to fulfill its proper function to be the board.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 She said that \uc2 Nigel Railton\uc2 , the interim chair of the \uc2 Post Office\uc2  appointed following the firing of Henry Staunton in January, had cracked down on the practice putting strict limits on the length of board papers to stop executives shirking their responsibilities through information overload.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey now have to be pithy,\u8221\'20\'1D she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou have to say what you want, why you want it, and get out.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 European Union\uc2  is to adopt tariffs on China-made battery electric vehicles (BEVs) following a vote today.\par \par \uc2 In a statement the EC says:\par \par \uc2 Today, the \uc2 European Commission\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s proposal to impose definitive countervailing duties on imports of battery electric vehicles (BEVs) from China has obtained the necessary support from EU Member States for the adoption of tariffs.\par \par \uc2 This represents another step towards the conclusion of the Commission\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ec.europa.eu/commission/presscorner/detail/en/ip_23_4752"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 anti-subsidy investigation}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 enltr?? Just in: due to a high number of abstentions during today's vote, \uc2 EU\uc2  countries failed to agree on a proposal to slap steep tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/euronews?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @euronews}}}\uc2  has learned.\par \par \uc2 This means the Commission will break the impasse and introduce the tariffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jorge Liboreiro (@JorgeLiboreiro) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JorgeLiboreiro/status/1842127769993437493?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Back in June, the EC announced it intended to impose tariffs of up to 38% on imports of Chinese electric vehicles, triggering duties of more than \u8364\'20\'AC2bn (\u163\'00\'A31.7bn) a year.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jun/12/how-will-new-eu-tariffs-on-chinese-electric-vehicles-work"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How will new EU tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles work?}}}\par \par \uc2 According to Reuters, \uc2 EU\uc2  countries failed to vote clearly in favour or against tariffs on Chinese electric vehicles, leaving the \uc2 European Commission\uc2  to decide what to do.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/article/eu-duties-electric-cars-euoropean-union-autos/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Germany has opposed the plan}}}\uc2 , concerned that a trade war with China would hurt German exports when its economy is already weak.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.36am BST\par \par \uc2 The London stock market is in the red this morning, with the FTSE 100 share index down 0.3% or 25 points at 8257 points, despite a pick-up in bank shares.\par \par \uc2 Joshua Mahony, analyst at Scope Markets, says:\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 has struggled for positive momentum in early trade, with much of the index losing ground despite a sharp rise in the latest construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  figure.\par \par \uc2 The banking sector has provided the main tailwind this morning, with traders taking a more cautious approach following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/04/pound-worst-week-bank-of-england-us-dollar-jobs-report-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66ffa2048f0809b6ab31a38f#block-66ffa2048f0809b6ab31a38f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 comments from BoE Chief Economist Pill}}}\uc2  that struck a much less dovish tone than that from Bailey yesterday.\par \par \uc2 While markets remain optimistic that we will see cuts in both November and December, Pill\u8217\'20\'19s preference to remain restrictive in a bid to drive down underlying inflation does highlight the lack of a central dovish narrative that markets might believe exists.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  has \u8220\'20\'1Cpoured cold water\u8221\'20\'1D over yesterday\u8217\'20\'19s dovish comments from governor Andrew Bailey, says Matthew Ryan, head of market strategy at global financial services firm Ebury.\par \par \uc2 Ryan explains:\par \par \uc2 Pill reiterated the MPC\u8217\'20\'19s official stance that the bank remains wary of cutting rates too deeply or too quickly, highlighting continued concerns over structural issues that could keep UK inflation elevated for longer.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CHis communications provide an element of validation to our view that markets perhaps took Bailey\u8217\'20\'19s words too literally, and as a confirmation of faster cuts ahead, rather than merely a warning that this is a possibility. Indeed, we were somewhat perplexed and thrown off guard by Bailey\u8217\'20\'19s comments, as we do not believe that UK data since the last MPC meeting has necessarily deteriorated to an extent that would warrant a shift to a more dovish stance.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.11am BST\par \par \uc2 enltrPolitical certainty + reducing interest rates accelerated construction growth at the start of the autumn term. Civil engineering excelled with increased demand for renewable energy infrastructure and an uplift in work on major projects. Meanwhile commercial and house building\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/xzE8ZEW6bf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/xzE8ZEW6bf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1842129545945555401?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.01am BST\par \par \uc2 UK construction sector posts fastest upturn since April 2022\par \par \uc2 September was a strong month for the UK construction sector, partly due to falling borrowing costs, new data shows.\par \par \uc2 The latest survey of purchasing managers across UK building firms has found that business activity accelerated to its fastest rate in nearly two-and-a-half years.\par \par \uc2 Builders report a rise in new work, thanks to increased willingness-to-spend among clients and a more supportive economic backdrop.\par \par \uc2 This lifted the S&P Global UK Construction PMI to 57.2 in September, up from 53.6 in August, signalling faster growth (50 poinst = stagnation).\par \par \uc2 enltrThe S&P Global UK Construction PMI in September 2024 was 57.2, up from 53.6 in August. 50=no monthly change in construction activity so it reflects construction growth in September, for a 7th consecutive month, & its highest rate in 29 months. (1/n) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukconstruction?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukconstruction}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukhousing?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukhousing}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/uM38EHTkNy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/uM38EHTkNy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Noble Francis (@NobleFrancis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NobleFrancis/status/1842120970791759928?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Civil engineering, commercial building, and house building all strengthened.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the upturn in residential work was the fastest since March 2022, suggesting recent falls in mortgage rates are stimulating the market.\par \par \uc2 enltrHousing benefitted from improving market conditions & rising confidence in September (54.3) according to the \uc2 PMI\uc2  & it grew at its fastest since March 2022, but it was still slower than the other key construction sectors. (5/n) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukconstruction?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukconstruction}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ukhousing?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ukhousing}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lneaCtlCZL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lneaCtlCZL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Noble Francis (@NobleFrancis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/NobleFrancis/status/1842124484838314436?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUK construction companies indicated a decisive improvement in output growth momentum during September, driven by faster upturns across all three major categories of activity.\par \par \uc2 A combination of lower interest rates, domestic economic stability and strong pipelines of infrastructure work have helped to boost order books in recent months.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  also explained the \uc2 ICAEW\uc2  that the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  introduced a new form of communication, identifying three distinct cases to characterise the economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 He says:\par \par \uc2 The first case sees disinflation from here as a process largely independent of other developments in the economy. Disinflation owes to a self-sustaining virtuous cycle of declining headline inflation, falling inflation expectations, weaker pay growth and easing domestic services price inflation. Just as inflation rose on the back of external shocks, it will revert to target as those shocks recede.\par \par \uc2 The second case also foresees continued disinflation. This again owes to the selfsustaining virtuous cycle of declining headline inflation which I already outlined. But what is distinctive here is that this virtuous cycle relies on the maintenance of a restrictive monetary policy stance to bear down on inflationary pressures. Bank Rate will need to fall over time, but at a pace that ensures sufficient restriction is maintained in the transition for UK inflation to reach target in a lasting and sustained manner, not just fleetingly or in passing.\par \par \uc2 The third case posits deeper structural changes in the UK economy that threaten to impart a more lasting inflationary dynamic, if not met with an equally lasting monetary policy response necessary to return inflation to target and keep it there.\par \par \uc2 Those three scenarios cover the range of views on the MPC, from the doves who want to cut rates to the hawks who are more worried about inflation.\par \par \uc2 Pill says he sees merits in all three options, but that his \u8220\'20\'1Cmodal outlook\u8221\'20\'1D is probably closest to the second case.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  kicked off his speech to the chartered accountants with a joke, declaring:\par \par \uc2 In giving a talk to such a distinguished group of accountants, I am reminded of a famous joke about economists: \u8216\'20\'18An economist is someone who wanted to be an accountant, but didn\u8217\'20\'19t have the personality\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 At least, that is what passes for humour among economists. Perhaps it proves the point \u8211\'20\'13 although, on reflection, I am not sure which profession should take greater offence.\par \par \uc2 Pill\u8217\'20\'19s predecessor, \uc2 Andy Haldane\uc2 , was more of a punster, remarking once that \u8220\'20\'1CEconomists exist to make the weathermen look good\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/oct/01/andy-haldane-funnyman-bank-of-england-central-banker-not-good-at-maths"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andy Haldane: the funnyman central banker who's not great at maths}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.38am BST\par \par \uc2 Pill speech shows MPC "split" over interest rates\par \par \uc2 Simon French\uc2 , chief economist at investment bank Panmure Liberum, says \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cstriking a different tone\u8221\'20\'1D to Andrew Bailey in his interview with the Guardian.\par \par \uc2 French adds:\par \par \uc2 Market doesn\u8217\'20\'19t believe it with 92% likelihood of November cut, & 60% for a December follow-up cut. But it is a clear message even the internal MPC members are split on pace of removing restrictiveness:\par \par \uc2 enltrBoE Chief Economist, \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2 , striking a different tone to Governor Bailey's soundbite yesterday. Considerable caution and a preference for the Bank's Scenario 2 - prolonged restrictive monetary policy to bear down on persistent inflation components. Market doesn't believe it\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Simon French\uc2  (@Frencheconomics) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Frencheconomics/status/1842120367688573249?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.35am BST\par \par \uc2 enltrHuw Pill's view at {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/icaewAC24?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #icaewAC24}}}\uc2  giving us a lot of details on economic forecasts and the issues of using them but concludes that there are ample reasons to have concerns about reaching inflation targets and there will only be a gradual release of monetary restrictions. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ICAEW?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @icaew}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/GvSiRMlpHt"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/GvSiRMlpHt}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Malcolm Bacchus (@BaccmaConsult) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BaccmaConsult/status/1842120454980428126?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Pound stronger as Pill urges caution on rate cuts\par \par \uc2 The pound jumped as traders reacted to \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s call for a cautious approach to interest rate cuts.\par \par \uc2 It rallied as high as $1.3164, up from $1.312 last night,before settling back at $1.315\par \par \uc2 That recovers some of the pound\u8217\'20\'19s losses yesterday, and means this might only be sterling\u8217\'20\'19s worst week since July 2023.\par \par \uc2 enltrPound rising as Pill says it will be important to guard against the risk of cutting rates either too far or too fast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/H6mp1RIkhv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/H6mp1RIkhv}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/RePvHsvsNy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/RePvHsvsNy}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Conor Cooper (@Conor_Coops) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Conor_Coops/status/1842111933714964946?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.50am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.15am BST\par \par \uc2 BoE\uc2  chief economist urges caution over rate cuts\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The chief economist of the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  has warned against cutting interest rates \u8220\'20\'1Ctoo far or too fast\u8221\'20\'1D, a day after his boss signalled that the \uc2 BoE\uc2  could take a \u8220\'20\'1Cmore aggressive\u8221\'20\'1D position.\par \par \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  has told the \uc2 Institute of Chartered Accountants for England and Wales\uc2  this morning that there is \u8220\'20\'1Cample reason for caution\u8221\'20\'1D in assessing how far inflationary pressures have dissipated, and thus how quickly borrowing costs should fall.\par \par \uc2 enltrAt the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ICAEW?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @icaew}}}\uc2  annual conference today. \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2 . Chief economist at the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , just about to speak to a crowded room. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/DhWSc8WeSn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/DhWSc8WeSn}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Malcolm Bacchus (@MalcolmBacchus) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MalcolmBacchus/status/1842112227026833808?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 October 4, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Pill says:\par \par \uc2 While further cuts in Bank Rate remain in prospect should the economic and inflation outlook evolve broadly as expected, it will be important to guard against the risk of cutting rates either too far or too fast.\par \par \uc2 For me, the need for such caution points to a gradual withdrawal of monetary policy restriction.\par \par \uc2 This is quite a contrast with governor Andrew Bailey\u8217\'20\'19s comments to the Guardian this week \u8211\'20\'13 he told us there was a chance of the Bank becoming more \u8220\'20\'1Ca bit more activist\u8221\'20\'1D in its approach to cutting interest rates, if the news on inflation continued to be good.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/03/its-tragic-bank-of-england-governor-watching-middle-east-crisis-closely"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Very serious\u8217\'20\'19: Bank of England governor warns of Middle East oil shock risk}}}\par \par \uc2 Pill is a hawkish member of the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s monetary policy committee \u8211\'20\'13 he was one of four policymakers who opposed the rate cut in August, but were outvoted by the other five members of the MPC.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/01/bank-of-england-cuts-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Bank of England cuts interest rates to 5% in first reduction since March 2020}}}\par \par \uc2 He tells the \uc2 ICAEW\uc2  that he hopes to deliver \u8220\'20\'1Crobust monetary policy\u8221\'20\'1D that will guide inflation back to target, while avoiding volatility in economic activity and employment.\par \par \uc2 Sounding almost evangelical about the Bank\u8217\'20\'19s mission, Pill explains:\par \par \uc2 Rather price stability is a foundation \u8211\'20\'13 you could even argue, the foundation \u8211\'20\'13 of a thriving, modern, vigorous and growing UK market economy, which provides opportunities for all: precisely what I would envisage as an \u8216\'20\'18economy-fit-for-thefuture\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 Focusing monetary policy on the achievement of price stability is therefore not just a legal and institutional obligation for members of the MPC. It is the right thing to do. That is certainly my view; and I know that I am joined in this by my colleagues. We are in the price stability business.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Brent on track for strongest week in two years\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s rally means Brent crude has now climbed by 8.7% so far this week, to above $78/barrel.\par \par \uc2 That would be the biggest one-week gain in percentage terms since early October 2022.\par \par \uc2 Jim Reid\uc2 , strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , told clients this morning:\par \par \uc2 Markets have remained focused on the Middle East over the last 24 hours, as investors weigh up the likelihood of a fresh escalation and how Israel might respond to Iran\u8217\'20\'19s missile strikes last Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 That response from Israel is yet to materialise, but there was a fresh spike in oil prices after President Biden was asked whether he\u8217\'20\'19d support an Israeli strike on Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil facilities, and he said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re discussing that\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Brent crude now over $78/barrel\par \par \uc2 Oil prices are climbing again today, pushing Brent crude over $78 per barrel for the first time since the end of August.\par \par \uc2 The rally comes after former Israeli prime minister Ehud Barak has predicted that Israel is likely to mount a large-scale airstrike against Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil industry and possibly a symbolic attack on a military target related to its nuclear programme.\par \par \uc2 That follows president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s off-the-cuff comments yesterday that his administration has been \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscussing\u8221\'20\'1D possible Israeli plans to attack Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil industry.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/03/biden-says-us-discussing-possible-israeli-plans-to-attack-irans-oil-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden says US \u8216\'20\'18discussing\u8217\'20\'19 possible Israeli plans to attack Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil industry}}}\par \par \uc2 Today, the Israeli military tells residents of over 20 more southern towns in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/lebanon"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Lebanon}}}\uc2  to evacuate immediately, following overnight strikes on Beirut:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/oct/04/israel-lebanon-hamas-hezbollah-war-live-updates-hashem-safieddine-beirut-west-bank-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle East crisis live: Israel tells residents of over 20 southern Lebanon towns to evacuate; strike cuts off key crossing to Syria}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.44am BST\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s relief over in the US this morning, after the US ports strike that shut down shipping on the east and Gulf coasts for three days came to an end.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Longshoremen\u8217\'20\'19s Association\uc2  (ILA) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/story.php?story_fbid=941718281326238&id=100064642245982&rdid=TT0Wr0v8JAgjGU7a"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced}}}\uc2  that the union had reached an agreement with the United States Maritime Alliance (USMX) on wages, suspending their walkout until January. Work would resume immediately, the union said.\par \par \uc2 The strike \u8211\'20\'13 which involved 45,000 workers across 36 ports, from Texas to Maine \u8211\'20\'13 was the first to hit the east and Gulf coast ports of the US since 1977.\par \par \uc2 The tentative agreement is for a wage hike of around 62%, a source familiar with the matter told Reuters. Both sides said in a statement they would return to the bargaining table to negotiate all outstanding issues.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/oct/03/dock-workers-ports-strike-deal"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US dock workers agree on deal with port operators to end strike}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Reeves raises hopes of investment surge\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  also paved the way for a rise in capital spending at the budget later this month, dropping the broadest possible hint that she could change the government\u8217\'20\'19s fiscal rules to enable this.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19ll set out details of the fiscal rules at the budget, but we have got to make sure we unlock that space for capital investment,\u8221\'20\'1D she told journalists ahead of a visit to the Liverpool city region today to announce almost \u163\'00\'A322bn of funding over 25 years for carbon capture and storage projects.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  will be at the trip with the chancellor, prime minister \uc2 Keir Starmer\uc2 , and energy secretary \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2 , later this morning.\par \par \uc2 Reeves suggested she could outline upgraded investment plans at her 30 October tax and spending event. At the Conservatives\u8217\'20\'19 last budget in March forecasts showed public investment was set to fall from 2.4% of national output to about 1.7% by 2028-29. Labour\u8217\'20\'19s manifesto plans would limit that drop to about 1.8%.\par \par \uc2 However, Reeves hinted she could go further, saying of Tory plans to cut investment spending that she was \u8220\'20\'1Cnot going to make those mistakes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey were cutting back on investment, at exactly the time we need to be increasing investment in our economy.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI haven\u8217\'20\'19t hidden my ambitions to want to boost capital spending in the UK. I absolutely want to do that, it\u8217\'20\'19s how to break out of this sort of doom loop of low growth and deteriorating living standards. That means prioritising capital investment, particularly capital investment that leverages in the private sector.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Institute for Fiscal Studies\uc2  estimates that keeping investment at current levels as a share of GDP would cost \u163\'00\'A324bn a year of additional spending by 2028-29. To avoid real-terms cuts over the same period would mean a top up of about \u163\'00\'A318bn by that year.\par \par \uc2 Merseyside is currently the place to be for big Labour announcements. The chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s comments come after she used her conference speech in Liverpool last week to suggest she could relax her fiscal rules to engineer an investment boom.\par \par \uc2 There are more details on her options for doing so here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/uk-news/2024/sep/27/how-rachel-reeves-could-release-billions-more-for-investment-in-the-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 How Rachel Reeves could release billions more for investment in the budget}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.10am BST\par \par \uc2 Reeves warns of risk of inflation shock from Middle East crisis\par \par \uc2 Rachel Reeves\uc2  has said Britain\u8217\'20\'19s economy faces heightened risks of an inflation shock from a spiralling conflict in the Middle East.\par \par \uc2 Speaking to journalists ahead of a trip to the Liverpool city region today to announce a multibillion pound investment in carbon capture and storage, the chancellor said the Treasury was closely monitoring developments amid escalating tensions between Israel and Iran.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/04/labour-to-commit-almost-22bn-to-fund-carbon-capture-and-storage-projects"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labour to commit almost \u163\'00\'A322bn to fund carbon capture and storage projects}}}\par \par \uc2 Reeves referred to the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/03/its-tragic-bank-of-england-governor-watching-middle-east-crisis-closely"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Guardian\u8217\'20\'19s exclusive interview with the Bank of England governor, Andrew Bailey}}}\uc2 , on Thursday, where he warned of \u8220\'20\'1Cvery serious\u8221\'20\'1D risks to the economy from the worsening conflict with potential to reignite inflationary pressures.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI very much accept what the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  governor said yesterday,\u8221\'20\'1D the chancellor said, adding:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis is a very real risk to the UK and global economy\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a risk both on inflation and on GDP. It\u8217\'20\'19s something we\u8217\'20\'19ll keep a close eye on, these things. So far the response has been quite minimal but obviously these are important things to keep an eye on.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Reeves said that although oil prices had risen sharply in recent days ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/oct/04/pound-worst-week-bank-of-england-us-dollar-jobs-report-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66ff90408f0818b7c81cc36d#block-66ff90408f0818b7c81cc36d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see earlier post}}}\uc2  ) they remained significantly lower than the levels seen a year ago, while highlighting that equity markets had remained relatively calm despite the mounting fears over the Middle East conflict.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI recognise that there are big risks, there, if this does become a full blown regional conflict,\u8221\'20\'1D she said. The chancellor warned the main dangers for inflation were currently coming from disruption in the Red Sea to global shipping, which was driving up the cost of freight shipments, where Houthi rebels, backed by Iran, have been attacking international freight.\par \par \uc2 She said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe biggest impact so far in terms of economics of what\u8217\'20\'19s happening in the Middle East is on shipping costs.\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Oil prices spiked on Thursday after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/03/biden-says-us-discussing-possible-israeli-plans-to-attack-irans-oil-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 President Joe Biden suggested his administration has been \u8220\'20\'1Cdiscussing\u8221\'20\'1D possible Israeli plans to attack Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil industry}}}\uc2  in retaliation for the Iranian ballistic missile attack on Tuesday. The price of a barrel of oil shot up by about 5% to about $77. However, that remains below a peak this year of over $90 a barrel back in April.\par \par \uc2 Economists warn a spiralling conflict pushing up oil prices and freight costs could unpick progress to bring down inflation over the past year, which has enabled the world\u8217\'20\'19s most powerful central banks to begin cutting interest rates. Bailey told the Guardian on Thursday that if the news on inflation remained good, the Bank could be in a position to be \u8220\'20\'1Ca bit more aggressive\u8221\'20\'1D on cutting borrowing costs.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/03/sterling-pound-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-aggressive-interest-rate-cuts-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sterling drops after Bank of England boss hints at \u8216\'20\'18aggressive\u8217\'20\'19 rate cuts}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Oil on track for biggest weekly gain in over a year\par \par \uc2 The oil price is on track for its biggest monthly gains in over a year, as tensions in the Middle East have risen alarmingly.\par \par \uc2 Brent crude, the international benchmark, has climbed by 7.8% so far this week, to $77.60 per barrel, a gain of $5.60.\par \par \uc2 That would be its biggest percentage gain since February 2023, and largest dollar rise since October 2023.\par \par \uc2 Fears of potential supply disruption have lifted oil.\par \par \uc2 It jumped on Tuesday, when Iran launched its missile attack on Israel, and again yesterday when US president \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  said the US was discussing with Israel the possibility of Israeli strikes on Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil infrastructure.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/2024/oct/03/biden-says-us-discussing-possible-israeli-plans-to-attack-irans-oil-industry"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Biden says US \u8216\'20\'18discussing\u8217\'20\'19 possible Israeli plans to attack Iran\u8217\'20\'19s oil industry}}}\par \par \uc2 Brent crude is currently at a one-month high.\par \par \uc2 But, it\u8217\'20\'19s still effectively flat for the year, and much lower than after Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine in 2022 when oil jumped over $100/barrel.\par \par \uc2 Derren Nathan, head of equity research at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says the potential for disruption to Iranian production has been the key driver of the oil price this week.\par \par \uc2 Nathan explains:\par \par \uc2 Despite US sanctions, Iranian exports have increased to 1.7mn barrels per day this year.\par \par \uc2 But to put that in context, OPEC+ nations have treble that amount in spare capacity, so unless other producing nations suffer disruption, they should be able to take up that slack. However, a more worrying scenario would be the closure of the Straight of Hormuz, a vital shipping channel between Iran and Oman which has recently carried as much as 15mn barrels per day of crude, leaving potential for a significant oil price shock if marine traffic is blocked.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: A bad week for the pound\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets, and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 After a bruising few days, the pound is on track for its worst week in over a year.\par \par \uc2 Sterling has lost around 1.7% against the US dollar so far this week, as it dropped by 2.2 cents since Monday to around $1.314 this morning. That puts the pound on track for its biggest weekly loss since July 2023 \u8211\'20\'13 any weaker, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll be back to February 2023.\par \par \uc2 It has weakened as global investors have rushed into safe-haven assets, while economists are anticipating faster cuts to UK interest rates than previously expected.\par \par \uc2 As we covered yesterday, the pound hit a three-week low against the dollar on Thursday after \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  governor told \uc2 The Guardian\uc2  that the \uc2 BoE\uc2  could become \u8220\'20\'1Ca bit more activist\u8221\'20\'1D and \u8220\'20\'1Ca bit more aggressive\u8220\'20\'1C in its approach to lowering rates, if inflation pressures keep weakening.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/oct/03/sterling-pound-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-aggressive-interest-rate-cuts-inflation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sterling drops after Bank of England boss hints at \u8216\'20\'18aggressive\u8217\'20\'19 rate cuts}}}\par \par \uc2 That put the skids under the pound:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://interactive.guim.co.uk/uploader/embed/2024/10/pounddollar-zip/giv-45590yqwV6vMiW39"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Interactive}}}\par \par \uc2 Before Bailey\u8217\'20\'19s comments, traders had expected the \uc2 BoE\uc2  to be more cautious than its US and eurozone counterparts.\par \par \uc2 Not any more! \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 , for example, now predict the Bank could cut rates by a quarter-point at its next five meetings, bringing rates down from 5% today to 3.75% by next May.\par \par \uc2 The money markets predict slightly less easing, though \u8211\'20\'13 they indicate rates could have fallen to 4% by next May.\par \par \uc2 The second factor hitting the pound is a surge of money into the US dollar, which is trading near a six-week high against a basket of currencies.\par \par \uc2 Investors have sought out the safety of the greenback as Middle East tensions have risen this week, with Iran launching a missile attack at Israel and the Israeli military striking parts of Beirut.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/live/2024/oct/04/israel-lebanon-hamas-hezbollah-war-live-updates-hashem-safieddine-beirut-west-bank-strikes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Middle East crisis live: Beirut strikes reportedly target potential Hezbollah leader; 18 killed in Israeli strike on West Bank, officials say}}}\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://click.m.capitaleconomics.com/?qs=0fd952fb1db5d8f5382651c0ef9f6feb8004d550530b3bb7d8ccc93f47a01ec6d04d33003ff18faebe023f6554caed5bf6fd8410c61643dc91a736443f79ebde"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Hubert de Barochez}}}\uc2  , senior markets economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , fears the pound could be heading for more weakness, telling clients:\par \par \uc2 The British pound fell sharply on Thursday, and we suspect that it will weaken more over the next year or so given our dovish view of \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  policy, the currency\u8217\'20\'19s still-high valuation, and stretched speculative positioning.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll hear from \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2 , one of the more hawkish policymakers at the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 , this morning.\par \par \uc2 Investors are bracing for the latest US jobs report today, the last-but-one non-farm payroll before the US presidential election. It\u8217\'20\'19s expected to show around 140,000 jobs were created in America last month, very slightly lower than in August.\par \par \uc2 And in the UK, the government has pledged nearly \u163\'00\'A322bn to fund carbon capture and storage projects as it tries to hit climate targets.\par \par \uc2 The chancellor, prime minister and the energy secretary, \uc2 Ed Miliband\uc2 , will unveil the details on a visit to the Liverpool city region on Friday declaring a \u8220\'20\'1Cnew era\u8221\'20\'1D for clean energy jobs.\par \par \uc2 The plan means {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/rachel-reeves"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Rachel Reeves}}}\uc2  is paving the way for a multibillion-pound increase in public-sector investment at the budget\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/04/labour-to-commit-almost-22bn-to-fund-carbon-capture-and-storage-projects"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labour to commit almost \u163\'00\'A322bn to fund carbon capture and storage projects}}}\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 8.30am BST: Eurozone construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 8.55am BST: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief economist \uc2 Huw Pill\uc2  to speak at the Institute Chartered Accountants in England and Wales annual conference\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: UK new car registrations for September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: \uc2 UN\uc2  food price index for September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: UK construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for September\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll jobs report for September\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.17pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241004eka4000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
