\par\par \uc2 Environment\par \b \uc2 No water, no shade. Life as a roofer in the sweltering Florida heat: \u8216\'20\'18It feels like 120F\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato in Florida \par \uc2 946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par 18:31 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Workers struggle with dehydration, fatigue dizziness and headaches \u8211\'20\'13 but state laws have stripped their protectionsLife as a California warehouse worker: \u8216\'20\'18Products matter more than people\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Every day, Raquel Atlahua begins her work as a roofer bracing for the blistering sun.\par \par \uc2 On the roof, there is no escape from the direct light and heat, and the temperatures in Florida {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://climatecenter.fsu.edu/topics/specials/floridas-hot-season"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 quickly climb}}}\uc2  as the day progresses. The high humidity and lack of shade make it feel even hotter, and even more difficult to cool down.\par \par \uc2 She and her co-workers get going at 7am, sometimes earlier. They hope the early start means they can get as much work done before temperatures peak in the afternoon \u8211\'20\'13 before constant physical exertion, heat and humidity make them feel drained. They battle the climate while rushing to finish a job where the pay is based on the work they complete, rather than by an hourly or daily rate.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CSometimes we see that the thermometer is 95 degrees, but really when you\u8217\'20\'19re up roofing, it feels like 120 degrees, so there\u8217\'20\'19s a big difference from what people see to what we\u8217\'20\'19re feeling up there,\u8221\'20\'1D Atlahua said.\par \par \uc2 Employers often don\u8217\'20\'19t provide workers water even though they are supposed to, so she and her co-workers make sure they have plenty. She adds lemon, sugar and salt to her water to keep up her electrolytes, as she is constantly sweating. She has learned to avoid drinks like soda to keep up her hydration and eats as healthy as she can; she often sees co-workers struggle with the fatigue of being exposed to intense heat. Fatigue, dizziness, headaches and soreness are constant issues.\par \par \uc2 Atlahua has worked outdoors for 20 years in central Florida. She began as a roofer in 2004, then became a farm worker in 2009 when the economic recession {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.professionalroofing.net/Articles/Lasting-effects--09-01-2014/3520#:~:text=Specialty%20construction%20contractors%2C%20including%20roofing,U.S.%20(see%20Figure%201)."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 slowed down}}}\uc2  roofing work, before returning to roofing in 2015.\par \par \uc2 Atlahua said she has noticed a significant increase in the intensity and number of hot days she experiences since she began two decades ago.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COne of the things I remember in 2004 when I started was that intense heat days would start around May. Now those heatwaves are felt as early as January,\u8221\'20\'1D said Atlahua.\par \par \uc2 Atlahua\u8217\'20\'19s experiences, about the weather in Florida getting hotter, are backed by data and climate science research. A 2024 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.pnj.com/story/weather/2024/02/27/florida-hottest-state-us/72758334007/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 study}}}\uc2  conducted by Payless Power utilizing data from the World Weather Online API found Florida is now the hottest state in the US, with an average temperature of 74.1F (23.4C) over the past 15 years. Unusually hot conditions in Florida have been made {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.climatecentral.org/climate-shift-index-alert/climate-shift-index-alert-florida-may-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 five times more likely}}}\uc2  due to the climate crisis.\par \par \uc2 Globally, 2024 is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://business.weatherzone.com.au/climate/2024-likely-to-be-earths-warmest-year-on-record-new-data-shows/#:~:text=The%20global%20mean%20temperature%20during,Earth's%20warmest%20year%20on%20record."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 on track}}}\uc2  to become the hottest year on record, which is currently held by 2023.\par \par \uc2 There are an estimated 2 million outdoor workers in Florida. But despite warming trends, workers here and throughout the US currently have no heat protections on the job. Earlier this year, Republicans in Florida {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/08/florida-bill-extreme-heat-worker-protection"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 passed}}}\uc2  a law banning cities and counties from enacting heat protections for workers, amid industry pressure. The Biden administration has proposed a federal rule to provide heat protections for workers, but it could be several more years until it is approved and enacted.\par \par \uc2 Atlahua explained employers provide no heat protections or resources to her and her co-workers who are directly exposed to the sun on the roofs of houses and buildings. Instead, she and her co-workers are left to try to take care and support themselves.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t give anything to the workers,\u8221\'20\'1D Atlahua noted in reference to whether employers provide any water, training or support for workers around the heat.\par \par \uc2 As roofers, she and her co-workers aren\u8217\'20\'19t provided any shade, either. If they have to use the bathroom, they are forced to drive to the nearest gas station to use one there and rely on the air conditioning in the gas station and their vehicles to cool down on breaks.\par \par \uc2 Atlahua argued that she would like to see improvements in education for workers on how to protect themselves from the heat, and for more patience and support from employers, who often won\u8217\'20\'19t allow workers to work too early or too late in the day when the temperatures and sun exposure aren\u8217\'20\'19t as hot.\par \par \uc2 She also noted due to the nature of the work, with contractors often utilizing subcontractors who have their own employees, that the lack of education and protections around heat get dismissed and the onus is put on workers to try to protect themselves the best they can.\par \par \uc2 Though employers are {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.osha.gov/faq#:~:text=OSHA%20Standards%20require%20an%20employer,for%20water%20that%20is%20provided."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 supposed}}}\uc2  to provide clean, drinkable water for free to workers under Osha\u8217\'20\'19s general duty clause, employers will come up with excuses against doing so. \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s because of the laws in Florida and the message Florida sends to employers. Employers also give the excuse that they are liable if they give us water and we get sick,\u8221\'20\'1D she added.\par \par \uc2 She also has a message for the owners whose homes and buildings she\u8217\'20\'19s repairing: \u8220\'20\'1COwners should think more about the workers and let us sit down and rest when needed. I often see co-workers suffering muscle issues, constantly drinking water, and how much it affects them and how hard it is for them to work in the heat.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 This is the first of three stories about the US workers who are struggling to survive a summer of extreme heat that shattered records from coast to coast.\par \par \uc2 Read part two {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/environment/2024/oct/03/california-warehouse-employees-working-conditions-heatwave"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 . Part\par \par \uc2 three\par \par \uc2 coming soon\par \par \uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241001eka10028r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
