\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Striking US port workers sound resolute note: \u8216\'20\'18This is for our future generations\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Edward Helmore in Elizabeth, New Jersey \par \uc2 653 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 October 2024\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 International Longshoremen\u8217\'20\'19s Association\uc2  members walk out on east and Gulf coasts over pay and automation dispute\par \par \uc2 A parade of tractors without their trailers made their way through the Port Newark-Elizabeth Marine terminal on Wednesday morning, blaring their horns, to cheers from the picket lines.\par \par \uc2 Tens of thousands of longshoremen walked off the job at dozens of ports across the east and Gulf coasts earlier this week. The \uc2 Port Authority of New York and New Jersey\uc2  confirms most operations have been shut down as a result of the strike.\par \par \uc2 On the picket lines at Elizabeth, New Jersey \u8211\'20\'13 one of the largest container ports in the US \u8211\'20\'13 strikers have been instructed to man the picket lines 24 hours a day, seven days a week, until the \uc2 International Longshoremen\u8217\'20\'19s Association\uc2  (ILA) and the US Maritime Alliance (USMX) reach a deal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf a monsoon comes, we\u8217\'20\'19re gonna stay out, because we\u8217\'20\'19re used to working in a monsoon,\u8221\'20\'1D one striker said. The warm October sun was shining \u8211\'20\'13 at least for now.\par \par \uc2 Disagreements over pay and the automation of machinery are at the heart of the dispute.\par \par \uc2 Joe Mosquera, a crane operator and union organizer with ILA, local 1235, said its goals were straightforward. \u8220\'20\'1CThe action is going to give us a fair contract and we can get back to work to get people the goods they need , \u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CThis is for our future generations. To keep automation out is to keep our jobs for the future. And if anything becomes automated , we want to make sure that there\u8217\'20\'19s a worker to back it up.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The ILA has not had a new contract for six years. \u8220\'20\'1CWe honored our last contract,\u8221\'20\'1D said Mosquera, \u8220\'20\'1C even through Covid when we had to work double or triple. We never shut down, so everybody could get their goods. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t ask for a pay raise. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t walk out to try to get leverage when we could have, because we were honoring our agreement.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Mosquera, 47, is a third-generation longshoreman in his family. His grandfather was hired in 1954, and he hoped his family would work at the port for generations to come. \u8220\'20\'1CI hope to keep it going, yes,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Iwona Purwin, an inventory checker who has worked at the port since 1998, said she had been out all night and the strikers would stay for \u8220\'20\'1Cas long as it takes\u8221\'20\'1D. \u8220\'20\'1CI would like to see no automation take place at all and for the American people to keep their jobs.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The port management \u8220\'20\'1Care trying to take our jobs\u8221\'20\'1D, Purwin said. \u8220\'20\'1C I\u8217\'20\'19m a single mom of three. How would I provide for my family if I\u8217\'20\'19m not working?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Purwin, 46, drives an hour to work each day from central New Jersey, and described how she had shouldered increases \u8220\'20\'1Cin gas prices, tolls and everything else. We\u8217\'20\'19re not getting paid for any of this stuff. We didn\u8217\'20\'19t get a raise for inflation or for anything else. We were essential workers. We worked through Covid. And we didn\u8217\'20\'19t ask for anything.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The demands of the union workers, she said, had been mischaracterized. \u8220\'20\'1CThey don\u8217\'20\'19t understand how much we work and the sacrifices we make for our families. It\u8217\'20\'19s not a nine-to-five job. This is seven days a week around the clock, so we\u8217\'20\'19re here, often more than we are with our families.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Questions have been raised about the strike\u8217\'20\'19s potential impact on Thanksgiving and the holiday season. Mosquera expressed hope that a dea l will come sooner. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m a father and a husband, I worry about everything,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CI hope it will not last that long. We demand a fair contract , so we can get back to work.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020241003eka3001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
