\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Strike looms at busiest US ports as 45,000 workers prepare to walk off job\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 747 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 September 2024\par 11:01 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Longshoremen\u8217\'20\'19s union says wage demands must be met or members will strike on Tuesday for first time since 1977\par \par \uc2 Ten of the busiest ports in North America are at risk of shutdown next week, as a leading union warns that 45,000 workers stand ready to walk off the job if industry operators fail to make key concessions.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 International Longshoremen\u8217\'20\'19s Association\uc2  (ILA) has accused the United States Maritime Alliance (USMX), which represents 40 ocean carrier and terminal operators, of making \u8220\'20\'1Cinsulting offers\u8221\'20\'1D to staff demanding higher pay and commitments on automation.\par \par \uc2 Workers are slated to strike on Tuesday if a new agreement is not reached, hitting 36 ports along the east and Gulf coasts of the US in what would be the first work stoppage since 1977.\par \par \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  analysts have estimated a strike could cost the US economy $5bn per day. A one-day strike backlog could take an estimated four to six days to clear, according to analysts at Sea-Intelligence, a shipping advisory firm.\par \par \uc2 Bargaining between the two sides came to a halt in June after the union accused the USMX of violating the current contract by implementing the use of automation at ports, including in Mobile, Alabama. ILA leaders claimed the technology, an auto-gate system used to automate the processing of trucks into ports, was being used to replace union labor.\par \par \uc2 The union says it has been in contact with the USMX several times in recent weeks, but that the USMX has so far declined to increase its offer on pay.\par \par \uc2 The ILA is demanding significant wage increases, healthcare improvements, and no automated terminals or semi-automated terminals at the ports. For years, labor unions representing US port workers have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://therealnews.com/automation-and-the-trade-deficit-are-decimating-us-ports"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 criticized}}}\uc2  automation for resulting in significant job losses, while shipping corporation profits soared.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUSMX knows what our bottom line with wages needs to be for our ILA rank and file to ratify a new master contract agreement,\u8221\'20\'1D said Harold J Daggett, the ILA\u8217\'20\'19s international president and chief negotiator. \u8220\'20\'1CThey call me several times each week trying to get the ILA to accept a low-ball wage package.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy ILA members are not going to accept these insulting offers that are a joke considering the work my ILA longshore workers perform, and the billion-dollar profits the companies make off the backs of their labor.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, Daggett addressed members in a video on the looming strike, describing how port workers continued to work through the Covid pandemic \u8211\'20\'13 including those who died from the virus \u8211\'20\'13 as shipping corporations made billions of dollars in profits.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe blame for a coast-wide strike in a week that will shut down all ports on the Atlantic and Gulf coasts falls squarely on the shoulders of USMX,\u8221\'20\'1D Daggett argued.\par \par \uc2 The union has pushed back on USMX claims about its wage demands, arguing a $5-an-hour wage increase for each year of the six-year contract is only a 9.98% annual increase. It has also alluded to garnering international support if workers do walk out on strike.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWithout a doubt, we are going to get support from the international dock-worker movement,\u8221\'20\'1D Dennis Daggett, executive vice-president of the ILA, said in a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://ilaunion.org/ila-president-harold-daggett-and-executive-vice-president-dennis-daggett-discuss-strike-potential/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 video address}}}\uc2  to members this month. \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19m not going to elaborate on that, but I will tell you this, it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be the biggest display of international worker solidarity that this world has ever seen.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 In August last year, port workers on the west coast {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/us-west-coast-port-workers-ratify-contract-agreement-2023-08-31/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ratified}}}\uc2  a six-year agreement, covering 22,000 workers at 29 ports. The agreement included 32% wage increases for the life of the contract, which was retroactive to July 2022.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the USMX shared the latest negotiating update, adding that it would be open to federal mediation. The union and USMX {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.freightwaves.com/news/ila-port-strike-what-you-need-to-know"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 submitted}}}\uc2  forms in August to the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2 , informing the agency of their dispute.\par \par \uc2 The USMX said: \u8220\'20\'1CDespite additional attempts by USMX to engage with the ILA and resume bargaining, we have been unable to schedule a meeting to continue negotiations on a new master contract. We remain prepared to bargain at any time, but both sides must come to the table if we are going to reach a deal, and there is no indication that the ILA is interested in negotiating at this time.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240926ek9q001p6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
