\par\par \uc2 Australia news\par \b \uc2 NSW nurses and midwives announce strike \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Josh Taylor and Emily Wind (earlier) \par \uc2 15,322 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 September 2024\par 07:43 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 This blog is now closedGreens MP to tour Sydney Jewish Museum and donate funds after offensive \u8216\'20\'18tentacles\u8217\'20\'19 tropeGet our breaking news email, free app or daily news podcast\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.31am BST\par \par \uc2 What we learned, Friday 20 September\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re wrapping up the blog for today. Here\u8217\'20\'19s what made the news:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Former Liberal prime minister \uc2 John Howard\uc2  has taken aim at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , describing him as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec36cd8f089e4a13dfa7de#block-66ec36cd8f089e4a13dfa7de"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cnot compatible with democracy\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Tasmanian senator \uc2 Jacqui Lambie\uc2  has announced her former upper house colleague \uc2 Rex Patrick\uc2  will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec2cee8f081d8429b1bd45#block-66ec2cee8f081d8429b1bd45"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lead the minor party\u8217\'20\'19s ticket in South Australia}}}\uc2  in the upcoming federal election.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Mortgage holders may have to wait longer for an interest rate reprieve, after {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec8d508f089e4a13dfad0d#block-66ec8d508f089e4a13dfad0d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 stronger than expected jobs figures}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The federal government {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec36838f081d8429b1be03#block-66ec36838f081d8429b1be03"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced a boost to rent assistance}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s housing crisis has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec8e108f089e4a13dfad13#block-66ec8e108f089e4a13dfad13"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 worsened on nearly every key indicator}}}\uc2  one year since the state government\u8217\'20\'19s housing statement, which set to tackle affordability by building 800,000 new homes and increasing renters\u8217\'20\'19 rights.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The NDIS minister, \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2 , has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ec9a428f089e4a13dfad60#block-66ec9a428f089e4a13dfad60"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 described the Greens as \u8220\'20\'1Cformidable and destructive\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2  after a week of fighting between the two parties over housing legislation that stalled in the Senate.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 There is free train travel in Sydney this weekend in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66eca4d38f089e4a13dfadab#block-66eca4d38f089e4a13dfadab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a bid to avoid industrial action}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NSW Nurses and Midwives\u8217\'20\'19 Association\uc2  said members {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ecd32d8f08098f3f162e2e#block-66ecd32d8f08098f3f162e2e"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 will strike for 24 hours next week}}}\uc2 , as pay negotiations continue with the state government.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jewish students are being singled out, spat on, removing identifiable symbols and \u8220\'20\'1Cfearing for their safety\u8221\'20\'1D, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ecb0c98f089e4a13dfae05#block-66ecb0c98f089e4a13dfae05"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Senate inquiry has heard}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The prime minister, Anthony Albanese has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66ecc9948f081d8429b1c48d#block-66ecc9948f081d8429b1c48d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arrived in Philadelphia, US}}}\uc2 , ahead of the Quad leaders meeting.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Graham Arnold will {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?page=with:block-66eccf358f089e4a13dfaead#block-66eccf358f089e4a13dfaead"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 depart immediately}}}\uc2  as head coach of the Socceroos.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.38am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.04am BST\par \par \uc2 [Continued from previous post]\par \par \uc2 The Minns government\u8217\'20\'19s legislation was also developed in response to an independent review of the state's environmental laws by former top public servant Dr \uc2 Ken Henry\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 While conservation groups are expected to call for the reforms to be even stronger, the inquiry has also heard from developer and industry lobby groups opposed to the reforms.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in today\u8217\'20\'19s hearing, the policy director of the NSW Minerals Council, Claire Doherty, claimed that development wasn\u8217\'20\'19t the biggest contributor to biodiversity loss. She said:\par \par \uc2 Development is a very small proportion of the impacts on nature in NSW.\par \par \uc2 Whether the BOS [Biodiversity Offsets Scheme] is the very highest priority in terms of making changes, I would question that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.12am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18Loopholes\u8217\'20\'19 need fixing in NSW\u8217\'20\'19s environment laws that allow developers to \u8216\'20\'18offset\u8217\'20\'19 habitat clearing, ecological association says\par \par \uc2 The vice-president of the New South Wales Ecological Consultants Association, Rebecca Hogan, says the state government\u8217\'20\'19s proposed biodiversity offset reforms should be amended to fix a range of \u8220\'20\'1Cloopholes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 A parliamentary inquiry is examining the Minns Labor government\u8217\'20\'19s legislation that would strengthen the state\u8217\'20\'19s environmental laws that allow developers to \u8220\'20\'1Coffset\u8221\'20\'1D habitat clearing.\par \par \uc2 At a hearing this afternoon, Hogan said:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re in support of most of the reforms proposed, but are deeply concerned by the lack of definition around phrases and terms used.\par \par \uc2 The industry needs clear direction and interpretation guidelines. There are loopholes all over the place.\par \par \uc2 Biodiversity offsetting usually involves conserving and restoring bushland to compensate for clearing of ecosystems in another area for development.\par \par \uc2 The environment minister, Penny Sharpe, promised to reform the system after an earlier inquiry found there was too much flexibility for threatened habitat to be \u8220\'20\'1Ctraded away for cash\u8221\'20\'1D when offsets should be a genuine last resort.\par \par \uc2 [Continued in next post]\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Private companies could be banned from running NSW elections\par \par \uc2 Private companies could be banned from running elections in NSW after reports of three-hour waits to vote at recent council polls, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The state\u8217\'20\'19s premier, \uc2 Chris Minns\uc2 , floated the ban on Friday, suggesting he would introduce changes that outlawed the practice as long delays could disfranchise voters and potentially threaten democratic principles.\par \par \uc2 Two councils \u8211\'20\'13 Liverpool and Fairfield \u8211\'20\'13 used private companies at local elections on Saturday, while the \uc2 NSW Electoral Commission\uc2  ran the remaining polls.\par \par \uc2 Minns, who claimed the significant delays were only experienced at polling stations run by private contractors, said it \u8220\'20\'1Cwasn\u8217\'20\'19t good enough\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 It has to be as convenient as possible for a voter to walk into a booth, cast their vote, be part of democracy and get on with their Saturday.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not acceptable to have a three-hour wait just to cast your vote for a local council election and, I think, a lot of people would have thrown their arms up in frustration and left if they weren\u8217\'20\'19t going to get a fine.\par \par \uc2 But Fairfield council staff disputed the premier\u8217\'20\'19s stance and said voters experienced no delays.\par \par \uc2 Using a private provider was as simple as comparing the options and picking the one with the best value for taxpayers, a spokesperson told \uc2 AAP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.00am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Firefighters battle industrial fire in Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s south-west\par \par \uc2 Fire and Rescue NSW\uc2  are battling a large fire at a commercial complex on Kaleski Street in Moorebank in Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s south-west.\par \par \uc2 More than 100 firefighters and 26 trucks are either on the scene or en route.\par \par \uc2 Fire and Rescue NSW\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 On arrival, crews were confronted with large volumes of smoke coming from the property and immediately set about tackling the blaze with multiple lines of hose.\par \par \uc2 Two aerial platforms are also being used to douse the flames from above.\par \par \uc2 Residents in surrounding buildings have been evacuated and there is a large amount of smoke in the vicinity.\par \par \uc2 Nearby residents are being urged to stay inside, close windows and doors and avoid the area.\par \par \uc2 Operations are ongoing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.53am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Victorian drivers spared on-the-spot traffic fines as police beef up industrial action before \uc2 AFL\uc2  grand final\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s treasurer has urged police to think about the consequence of their actions as officers beef up industrial action ahead of the \uc2 AFL\uc2  grand final, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Tim Pallas accused the police union of taking action against the community as a consequence of their own failure to vote for a previous pay offer.\par \par \uc2 He said:\par \par \uc2 I would urge police to think a bit sympathetically about the consequence of the actions.\par \par \uc2 The police association knows full well they reached an agreement in principle with the government, they failed to be able to deliver that agreement through a vote of their membership.\par \par \uc2 It seems a little perverse that they\u8217\'20\'19re taking action against the community and the community\u8217\'20\'19s interest as a consequence of their own failure.\par \par \uc2 Pallas flagged the state would seek \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  intervention to reconcile differences in the bargaining.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not going to see this as some sort of game in an ongoing negotiation process.\par \par \uc2 The verbal blast followed 15,000 officers voting to escalate efforts for better pay and working hours, including refusing to hand out on-the-spot traffic fines to drivers from Friday morning.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.04am BST\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 Manhunt under way after inmate escapes Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s Silverwater prison\par \par \uc2 An inmate has escaped custody at Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s maximum security Silverwater prison, say NSW police.\par \par \uc2 Gary Glover, 44, was last seen on Jamieson Street, Silverwater, in the city\u8217\'20\'19s south-west, about 1.30pm today. He is described as 170cm tall, Aboriginal/Torres Strait Islander in appearance and having a large build, brown eyes, black hair and a tattoo of a flag on the left side of his neck.\par \par \uc2 He was last seen wearing a green shirt and pants, black boots and a yellow hi-visibility vest.\par \par \uc2 Police are appealing for information and urged the public not to approach the man but instead call triple zero (000) immediately.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.50am BST\par \par \uc2 University antisemitism inquiry risks marginalising \u8216\'20\'18other communities who face daily discrimination\u8217\'20\'19, new Muslim group says\par \par \uc2 A judicial inquiry into antisemitism on campuses risks marginalising Muslim and Palestinian university students concurrently facing a rise in discrimination, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/17/muslim-votes-matter-says-labor-afraid-of-backlash-after-taking-islamic-community-for-granted"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Muslim Voices Matter}}}\uc2  has warned.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the group Ghaith Krayem told a committee holding a Senate inquiry into the bill that the body fully supported efforts to combat antisemitism \u8220\'20\'1Cwhen it genuinely exists\u8221\'20\'1D, but questioned the necessity of the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Muslim Votes Matter is one of the new organisations seeking to mobilise Australian Muslims at the next federal election, describing itself as \u8220\'20\'1Cthe largest, and among the fastest growing, minority groups in Australia\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Krayem:\par \par \uc2 What evidence exists to suggest antisemitic incidents at Australian universities are significantly more prevalent or severe than other forms of discrimination such as anti-Muslim or anti-Palestinian racism? We\u8217\'20\'19re not saying it doesn\u8217\'20\'19t exist or it hasn\u8217\'20\'19t increased, we\u8217\'20\'19re asking the community to separate out activism against Israel and its application of Zionism from genuine antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 Kraymen questioned why the bill didn\u8217\'20\'19t concurrently address a parallel rise in anti-Muslim and anti-Palestinian sentiments on campuses. He said conflating criticism of Israel with antisemitism could have a \u8220\'20\'1Cchilling effect\u8221\'20\'1D on campuses and undermine free speech.\par \par \uc2 By introducing legislation that addresses only one form of racism, we risk further marginalising other communities who face daily discrimination.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Meet Coles Jetson: Robotic online grocery shopping has arrived\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  has launched an automated warehouse for grocery delivery in Victoria.\par \par \uc2 The supermarket chain said the Truganina site was more than 87,000 square metres \u8211\'20\'13 four times the size of the Melbourne Cricket Ground \u8211\'20\'13 and, when fully operational, would hold 3m units of stock and be able to process more than 10,000 customer orders a day when running at capacity.\par \par \uc2 Coles\uc2  said a centralised hub would contain a fleet of more than 700 bots able to fill a customer order containing 50 items in five minutes, using AI air traffic control to guide the bots around a 3D grid. However, \uc2 Coles\uc2  staff would pack and deliver the items.\par \par \uc2 A second site in Wetherill Park, New South Wales, will open next month.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Coles\uc2  chief executive, \uc2 Leah Weckert\uc2 , said it was the first of its kind in the country:\par \par \uc2 This transition from a local, store-based fulfillment model to a central, world-class facility will enable us to better serve the greater Melbourne region, home to more than five million people.\par \par \uc2 Amazon\uc2  has similar facilities and is planning to launch more automated delivery centres in Australia soon.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.09am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.16am BST\par \par \uc2 ABS told Labor excluding gender identity questions posed risk to census\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government was warned that excluding questions on sexual orientation and gender identity from the census could increase feelings of exclusion in the LGBTQ+ community and even risk the success of the data collection exercise, newly released documents reveal.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Bureau of Statistics\uc2  raised concerns in ministerial submissions about strong public criticism to scrapping the proposed questions, potential \u8220\'20\'1Cdamage\u8221\'20\'1D to relationships with LGBTQ+ expert groups advising on the census and limitations in the quality of data the census collects.\par \par \uc2 Read more:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/20/australia-census-lgbtq-questions-abs-warning"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ABS warned Albanese government that excluding LGBTQ+ questions risked the success of census}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 6.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Many thanks for joining me on the blog today, Josh Taylor will be here to take you through the rest of our rolling coverage. Take care, and enjoy your weekend.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Overheated market could burn first-time homebuyers, expert says\par \par \uc2 One expert says pushing people to enter the \u8220\'20\'1Coverheated\u8221\'20\'1D housing market is a bad idea, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, with new data finding first-time buyers want to buy property as soon as possible.\par \par \uc2 A {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://helia.com.au/media/vohhow3z/helia-home-buyer-sentiment-report-2024-digital.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 survey from insurance company Helia}}}\uc2  found 71% of Australians agree \u8220\'20\'1Cnow is a good time\u8221\'20\'1D to buy a property, despite cost-of-living stresses and persistent inflation at 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 Of the 3002 respondents, 1965 were first-timers and most (87%) agreed with the statement they were \u8220\'20\'1Cfeeling a growing sense of urgency to buy [their] first property as prices keep going up\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Prof Emma Baker, director of the Australian Centre for Housing Research, warned that focusing on home-buying was not in the nation\u8217\'20\'19s best interest.\par \par \uc2 Should we be encouraging people into an overheated market? No. It\u8217\'20\'19s not fair on them and bringing forward demand in a tight market only contributes to greater unaffordability.\par \par \uc2 She said young householders seeking to become owners \u8220\'20\'1Cclearly face more barriers than their parents did\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 House prices have risen consistently for 50 years, moving from three times to around 10 times average income \u8230\'20\'26 Many young people will be lifetime renters. Over the last couple of Censuses, Australia has begun to shift \u8230\'20\'26 to a nation of home purchasers and renters.\par \par \uc2 Baker suggested more discussion should focus on how to make rental markets more appealing \u8220\'20\'1Crather than obsessing about how to bring more people into home ownership\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 6.10am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.31am BST\par \par \uc2 Prepare to leave warning issued for rural Queensland town amid fast-moving fire\par \par \uc2 A \u8216\'20\'18prepare to leave\u8217\'20\'19 bushfire warning has been issued for Rosenthal Heights, near Warwick, 130km south-west of Brisbane.\par \par \uc2 The Queensland fire department says a fast-moving fire is burning on the corner of Ford and Kingsleigh roads, travelling towards Inverleigh and Ranger roads.\par \par \uc2 Conditions could get worse quickly. Firefighters are working to contain the fire. You should not expect a firefighter at your door.\par \par \uc2 The warning said some properties were at risk and the fire was likely to hit the community in the coming hours. Roads, power, water and mobile phone services may be affected as the fire approaches.\par \par \uc2 enltrPREPARE TO LEAVE - Rosenthal Heights (near Warwick) - fire as at 2:10pm Friday, 20 September 2024\par \par \uc2 For all current warnings, updates and mapping go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/J7gghFTxkA"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/J7gghFTxkA}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/lOOnjcXwmN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/lOOnjcXwmN}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Queensland Fire Department (@QldFireDept) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/QldFireDept/status/1836981273383874763?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 20, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.50am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Greens warn Danish immigration minister to \u8216\'20\'18not go down this path\u8217\'20\'19 on asylum policy\par \par \uc2 Continuing from our last post: Greens immigration spokesperson, \uc2 David Shoebridge\uc2 , met Kaare Dybvad Bek and told him, \u8220\'20\'1CVery clearly: do not go down this path\u8221\'20\'1D and copy the Australian playbook.\par \par \uc2 Shoebridge told \uc2 AAP\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 A national asylum policy that deliberately harms innocent people who are only seeking protection is a race to the bottom, where you will squander billions in public funds and degrade your collective values.\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s more recent history on asylum seekers should also be a lesson, but in what not to do and how to avoid your politics sinking to a moral low.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Human Rights Watch\uc2  Australia director, Daniela Gavshon, also told Dybvad Bek that Australia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed offshore detention regime\u8221\'20\'1D on Nauru and Manus Island in Papua New Guinea had caused \u8220\'20\'1Cimmense suffering\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Vibe Klarup, Amnesty International Denmark\u8217\'20\'19s secretary general, likened Nauru to \u8220\'20\'1Can open-air prison\u8221\'20\'1D and said, \u8220\'20\'1CFrom a human rights perspective, there are no good lessons learned from the very costly Australian model.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.36am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Danish immigration minister hails \u8216\'20\'18valuable\u8217\'20\'19 Nauru tour\par \par \uc2 Denmark\u8217\'20\'19s immigration minister says he gained \u8220\'20\'1Cvaluable insights\u8221\'20\'1D on a trip to Nauru to study Australia\u8217\'20\'19s controversial offshore asylum seeker processing setup, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Danish immigration minister, Kaare Dybvad Bek, last week travelled more than 13,000km from Copenhagen to the tiny Pacific Island nation of Nauru, which has hosted an Australian-run immigration detention centre on and off since 2001. He told \uc2 AAP\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 I had a prolific trip, which gave me lots of valuable insights and lessons. I learned much about both the pros and the cons of the cooperation between Australia and Nauru.\par \par \uc2 Dybvad Bek said since 2014 more than 30,000 people have drowned or disappeared on their way to Europe, and the \u8220\'20\'1Ccurrent situation is deeply inhumane and an insult to humanity.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The Danish government only wishes to engage in solutions in line with our international obligations and responsibilities, including the European convention on human rights.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you more on this in a moment.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.20am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.13am BST\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has issued a severe weather warning for damaging winds about the Snowy Mountains from tomorrow morning.\par \par \uc2 Damaging winds around 60-70km/h, with peak gusts above 90km/h, are possible over elevations above 1000m of parts of the Snowy Mountains from early tomorrow.\par \par \uc2 This will follow the passage of a cold front across Victoria. Winds are expected to ease by early Saturday afternoon, the Bureau said.\par \par \uc2 Locations which may be affected include Jindabyne, Perisher Valley, Charlotte Pass, Thredbo and Adaminaby.\par \par \uc2 enltrSevere Weather Warning for DAMAGING WINDS\par \par \uc2 DAMAGING WINDS, averaging 60 to 70 km/h with peak gusts in excess of 90 km/h are possible over elevations above 1000m of parts of the Snowy Mountains from early Saturday morning. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EIDIQ98q7d"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/EIDIQ98q7d}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/DamagingWinds?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #DamagingWinds}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/SnowyMountains?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #SnowyMountains}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4YxW3sHg1w"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/4YxW3sHg1w}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, New South Wales (@BOM_NSW) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_NSW/status/1836971594427572368?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 20, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Union asked if rejecting 3% offer would give it less leverage in negotiations\par \par \uc2 Over four months of negotiations, Shaye Candish said the union wasn\u8217\'20\'19t able to resolve \u8220\'20\'1Ceven the most simple claims\u8221\'20\'1D such as whether nurses should be entitled to have two days off in a row.\par \par \uc2 However, two small no-cost claims have been settled, in relation to having notice boards in tea rooms and nurses not working night shifts before they go on leave.\par \par \uc2 In response to a question suggesting that rejecting the 3% offer would give the union less leverage in negotiations, Caddish said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe would never stand in the way of our members receiving an increase to their pay\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But the reality for us is that members have already rejected this offer, and 3% doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go nearly far enough.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Low wages were undermining their ability to deliver high quality care, Shaye Candish added, and leaving nurses and midwives feeling undervalued.\par \par \uc2 NSW midwives are moving to states like Victoria and Queensland, where wages are between 10 and 22% higher, she said.\par \par \uc2 Unfortunately they\u8217\'20\'19re in a position where they\u8217\'20\'19re forced to take this action to the government to the table and offer something meaningful.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not acceptable for the state government to continue turning a blind eye to the pay inequity that is seriously undermining this state\u8217\'20\'19s largest female-dominated workforce. We now have the lowest paid nurses and midwives in the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.57am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW Nurses and Midwives Association\uc2  says members \u8216\'20\'18furious\u8217\'20\'19 amid pay negotiations\par \par \uc2 NSW nurses and midwives will strike for 24 hours next Tuesday, after the government has given \u8220\'20\'1Cno indication they intend to move\u8221\'20\'1D on their initial offer of an annual pay rise of 3%.\par \par \uc2 NSW health minister, Ryan Park, this morning said the 3% pay rise was conditional on no further industrial action.\par \par \uc2 NSW Nurses and Midwives\u8217\'20\'19 Association (NSWNMA)\uc2  general secretary, Shaye Candish, said the government\u8217\'20\'19s offer of a 3% increase was \u8220\'20\'1Calready rejected\u8221\'20\'1D by NSWNMA members, who have called for a wage increase of 15%.\par \par \uc2 The union has \u8220\'20\'1Cgenuinely tried to avert this action, but the government has simply failed to demonstrate a willingness to move\u8221\'20\'1D, she said.\par \par \uc2 Our members are furious \u8230\'20\'26 it\u8217\'20\'19s a really difficult decision for a nurse or midwife to decide to go on strike.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.14am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Nuclear cannot deliver in timeframe needed for climate and energy security, independent MP says\par \par \uc2 Independent MP for Wentworth, Allegra Spender, says that nuclear power cannot deliver results in the timeframe needed for \u8220\'20\'1Cclimate and for energy security\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 In a series of tweets, she responded to reports that the Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jun/19/coalition-austrailan-nuclear-power-plan-energy-sector-business-scepticism"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 plan for seven nuclear power plants}}}\uc2  could lift power bills for average households by $665 a year:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/20/coalition-nuclear-power-plan-will-add-665-dollars-to-average-power-bill-a-year-report-warns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Coalition\u8217\'20\'19s nuclear plan will add $665 a year to average power bill, report warns}}}\par \par \uc2 Spender said:\par \par \uc2 The next 10 years is what counts for climate and for energy security, and nuclear simply cannot deliver in that timeframe.\par \par \uc2 This is not about being \u8220\'20\'1Cpro\u8221\'20\'1D or \u8220\'20\'1Canti\u8221\'20\'1D particular technologies. It\u8217\'20\'19s about basing energy policy on the facts. We\u8217\'20\'19re already at ~40% renewables. We need to accelerate action on what we know works, rather than causing huge investment uncertainty by ripping up \uc2 AEMO\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plan.\par \par \uc2 We can remain open to the possibility that new technologies may develop on future, but that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean we should bet the house on them and ignore the evidence of what works today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.52am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Taking questions, James Johnson said the Socceroos have a match in less than three weeks \u8211\'20\'13 which is why they\u8217\'20\'19re looking to appoint a new, permanent coach, rather than an interim one.\par \par \uc2 We will look locally and globally. But ultimately, we need a coach that will come in and get the best out of an Australian group of players and take our great players through a complicated Asian qualifying process.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.38am BST\par \par \uc2 FA hopes to appoint new Socceroos coach \u8216\'20\'18in the coming days\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 James Johnson said Football Australia was already searching for a new coach:\par \par \uc2 We know the market, we know who is available, and I want to confirm that we will move quickly to appoint a permanent coach before the next window. And hopefully, in the coming days or next week or two, we would like to convene you here with us so we can share the news of who the new Socceroos coach will be.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.41am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.37am BST\par \par \uc2 James Johnson from Football Australia has been speaking to reporters, following news that Socceroos head coach Graham Arnold has resigned.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/football/2024/sep/20/graham-arnold-socceroos-coach-quits-resignation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Graham Arnold resigns as Socceroos coach ahead of next World Cup qualifiers}}}\par \par \uc2 Johnson said the resignation transpired \u8220\'20\'1Cvery quickly\u8221\'20\'1D, off the back of the last match in the qualifiers against Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 I want to say very clearly that we appreciate Graham Arnold\u8230\'20\'26 [He] has been a key person within the Subway Socceroos set-up for the past six years, and under his leadership, there have been many outstanding achievements that have been made by the Subway Socceroos\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 He\u8217\'20\'19s had an outstanding playing career and he\u8217\'20\'19s done a lot for Australian football. So we wish Graham Arnold, we wish his family, all the very best in the future and on behalf of the Football Australia board, I do want to thank Graham, personally, for all of the work that he\u8217\'20\'19s done over the past six years.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.33am BST\par \par \uc2 Aussie shares hit all-time high for fourth straight day\par \par \uc2 The local share market has hit a record high for a fourth straight session and is on track for its seventh straight day of gains, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 At noon the benchmark S&P/ASX200 index was up 36.4 points, or 0.44%, to 8,228.3, and had been as high as 8,246.2. For the week it was on track for a 1.5% gain, its best week in five weeks. The broader All Ordinaries was up 41 points, or 0.49%, to 8,458.0.\par \par \uc2 Every sector of the \uc2 ASX\uc2  was higher at midday, with tech the biggest gainer, up 1.2% as \uc2 Technology One\uc2  grew 2.2% to an all-time high of $24.02 and \uc2 Life360\uc2  rose 5.4% to a 10-day high of $18.13.\par \par \uc2 In the heavyweight mining sector, goldminers were gaining as the precious metal changed hands just below its all-time high of $2,600 set Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Northern Star\uc2  was up 1.0%, Evolution had added 0.9% and West African Resources had grown 2.7%. Elsewhere in the sector, \uc2 BHP\uc2  and \uc2 Fortescue\uc2  were both up 0.6%, while \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  had edged 0.1% higher.\par \par \uc2 The big four banks were mixed, with ANZ up 0.7% and \uc2 Westpac\uc2  adding 0.2%, while \uc2 CBA\uc2  and \uc2 NAB\uc2  were both down 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Insurance companies \uc2 IAG\uc2  and \uc2 Suncorp\uc2  had dropped 0.6 and 1.4%, respectively, while Computershare had dropped 2.8%. Lower interest rates tend to hurt the bottom lines of all three companies, which need to have billions stashed in short-term investments.\par \par \uc2 Myer\uc2  was down 5.1% to a nine-day low of 83c after the department store chain {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66ecbac58f08098f3f162d8f#block-66ecbac58f08098f3f162d8f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reported a 26% drop in full-year profit}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.26am BST\par \par \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18did not always meet expectations of Jewish community\u8217\'20\'19: vice-chancellor\par \par \uc2 The vice-chancellor of the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2 , Prof Mark Scott, has apologised to students and staff who provided testimonies of antisemitism on his campus, acknowledging his institution \u8220\'20\'1Cdid not always meet the expectations of the Jewish community\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a Senate inquiry into antisemitism, where the Committee heard the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  was one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cworst places\u8221\'20\'1D to be a Jewish student, he said he had \u8220\'20\'1Cnever encountered an issue that\u8217\'20\'19s generated more anguish or grief or anger\u8221\'20\'1D as the events of, and after, 7 October:\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve read the complaints that have been made to the university and all those shared in submissions to this inquiry \u8230\'20\'26 and the testimonials are heartbreaking and unacceptable and for that I am sorry. No one should feel at risk, unsafe or unwelcome at any place of learning.\par \par \uc2 Scott said the establishment of a pro-Palestine encampment at his university in March \u8211\'20\'13 the first in Australia \u8211\'20\'13 presented a \u8220\'20\'1Cdifficult period for the university, its students and staff\u8221\'20\'1D. He said management was meeting daily in a \u8220\'20\'1Ccomplex, fast moving environment\u8221\'20\'1D to navigate campus safety.\par \par \uc2 I recognise we did not always meet the expectations of the Jewish community. I regret we did not communicate the agreement to end the encampment with the Jewish community before it was announced. I realise there is a lot of work to do to win back the trust and confidence of the university\u8217\'20\'19s Jewish community and I\u8217\'20\'19m committed to doing that.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.13am BST\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a Friday afternoon, which can only mean one thing: it\u8217\'20\'19s time to get stuck into the latest Weekly Beast from Amanda Meade :\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/commentisfree/2024/sep/20/andrew-o-keefe-media-coverage-seven-paparazzi-ntwnfb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Seven\u8217\'20\'19s Andrew O\u8217\'20\'19Keefe coverage turns ugly as goading cameraman crosses a line | Weekly Beast}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.02am BST\par \par \uc2 The Bureau of Meteorology has published a weather outlook for the weekend ahead, with rain set to affect the north-west:\par \par \uc2 enltrWeather Update: Rain to impact the north-west this weekend, and many areas next week. Video current: 11:30am AEST 20 September 2024. For the latest forecasts and warnings, go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/4W35o8iFmh}}}\uc2  or the BOM Weather app. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/QOHtxwVjoB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/QOHtxwVjoB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1836963089327239190?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 20, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.03am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Australia needs \u8216\'20\'18strong, decisive\u8217\'20\'19 response to deaths in custody: Senator Thorpe\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, independent senator \uc2 Lidia Thorpe\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s motion for the attorney general to provide quarterly reports on deaths in custody passed the Senate.\par \par \uc2 It passed with the support of all non-government senators. The quarterly statements will detail, by state and territory, the number of:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Deaths in custody, including breakdown by age groups and cause of death\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ongoing coronial inquests;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Incidents of self-harm in custodial settings; and\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Miscarriages and stillbirths in custodial settings.\par \par \uc2 In a statement today, Thorpe said Australia needs \u8220\'20\'1Ca strong, decisive federal response and nation leadership on these critical issues\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday Labor senators were alone in opposing this motion. The entire crossbench and the Coalition are united on this. We want to see the federal government finally start taking responsibility for what is happening in this country\u8217\'20\'19s criminal legal system.\par \par \uc2 Labor will make the excuse that these are just \u8216\'20\'18state issues\u8217\'20\'19, but we should not accept this weak cop-out. The attorney-general meets regularly with the states and territories, he can and should require them to provide this data.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Lifeline (13 11 14), 13YARN (13 92 76)\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.08am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Further Cook Strait ferry drama in New Zealand\par \par \uc2 A ferry containing 88 passengers and crew lost power and drifted for two hours in New Zealand\u8217\'20\'19s notoriously dangerous Cook Strait, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 In the latest drama to hit ferry sailings between the North and South islands, the Connemara was tugged back to Wellington early on Friday morning after the mishap.\par \par \uc2 Wellington harbourmaster Grant Nalder told Radio NZ the ferry, run by private operators Bluebridge, lost power about 10.30pm as it was sailing off the south coast of Wellington, bound for Picton.\par \par \uc2 The incident occurred in thankfully light winds, and the Connemara was drifting south-east, away from land.\par \par \uc2 The NZ transport minister, Simeon Brown, called the power outage an \u8220\'20\'1Cunfortunate incident\u8221\'20\'1D that would prompt the Connemara\u8217\'20\'19s grounding, and told Radio NZ:\par \par \uc2 The vessel won\u8217\'20\'19t be allowed to go back into service until [\uc2 Maritime New Zealand\uc2 ] has investigated and is assured that it is safe for it to do so.\par \par \uc2 Bluebridge said 24 passengers, 13 freight drivers and crew were on board the overnight freight sailing, which returned to its Wellington wharf at sunrise.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.44am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Candidates for abolished seat of Higgins target Senate \u8216\'20\'18job share\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Two Melbourne women who were planning to run for the now-abolished seat of Higgins on a job-sharing basis now say they will run for a Senate spot.\par \par \uc2 Lucy Bradlow, a political communications expert, and Bronwen Bock, an investment banker, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/apr/21/double-or-nothing-australias-first-job-sharing-political-candidates-raise-constitutional-questions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 announced in April}}}\uc2  they planned to run for federal parliament as independent \u8220\'20\'1Cjob-sharing candidates\u8221\'20\'1D for the Melbourne metropolitan seat. If they were successful, they planned to share a laptop, as well as split official duties and travel to Canberra on alternate weeks.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Electoral Commission\uc2  has since abolished Higgins in its redistribution prior to the next federal election. And the pair said in {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.facebook.com/bradlowbock/posts/pfbid0iJdCdx28heUfU5TN9CnjtHqzA4yd7rtsvxFSW9tDy4hMkkCcFHtfUGt9wVgMfykPl?__cft__[0]=AZXVg_ohja8nV6T-Qzx_3bBQ_YC-pmMMEHrcszx2fK0qVTM4ErJTCnnmscwev4uXIAaKJBfvaL-7pPe5DMxZrYspztVYmZiN2Zx0CkpjV5-kMHUU4UcEBPT9yXKxaq6QkGZe9OfYShvYXHu5nBncMM3yaMz1gyYxQeNkJKFxFtrJ-A&__tn__=%2CO%2CP-R"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a Facebook post}}}\uc2  on Friday they now plan to run for the Senate \u8211\'20\'13 as a single senator:\par \par \uc2 Higgins no longer exists, but our belief remains. We believe Australians deserve real representation, real action, and a government that works for its constituents. That\u8217\'20\'19s why today, we are launching the Better Together Party \u8211\'20\'13 our campaign to be the independent job-sharing Senator for Victoria. We want to give Victorians a real voice in the federal Senate. We stand for a new era of politics, where collaboration and inclusivity drive innovative policies and meaningful change for all Australians.\par \par \uc2 But we can\u8217\'20\'19t do it alone. We need you to join us on our journey. If you\u8217\'20\'19re registered to vote ANYWHERE in Australia, you can be part of the Better Together Party and have a voice in our future. It\u8217\'20\'19s easy and free to sign up. Visit our website TODAY to join us and learn more about our policies and values.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.39am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Consumer advocate says insurance sector faces \u8216\'20\'18market failure\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 A consumer advocacy group has told a parliamentary inquiry the insurance sector has suffered a \u8220\'20\'1Cmarket failure\u8221\'20\'1D, with fast-rising home premiums leaving families exposed.\par \par \uc2 Tyrone Shandiman, founder of the Australian Consumers Insurance Lobby, cited the example of a pensioner in Townsville, in Queensland\u8217\'20\'19s north, paying 40% of her pension for home insurance. Shandiman said:\par \par \uc2 We believe in the first instance insurers should have the opportunity to deal with problems within this market, but we do not support market failure, and the market is failing.\par \par \uc2 We believe that when the insurance market is not meeting the needs for consumers, governments must intervene.\par \par \uc2 Australia is grappling with an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/26/report-warns-australian-households-struggling-to-insure-their-homes"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 insurance crisis}}}\uc2 , leaving many households either unwilling or unable to insure their homes, with those in and around areas prone to natural disasters the most affected.\par \par \uc2 Shandiman said that high premiums were forcing people to under-insure or drop insurance altogether, which meant they required government assistance when things went wrong.\par \par \uc2 The Greens-chaired select committee on the impact of climate risk on insurance is holding a public hearing in Sydney today.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.34am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Sydney rail chaos averted as free fares get union tick\par \par \uc2 Earlier, we brought you news that Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s business community was pleading with rail workers to call off industrial action, ahead of meetings to confirm free-travel arrangements for this weekend\u8217\'20\'19s footy finals. You can read all the details {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66eca4d38f089e4a13dfadab#block-66eca4d38f089e4a13dfadab"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier in the blog here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 As \uc2 AAP\uc2  now reports, \uc2 Unions NSW\uc2  assistant secretary and lead negotiator, Thomas Costa, said trains could run as needed while the free-travel offer was in place. He told ABC Radio:\par \par \uc2 If you\u8217\'20\'19re offering free fares over the weekend, we\u8217\'20\'19re happy to run the trains as normal. Whether the trains run as normal or not will be up for management and their ability to manage the timetables.\par \par \uc2 The rail union\u8217\'20\'19s opposition to the conversion of a stretch of the Bankstown line to metro train is running in parallel to an enterprise agreement claim for a 32% pay rise over four years and a 35-hour work week.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/19/sydney-commuter-delays-and-shutdown-of-t3-line-possible-if-nsw-fails-to-reach-agreement-with-transport-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sydney train delays and shutdown of T3 line possible if NSW fails to reach agreement with transport union}}}\par \par \uc2 Transport for NSW secretary, Josh Murray, said there would still be interruptions over the weekend as officials worked through last-minute schedule changes:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re putting those special event services into the timetable and we\u8217\'20\'19ll provide more information about what the regularity of those will be because it\u8217\'20\'19s last minute.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.55am BST\par \par \uc2 NSW nurses and midwives to strike next week\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 NSW Nurses and Midwives\u8217\'20\'19 Association\uc2  says members will strike for 24 hours next week, as pay negotiations continue with the state government.\par \par \uc2 Thousands of nurses and midwives will strike from the start of morning shift next Tuesday. The union says the Minns government failed to demonstrate a willingness to negotiate in good faith by close of business yesterday.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66ecb9818f081d8429b1c43f#block-66ecb9818f081d8429b1c43f"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Earlier this morning}}}\uc2 , NSW health minister, Ryan Park, said the government accepted to pay nurses an interim 3% increase, back-paid to 1 July this year, contingent on the industrial action ceasing.\par \par \uc2 NSWNMA general secretary, Shaye Candish, said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 We have genuinely tried to avert this action, but the government has simply failed to demonstrate a willingness to move.\par \par \uc2 Our members are desperate to provide the safe level of care NSW patients need when seeking treatment inside public hospitals, but instead of their skilled work being remunerated accordingly, their employer believes a baseline 3% pay offer is enough.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s not acceptable for the state government to continue turning a blind eye to the pay inequity that is seriously undermining this state\u8217\'20\'19s largest female-dominated workforce. We now have the lowest paid nurses and midwives in the country.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.06am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Jewish council calls for inquiry into all kinds of racism on university campuses\par \par \uc2 A judicial inquiry into antisemitism on campuses risks silencing Palestinian voices and stoking a culture war, the Jewish Council of Australia has warned.\par \par \uc2 Sarah Schwartz, executive officer of the Jewish Council of Australia and an academic at the \uc2 University of Melbourne\uc2 , has told a Senate inquiry into the proposed bill that it risked increasing antisemitism by conflating Judaism with the state of Israel.\par \par \uc2 We have never been more visible. Our identities and communities are constantly platformed, written about in the media and weaponised by politicians to justify support for a nation state accused of genocide.\par \par \uc2 Such an inquiry risks pitting racialised groups against each other. An inquiry into antisemitism alone places it in a category separate to other forms of racism, thus creating a perception of a hierarchy of racism which we say will only generate division between Jewish and other racialised groups.\par \par \uc2 Instead, she said the body would support an inquiry into all kinds of racism on university campuses \u8211\'20\'13 including Islamophobia and racism towards Indigenous Australians, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/feb/25/cutting-debt-and-paid-internships-key-reforms-in-the-australian-universities-blueprint"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as was recommended in the Australian Universities Accord}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Senate inquiry into the proposed bill has received more than 600 submissions, including from students who have expressed fear of attending campus due to rising antisemitism.\par \par \uc2 However others, including the Loud Jew Collective, warned the proposed commission of inquiry was being proposed \u8220\'20\'1Cnot to combat antisemitism but rather to exceptionalise Jews and to demonise Palestinians and their supporters\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 NT teachers to be highest paid in country under new deal, education minister says\par \par \uc2 The NT\u8217\'20\'19s education minister, Jo Hersey, says teachers in the territory will become the highest paid in Australia under a new pay deal.\par \par \uc2 In a statement, she said more than 90% of teachers voted in support of the Territory government\u8217\'20\'19s pay offer of a 12.9% pay increase over three years.\par \par \uc2 The Territory must be competitive if we are going to attract and retain excellent teachers and this deal delivers the country\u8217\'20\'19s best pay deal.\par \par \uc2 Other benefits include a $1,000 increase in the Katherine and Alice Springs attraction and retention allowance, she said, which is now worth $4,500 a year.\par \par \uc2 Over the life of the agreement, the increase represents a 13.46% compounded pay rise.\par \par \uc2 The commissioner for public employment will lodge the agreement with the \uc2 Fair Work Commission\uc2  for approval, and until then, the current enterprise agreement continues to apply.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.46am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Man in critical condition after alleged shooting in rural NSW\par \par \uc2 An investigation is under way after a 46-year-old man was shot in the abdomen at a rural property in New South Wales.\par \par \uc2 Officers were called to the Hillston property, 100km north-west of Griffith, about 2.30am and were told the man and two women \u8211\'20\'13 31 and 56 \u8211\'20\'13 were in the home when three people allegedly entered, one armed with a firearm.\par \par \uc2 Police allege the resident was shot twice before the alleged intruders left.\par \par \uc2 The man was treated by paramedics and taken to Griffith hospital, and has since been airlifted to a major metropolitan hospital in a critical condition with life-threatening injuries.\par \par \uc2 Inquiries are now under way, police said.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.40am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Graham Arnold to depart immediately as Socceroos head coach\par \par \uc2 Football Australia has released a statement, confirming Graham Arnold will depart immediately as head coach of the Socceroos.\par \par \uc2 FA said Arnold advised them of his decision to resign earlier this week.\par \par \uc2 Arnold has served as the head coach since August 2018, marking his second term with the team after a brief interim role in 2006-2007.\par \par \uc2 James Johnson, CEO of Football Australia, said:\par \par \uc2 While we respect his decision and are saddened to see Graham leave the national team set-up, this scenario is not uncommon in international football. We will act swiftly in appointing a new head coach to ensure continuity and stability, particularly as we approach the crucial second window of the AFC Asian Qualifiers next month.\par \par \uc2 Arnold said that \u8220\'20\'1Cleading the Subway Socceroos has been the pinnacle of my career and a true honour\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19ve given 40 years of service to Australian football, with the last six years in my role as Socceroos\u8217\'20\'19 head coach. I said after our game against Indonesia that I had some decisions to make, and after deep reflection, my gut has told me it\u8217\'20\'19s time for change, both for myself and the program.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.46am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.25am BST\par \par \uc2 Sydney academics concerned policy requiring permission for megaphones and posters on campus may be illegal\par \par \uc2 More than 50 academics at the University of Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s Law School have signed an open letter expressing concerns a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jul/08/university-of-sydney-students-and-staff-blast-new-draconian-protest-crackdown"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new campus policy}}}\uc2  which requires explicit permission for megaphones to be used or posters to be put up on campus may be illegal.\par \par \uc2 The letter, delivered to the chancellor, vice-chancellor and Senate on Friday, said the policy \u8220\'20\'1Cunreasonably limits\u8221\'20\'1D the right to free protest on campus and was introduced without consultation, breaching the university\u8217\'20\'19s own policies and inconsistent with Australia\u8217\'20\'19s obligations under international law.\par \par \uc2 The policy breaks with a proud history and tradition of protest at the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2 . It disproportionately curtails the freedoms of expression, assembly and movement that have long been enjoyed responsibly by the staff, students and the general public at the university.\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for the \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  said it always welcomed feedback and appreciated the \u8220\'20\'1Ctime and consideration\u8221\'20\'1D colleagues had taken to share their views about its new policy, adding they planned to review it before the end of the year, with formal submissions encouraged.\par \par \uc2 We updated the policy to ensure we are striking the appropriate balance between protecting freedom of speech and academic freedom, while making sure our campuses are safe and welcoming for all our students and staff, and our core operations can take place without disruption.\par \par \uc2 It comes as the university is under fire on Friday at a Senate inquiry on antisemitism on campus. The Zionist Federation of Australia president, \uc2 Jeremy Leibler\uc2 , told the inquiry the vice-chancellor\u8217\'20\'19s engagement with Jewish and Israeli students was \u8220\'20\'1Cextremely sub-standard\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 That provided anti-Israel protesters with a powerful lesson and a disturbing lesson.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.20am BST\par \par \uc2 The PM was asked why he doesn\u8217\'20\'19t go to New York for the \uc2 UN\uc2  next week, which he hasn\u8217\'20\'19t attended since taking office?\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese said he was coming to the US just for the Quad and had \u8220\'20\'1Cbeen to the overseas meetings that I have to go to.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He listed upcoming meetings he would be attending, including the G20, Chogm \u8211\'20\'13 and hosting \uc2 King Charles\uc2  and Queen Camilla during their visit \u8211\'20\'13 plus the Asean and East Asia summit in Laos.\par \par \uc2 I have prioritised the visits that I have to make and I know I get criticised for not doing more international travel. The irony of that, when compared with some of the coverage of when I do travel, is not lost.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he didn\u8217\'20\'19t view the \uc2 UN\uc2  as important, he said \u8220\'20\'1Cof course it is, but \uc2 Penny Wong\uc2 , our foreign minister, will be attending.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Also asked if he would be meeting with \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , Albanese said \u8220\'20\'1CI am meeting the president of the United States. It is \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  and I will meet with him.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.26am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.15am BST\par \par \uc2 Albanese says alleged Indian spying will \u8216\'20\'18no doubt\u8217\'20\'19 be raised with Modi at Quad meeting in US\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese was asked if he had the chance to speak with \uc2 Narendra Modi\uc2  since the announcement Australia expelled two Indian spies in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/may/01/australia-india-nest-of-spies"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia expelled two Indian intelligence operatives in 2020 as part of \u8216\'20\'18nest of spies\u8217\'20\'19, reports claim}}}\par \par \uc2 The PM was asked, what will you be saying to him to get an ally, a friend, to cease and desist from that kind of spying on Australian soil? He responded:\par \par \uc2 What I do is act diplomatically and have those discussions, and that will no doubt be something that is raised\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 I spoke with prime minister Modi a couple of weeks ago in August \u8230\'20\'26 after his re-election and congratulated him\u8230\'20\'26 I raise issues privately [which is] how we deal with things diplomatically, and I\u8217\'20\'19ll continue to do so.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.22am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.11am BST\par \par \uc2 PM responds to release of footage showing interaction between Chinese and Australian aircrafts in 2022\par \par \uc2 The White House has said China would be high on the agenda during Quad discussions.\par \par \uc2 The PM was asked if he agrees with this, and if the release of footage showing an interaction between a Chinese aircraft and an Australian aircraft was \u8220\'20\'1Cperhaps some sort of preemptive warning to Australia about Chinese strength?\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/18/china-adf-plane-intercept-footage-j-16"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Video of Chinese jet\u8217\'20\'19s apparent intercept with Australian defence plane \u8216\'20\'18deeply troubling\u8217\'20\'19 propaganda, Coalition says}}}\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese responded:\par \par \uc2 We know that in our region there is strategic competition, and of course that is a factor when we look at the way that our region is operating, the relations between the rise of China with nations in our region.\par \par \uc2 But that is not the only focus of course. This is a focus on our four nations, the security and stability that democracies can provide and we want to, with regard to China, my position is very clear. It is that we will cooperate when we can, we will disagree where we must, but we will engage in our national interest.\par \par \uc2 And that is something that has led to an improvement in the relationships with China, and that does not mean there aren\u8217\'20\'19t differences there, there are. We talk about them and we discuss them in appropriate forms and we discuss them very directly.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.08am BST\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese was asked if he is concerned \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would walk away from the Quad alliance, if elected in November.\par \par \uc2 The PM said he was not concerned, because \u8220\'20\'1Cthis is a relationship between our two great nations forged during World War II\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Ever since then, we have relied upon our friends in the United States and they have been able to rely upon us as well.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.11am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.06am BST\par \par \uc2 PM arrives in Philadelphia for Quad leaders meeting\par \par \uc2 Anthony Albanese has arrived in Philadelphia, US, ahead of the Quad leaders meeting, and has been speaking to reporters.\par \par \uc2 The prime minister said the Quad meeting provides \u8220\'20\'1Can opportunity to talk directly to the leaders of the United States in president [Joe] Biden, my friend prime minister [Narendra] Modi from India and my friend the prime minister [ \uc2 Fumio Kishida\uc2 ] of Japan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 We will be discussing ways in which we can provide further support in the region, to developing nations, including the action that we are taking together on climate change and supporting their energy security\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 As I said in my Shangri-La dialogue address last year in Singapore, peace and security do not just happen, you have to work on it. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got to work for stability and that is what the Quad focus will be over the next couple of days.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.10am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.00am BST\par \par \uc2 Qantas\uc2  says teams working to get footy fans to Sydney for the game\par \par \uc2 Qantas\uc2  has just issued a statement following this morning\u8217\'20\'19s cancelled flight from Adelaide to Sydney. A spokesperson said:\par \par \uc2 We understand this disruption is frustrating and apologise to passengers the inconvenience.\par \par \uc2 Our teams have worked hard to get footy fans on their way as soon as possible and all customers impacted are due to arrive into Sydney in time for the game.\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s understood the QF734 flight was cancelled due to an engineering issue, and customers have been reaccommodated onto the earliest possible services \u8211\'20\'13 with the latest customers arriving to Sydney by 6.45pm.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.57am BST\par \par \uc2 More on the flights from Adelaide to Sydney, ahead of tonight\u8217\'20\'19s game:\par \par \uc2 A search of the next flights available with \uc2 Qantas\uc2  shows customers may have to spend thousands to get a seat on board, after the 10am flight was cancelled.\par \par \uc2 An 11.25am flight from Adelaide to Sydney, via Canberra, was listed from $2,207 about 10.50am.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile a midday flight from Adelaide to Melbourne, then Melbourne to Sydney via Jetstar, was listed for $1,329.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.48am BST\par \par \uc2 Adelaide-Sydney flight cancelled ahead of tonight\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 AFL\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight from Adelaide to Sydney has been cancelled at the last minute, ahead of Port Adelaide\u8217\'20\'19s preliminary final against the Swans at the SCG.\par \par \uc2 Flight QF734 from Adelaide to Sydney, set to depart at 10am, has been cancelled. Some people {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/AaronBryans/status/1836913010176315429"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reportedly}}}\uc2  paid over $900 for a seat on board.\par \par \uc2 Another \uc2 Qantas\uc2  flight from Adelaide to Sydney is scheduled for 11am, while a \uc2 Virgin\uc2  flight is scheduled for 11.25am and a Jetstar flight at midday.\par \par \uc2 An \uc2 Adelaide Airport\uc2  spokesperson said it is unsure of the reason for the cancellation and had contacted \uc2 Qantas\uc2  for more information.\par \par \uc2 We have also reached out to \uc2 Qantas\uc2  for comment, and will bring you the latest when we can.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43am BST\par \par \uc2 As we flagged just earlier, a commemorative service has been taking place in Canberra to mark 25 years since Australian troops were first deployed to Timor-Leste.\par \par \uc2 Some photos have begun filtering through from the service:\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.54am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Graham Arnold is to step down as Socceroos manager before Australia\u8217\'20\'19s next World Cup qualifier in October, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 Sources familiar with negotiations told \uc2 AAP\uc2  that Arnold has been in dialogue with Football Australia this week, ironing out an exit strategy from a role he has held since 2018.\par \par \uc2 Arnold\u8217\'20\'19s departure comes after the Socceroos picked up just one point from a possible six in their opening two games of third round World Cup qualifying.\par \par \uc2 Australia fell to Bahrain earlier this month and then were held scoreless in a 0-0 draw with Indonesia.\par \par \uc2 FA declined to comment when contacted by \uc2 AAP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.46am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.21am BST\par \par \uc2 Australia marks 25th anniversary of East Timor service\par \par \uc2 Today marks 25 years since Australian troops were first deployed to Timor-Leste.\par \par \uc2 Around 5,500 Australian service personnel were sent in 1999, the largest deployment of Australian troops since the Vietnam War, after the Timorese voted for independence from Indonesia in 1999.\par \par \uc2 The minister for defence personnel, Matt Keogh, said in a statement:\par \par \uc2 We pay tribute to the six Australians who tragically lost their lives while deployed, or as a result of their service. We also recognise the remarkable resilience of the Timorese people and the enduring friendship between our two nations.\par \par \uc2 We are grateful to the many Australians who served in Timor-Leste who have regularly returned over these 25 years, strengthening the bonds and productivity of local communities.\par \par \uc2 As commemorative services are held all over Australia, we thank all who served and continue to serve to this day.\par \par \uc2 A commemorative service has been taking place at the Australian Peacekeeping Memorial on Anzac Parade in Canberra, where Keogh is delivering an address. We\u8217\'20\'19ll bring you more from this, and photos from the service, as they filter through.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.25am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.13am BST\par \par \uc2 Advocates fear government\u8217\'20\'19s support at home provisions based on misleading data\par \par \uc2 Advocates for older people have expressed fears that the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s support at home provisions in its new aged care bill are based on misleading data, in the wake of a report today from Anglicare.\par \par \uc2 Anglicare Australia surveyed home and community care providers servicing 28,000 older people. All those providers reported being unable to meet the demand for at-home services under the commonwealth home support programme within their community.\par \par \uc2 Describing the current funding model as \u8220\'20\'1Crestrictive and rationed\u8221\'20\'1D, the report also noted, as Guardian Australia has previously reported, that the government\u8217\'20\'19s published wait times for the more substantial home care packages do not include the time it takes for a person to be assessed or reassessed. Current wait times exceed 15 months for some packages.\par \par \uc2 You can read more about the way the home care funding model currently works and why older people and their advocates have been pushing so hard for the new model here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/21/aged-care-in-the-home-improves-lives-and-saves-money-so-why-are-australians-being-made-to-wait"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Aged care in the home improves lives and saves money. So why are Australians being made to wait?}}}\par \par \uc2 In announcing the new aged care act last week, the prime minister, Anthony Albanese, said the new model would reduce wait times for in-home care, with a target of a three-month wait by July 2027.\par \par \uc2 Corey Irlam, acting chief executive of older person\u8217\'20\'19s advocacy organisation Cota, said that benchmark needed to include all aspects of the waiting process, including the wait for assessment.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re not talking about a difference of a week here and there \u8211\'20\'13 we\u8217\'20\'19re talking about many, many months of an older person\u8217\'20\'19s life. With reports of around 10,000 people dying a year on the waitlist, sadly that\u8217\'20\'19s often time people don\u8217\'20\'19t have.\par \par \uc2 Not only will we be pushing the government to ensure it\u8217\'20\'19s not fudging the figures on how long people are actually waiting for care, but we\u8217\'20\'19ll also be calling on the Parliament to ensure the new Aged Care Act has a legal requirement for Government to publish a full waitlist report making the entire waiting journey transparent from application until your aged care services commence.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.24am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.03am BST\par \par \uc2 Myer\uc2  profit falls as brands underperform\par \par \uc2 Department store chain \uc2 Myer\uc2  has suffered a steep fall in full-year profit, with the result weighed down by weak sales in underperforming brands Sass & Bide, Marcs and David Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 Myer\uc2  posted a $52.6m net profit result for 2023-24, according to results released today, down 26% from a year earlier. Sales also fell, dragged lower by store closures.\par \par \uc2 The company attributed the fall in profit to challenging trading conditions, inflationary cost pressures and underperformance of various fashion labels.\par \par \uc2 Myer\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s executive chair, \uc2 Olivia Wirth\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s result reflects the challenging macroeconomic environment for Australian retailers.\par \par \uc2 The recent earnings season showed that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/24/from-cars-to-coffee-machines-heres-how-australian-spending-habits-are-weathering-the-high-cost-of-living"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  consumers were reducing their spending on many discretionary items}}}\uc2 , with furniture and clothing retailers among the most affected.\par \par \uc2 Myer\uc2  disclosed that about half of its decline in net profit was attributable to the underperformance of brands Sass & Bide, Marcs and David Lawrence.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.58am BST\par \par \uc2 The NSW government says it will accept a recommendation put forward by the Industrial Relations Commission as pay negotiations continue with the Nurses and Midwives\u8217\'20\'19 Association.\par \par \uc2 Nurses and midwives walked off the job across the state for 12 hours earlier this month, after demands for a 15% pay rise this year were rebuffed.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/sep/10/nurses-midwives-strike-nsw-hospitals-pay-rise-premier-chris-minns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 NSW nurses and midwives strike: hospital wait times up and surgery delays expected}}}\par \par \uc2 In a statement this morning, NSW health minister Ryan Park said the IRC had recommended both parties enter four weeks of intensive discussions.\par \par \uc2 The IRC recommends \uc2 NSW Health\uc2  pay nurses an interim 3% increase, back-paid to 1 July this year, contingent on the industrial action ceasing. Park said:\par \par \uc2 The Government will accept this, and has notified the Association of its intention to do so, on the basis the Association also agrees to the recommendation.\par \par \uc2 Park said this is \u8220\'20\'1Can opportunity for nurses to receive increased pay and patients to continue to receive care while the broader dispute is in the process of being resolved by the parties.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.36am BST\par \par \uc2 Lithium-ion batteries \u8216\'20\'18fastest growing fire risk\u8217\'20\'19 in NSW, says Fire and Rescue\par \par \uc2 Fire and Rescue NSW\uc2  says that lithium-ion batteries are the \u8220\'20\'1Cfastest growing fire risk\u8221\'20\'1D in the state.\par \par \uc2 Since 1 January this year, FRNSW says it has attended more than 210 lithium-ion battery fires where people were injured and property severely damaged, with two fatalities.\par \par \uc2 This comes as the firefighting agency says it recorded 12 preventable residential fire deaths across NSW this winter \u8211\'20\'13 an increase of four on last year\u8217\'20\'19s fatality rate.\par \par \uc2 There were 1,001 residential fires and 105 people injured this winter according to FRNSW, who released the following data:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 In 422 cases, fires broke out in kitchens, 95 in the bedroom, 79 in lounge rooms and 51 in chimneys.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The leading cause of residential fires was human activity (494) followed by arson (138), undetermined causes (123), electrical faults (77) and overheating (40).\par \par \uc2 FRNSW commissioner Jeremy Fewtrell said the statistics reinforce the importance of having a working smoke alarm:\par \par \uc2 In almost half of all fires we attend each winter, there is no smoke alarm or it is not in working order. These figures are very sobering and should be a wake-up call for people to take home fire safety seriously.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.55am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.28am BST\par \par \uc2 Senior constable charged with allegedly supplying prohibited drugs\par \par \uc2 A police officer has been charged with allegedly supplying prohibited drugs in southern New South Wales, police said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Following extensive inquiries, a search warrant was executed at a home in Wagga Wagga yesterday where a number of items were seized for forensic examination.\par \par \uc2 A 36-year-old male senior constable \u8211\'20\'13 attached to a command within the southern region \u8211\'20\'13 was arrested and taken to Wagga Wagga police station. He was charged with three counts of supplying prohibited drugs and knowingly dealing in proceeds of crime.\par \par \uc2 The man was granted conditional bail to appear before Wagga Wagga local court on 13 November. The man is suspended with pay and his employment status is under review.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.18am BST\par \par \uc2 Jewish students being singled out, spat on and \u8216\'20\'18fearing for their safety\u8217\'20\'19, inquiry told\par \par \uc2 Jewish students are being singled out, spat on, removing identifiable symbols and \u8220\'20\'1Cfearing for their safety\u8221\'20\'1D, a Senate inquiry has heard.\par \par \uc2 President of the Australasian Union of Jewish Students (AUJS), Noah Loven, told the inquiry into antisemitism on campuses that the current situation was \u8220\'20\'1Cuntenable\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We are in a crisis and this crisis has gone on for far too long. Some have stepped up but others have allowed this hate to fester \u8230\'20\'26 letting Jewish students fend for themselves.\par \par \uc2 Loven said not all criticism of Israel was antisemitic but \u8220\'20\'1Csome undeniably crosses the line\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We fully support the right to protest but when protests glorify violence against Jews, insult or harass them, deface campuses with swastikas, it crosses a line.\par \par \uc2 Assoc Prof Efrat Eilam, from the Australian Academic Alliance against Antisemitism, said staff and students were being subjugated to a \u8220\'20\'1CJewish cancel culture\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The two bodies have backed a judicial inquiry, with the AUJS noting the situation had become \u8220\'20\'1Cpoliticised\u8221\'20\'1D and a unified response was necessary to draw a line.\par \par \uc2 The inquiry has received more than 600 submissions, with some Jewish community members citing antisemitism and others noting heightened levels of Islamophobia and expressing concern over conflation with antisemitism and criticism of Israel.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.58am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.07am BST\par \par \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  one of 'worst places' to be Jewish student, Senate inquiry told\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 University of Sydney\uc2  is one of the \u8220\'20\'1Cworst places\u8221\'20\'1D to be a Jewish student, a Senate inquiry has heard.\par \par \uc2 Asked about management\u8217\'20\'19s response to an ongoing pro-Palestine encampment, national vice-president of the Australasian Union of Jewish Students, Zachary Morris, said the university was \u8220\'20\'1Cone of the worst places\u8221\'20\'1D to be a Jewish student, adding the vice-chancellor had \u8220\'20\'1Cfailed his students\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We have tried repeatedly to engage, after the first meeting his chief of staff had to apologise for his behaviour as soon as he left the office \u8230\'20\'26 The issue is no longer with what is happening with students, the issue is with how the administration has responded.\par \par \uc2 If it was just about flags we wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t be here \u8230\'20\'26 there\u8217\'20\'19s a marked reduction in attendance.\par \par \uc2 Morris cited graffiti of swastikas in a tunnel left for two weeks, students being filmed and receiving death threats and academics \u8220\'20\'1Cterrified to leave their offices\u8221\'20\'1D. In June, the university instructed protestors to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/jun/18/university-of-sydney-pro-palestine-camp-told-to-leave-israel-gaza-war"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disband their encampment}}}\uc2 , and have since enacted a strict Campus Access Policy requiring additional approval for some protests.\par \par \uc2 Jewish groups and vice-chancellors will be among those to appear at the Senate inquiry today into shadow education minister Sarah Henderson\u8217\'20\'19s private senator\u8217\'20\'19s bill to establish a judicial inquiry into antisemitism on campuses.\par \par \uc2 Henderson said there had been a \u8220\'20\'1Cfailure of leadership\u8221\'20\'1D from vice-chancellors who had allowed \u8220\'20\'1Chate and incitement to run rampant over many months\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.32am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.01am BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s a look at today\u8217\'20\'19s weather forecasts across the country, thanks to the Bureau of Meteorology :\par \par \uc2 enltrHere's today's forecast around Australia.\par \par \uc2 For the latest forecasts and warnings, visit our website {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/jlOoTZL1iF"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/jlOoTZL1iF}}}\uc2  or the BOM Weather app. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/WnRbjMWvCB"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/WnRbjMWvCB}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bureau of Meteorology, Australia (@BOM_au) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BOM_au/status/1836872484391411927?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 19, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 Portable wi-fi stations to support disaster-hit communities in NSW\par \par \uc2 The NSW government is rolling out portable tech to deliver back-up internet connectivity during natural disasters, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports, so communities and emergency responders can stay connected.\par \par \uc2 The technology includes trailer-mounted, solar-powered satellite internet stations that can provide wi-fi coverage to a roughly 500 metre radius. There are four portable connectivity units on the way that can be combined to cover a larger area.\par \par \uc2 The units will also be available in Service NSW disaster recovery centres, alongside a satellite-equipped vehicle so the agency can set up temporary offices with internet access.\par \par \uc2 The supplied connectivity will also be handy for contacting family members, applying for government support and lodging insurance claims after disaster events.\par \par \uc2 The government is investing about $5m in the new technology, which will be delivered this summer.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.09am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rishworth questioned on calls for total ban on gambling advertising\par \par \uc2 Moving to calls for a total ban on gambling advertising, Amanda Rishworth was asked why the government won\u8217\'20\'19t accept this recommendation.\par \par \uc2 She said the government has \u8220\'20\'1Cintroduced bet stop [and] activity statements\u8221\'20\'1D, so are \u8220\'20\'1Clooking at the evidence of what actually makes a difference\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Now, when it comes to the late \uc2 Peta Murphy\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s report, we\u8217\'20\'19re of course working through those recommendations. 21 of those recommendations have really significant interactions or require co-governance with states and territories, and I\u8217\'20\'19m working with my state and territory colleagues about how we progress many of these recommendations.\par \par \uc2 So, this is a joint effort. But to suggest we haven\u8217\'20\'19t been doing anything is just not right. We have taken the most significant action.\par \par \uc2 What about comments from health experts that banning gambling ads would go further to tackle the scourge of problem gambling and help families? Rishworth said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe\u8217\'20\'19re looking at that recommendation very closely\u8221\'20\'1D and repeated that she was working with state and territory colleagues.\par \par \uc2 Asked when a decision on this is expected, she repeated a similar answer:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re working through all the issues. I continue to talk with my state and territory colleagues, and we\u8217\'20\'19ll keep working on this.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.07am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Rishworth defends jobseeker increase\par \par \uc2 Asked if the government should do more to increase JobSeeker \u8211\'20\'13 going up roughly a dollar to $56 a day, still well below the Henderson poverty line \u8211\'20\'13 Amanda Rishworth said:\par \par \uc2 In terms of those on the lowest incomes, we\u8217\'20\'19re very focused on supporting those people. Many people on JobSeeker will also get Commonwealth rent assistance. They will also benefit from our freeze to medicines, will benefit two years in a row from our $300 discount on energy bills.\par \par \uc2 So, these are a suite of measures. We\u8217\'20\'19ll always look at where we can better support people. Part of that is making sure that we do every budget look at what we can budget for. At the same time as making sure we\u8217\'20\'19re not adding to the inflation challenge. That\u8217\'20\'19s the balance we\u8217\'20\'19re doing.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.04am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Amanda Rishworth questioned on increase to rent assistance\par \par \uc2 The minister for social services, Amanda Rishworth, was also on ABC News Breakfast to discuss the increase to jobseeker and rent assistance from today.\par \par \uc2 She was asked about those comments from Dr \uc2 Cassandra Goldie\uc2  (see {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66eca28d8f081d8429b1c3bd#block-66eca28d8f081d8429b1c3bd"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 earlier}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2024/sep/20/howard-trump-democracy-rba-interest-rates-economy-real-estate-politics-albanese-dutton-labor-liberal-coalition-housing-weather-nsw-vic-qld-ntwnfb?CMP=share_btn_url&page=with%3Ablock-66eca3a28f08098f3f162d17#block-66eca3a28f08098f3f162d17"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 posts}}}\uc2  ) that while extra money is welcome for people receiving government payments, rent increases for a single person is only $1.64 a day.\par \par \uc2 Is that enough? Rishworth responded:\par \par \uc2 Obviously it\u8217\'20\'19s a 12% increase in the support of rent assistance on those maximum rates. But since we\u8217\'20\'19ve had come to government, people have had $2,000 on average extra in rent assistance for those receiving commonwealth rent assistance.\par \par \uc2 The minister said this \u8220\'20\'1Chelped to drive down rents across the board\u8221\'20\'1D and is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot the only measure\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Free weekend trains in Sydney in bid to support football finals amid industrial action\par \par \uc2 Sydney\u8217\'20\'19s business community is pleading with rail workers to call off industrial action ahead of meetings to confirm travel arrangements for this weekend\u8217\'20\'19s footy finals, \uc2 AAP\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 The Rail, Tram and Bus Union failed to reach a breakthrough in emergency talks with the NSW government and threatened to not provide the extra services needed for a marquee weekend of sport.\par \par \uc2 In Sydney there are NRL and \uc2 AFL\uc2  finals \u8211\'20\'13 including the Swans\u8217\'20\'19 sold-out game against Port Adelaide at the SCG \u8211\'20\'13 and the Bledisloe Cup rugby union fixture.\par \par \uc2 The state\u8217\'20\'19s transport minister Jo Haylen says trains will be free on the weekend, a concession to the union hoped to allow for timetable changes so special event services can run. She told ABC Radio today:\par \par \uc2 One of the bans that the union has put in place is around altering the timetable, so that means no ability for Sydney Trains to run those special event services.\par \par \uc2 [The union and Sydney Trains] are meeting now and we\u8217\'20\'19re hopeful that given we have opened the gates \u8230\'20\'26 that we will be able to insert those special event services to get people out to Sydney Olympic Park.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/19/sydney-commuter-delays-and-shutdown-of-t3-line-possible-if-nsw-fails-to-reach-agreement-with-transport-union"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Sydney train delays and shutdown of T3 line possible if NSW fails to reach agreement with transport union}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 Acoss\uc2  wants jobseeker lifted to same rate as pension\par \par \uc2 Q : I know you\u8217\'20\'19ve been pressuring the government on the jobseeker front for many years now. What ideally would you like to see the figure increased to, per week?\par \par \uc2 Acoss\uc2  CEO Dr \uc2 Cassandra Goldie\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 We have consistently said that payment needs to go up to the same rate as the pension, so it would go from $56 per day now to $82 per day.\par \par \uc2 This is a modest increase. It was recommended that we get a substantial increase to jobseeker \u8230\'20\'26 repeatedly by the independent Economic Inclusion Advisory Committee which was created by the Labor government.\par \par \uc2 Very generous tax cuts were delivered for people on higher incomes, and here we are today with again a couple of dollars extra for people who are on the pension and jobseeker \u8211\'20\'13 just $1 extra per day to cover very serious increases in costs.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.20pm BST\par \par \uc2 Jobseeker\u8217\'20\'19s $1 a day increase \u8216\'20\'18nowhere near what it needs to be\u8217\'20\'19, \uc2 Acoss\uc2  CEO says\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of the \uc2 Australian Council of Social Service\uc2 , Dr \uc2 Cassandra Goldie\uc2 , spoke with ABC News Breakfast earlier as the government\u8217\'20\'19s promised 10% increase to commonwealth rent assistance begins today.\par \par \uc2 Goldie said that jobseeker payments will go from $55 per day to $56 per day, and for single pensioners it will go from $80 per day to $82 per day. The commonwealth rent assistance would go from $94 per week to $105 per week, she added.\par \par \uc2 I think people can see that every dollar does make a difference, but these are not big dollars \u8211\'20\'13 and these are coming off the back of poverty payments already.\par \par \uc2 Jobseeker is nowhere near what it needs to be as the unemployment payment to help people out there trying to cover those big rent increases, those ongoing increases in food prices and of course, medicines and other essentials.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.36pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Shorten skirts question on whether \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is compatible with democracy\par \par \uc2 As the wide-ranging interview wrapped up, \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  was also asked about comments from former PM \uc2 John Howard\uc2  that \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  is \u8220\'20\'1Cnot compatible with democracy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Shorten said Howard is \u8220\'20\'1Centitled his opinion\u8221\'20\'1D but the comments were \u8220\'20\'1Ca pretty interesting development [and] I think his party should listen carefully to what he says\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Asked if Trump is incompatible with democracy, as Howard suggested, Shorten said he currently serves in the government who \u8220\'20\'1Cwill work with whoever America produces [via] their electoral processes\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 But what does Shorten, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/sep/05/bill-shorten-retirement-quit-politics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the soon-to-be vice-chancellor}}}\uc2 , think? He said:\par \par \uc2 Get back to me on the first of February \u8230\'20\'26 or sometime in February on that one.\par \par \uc2 But I do think that the January 6 riots were shocking. I do think it\u8217\'20\'19s a matter of record that more could have been done by the outgoing president. But you know, \uc2 John Howard\uc2  has got the freedom of not being in politics, and I have the privilege of serving in a cabinet.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.32pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.07pm BST\par \par \uc2 Shorten questioned on role of group homes moving forward with NDIS\par \par \uc2 Moving to the NDIS, \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  was asked how the government is progressing with recommendations to phase out segregated employment by 2034 and group homes within 15 years \u8211\'20\'13 which the government said needed further consideration.\par \par \uc2 He responded:\par \par \uc2 I think we\u8217\'20\'19re capable of providing independence, innovation and the accommodation options for people with profound and severe disabilities, which will ultimately make the need for sort of group homes \u8230\'20\'26 not so necessary. But it\u8217\'20\'19s going to take a lot of work to get that right.\par \par \uc2 See, a lot of the housing we use is sort of what you call was the legacy housing \u8211\'20\'13 the states might have built it in the 50s and 60s, and they passed that over to the NDIS accommodation. Sometimes we\u8217\'20\'19re fitting people around old houses rather than working out what new tech could mean for innovation, independence for people.\par \par \uc2 It is a complex task, and it\u8217\'20\'19s going to be a multiple election cycle task, but we are now as a nation investing the money in it, which wasn\u8217\'20\'19t always the case.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Asked about \uc2 UN\uc2  vote, Shorten says \u8216\'20\'18mainstream Australia\u8217\'20\'19 wants comprehensive ceasefire deal\par \par \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  was also asked to respond to comments from opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2 , that the government is damaging its relationship with the US and Israel, after Australia decided to abstain from a \uc2 UN\uc2  motion calling for a withdrawal from Gaza and the West Bank.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, deputy leader of the Greens Mehreen Faruqi said Labor had \u8220\'20\'1Cshown itself to be gutless fence-sitters\u8221\'20\'1D and voters would \u8220\'20\'1Cnot forgive and forget\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/19/australia-abstains-from-un-vote-on-occupation-of-palestine-after-disappointment-with-resolutions-scope"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Albanese government called \u8216\'20\'18gutless\u8217\'20\'19 after sitting out UN vote on Israeli occupation of Palestinian territories}}}\par \par \uc2 Shorten said Dutton\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cjob is to just oppose things that the government are doing\u8221\'20\'1D and defended the government\u8217\'20\'19s position:\par \par \uc2 The fact that we\u8217\'20\'19re pressing for a ceasefire, that we want increased humanitarian access \u8211\'20\'13 so we want the release of hostages, which we\u8217\'20\'19ve been very consistent on and that we want to prevent regional escalation \u8211\'20\'13 is the right way to go.\par \par \uc2 In opposition [it\u8217\'20\'19s] easy just to throw rocks, but when you\u8217\'20\'19re in government you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to take into account all the perspectives and the long-term interests. And I think that the fact we\u8217\'20\'19re supporting a comprehensive ceasefire deal is actually where mainstream Australia is.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Q : The Fed has cut rates by half a percentage point. Should the \uc2 RBA\uc2  be looking to do the same?\par \par \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  says \u8220\'20\'1Cthe Fed is different to the \uc2 RBA\uc2 \u8221\'20\'1D and while the \uc2 RBA\uc2  is independent \u8220\'20\'1Cthat doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean that they\u8217\'20\'19re immune to getting advice from other people or hearing different points of view\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 I think it is significant that the Fed has decided to lower interest rates in America by half a per cent, but our economies are not identical. So how long that takes to flow through and the impact that has remains to be seen.\par \par \uc2 I noticed that our dollar rallied on the basis that the Fed was cutting rates and of course, with a rallying dollar, it means \u8230\'20\'26 it takes some pressure, inflationary pressure [off] us.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.55pm BST\par \par \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  was asked if the Greens had outperformed the government, at least in the messaging on housing.\par \par \uc2 He told host Patricia Karvelas :\par \par \uc2 The reality is most Australians see through the Greens.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve got cost of living pressures, which are absolutely real. With the Reserve Bank increasing interest rates 13 times, what you\u8217\'20\'19ve got is mortgage holders under pressure [and] what you\u8217\'20\'19ve got is renters under pressure, and it is a challenge. And that is why we\u8217\'20\'19re trying to increase, through various mechanisms, the amount of supply of housing in this country.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.59pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Shorten calls Greens \u8216\'20\'18formidable and destructive\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 The Labor MP \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  has also been speaking with ABC RN and has been scathing of the Greens \u8211\'20\'13 after a bitter housing standoff this week. Josh Butler has an full explainer on this below:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/19/housing-bill-explained-whats-at-the-heart-of-the-bitter-standoff-between-labor-greens-and-coalition"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Housing bill explained: what\u8217\'20\'19s at the heart of the bitter standoff between Labor, Greens and Coalition?}}}\par \par \uc2 Shorten described the Greens as a \u8220\'20\'1Cformidable and destructive political part of Australian life\u8221\'20\'1D and said:\par \par \uc2 Labor and the Liberals, whatever you think of us or the Liberals, we seek to form governments in Australia, and when you form a government, that means you\u8217\'20\'19ve actually got to get things done. The Greens are playing a sort of competition \u8230\'20\'26 a party in protest or an outrage factory so they can [be all] things to all people, because they only have to implement their policies.\par \par \uc2 So they play by different set of rules, and what they do is they create anxiety. They create anxiety for people who might want to buy their first home. They create anxiety for our NDIS reforms.\par \par \uc2 They think that [they are morally] superior to people who disagree with them, and I found in political life, [just] because someone disagrees with you doesn\u8217\'20\'19t make them morally inferior, they just have a different proposition or set of values.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dutton responds to modelling showing power bills could rise under nuclear plan\par \par \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  was questioned on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/20/coalition-nuclear-power-plan-will-add-665-dollars-to-average-power-bill-a-year-report-warns"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 new modelling showing power bills could rise by about $665 a year under the Coalition plan to build nuclear plants}}}\uc2  around Australia.\par \par \uc2 He responded by arguing that power bills had gone up under Labor, and decrying a loss of baseload power under renewables.\par \par \uc2 However, Ameo last month said that Australia\u8217\'20\'19s main power grid would remain reliable as it shifts from coal domination to running overwhelmingly on renewable energy \u8211\'20\'13 but only if investments in new generation are delivered \u8220\'20\'1Con time and in full\u8221\'20\'1D. Adam Morton had the full story:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/29/australias-electricity-grid-to-remain-reliable-if-renewable-projects-delivered-on-time-and-in-full-aemo-says"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s electricity grid to remain reliable if renewable projects delivered \u8216\'20\'18on time and in full\u8217\'20\'19, Aemo says}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Shorten and Dutton respond to latest job figures\par \par \uc2 The outgoing NDIS minister \uc2 Bill Shorten\uc2  and opposition leader \uc2 Peter Dutton\uc2  made their regular Friday morning appearance on the Today show just earlier.\par \par \uc2 Shorten was asked about the latest job figures, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/sep/19/australias-unemployment-rate-remained-flat-in-august-dampening-hopes-of-rba-interest-rate-cut"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 released yesterday}}}\uc2 , which dampened hopes of an interest rate cut. He said:\par \par \uc2 Like everyone I hope to see downward pressure on interest rates, although I think it is good that we\u8217\'20\'19re seeing such strong job numbers (with 47,500 jobs added last month).\par \par \uc2 I mean, when you see that under this government nearly a million new jobs are being created, that\u8217\'20\'19s got to be good news. The majority of them are full-time jobs, and most of them are going to women.\par \par \uc2 Dutton responded that people are picking up second and third jobs and working more hours, so \u8220\'20\'1Cbusinesses are more reluctant to put people on full time, which is why we see full time numbers come back under this latest announcement\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.24pm BST\par \par \uc2 AMA urging stop to \u8216\'20\'18coercive and deceptive\u8217\'20\'19 marketing of commercial foods for infants\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Australian Medical Association\uc2  is calling on the government to implement best practice standards for nutrition in commercial foods for infants and young children.\par \par \uc2 As part of its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.ama.com.au/articles/ama-submission-improving-commercial-food-infants-and-young-children"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 submission}}}\uc2  to the Department of Health and Aged Care\u8217\'20\'19s public consultation, the AMA is urging the government to put a stop to what it says is coercive and deceptive marketing of products.\par \par \uc2 This comes as a study found not a single infant or toddler food product stocked in Australian supermarkets meets standards set by the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/world-health-organization"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 World Health Organization}}}\uc2 . Natasha May had the full story last month:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/13/no-baby-or-toddler-foods-in-australian-supermarkets-meet-who-standards-study-finds"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 No baby or toddler foods in Australian supermarkets meet WHO standards, study finds}}}\par \par \uc2 President Professor \uc2 Steve Robson\uc2  said parents and guardians should be able to rely on the government to ensure commercial foods are labelled and marketed responsibly:\par \par \uc2 Many commercial foods for infants and young children fail to support optimal health, growth and development. The nutritional content of many of these products is inadequate and they fall short of international standards for labelling and promotion.\par \par \uc2 Parents and guardians are being bombarded by coercive marketing but do not have enough information about the nutritional quality of the commercial foods being sold to them to be able to make informed choices.\par \par \uc2 To protect our youngest Australians, comprehensive changes to the composition, texture, and labelling of commercial foods for infants and young children are imperative.\par \par \uc2 The AMA says the changes must be mandatory, and compliance must be strictly monitored and enforced.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.16pm BST\par \par \uc2 Australia\u8217\'20\'19s \u8216\'20\'18sex report card\u8217\'20\'19 released\par \par \uc2 The latest Australian Study of Health and Relationships was revealed at a conference in Sydney this week held by the International Union Against Sexually Transmitted Infections.\par \par \uc2 It doesn\u8217\'20\'19t sound much fun but the results are basically our sex report card and make for fascinating reading.\par \par \uc2 They show, for example, that one third of Australians have searched for a partner online. Natasha May has the full story here:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/lifeandstyle/2024/sep/20/andrew-and-josella-met-online-its-now-the-norm-for-more-than-half-of-young-australians"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Andrew and Josella met online \u8211\'20\'13 it\u8217\'20\'19s now the norm for more than half of young Australians}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.11pm BST\par \par \uc2 Welfare advocates warn rent assistance rise will do little amid housing crisis\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government\u8217\'20\'19s promised 10% increase to commonwealth rent assistance begins today, but welfare advocates warn it will do little to help.\par \par \uc2 The changes will come in line with indexation, which varies based on the payment. Single recipients receiving the maximum rate of rent assistance will get an extra $23 per fortnight If they are renting on their own. Families with one or two children get an extra $27.02. But welfare advocates said it will do little help in the midst of a housing crisis.\par \par \uc2 Antipoverty Centre spokesperson Kristin O\u8217\'20\'19Connell said:\par \par \uc2 Rent assistance went up by $25 and \u8230\'20\'26 average rents have gone up by more than $100. What might look like a big percentage increase is, frankly, fuck all, and that\u8217\'20\'19s one of the reasons that this is so upsetting.\par \par \uc2 The latest Quarterly Rental Review Report showed the median weekly rent value across all Australian dwellings hit a fresh record high of $627 per week, and rental costs range from $770 per week in Sydney, to $547 in Hobart.\par \par \uc2 A major issue with rent assistance is that around 300,000 people who receive it actually lose money every six months when it is indexed, O\u8217\'20\'19Connell said:\par \par \uc2 When CRA is indexed, the amount of rent that you have to pay before you get any rent assistance increases. So the proportion of your rent, where you qualify for it, reduces if you aren\u8217\'20\'19t receiving the maximum payment.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.45pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s housing crisis worsening on nearly every key indicator, index shows\par \par \uc2 Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s housing crisis has worsened on nearly every key indicator one year since the state government\u8217\'20\'19s housing statement, which set to tackle affordability by building 800,000 new homes and increasing renters rights.\par \par \uc2 The Council to Homeless Persons\u8217\'20\'19 new Housing Insecurity Index shows:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Annual rents rose 13.3% across the state, with a 14.6% increase in Melbourne, and 5.3% in regional areas.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The public housing waiting list is now at 61,587.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The number of people who received support from specialist homelessness services in Victoria increased from 30,510 in July 2017 to 32,496 in June 2024.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The number of people accessing specialist homelessness services because of housing stress has been above 10,000 for every month since January 2023.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Proportion of social housing in Victoria\u8217\'20\'19s total housing stock is just 2.8% \u8211\'20\'13 the lowest in Australia.\par \par \uc2 CHP chief executive Deborah Di Natale says the index shows the state government must show more ambition:\par \par \uc2 Things have actually gotten worse for Victorians at the brink of homelessness since the government unveiled its Housing Statement last year.\par \par \uc2 She called on the government to commit to building at least 6000 new public and community homes each year for a decade.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.03pm BST\par \par \uc2 Good morning\par \par \uc2 And happy Friday \u8211\'20\'13 thanks to Martin for kicking things off. I\u8217\'20\'19m Emily Wind, and I\u8217\'20\'19ll be with you on the live blog for most of today.\par \par \uc2 As always, you can reach out with any tips or questions via X, @emilywindwrites, or you can send me an email: emily.wind@theguardian.com.\par \par \uc2 Let\u8217\'20\'19s get started.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.56pm BST\par \par \uc2 Federal Labor announce boost to rent assistance\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government has announced a range of measures to combat cost of living pressures, including what it claims is the the first back-to-back increase to commonwealth rent assistance ever and the indexation of pensions and payments.\par \par \uc2 In a joint statement today, treasurer \uc2 Jim Chalmers\uc2  and minister for social services Amanda Rishworth said \u8220\'20\'1Cwe recognise that people are under the pump and we\u8217\'20\'19re doing something about it\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re already rolling out tax cuts and energy rebates and from today we\u8217\'20\'19ll give people more help to pay the rent and deliver an increase to pensions and payments including the age pension.\par \par \uc2 Some of the measures include:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Increasing rent assistance by 10% plus indexation, which they claim will benefit nearly a million households around the country.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The latest round of payment indexation including for the age pension, disability support pension, jobseeker, carer payment and parenting payment, set to benefit more than five million households.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Jobseeker recipients with an assessed partial capacity to work of less than 15 hours per week will move to the higher rate, receiving an increase of $71.20 a fortnight combined with indexation.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Income limits for the commonwealth seniors health card will also increase to $99,025 for singles and $158,440 for couples (combined).\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.50pm BST\par \par \uc2 Job figures could lead to longer wait for rate relief\par \par \uc2 Mortgage holders may have to wait longer for an interest rate reprieve, following stronger than expected jobs figures, reports \uc2 AAP\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  has shifted its forecast for when the Reserve Bank will lower the official cash rate, now saying a 0.25 percentage point reduction will happen in December, as opposed to previous predictions of a November rate cut.\par \par \uc2 The revised forecast followed the unemployment rate staying at 4.2% for August, with the number of jobs added to the market almost double what economists had predicted.\par \par \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s head of Australian economics \uc2 Gareth Aird\uc2  said:\par \par \uc2 The recent strength in employment growth coupled with still relatively hawkish rhetoric from the \uc2 RBA\uc2  governor means we now see December as the more likely month for the start of normalising the cash rate.\par \par \uc2 We expect the \uc2 RBA\uc2  will commence an easing cycle before it declares we have hit full employment given policy is currently restrictive.\par \par \uc2 Not all the ducks have lined up for a November rate cut.\par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank will meet on Tuesday to determine whether to cut the official cash rate of 4.35% or leave it on hold.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.44pm BST\par \par \uc2 Lambie says \uc2 Rex Patrick\uc2  will lead her federal Senate ticket in SA\par \par \uc2 Tasmanian senator \uc2 Jacqui Lambie\uc2  has announced her former upper house colleague \uc2 Rex Patrick\uc2  will lead the minor party\u8217\'20\'19s ticket in South Australia in the upcoming federal election.\par \par \uc2 In a statement on Friday morning, Lambie said she shared the same values as Patrick, who was a senator between 2017 and 2022, and Australia needed him back in Canberra. Lambie said:\par \par \uc2 Rex\uc2  is known as the \u8216\'20\'18Transparency Warrior\u8217\'20\'19 for good reason, he works hard to hold the government accountable. He should be in the Senate where he can continue to represent the interests of everyday Australians.\par \par \uc2 Rex\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s departure from the Senate was a loss for all of us, but particularly South Australia. We need to get \uc2 Rex\uc2  back into the Senate so SA once again has a sensible voice representing them in Canberra.\par \par \uc2 Since leaving the federal parliament at the last election, Patrick has ruffled feathers in Canberra for his work as an avid freedom of information campaigner.\par \par \uc2 One of his most recent fights in \uc2 the Administrative Appeals Tribunal\uc2  is t {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/14/rex-patrick-labor-foi-freedom-of-information-documents-secret-albanese"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 o prevent a common practice of shredding documents}}}\uc2  once a minister leaves office. The act prevents attempts at successfully attaining documents through FoI requests.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/article/2024/aug/14/rex-patrick-labor-foi-freedom-of-information-documents-secret-albanese"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Labor urges court to bin obligations so it can keep documents secret in event of ministry changes}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.15pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 Howard says US political system \u8216\'20\'18far inferior\u8217\'20\'19 to Australia\u8217\'20\'19s\par \par \uc2 In his interview with Nine, \uc2 John Howard\uc2  said political events seen in the US showed its system had major deficiencies compared to Australia\u8217\'20\'19s.\par \par \uc2 Everything we\u8217\'20\'19re witnessing in America tells us is that their political system is far inferior to ours.\par \par \uc2 In a parliamentary system, \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2  would never have got to the leadership of the Republicans, and I don\u8217\'20\'19t think \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  would ever have got to the leadership of the Democrats.\par \par \uc2 We wouldn\u8217\'20\'19t have gone through the agony the Democrats went through regarding \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They have a different system. And my take, my very strong view, is that a parliamentary system is much better than a presidential system.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.41pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 John Howard\uc2  says \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's behaviour 'not compatible with democracy'\par \par \uc2 Former Liberal prime minister \uc2 John Howard\uc2  has taken aim at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , describing him as \u8220\'20\'1Cnot compatible with democracy\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Howard, who took Australia into Afghanistan and the Iraq war alongside the United States during his time in office, said the Republican candidate\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to accept the result of the 2020 election meant he would not want Trump to win this time.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.9news.com.au/national/john-howard-says-he-could-not-vote-for-donald-trump-exclusive-interview/886b545d-5bdd-47d3-aa5f-fe7ac4633760"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Howard said in an interview with Nine News}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 In normal circumstances, I would unhesitatingly favour a Republican victory, but there are reasons that prevent me doing that on this occasion.\par \par \uc2 In other words, I think his refusal to accept the result of the last election and various attempts to overturn that result not compatible with democracy.\par \par \uc2 When you play the democratic game, you\u8217\'20\'19ve got to accept the democratic result\u8230\'20\'26 The choice the American people must make is a difficult choice - I\u8217\'20\'19m glad I don\u8217\'20\'19t have a vote there, because I think it would be very hard.\par \par \uc2 I\u8217\'20\'19m not impressed with \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . I don\u8217\'20\'19t think she\u8217\'20\'19s got a very good idea of how to run the American economy.\par \par \uc2 I worry that she might lead an administration that will spend far too much, and some of that expenditure will be irresponsible... she leads a party that, generally speaking, favours far too much government intervention for my liking.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.23pm BST\par \par \uc2 Welcome\par \par \uc2 Good morning and welcome to our Friday rolling news blog. Emily Wind will be along soon to bring you all the breaking stories of the day but before that this is Martin Farrer with the best of the overnight stories.\par \par \uc2 After a week in which a Guardian Australia investigation has revealed the huge profits being made by online real estate portals from charging sellers, the former chair of Australia\u8217\'20\'19s competition law enforcer says he believes the regulator should consider investigating the behaviour of the market\u8217\'20\'19s leading portal, realestate.com.au, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/2024/sep/20/rod-sims-says-worth-considering-whether-realestatecomau-engaged-in-anti-competitive-practices-as-greens-blast-rigged-system"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 for potential anti-competitive behaviour.}}}\uc2  Rod Sims told us the behaviour of realestate.com.au was \u8220\'20\'1Cwell worth considering under section 45 of the Competition and Consumer Act\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Former Liberal prime minister \uc2 John Howard\uc2  has taken aim at \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , describing him as \u8220\'20\'1Cnot compatible with democracy\u8221\'20\'1D. Howard, who took Australia into Afghanistan and the Iraq war alongside the United States during his time in office, said the Republican candidate\u8217\'20\'19s refusal to accept the result of the 2020 election meant he would not want Trump to win this time. More on this soon.\par \par \uc2 The Albanese government has announced a range of measures to combat cost of living pressures, including what it claims is the the first back-to-back increase to Commonwealth rent assistance ever and the indexation of pensions and payments.\par \par \uc2 In less good news for households, the \uc2 Commonwealth Bank\uc2  has pushed back its forecast for when the Reserve Bank will lower the official cash rate, now saying a 0.25 percentage point reduction will happen in December, as opposed to previous predictions of a November rate cut. More coming up.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240919ek9j00361\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
