\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Burned-out firefighters are fleeing the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  amid labor disputes: \u8216\'20\'18We are decimated\u8217\'20\'19\b0\par\par\uc2 Gabrielle Canon \par \uc2 1,739 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2024\par 21:07 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 As increasingly explosive fire seasons ravage the country, federal firefighters say their employer is exacerbating exhaustion by misclassifying their jobs\par \par \uc2 Firefighters are feeling the strain of another long and intense season, with months to go before the highest risks subside. But as they battle the flames, the thousands of people working for the \uc2 US Forest Service\uc2  (\uc2 USFS\uc2 ), the largest federal employer of firefighters, are also fighting for changes within the agency to tackle issues they say have made the work even harder .\par \par \uc2 Federal firefighters have been waiting for years for revisions to outdated job descriptions, which have forced them to do more for less. Many have opted to leave altogether.\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://nffe.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/08/NFFE-Ltr-to-USDA-FS-on-Wildland-Firefighter-Occ-Series-August-27-2024.pdf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 scathing letter sent last month to top officials}}}\uc2  at the \uc2 USFS\uc2  and the \uc2 United States Department of Agriculture (USDA)\uc2 , the National Federation of Federal Employees (NFFE), the union that represents federal wildland firefighters, accused the agency of decades of wage theft and job misclassification.\par \par \uc2 The issue is among many \u8211\'20\'13 from a stalled pay raise to short staffing and escalating job hazards \u8211\'20\'13 that have contributed to severe burnout and struggles with recruitment and retention, just as fires become more difficult \u8211\'20\'13 and more dangerous \u8211\'20\'13 to fight.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe have fewer people willing to stick it out and get to the positions that pay them for the work they are already doing,\u8221\'20\'1D said Morgan Thomsen, a firefighter with the \uc2 USFS\uc2  for the last two decades who was speaking from his position as a union steward. The exodus has not only created gaps in experience, tactical planning and coverage, but has also exacerbated the exhaustion felt by firefighters still in the trenches. \u8220\'20\'1CThose of us who have stuck around keep getting more and more stuff piled on because everybody else is leaving.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2021/aug/04/infrastructure-bill-bipartisan-visual-explainer"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2021 infrastructure act}}}\uc2  required the \uc2 USFS\uc2  and other land management agencies to issue updates to what\u8217\'20\'19s known as an \u8220\'20\'1Coccupational series\u8221\'20\'1D, a set of guidelines that outlines the expertise, credentials and duties that federal employees need to perform at each level.\par \par \uc2 Many of the position descriptions in the current series are outdated, including some sketched in the mid-20th century before complex firefighting needs arose.\par \par \uc2 But after nearly three years of work, the NFFE said in its August letter, the new descriptions for firefighting roles laid out by the \uc2 USFS\uc2  were essentially \u8220\'20\'1Ccopied and pasted\u8221\'20\'1D with minor changes.\par \par \uc2 Those of us who have stuck around keep getting more and more stuff piled on because everybody else is leaving\par \par \uc2 Morgan Thomsen, firefighter and union steward\par \par \uc2 A major point of contention has been the designation of emergency medical technician (EMT) and emergency medical services (EMS) work. The union has called for positions that better reflect the emergency-response training and qualifications firefighters often need to perform their duties, and better access to the pay raises that go with them..\par \par \uc2 The misclassification of someone\u8217\'20\'19s job can have a cumulative affect on their life, affecting their salary, overtime pay, retirement and abilities to advance. A marked stagnation in career development and opportunities has pushed more experienced and management-level firefighters out the door.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 USFS\uc2  has {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.propublica.org/article/wildfires-forest-service-firefighters-preparedness-level-five"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 lost nearly half of its permanent employees}}}\uc2  in the last three years, according to data reported on by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 . New recruits tend to be entry-level and inexperienced, leaving the agency scrambling to fill leadership positions during the most active parts of the year.\par \par \uc2 Even though the \uc2 USFS\uc2  says it exceeded its hiring goals this year, thousands of key roles went unfilled as large wildfires began to rage this summer. The impact is severe, leaving firefighters, communities and landscapes at risk. \u8220\'20\'1CYou don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough people overseeing things,\u8221\'20\'1D Thomsen said. \u8220\'20\'1CThe ripple effect is a huge safety concern.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8216\'20\'18There\u8217\'20\'19s no stop to it\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 More than {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nifc.gov/fire-information/nfn"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 7m acres (2.8m hectares) have burned across the US}}}\uc2  this year \u8211\'20\'13 a total 26% higher than the 10-year average \u8211\'20\'13 and the season shows no sign of slowing. The National Interagency Fire Center, a federal firefighting coordination agency, has set firefighting activity across the US at its highest level for most of the summer, an indication that national resources are fully committed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere really is no stop to it,\u8221\'20\'1D said Abel Martinez, a fire captain at Angeles national forest in southern California.\par \par \uc2 Martinez is experiencing the strain first-hand. Many firefighters have accumulated more than a thousand hours of overtime this year, with months of work still to go. Even the colder seasons, which used to be reserved for rest and recuperation, are filled with controlled burns \u8211\'20\'13 essential projects that nonetheless add to the exhaustion.\par \par \uc2 You don\u8217\'20\'19t have enough people overseeing things. The ripple effect is a huge safety concern\par \par \uc2 Morgan Thomsen, firefighter and union steward\par \par \uc2 According to Martinez, overwork has led to an increase in accidents and injuries. And, there\u8217\'20\'19s a direct line, he said, between these issues and the lag on pay and promotions. \u8220\'20\'1CWe are decimated with the retention so a lot of the positions aren\u8217\'20\'19t filled,\u8221\'20\'1D he said. \u8220\'20\'1CBecause they are not filled, other people pick up the slack.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Dozens of middle-management workers have left Angeles national forest since last December, Martinez said, including 11 captains and four engine operators, and their experience is difficult to make up for. The forest service is beginning to encourage better work-life balance to stave off burnout, but Martinez said it was a challenge to take time off with staffing so sparse.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CYou are on a constant cycle of physical and mental fatigue,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, adding that even if the culture changes, conditions keep getting worse. \u8220\'20\'1CI don\u8217\'20\'19t think the agency has a good plan for burnout.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The issues have caused some to question whether the \uc2 USFS\uc2  is equipped to oversee the federal government\u8217\'20\'19s largest firefighting force into the future. The agency was originally created in 1905 to manage land \u8211\'20\'13 to oversee timber harvest and recreation within the nation\u8217\'20\'19s forests \u8211\'20\'13 but has watched its mission and budget be consumed by the need to fight fires that have grown more frequent and severe.\par \par \uc2 Roughly a third of its sprawling workforce is now made up of firefighters. Some have voiced concerns that their positions are being kept arbitrarily beneath those of land managers, who have traditionally been at the top of the agency\u8217\'20\'19s hierarchy. That\u8217\'20\'19s left a disconnect between leadership and the crews battling the flames.\par \par \uc2 There was a reason, Martinez said, that the \u8220\'20\'1CLos Angeles county fire department isn\u8217\'20\'19t run by by the county\u8217\'20\'19s parks and recreation department\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Like Thomsen, Martinez serves as a representative in the union, which is why he is able to speak more freely.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 USFS\uc2  has acknowledged an urgent need to update the occupational series and has called on Congress for more funding. But the agency firmly disputed the union\u8217\'20\'19s characterization of their work together.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe engaged in an unprecedented level of collaboration with NFFE,\u8221\'20\'1Da USFS spokesperson, Wade Muehlhof, told the Guardian in an emailed response.\par \par \uc2 Highlighting that the agency had also worked closely with the \uc2 US Office of Personnel Management\uc2  on the series, Muehlhof said that the \uc2 USFS\uc2  agreed withthe NFFE on the need to include more accurate EMT duties in future position descriptions, but that it will take time. The \uc2 USFS\uc2  also responded that they rely on professional classifiers to ensure position descriptions align with the standards set by the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) and that no group of employees is given a preference over the others.\par \par \uc2 He added that the \uc2 USFS\uc2  remains committed to working with the union.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CA new and unique series for federal wildland firefighters is not something any federal agency has had for 50 years,\u8221\'20\'1D Muehlhof said. \u8220\'20\'1CGetting it in place successfully requires ongoing collaboration and adjustments as we move forward in implementation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Budget woes and rising costs\par \par \uc2 Adding to the stress over job descriptions is a protracted battle about firefighters\u8217\'20\'19 pay that has dragged on for years. Federal wildland firefighters make far less than their state- and city-employed counterparts, with paychecks that rival those of fast-food employees.\par \par \uc2 A temporary pay raise issued through the Inflation Reduction Act and the Bipartisan Infrastructure Law, which set minimums at $15 an hour and boosted salaries by the lower of either 50% or $20,000, has yet to be made permanent by Congress. There are hopes this could soon change, but sharply divided legislators are facing the looming 30 September {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/homenews/house/4868263-government-funding-shutdown-congress/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deadline}}}\uc2  to pass the next federal budget.\par \par \uc2 Muehlhof emphasized that the \uc2 USFS\uc2  had been behind efforts to increase pay for federal wildland firefighters and requested more than $1.3bn as part of the 2025 federal budget to support pay raises and new hiring, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/13/firefighter-housing-crisis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 and to improve firefighters\u8217\'20\'19 housing}}}\uc2 . He also clarified that work on the series wasn\u8217\'20\'19t related to the budget, rejecting concerns that promotions were stifled due to funding.\par \par \uc2 But the agency is under immense financial pressure as it grapples with both rising expenses and what the \uc2 USFS\uc2  chief, Randy Moore, recently described {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.fs.usda.gov/inside-fs/leadership/fy25-budget-updates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 as a \u8220\'20\'1Cbudget-limited future\u8221\'20\'1D}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The costs of fighting fires are increasing as the climate crisis fuels more extreme conditions. A federal Climate Financial Risk report released in July of this year, which \uc2 USFS\uc2  researchers contributed to, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://research.fs.usda.gov/srs/products/compasslive/economic-risks-forest-service-estimates-costs-fighting-wildfires-hotter"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 found that lands in the national forest system could see double the area burned by mid-century}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 and that\u8217\'20\'19s one of the more conservative estimates. One scenario showed risks quadrupling.\par \par \uc2 Suppression costs, which have averaged $2.9bn a year over the last decade, are expected to rise accordingly. By 2050, suppression expenditures are expected to jump to $3.9bn a year, a 42% increase, but could spike as high as 84%, according to the same report.\par \par \uc2 But the exodus of knowledge and skill will only increase if the issues aren\u8217\'20\'19t solved quickly. Federal firefighters have long borne the brunt of financial strain, wreaking havoc on their physical and mental health, their personal lives, and their ability to do safe work in the harshest conditions.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI could see more people leaving because of this,\u8221\'20\'1D Thomsen said, referring to delays in new job descriptions. Even if Congress is able to pass an appropriations bill that secures pay raises for firefighters, progress will be slow without an adequate job series. \u8220\'20\'1CIt is a step in the right direction,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, \u8220\'20\'1Cbut it would be like putting a Band-Aid on a cut throat at this point.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240912ek9c001rz\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
