\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  workers vote to remove CEO after \u8216\'20\'18inhumane\u8217\'20\'19 layoffs\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 853 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 September 2024\par 11:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Staffers claim June mass layoffs at civil rights non-profit was a union-busting tactic that \u8216\'20\'18destroyed lives\u8217\'20\'19\par \par \uc2 Workers at the civil rights non-profit \uc2 Southern Poverty Law Center\uc2  (SPLC) have voted on a motion of no confidence in its chief executive, Margaret Huang, following a mass layoff of staffers in June 2024 that they characterized as a union-busting tactic.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 SPLC\uc2  union announced this week that 92% of members who voted on the motion supported the no-confidence motion, with the union calling for Huang\u8217\'20\'19s removal, the reversal of the layoff of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/letters/reverse-splc-layoffs-now"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 25% }}}\uc2  of the organization\u8217\'20\'19s staff, and a call to involve the union in the hiring of a new CEO. The union also started a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://actionnetwork.org/letters/splc-union-staff-vote-no-confidence-in-ceo-margaret-huang/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 public petition}}}\uc2  to rally support for their demands.\par \par \uc2 In June 2024, the \uc2 SPLC\uc2  announced it would be laying off nearly 80 workers, including 61 union-eligible staff.\par \par \uc2 Lisa Wright, the chair of the union\u8217\'20\'19s bargaining committee and an employee of 23 years at the \uc2 SPLC\uc2 , was one of the employees impacted by the layoffs.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CShock, horror, confusion. It\u8217\'20\'19s been sloppy, dispassionate, inhumane. It has been the absolute opposite of what the organization says they stand for and absolute chaos since then,\u8221\'20\'1D Wright said.\par \par \uc2 Workers at the \uc2 SPLC\uc2  first unionized in 2019 in the wake of management changes at the organization following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.newyorker.com/news/news-desk/the-reckoning-of-morris-dees-and-the-southern-poverty-law-center"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 allegations}}}\uc2  of discrimination and sexual harassment within the organization.\par \par \uc2 Huang assumed the role a year later. A first union contract was not {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/news/2022/07/01/splc-management-workers-reach-agreement-contract"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reached}}}\uc2  until July 2022 and new contract negotiations were set to begin soon ahead of the contract expiration in July 2025.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt was just a real kick in the teeth. This whole situation could have been handled so much better,\u8221\'20\'1D said Esteban Gil, a current employee at \uc2 SPLC\uc2  who is now chair of the union after Wright\u8217\'20\'19s layoff.\par \par \uc2 He criticized the \uc2 SPLC\uc2  for not contacting the union to negotiate terms of the changes and layoffs. He also emphasized the negative impact of the layoffs on immigration legal services at the organization \u8211\'20\'13 all the more pronounced given a prominent policy of \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 , the Republican presidential candidate, is the mass deportation of immigrants.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis was designed with trauma as a feature, not a bug \u8211\'20\'13 to keep us confused, hopeless, afraid, divided,\u8221\'20\'1D he added. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has been strategic, sloppily strategic, but it\u8217\'20\'19s to bust our union \u8211\'20\'13 not to make the organization stronger, not to develop our programs, not to do any of those things. Because to me, it screams incompetence if you can\u8217\'20\'19t change directions and move the pieces on the board without knocking all the pieces off the board to make a new plan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Thereatha Redding, an employee at \uc2 SPLC\uc2  for nearly three years and a union steward, worked in Stewart county, Georgia, one of the poorest counties in the state, providing legal services to immigrants held at an {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.splcenter.org/attention-on-detention/healing-open-wounds-injustice-stewart-county-georgia"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ICE detention center}}}\uc2 . She and the entire legal team were laid off and no communication or support was offered to the community members who rely on their services, she said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey talk about working with people in the communities, the deep south, and they just dismantled a program that was helping individuals in the deep south,\u8221\'20\'1D said Redding. \u8220\'20\'1CThey did not even have the sense or the notion to even notify the people in our community.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 LaShanwda Surles Dudley, a legal administrative assistant for over two years, was also laid off and explained it\u8217\'20\'19s been devastating to her.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI was just in tears. It was disheartening because I thought this was my career job,\u8221\'20\'1D said Surles Dudley. \u8220\'20\'1CIt has destroyed a lot of lives, peoples\u8217\'20\'19 livelihoods. People depended on this job. This was everything. It was not just financial. It was about helping others. It was that contribution that I felt, that I was doing when it was coming to people asking for help or support, and I thought this organization did that, but now I see they really don\u8217\'20\'19t care about the people.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 SPLC\uc2  did not comment on the union-busting allegations but dismissed the union\u8217\'20\'19s request to remove the organization\u8217\'20\'19s CEO.\par \par \uc2 In an email, a \uc2 SPLC\uc2  spokesperson said: \u8220\'20\'1CThe \uc2 SPLC\uc2  Board was unanimous in re-affirming its support of the recent reorganization and in its recognition of the leadership of CEO Margaret Huang. We respect the bargaining unit\u8217\'20\'19s right to oppose the changes to the \uc2 SPLC\uc2  programs and activities, and we empathize with all employees who were impacted by the staff restructure. These decisions are never easy, but necessary to strengthen our strategic framework and focus so that we can meet the challenges of this decade and beyond.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur mission at \uc2 SPLC\uc2  is clear \u8211\'20\'13 eradicate poverty, dismantle white supremacy, decarcerate and decriminalize communities of color, promote democracy and civic engagement, and champion racial justice, especially for Black and Brown communities in the Deep South,\u8221\'20\'1D the statement continued. \u8220\'20\'1CThrough the recent operational changes and realignment of programmatic priorities, we will ensure that we can invest where the needs are the greatest while maintaining the highest level of impact.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240912ek9c001gv\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
