\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 US economy adds 142,000 jobs in August as Fed plans to cut interest rates\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 651 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par 20:30 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par 45\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Reading for month was shy of the average forecast as headline unemployment rate decreased from 4.3% to 4.2%\par \par \uc2 US employers added 142,000 jobs last month, the labor department announced on Friday, in one of the year\u8217\'20\'19s most closely watched economic news releases.\par \par \uc2 The release comes as the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  prepares to cut interest rates for the first time since March 2020, and November\u8217\'20\'19s election puts a spotlight on the state of the US economy.\par \par \uc2 The reading for August was shy of the average forecast increase of 163,000 jobs by economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The labor department also cut its figures for the number of new hires for June and July by a combined 86,000 jobs, a further sign that the remarkable post-pandemic hiring boom is weakening.\par \par \uc2 The headline unemployment rate also decreased from 4.3% to 4.2%, in line with expectations.\par \par \uc2 US stock markets fell as investors worried about a weakening economy and fears that the artificial intelligence boom may prove less lucrative than first thought. The tech-heavy Nasdaq dropped 2.6% with the broader S&P 500 down 1.7% and Dow Jones industrial average down 1%.\par \par \uc2 Over five trading sessions, the S&P suffered its worst week since 2023. The Nasdaq endured its worst week since 2022. Tech companies including \uc2 Nvidia\uc2 , the chips giant, and \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , owner of \uc2 Google\uc2  and \uc2 YouTube\uc2 , led Friday\u8217\'20\'19s sell-off.\par \par \uc2 The economy is {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://thehill.com/business/4828253-most-voters-in-new-poll-say-economy-immigration-most-important-issues-in-2024/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ranked}}}\uc2  as one of the most important issues heading into November\u8217\'20\'19s presidential election, amid significant unease over its strength. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/donaldtrump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Donald Trump}}}\uc2  led {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/kamala-harris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris}}}\uc2  by 3% in a recent {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/world/us/trumps-edge-over-harris-economy-crime-slips-reutersipsos-poll-finds-2024-08-27/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Reuters/Ipsos poll}}}\uc2  on the candidates\u8217\'20\'19 approaches to the economy and employment.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  made a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/21/us-job-market-employment-numbers"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 significant revision,}}}\uc2  lowering the number of job created in the 12 months to March by 818,000, or 0.5%, the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cnbc.com/2024/08/21/nonfarm-payroll-growth-revised-down-by-818000-labor-department-says.html#:~:text=Nonfarm%20payroll%20growth%20revised%20down%20by%20818%2C000%2C%20Labor%20Department%20says,-Published%20Wed%2C%20Aug&text=The%20revision%20to%20the%20total,358%2C000%20less%20than%20initially%20reported."}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 largest revision}}}\uc2  since 2009. The revision tapered job increase reports to about 174,000 jobs a month added, compared with the previously reported rate of about 242,000 jobs a month.\par \par \uc2 The revision came amid signs that the jobs market is weakening. On Wednesday the labor department reported that the number of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bls.gov/news.release/jolts.nr0.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 job openings}}}\uc2  has fallen to its lowest level in three and a half years.\par \par \uc2 The Fed chair, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/23/us-feds-cut-interest-rates-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Jerome Powell}}}\uc2 , recently signaled plans to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/23/us-feds-cut-interest-rates-powell"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 reduce interest rates}}}\uc2  when the central bank holds its {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomccalendars.htm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 next meeting }}}\uc2  on 17 and 18 September, declaring that \u8220\'20\'1Cthe time has come\u8221\'20\'1D for the Fed to act.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe direction of travel is clear, and the timing and pace of rate cuts will depend on incoming data, the evolving outlook, and the balance of risks,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Two years ago, when inflation was soaring during the pandemic, policymakers at the Fed scrambled to cool the US economy by raising rates to a two-decade high. Now that price growth is falling back \u8211\'20\'13 it rose at an annual rate of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/14/us-july-inflation-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 2.9% in July}}}\uc2 , having faded from a peak of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/jul/13/high-us-gas-food-prices-june-led-to-largest-annual-increase-inflation-40-years"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 9.1% in June 2022 }}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 they are preparing to cut rates, but have yet to do so.\par \par \uc2 Officials hope to guide the US to a so-called \u8220\'20\'1C {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/mar/17/talk-of-soft-landing-for-global-economy-premature-many-dark-scenarios-are-lurking"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 soft landing}}}\uc2  \u8221\'20\'1D, whereby inflation is normalized, and recession avoided. The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s target for inflation is 2%.\par \par \uc2 In recent months, critics of the Fed have accused the central bank of sitting on its hands and derailing the US economy, amid unease over its direction. An unexpectedly weak {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/02/us-economy-jobs-report"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 jobs report for July}}}\uc2 , which came a day after the Fed again chose to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/31/federal-reserve-interest-rates"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 hold rates steady}}}\uc2 , sparked a fleeting global sell-off.\par \par \uc2 Paul Ashworth\uc2 , chief North America economist at \uc2 Capital Economics\uc2 , said the report showed \u8220\'20\'1Cthe labor market is clearly experiencing a marked slowdown\u8221\'20\'1D but \u8220\'20\'1Coverall, still consistent with an economy experiencing a soft landing rather than plummeting into recession\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240906ek96001xh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
