\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 Telegram to shake-up features; markets slide after US job creation misses forecasts \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 8,271 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par 16:57 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Telegram boss \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2  announces a new approach towards moderating content and will remove some features that had been abused for illegal activity.Telegram to drop \u8216\'20\'18people nearby\u8217\'20\'19 feature and improve moderationPavel Durov: Telegram founder says France arrest is \u8216\'20\'18misguided\u8217\'20\'19Full story: US economy adds 142,000 jobs in August as Fed plans to cut interest rates\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.35pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time to wrap up\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 The chief executive of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/telegram"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Telegram}}}\uc2 , \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2 , has announced the messaging app will improve moderation on the platform and has removed some features that have been used for illegal activity.\par \par \uc2 The app\u8217\'20\'19s founder unveiled the changes on Friday hours after calling his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/25/telegram-app-founder-pavel-durov-to-appear-in-court-after-arrest-in-paris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arrest by the French authorities}}}\uc2  last month \u8220\'20\'1Cmisguided\u8221\'20\'1D. Durov has since been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/28/telegram-ceo-charged-france-allowing-criminal-activity-app"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 charged with allegedly allowing criminal activity on the app}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://x.com/durov/status/1832054680899215647"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 post on X}}}\uc2 , the platform formerly known as \uc2 Twitter\uc2 , he said the messaging app was \u8220\'20\'1Ccommitted to turn moderation on Telegram from an area of criticism into one of praise\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The changes announced by Durov included removing the app\u8217\'20\'19s People Nearby feature which he said had \u8220\'20\'1Cissues with bots and scammers\u8221\'20\'1D and replacing it with Businesses Nearby, featuring legitimate businesses; and disabling media uploads on the app\u8217\'20\'19s blogging tool, Telegraph, which Durov said was being \u8220\'20\'1Cmisused by anonymous actors\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Durov added that Telegram\u8217\'20\'19s nearly 1 billion users had been let down by a minority.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/sep/06/telegram-to-drop-people-nearby-feature-and-improve-moderation"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Telegram to drop \u8216\'20\'18people nearby\u8217\'20\'19 feature and improve moderation}}}\par \par \uc2 Durov has also called his recent arrest \u8216\'20\'18\u8220\'20\'1Cmisguided\u8221\'20\'1D, and defended Telegram.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/sep/06/pavel-durov-telegram-founder-post-comments-arrest-france"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pavel Durov: Telegram founder says France arrest is \u8216\'20\'18misguided\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 US employers added 142,000 jobs last month, the labor department announced on Friday, in one of the year\u8217\'20\'19s most closely watched economic news releases.\par \par \uc2 The release comes as the US {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/federal-reserve"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Federal Reserve}}}\uc2  prepares to cut interest rates for the first time since March 2020, and November\u8217\'20\'19s election puts a spotlight on the state of the US economy.\par \par \uc2 The reading for August was shy of the average forecast increase of 163,000 jobs by economists surveyed by \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/06/august-us-jobs-report-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 US economy adds 142,000 jobs in August as Fed plans to cut interest rates}}}\par \par \uc2 The weaker-than-forecast job creation has caused wobbles in the markets \u8211\'20\'13 the S&P 500 share index is now down 1.7%, and the oil price has slipped to its lowest since May 2023.\par \par \uc2 In London, the FTSE 100 lost 60 points to close at 8181 points, its 6th daily fall in a row.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.cmegroup.com/markets/interest-rates/cme-fedwatch-tool.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The bond markets currently indicate}}}\uc2  there\u8217\'20\'19s a 75% chance of a small, 25 basis point, cut to US interest rates this month, and a 25% chance of a larger, 50-basis point cut.\par \par \uc2 In the UK, house prices hit a two-year high last month, in the latest sign that the property market has recovered from the aftermath of \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s infamous mini-budget that sent borrowing costs soaring, according to the latest figures from Halifax.\par \par \uc2 The lender said the cost of an average house increased by 0.3% last month, after rising 0.9% in July, pushing the average cost of a property to \u163\'00\'A3292,505.\par \par \uc2 This is the highest level since August 2022, the month before Truss\u8217\'20\'19s {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/oct/20/the-mini-budget-that-broke-britain-and-liz-truss"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 disastrous mini-budget}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/06/uk-house-prices-hit-two-year-high-as-market-recovers-from-truss-mini-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK house prices hit two-year high as market recovers from Truss mini-budget}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 5.38pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 5.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 Incidentally, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  reported this morning that the \uc2 European Union\uc2  is quietly gathering evidence to support the case that Telegram should be subject to the bloc\u8217\'20\'19s strictest content-moderation regulations.\par \par \uc2 They reported:\par \par \uc2 Officials at the \uc2 EU\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s executive arm are using data from several web traffic monitoring firms to establish that Telegram\u8217\'20\'19s social-media platform draws more than 45 million monthly active users, a threshold that would subject it to extra scrutiny under the bloc\u8217\'20\'19s new Digital Services Act, according to a \uc2 European Commission\uc2  official.\par \par \uc2 More here: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2024-09-06/eu-builds-case-to-place-telegram-under-stricter-content-scrutiny?srnd=phx-politics&sref=Xl91GI8N"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 EU Builds Case to Place Telegram Under Stricter Content Scrutiny}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 Telegram has also removed language from its Frequently Asked Questions page saying that it does not process reports about illegal content in private chats because such chats are protected, Reuters adds.\par \par \uc2 This post appears to show the change:\par \par \uc2 enltrTelegram quietly updated FAQ, removing:\par \par \uc2 "All Telegram chats and group chats are private amongst their participants. We do not process any requests related to them."\par \par \uc2 ?????? {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/fJvKYZPMVg"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/fJvKYZPMVg}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ?GM? (@fatherofogham) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/fatherofogham/status/1832074690979934237?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 4.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Katie Harbath, a former public policy director at \uc2 Meta\uc2  who now advises companies on technology issues, says ( via Reuters ):\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s good that Durov is starting to take content moderation seriously but, just like Elon (Musk) and other tech CEOs who run speech platforms have found, if he thinks this will be as simple as making a few small changes, he\u8217\'20\'19s in for a rude awakening.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 How the Telegram saga unfolded\par \par \uc2 The drama over Telegram began on Saturday 24 August, when \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2  was {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/aug/24/telegram-app-founder-pavel-durov-arrested-at-french-airport"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  arrested at the Bourget airport outside Paris}}}\uc2 , where he had flown on his private jet.\par \par \uc2 French investigators had issued a warrant for Durov\u8217\'20\'19s arrest as part of an inquiry into allegations of fraud, drug trafficking, organised crime, promotion of terrorism and cyberbullying. And on the following day, Sunday 25th August, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/25/telegram-app-founder-pavel-durov-to-appear-in-court-after-arrest-in-paris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 his detention was extended}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/25/telegram-app-founder-pavel-durov-to-appear-in-court-after-arrest-in-paris"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Authorities extend detention of Telegram app founder Pavel Durov after Paris arrest}}}\par \par \uc2 Durov\u8217\'20\'19s arrest quickly became a major issue, with French authorities criticised by free-speech advocates, far-right figures and authoritarian governments around the world. X\u8217\'20\'19s owner, \uc2 Elon Musk\uc2 , fuelled the row by posting \u8216\'20\'18#FreePavel\u8217\'20\'19 and claiming the arrest was an example of the world entering \u8220\'20\'1CDangerous times\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Durov\u8217\'20\'19s lawyer David-Olivier Kaminski told French media,\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1Cit\u8217\'20\'19s totally absurd to think that the person in charge of a social network could be implicated in criminal acts that don\u8217\'20\'19t concern him, directly or indirectly.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 On Monday 27th August, President Emmanuel Macron {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.politico.eu/article/telegram-pavel-durov-arrest-france-emmanuel-macron/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 insisted }}}\uc2  the French government was not involved in Durov\u8217\'20\'19s arrest. On the same day, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/media/article/2024/aug/26/telegram-founder-suspected-allowing-criminal-activity-pavel-durov-prosecutors"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prosecutors announced}}}\uc2  that Durov had been detained in connection with an investigation into criminal activity on the platform and a lack of cooperation with law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 The move intensified focus on the regulation of social media firms.\par \par \uc2 As Telegram is not end-to-end encrypted by default (despite being marketed as a \u8220\'20\'1Csecure messenger.\u8221\'20\'1D) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/27/what-does-the-telegram-founders-arrest-mean-for-the-regulation-of-social-media-companies-pavel-durov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  it is harder to rebuff law enforcement}}}\uc2  if they come seeking information about a user:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/27/what-does-the-telegram-founders-arrest-mean-for-the-regulation-of-social-media-companies-pavel-durov"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What the Telegram founder\u8217\'20\'19s arrest means for the regulation of social media firms}}}\par \par \uc2 Durov was then charged on Wednesday 28 August with allegedly allowing criminal activity on the messaging app.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/28/telegram-ceo-charged-france-allowing-criminal-activity-app"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Telegram CEO charged in France for \u8216\'20\'18allowing criminal activity\u8217\'20\'19 on messaging app}}}\par \par \uc2 Overnight, Durov claimed his arrest \u8216\'20\'18\u8220\'20\'1Cmisguided\u8221\'20\'1D, and insisted that Telegram was not an anarchic paradise.\par \par \uc2 He wrote:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe take down millions of harmful posts and channels every day.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/sep/06/pavel-durov-telegram-founder-post-comments-arrest-france"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pavel Durov: Telegram founder says France arrest is \u8216\'20\'18misguided\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 The shake-up at Telegram ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/sep/06/pavel-durov-telegram-founder-post-comments-arrest-france"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 see last post}}}\uc2  ) also comes just hours after founder\par \par \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2  said his arrest last month was \u8216\'20\'18misguided\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 Durov, writing on his {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/telegram"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Telegram}}}\uc2  channel early on Friday in his first public comments since his detention last month, denied any suggestion the app was an \u8220\'20\'1Canarchic paradise\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The Russian-born multi-billionaire said the investigation into the app was surprising in that French authorities had access to a \u8220\'20\'1Chot line\u8221\'20\'1D he had helped set up and they could have contacted Telegram\u8217\'20\'19s EU representative at any time.\par \par \uc2 He wrote:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIf a country is unhappy with an internet service, the established practice is to start a legal action against the service itself.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CUsing laws from the pre-smartphone era to charge a CEO with crimes committed by third parties on the platform he manages is a misguided approach.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/sep/06/pavel-durov-telegram-founder-post-comments-arrest-france"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Pavel Durov: Telegram founder says France arrest is \u8216\'20\'18misguided\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.21pm BST\par \par \uc2 Telegram to remove or disable services that have been misused\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The boss of the messaging service Telegram, \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2 , has announced that his app is removing or disabling some features that he says have been misused by scammers, bots and criminals.\par \par \uc2 Durov says Telegram will take a new approach towards moderating content and remove some features that had been abused for illegal activity. The move comes almost two weeks after Durov was arrested in France.\par \par \uc2 Writing on Telegram,and also on X (formerly \uc2 Twitter\uc2 ), Durov says the platform is tackling the \u8220\'20\'1C0.001% involved in illicit activities\u8221\'20\'1D on the plaform.\par \par \uc2 He says;\par \par \uc2 Telegram has reached 10 million paid subscribers. 10 million people are now enjoying Telegram Premium!\par \par \uc2 Today, we\u8217\'20\'19re introducing new features while phasing out a few outdated ones.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve removed the People Nearby feature, which was used by less than 0.1% of Telegram users, but had issues with bots and scammers.\par \par \uc2 In its place, we will be launching \u8220\'20\'1CBusinesses Nearby\u8221\'20\'1D, showcasing legitimate, verified businesses. These businesses will be able to display product catalogs and accept payments seamlessly.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve also disabled new media uploads to \uc2 Telegraph\uc2 , our standalone blogging tool, which seems to have been misused by anonymous actors.\par \par \uc2 While 99.999% of Telegram users have nothing to do with crime, the 0.001% involved in illicit activities create a bad image for the entire platform, putting the interests of our almost billion users at risk.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why this year we are committed to turn moderation on Telegram from an area of criticism into one of praise.\par \par \uc2 enltr?? Telegram has reached 10 million paid subscribers. 10 million people are now enjoying Telegram Premium!\par \par \uc2 ?? Today, we\u8217\'20\'19re introducing new features while phasing out a few outdated ones.\par \par \uc2 ?? We\u8217\'20\'19ve removed the People Nearby feature, which was used by less than 0.1% of Telegram\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Pavel Durov\uc2  (@durov) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/durov/status/1832054680899215647?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Durov was last week charged by the French judiciary for allegedly allowing criminal activity on the messaging app but avoided jail with a \u8364\'20\'AC5m bail.\par \par \uc2 The charges against Durov include complicity in the spread of sexual images of children and a litany of other alleged violations on the messaging app.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/aug/28/telegram-ceo-charged-france-allowing-criminal-activity-app"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Telegram CEO charged in France for \u8216\'20\'18allowing criminal activity\u8217\'20\'19 on messaging app}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 3.29pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.01pm BST\par \par \uc2 James Knightley\uc2 , chief international economist at ING, predicts the Fed will make a large cut to interest rates,of 50 basis points, this month\par \par \uc2 The US added fewer jobs than expected in August, but there was enough in the report to keep markets guessing on whether the Fed will cut by 25bp or 50bp on 18 September.\par \par \uc2 Lead indicators suggest further weakness lies ahead, and we believe the Fed will go for a 50bp move, but it\u8217\'20\'19s a close call.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.53pm BST\par \par \uc2 Biden: must focus on sustaining historic gains for American workers.\par \par \uc2 President \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  has responded to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/06/uk-house-prices-hit-two-year-high-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66daf4498f08a9d0891e9556#block-66daf4498f08a9d0891e9556"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report}}}\uc2 , saying it is important to sustain the \u8220\'20\'1Chistoric gains\u8221\'20\'1D that US workers have received.\par \par \uc2 In a statement issued by the White House. Biden says:\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s report showed unemployment fell to 4.2% and another 142,000 jobs were created last month. Thanks to our work to rescue the economy, nearly 16 million new jobs have been created, wages and incomes are rising faster than prices, businesses are investing in America, and millions of entrepreneurs are opening small businesses\u8212\'20\'14acts of hope and confidence in our economy.\par \par \uc2 With inflation back down close to normal levels, it is important to focus on sustaining the historic gains we have made for American workers.\par \par \uc2 And with the election looming, the president criticises the Republican party\u8217\'20\'19s economic policies:\par \par \uc2 The Vice President and I are fighting to expand opportunity and grow the middle class. We will keep working to support American workers and businesses, and to lower housing and health care costs for hardworking Americans.\par \par \uc2 The last thing we should do is turn back to the failed trickle-down economics pushed by Congressional Republicans, like cutting taxes for the wealthy and large corporations, raising taxes on middle class families by nearly $4,000 per year, or cutting Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid, and the Affordable Care Act.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/aug/16/kamala-harris-economy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Harris vows to build \u8216\'20\'18opportunity economy\u8217\'20\'19 and attacks Trump on tax}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s job report was \u8220\'20\'1Cmixed\u8221\'20\'1D, says Jumana Saleheen, \uc2 Vanguard\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s chief European economist.\par \par \uc2 And that means the debate about how deeply the Fed will cut rates this month continues\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 enltrToday\u8217\'20\'19s job report was mixed.\par \par \uc2 It contained good news (job growth is still decent) & concerning news(the pace of job growth is a bit lower than the level consistent with trend growth).\par \par \uc2 The debate of will the Fed do 25 or 50bps will continue until we get inflation data next week.\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Jumana Saleheen (@JumanaSaleheen) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/JumanaSaleheen/status/1832052983967998451?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.42pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street opens calmly\par \par \uc2 Trading has begun on Wall Street, with little early drama.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 share index is basically flat, losing just 1.2 points to 5,502 early doors.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks are lower again, pulling the Nasdaq Composite down by over 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 But the Dow Jones industrial average, of 30 large US companies, has gained 0.4%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report also shows a pick-up in pay growth.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s good news for workers, but might make the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  fret.\par \par \uc2 Average hourly earnings increased 0.4% in August, to $35.21 per hour, after rising 0.2% in July to $35.07.\par \par \uc2 Over the past 12 months, average hourly earnings have increased by 3.8%. That means wages are rising faster than prices, as US CPI inflation was 2.9% in July.\par \par \uc2 enltrJUST IN: A solid jobs report. The US added 142,000 jobs in August. That\u8217\'20\'19s just below expectations of ~160k and a decent rebound from 89,000 jobs in July.\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate fell back to 4.2% (down from 4.3% in July)\par \par \uc2 Wages: +3.8% in past year (well above 2.9% inflation)\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Yd8p4PBP6y"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Yd8p4PBP6y}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1832034166784885071?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.26pm BST\par \par \uc2 The next US inflation report will also influence how dramatically the Fed cuts interest rates this month, says Matthew Ryan, head of market strategy at global financial services firm Ebury.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhile markets have {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/06/uk-house-prices-hit-two-year-high-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66dafd848f0800d1df72d7bb#block-66dafd848f0800d1df72d7bb"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  ramped up their bets }}}\uc2  in favour of a jumbo rate reduction from the Fed this month, the data in itself hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been sufficient to seal the deal for a 50 basis point cut, which remains less than 60% priced in by futures markets.\par \par \uc2 Next week\u8217\'20\'19s CPI report for August will now be key, as a miss here may be required for the FOMC to go big in September.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Odds of 50bp US rate cut rise\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Wall Street investors are increasing their bets that the \uc2 US Federal Reserve\uc2  will slash US interest rates by half a percentage point at its next meeting, on 17 and 18 September.\par \par \uc2 CME Group\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s FedWatch Tool, which uses pricing data from the futures market to predict interest rates, is now indicating there\u8217\'20\'19s a 51% chance of a 50 basis point cut later this month.\par \par \uc2 Before today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll was released, a 50bp cut in September was only a 41% prospect, with a smaller, quarter-point cut seen as more likely.\par \par \uc2 Those odds have now narrowed to a effective coin-toss, after fewer jobs were created in August than expected \u8211\'20\'13 at just 142,000.\par \par \uc2 Traders are also digesting that June and July\u8217\'20\'19s payrolls were revised lower, accentuating the slowdown in hiring this summer.\par \par \uc2 However, Fed policymakers will also note that the US jobless rate has dropped back to 4.2% \u8211\'20\'13 not a move typically associated with a steep downturn.\par \par \uc2 [Interest rates are typically adjusted in moves of 25 basis point, or a quarter of one percentage point \u8211\'20\'13 but they can be moved by 50bp, or 75bp, or even more if central bankers feel the need to act dramatically].\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 2.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 US hospitality firms, construction, healthcare and local government added the most new jobs last month.\par \par \uc2 But jobs were lost in manufacturing, the retail trade, and at technology firms:\par \par \uc2 enltrJob gains in August: +142,000. Nice bounce in construction, healthcare and hospitality. Otherwise most industries slowing down.\par \par \uc2 Hospitality +46,000\par \par \uc2 Construction +34,000\par \par \uc2 Healthcare +31,000\par \par \uc2 Local gov't +22,000\par \par \uc2 Social aid +13,000\par \par \uc2 Finance +11,000\par \par \uc2 Biz services +8,000\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Heather Long (@byHeatherLong) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/byHeatherLong/status/1832036792805060960?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.54pm BST\par \par \uc2 The 142,000 increase in nonfarm payroll employment in August is \u8220\'20\'1Cin line with average job growth in recent months\u8221\'20\'1D, says the \uc2 BLS\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 However, it\u8217\'20\'19s below the average monthly gain of 202,000 over the prior 12 months, showing there\u8217\'20\'19s been a cooling in job creation in 2024.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.52pm BST\par \par \uc2 US interest rate cut 'inevitable' after jobs report\par \par \uc2 A cut to US interest rates later this month is \u8220\'20\'1Call but guaranteed\u8221\'20\'1D after today\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1Cworse than feared US jobs data\u8221\'20\'1D, says Richard Carter, head of fixed interest research at \uc2 Quilter Cheviot\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But the question remains whether the Fed will take a modest approach, with a quarter-point cut, or make a dramatic half-point cut.\par \par \uc2 Carter says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s US jobs data is expected to determine the size and pace of the highly anticipated Federal Reserve rate cuts, and with the increase in nonfarm payrolls coming in worse than feared at 142,000, we will no doubt see increased speculation that the Fed will take decisive action with a 50bps cut on the 18 th of September.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAlongside this disappointing August figure, July\u8217\'20\'19s nonfarm payrolls number was also revised down from 114,000 to just 89,000. Meanwhile, the unemployment rate fell slightly to 4.2% following a rise to 4.3% in July, and wage growth came in at 3.8% on an annual basis, up 0.2% compared to 3.6% reported last month.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMarkets have been pricing in significant cuts before year end, with many economists touting more than 1%, and today\u8217\'20\'19s labour market print could exacerbate this further. As was the case last month, this data release has proven notably weaker than had been hoped, suggesting the economy may be weakening more than is consistent with the Fed\u8217\'20\'19s aim of a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhen considered alongside signs of softening elsewhere in the economy, today\u8217\'20\'19s worse than expected jobs data will all but guarantee a shift in the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s stance. The Fed\u8217\'20\'19s decision making is highly data sensitive, and with many datapoints continuously suggesting a slowing economy, it seems inevitable that we will see the first cut confirmed this month.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.49pm BST\par \par \uc2 The BIG question on Wall Street, and in Washington DC, whether the US economy is at risk of recession, or on track for a soft landing.\par \par \uc2 And unfortunately,, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report doesn\u8217\'20\'19t entirely resolve the recession debate.\par \par \uc2 So explains \uc2 Seema Shah\uc2 , chief global strategist at Principal Asset Management, who says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRarely has there been such a make or break number \u8211\'20\'13 unfortunately, today\u8217\'20\'19s jobs report doesn\u8217\'20\'19t entirely resolve the recession debate.\par \par \uc2 Significant negative revisions to July\u8217\'20\'19s already weak number, coupled with a softer-than-expected August number, offset the good news from the fall in the unemployment rate and rise in hours worked. For the Fed, the decision comes down to deciding which is the bigger risk: reigniting inflation pressures if they cut by 50bps or threatening recession if they only cut by 25bps.\par \par \uc2 On balance, with inflation pressures subdued, there is no reason for the Fed not to err on the side of caution and frontload rate cuts.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 Analyst: It's a mixed picture\par \par \uc2 The August US labour market report painted something of a mixed picture of the employment situation, says \uc2 Michael Brown\uc2 , senior research strategist at brokerage Pepperstone.\par \par \uc2 Brown explains:\par \par \uc2 Headline nonfarm payrolls marginally missed expectations, at +142k last month, though such a print was well within the forecast range, even if a 2-month net revision of -86k is hardly anything to be pleased about.\par \par \uc2 On a more positive note, unemployment fell to 4.2%, as a degree of temporary weather-related weakness in July was unwound, while participation remained unchanged at 62.7%, just shy of cycle highs. Earnings, finally, were a touch hotter than expected, having risen by 0.4% MoM, bringing the annual rate 0.2pp higher to 3.8%.\par \par \uc2 All of this does little to clear-up the debate over the September Fed meeting, he adds:\par \par \uc2 Doves will point to a cooling pace of headline payrolls growth as potential reasoning for a larger 50bp cut. Hawks, meanwhile, will reasonably point towards the lack of further cooling compared to the July report, and hot-ish earnings growth, as reasons to kick-off the normalisation cycle with a more modest 25bp move.\par \par \uc2 My base case remains for the latter, particularly given the risk the Fed run of sparking a market panic were a larger cut to be delivered.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.47pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.43pm BST\par \par \uc2 Dollar weakens after jobs report\par \par \uc2 The dollar is weakening against other currencies, after fewer jobs were created in the US last month than expected.\par \par \uc2 That could be an indication that more investors are expecting a hefty cut to US interest rates when the Fed meets later this month.\par \par \uc2 The pound has gained half a cent against the dollar, to $1.323 \u8211\'20\'13 approaching August\u8217\'20\'19s two-year high.\par \par \uc2 The euro has risen by 0.2% to $1.1135.\par \par \uc2 enltr??JUST IN: US Non-Farm Payrolls +142k in August - lower than expected but higher than in July. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Bitcoin?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Bitcoin}}}\uc2  rips, Dollar dips as Fed rate cut expectations rise and yield curve uninverts again. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/IGqEjH9ny0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/IGqEjH9ny0}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andr\u233\'00\'E9 Dragosch, PhD | Bitcoin & Macro ? (@Andre_Dragosch) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Andre_Dragosch/status/1832034633279553907?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 Job gains occurred in construction and health care last month, the \uc2 Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.34pm BST\par \par \uc2 US economy added 142,000 new jobs in August\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: The US economy added fewer jobs than expected last month, but there\u8217\'20\'19s a welcome fall in the jobless rate.\par \par \uc2 Payroll employment across the US rose by 142,000 in August, the \uc2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics\uc2  reports.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s a little below expectations for around 160,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 Worryingly, June and July\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payrolls have been revised down too, to show fewer jobs created than first thought \u8211\'20\'13 a sign of softening in the labor market this summer.\par \par \uc2 The change in total nonfarm payroll employment for June was revised down by 61,000, from +179,000 to +118,000, and the change for July was revised down by 25,000, from +114,000 to +89,000.\par \par \uc2 In better news, the unemployment rate has dipped to 4.2%, down from July\u8217\'20\'19s 4.3%. That might calm some worries that the US was sliding into recession.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 As usual, there\u8217\'20\'19s a wide range of estimates for today\u8217\'20\'19s non-farm payroll from Wall Street\u8217\'20\'19s finest, from 120,000 to 205,000 new jobs.\par \par \uc2 enltr???? NFP due in ONE hour!\par \par \uc2 Consensus sees 160k jobs added and unemployment rate dropping to 4.2%\par \par \uc2 Trader view ??\par \par \uc2 - A big downside MISS would likely trigger USD weakness as markets price in bigger, faster, harder rate cuts\par \par \uc2 - A big upside BEAT would likely see USD strength as\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/G9g3rMwrXa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/G9g3rMwrXa}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Axi (@axi_official) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/axi_official/status/1832018739212005552?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 The NFP is notoriously tricky to predict \u8211\'20\'13 and is often revised in subsequent months too, adding to the challenge of assessing the US jobs market.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.22pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  are predicting we\u8217\'20\'19ll get a weaker-than-expected jobs report.\par \par \uc2 They estimate nonfarm payrolls rose by 155k in August, which they say is \u8220\'20\'1Cbelow consensus of +165k and the three-month average of +170k\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Goldman point out that job openings declined by 200,000 to 7.7m in July, according to the JOLTS survey this week, which could mean less hiring in August.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.10pm BST\par \par \uc2 The US dollar weakened to a one-month low against the yen this morning, ahead of the non-farm payrolls.\par \par \uc2 The yen hit \u165\'00\'A5142.05 to $1, its strongest position since the market turmoil of 5 August, before slipping back.\par \par \uc2 The dollar is flat against a basket of currencies today\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobs report coming up....\par \par \uc2 Investors are bracing for the final major economic news of the week \u8211\'20\'13 the US non-farm payroll for August.\par \par \uc2 The jobs report will have a crucial impact on interest rates in the US, with the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2  expected to lower borrowing costs this month to ward off a recession. A bad jobs report could spur the Fed into a deeper cut to interest rates.\par \par \uc2 August\u8217\'20\'19s NFP will also give an insight into the heath of the US labor market, with just two months to go until the presidential elections.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/sep/04/kamala-harris-leads-us-polls-state-level-data-race-knife-edge-donald-trump"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Kamala Harris now leads in US polls but state-level data puts race on knife-edge}}}\par \par \uc2 After {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 a shock slowdown in hiring in July}}}\uc2 , economists expect a pick-up in job creation in August. The NFP is forecast to rise by around 160,000 last month, up from July\u8217\'20\'19s 114,000 (which may be revised).\par \par \uc2 The unemployment rate, which jumped to 4.3% in July, may drop back to 4.2%.\par \par \uc2 Derren Nathan, head of equity research at \uc2 Hargreaves Lansdown\uc2 , says there are nerves in the markets, with European stocks a little lower today.\par \par \uc2 Nathan adds:\par \par \uc2 If growth meets or beats forecasts of 160,000 there may be something of a relief rally, but any miss could be punished harshly. To put things in context though, growth is still growth, and a soft-landing doesn\u8217\'20\'19t mean a slowdown can be completely avoided.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.51pm BST\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  wins bumper order from private customer\par \par \uc2 European aeroplane manufacturer \uc2 Airbus\uc2  has landed a bumper order from a private customer.\par \par \uc2 Reuters reports that \uc2 Airbus\uc2  landed a rare order from a single \u8220\'20\'1Cprivate customer\u8221\'20\'1D for three A350-900s and three A320neos worth well over $1bn at catalogue prices.\par \par \uc2 Reuters adds:\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  declined to comment but typically uses the \u8220\'20\'1Cprivate customer\u8221\'20\'1D designation to describe jets bought to be used by VIPs in luxury configurations or by governments. Demand for such aircraft remains strong in the Middle East, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 Airbus\uc2  also reported that it booked 46 new orders overall in August, and made 47 deliveries to 31 customers ( {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://aircraft.airbus.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2024-09-orders-deliveries-august-2024"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 details here}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 The CMA\u8217\'20\'19s findings come as \uc2 Alphabet\uc2 , the parent company of \uc2 Google\uc2 , braces for a showdown in the US courts over its business practices.\par \par \uc2 On Monday, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.reuters.com/technology/us-antitrust-trial-targets-googles-digital-ad-business-2024-09-04/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the company will face the US Department of Justice in a Virginia court over similar allegations}}}\uc2  that it exerts monopolistic control of digital advertising.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.13pm BST\par \par \uc2 Flat prices have underperformed all other property types in the UK over the past five years, new analysis from the \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  shows.\par \par \uc2 This is due to the shift to flexible working, landlords exiting the market and concerns about cladding hit the segment.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.ft.com/content/21caac74-41e3-4b8f-a42a-bb4add103313"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The FT reports}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 The average cost of an apartment has risen at half the pace of any other property type since 2019, before the Covid-19 pandemic, according to a \uc2 Financial Times\uc2  analysis of official data.\par \par \uc2 Figures from Land Registry, the register of property ownership, show the average flat cost \u163\'00\'A3232,400 in June, up 14 per cent on the same month in 2019. In the same period, the average property price rose by about 25 per cent to \u163\'00\'A3288,000, while detached, semi-detached and terraced house prices rose by almost 30 per cent.\par \par \uc2 enltrFT (\u163\'00\'A3) UK apartment prices underperform rest of market since 2019: Figures from Land Registry show the average flat rose 14% since 2019. In the same period, the average house price rose by about 25%. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/M7wSzSdA44"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/M7wSzSdA44}}}\uc2  via {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FT?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 @ft}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Peter Bill (@peterproperty) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/peterproperty/status/1831955842373185907?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.06pm BST\par \par \uc2 Looking back at UK house prices\u8230\'20\'26. \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  predicts a \u8220\'20\'1Crobust autumn selling season\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Following the news from Halifax that {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/06/uk-house-prices-hit-two-year-high-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66da938d8f08da0c09ad4150#block-66da938d8f08da0c09ad4150"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 prices hit a two-year high in August}}}\uc2 , \uc2 RBC\uc2  told clients:\par \par \uc2 House prices leapt by 4.3% in the year to August 2024, the highest rate of annual house price inflation since November 2022, as easing mortgage rates are boosting homebuyer confidence. We expect this positive momentum to continue for the remainder of 2024 and to see a robust autumn selling season as mortgage rates continue to fall.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.28am BST\par \par \uc2 The eurozone grew more slowly than first estimated this spring, in a blow to Europe\u8217\'20\'19s recovery.\par \par \uc2 Statistics body \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  has revised down its estimate for GDP growth in the euro area, from 0.3% to 0.2%.\par \par \uc2 Poland (+1.5%) recorded the highest increase of GDP compared to the previous quarter, followed by Greece (+1.1%) and the Netherlands (+1.0%). The highest decreases were observed in Ireland (-1.0%), Latvia (-0.9%) and Austria (-0.4%).\par \par \uc2 enltrEurozone growth is revised down to 0.2% from 0.3% in the second quarter, the latest bit of bad news for the \uc2 ECB{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Q8u3FR1KPm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/Q8u3FR1KPm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg (@business) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/business/status/1831986773120921878?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Eurostat\uc2  also reports that:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 household final consumption expenditure decreased by 0.1% in the euro area and increased by 0.1% in the EU (after +0.3% in the euro area and +0.4% in the EU in the previous quarter),\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 government final consumption expenditure increased by 0.6% in the euro area and by 0.7% in the EU (after +0.1% in both zones in the previous quarter),\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 gross fixed capital formation decreased by 2.2% in the euro area and by 1.8% the EU (after -1.8% and -1.7% respectively),\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 exports increased by 1.4% both in the euro area and in the EU\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.09am BST\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  disagrees with the CMA\u8217\'20\'19s view of its advertising technology, and will \u8220\'20\'1Crespond accordingly\u8221\'20\'1D, Reuters reports.\par \par \uc2 enltrALPHABET'S \uc2 GOOGLE\uc2  SAYS IT DISAGREES WITH THE UK COMPETITION REGULATOR'S VIEW ON ITS AD TECHNOLOGY AND WILL RESPOND ACCORDINGLY\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 First Squawk (@FirstSquawk) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/FirstSquawk/status/1831990401454010388?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.07am BST\par \par \uc2 The CMA isn\u8217\'20\'19t the only regulator concerned about \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ad-tech business.\par \par \uc2 Last June, the \uc2 EU\uc2  ordered {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/google"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Google}}}\uc2  to sell part of its advertising business, to address similar worries:\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2023/jun/14/eu-regulator-google-sell-ad-tech-business-competition-commission"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 EU regulator orders Google to sell part of ad-tech business}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.17am BST\par \par \uc2 CMA objects to \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s ad tech practices\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Britain\u8217\'20\'19s competition authority believes \uc2 Google\uc2  is using anti-competitive practices in the market for adverts on websites and apps, potentially harming thousands of UK publishers and advertisers.\par \par \uc2 Following an investigation, the \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  has provisionally found that \uc2 Google\uc2  has abused its dominant positions through the operation of both its publisher ad server and buying tools to restrict competition in the UK.\par \par \uc2 The CMA says \uc2 Google\uc2  \u8216\'20\'18self-preferences\u8217\'20\'19 its own ad exchange - harming competition and, as a result, advertisers and publishers.\par \par \uc2 This relates to the rapid series of auctions and transactions which takes place when a user opens a web page or app, to determine which ads will be shown.\par \par \uc2 The CMA accuses \uc2 Google\uc2  of abusing its dominant position to strengthen the market position of its AdX service \u8211\'20\'13 an ad exchange that matches bids from publishers and advertisers.\par \par \uc2 Google\uc2  also operates ad buying tools for publishers, and for advertisers, and the CMA believes it has conducted various practices that give AdX competitive advantages, and disadvantage \uc2 Google\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s rivals.\par \par \uc2 They include:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 providing AdX with exclusive or preferential access to advertisers that use \uc2 Google\uc2  Ads\u8217\'20\'19 platform;\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 manipulating advertiser bids so that they have a higher value when submitted into AdX\u8217\'20\'19s auction than when submitted into rival exchanges\u8217\'20\'19 auctions; and\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 allowing AdX to bid first in auctions run by DFP for online advertising space, effectively giving it an \u8216\'20\'18right of first refusal\u8217\'20\'19 - with rivals potentially not having any chance to submit bids.\par \par \uc2 The regulator has laid out its concerns in a statement of objections today.\par \par \uc2 Juliette Enser, interim executive director of enforcement at the CMA, explains:\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve provisionally found that \uc2 Google\uc2  is using its market power to hinder competition when it comes to the ads people see on websites.\par \par \uc2 Many businesses are able to keep their digital content free or cheaper by using online advertising to generate revenue. Adverts on these websites and apps reach millions of people across the UK \u8211\'20\'13 assisting the buying and selling of goods and services.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s why it\u8217\'20\'19s so important that publishers and advertisers \u8211\'20\'13 who enable this free content \u8211\'20\'13 can benefit from effective competition and get a fair deal when buying or selling digital advertising space.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 11.07am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.57am BST\par \par \uc2 Nationwide\u8217\'20\'19s \u163\'00\'A32.9bn takeover of rival \uc2 Virgin Money\uc2  is expected to complete next month after the UK\u8217\'20\'19s financial regulators cleared the deal.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.londonstockexchange.com/news-article/NBS/regulatory-approval-and-timetable-update/16652410"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The lenders told the City this morning}}}\uc2  that the \uc2 Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)\uc2  and the \uc2 Bank of England\u8217\'20\'19s Prudential Regulation Authority\uc2  (\uc2 PRA\uc2 ) have both approved the takeover.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/jul/19/nationwide-takeover-of-virgin-money-cleared-by-uk-watchdog"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Competition and Markets Authority gave its approval back in July}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.17am BST\par \par \uc2 World food prices ease slightly in August, \uc2 UN\uc2  says\par \par \uc2 Global food prices fell slightly last month, driven by cheaper sugar, meat and cereal quotations.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 UN\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s world food price index, just released, dipped to 120.7 points in August from 121 points in July.\par \par \uc2 The index gives important detail to the drivers of food commodity prices, which are lower than in their peak in 2022.\par \par \uc2 Today, it show that global wheat export prices fell month-on-month, due to \u8220\'20\'1Csluggish international demand\u8221\'20\'1D and strong competition among exporters, especially from \u8220\'20\'1Ccompetitively priced Black Sea supplies\u8221\'20\'1D. Higher than expected wheat production in Argentina and the US also pushed down prices.\par \par \uc2 The sugar price index fell by 4.7% compared with July.\par \par \uc2 The UN\u8217\'20\'19s Food and Agriculture Association says:\par \par \uc2 The decline in August was mainly driven by the improving production outlook for the 2024/25 season in Thailand and India, following favourable rainfall that benefited sugarcane crops. In addition, lower international crude oil prices exerted further downward pressure on sugar prices.\par \par \uc2 Meat prices fell by 0.7%, including a drop in poultry prices in Brazil where {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.poultryworld.net/health-nutrition/health/brazil-suspends-poultry-exports-due-to-newcastle-disease-outbreak/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  exports were suspended du to an outbreak of Newcastle Disease}}}\uc2  . World pig prices also dropped, due to a slowdown in import purchases, especially by China.\par \par \uc2 But not everything got cheaper. Vegetable oil prices rose by 0.8% month-on-month, due to higher world palm oil prices. Dairy prices rose by 2.2%, driven by whole milk powder, skimmed milk powder and butter.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.18am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.37am BST\par \par \uc2 Rolls-Royce\uc2  shares fall after \uc2 EASA\uc2  orders checks on \uc2 Airbus\uc2  A350-1000 engines\par \par \uc2 Shares in engineering firm Rolls-Royce have fallen over 1%, after Europe\u8217\'20\'19s air safety regulator ordered inspections on the Airbus A350-1000 fleet, powered by its Trent XWB-97.\par \par \uc2 The European Union Aviation Safety Agency (\uc2 EASA\uc2 ) acted afte {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/02/rolls-royce-ftse-100-biggest-faller-cathay-pacific-inspects-a350"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 r a Cathay Pacific A350-1000 suffered an engine fire on Monday}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 EASA\uc2  executive director Florian Guillermet said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis action is a precautionary measure, based on the information received from the initial investigation of the recent \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  serious incident and on the airline\u8217\'20\'19s findings in its own subsequent inspections.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will continue to follow closely all information that will be made available through the ongoing safety investigation.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 EASA\uc2  explains that the flight from Hong Kong to Zurich \u8220\'20\'1Cexperienced an in-flight engine fire shortly after take-off\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 The regulator adds:\par \par \uc2 The fire was promptly detected and extinguished, and the aircraft returned safely to Hong Kong.\par \par \uc2 Rolls-Royce\uc2  has confirmed that the \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  aircraft was powered by its Trent XWB-97 engines.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/03/what-happened-to-cathay-pacifics-a350-and-how-will-it-affect-rolls-royce"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 What happened to Cathay Pacific\u8217\'20\'19s A350 and how will it affect Rolls-Royce?}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.29am BST\par \par \uc2 Markets dip ahead of US jobs report\par \par \uc2 European stock markets are lower in early trading, as investors anticipate today\u8217\'20\'19s US employment report (at 1.30pm UK time).\par \par \uc2 The FTSE 100 index is down 41 points, or 0.5%, at 8200 points. That would be its sixth daily loss in a row, its worst run since May.\par \par \uc2 Germany\u8217\'20\'19s DAX has lost 0.45%, and France\u8217\'20\'19s CAC is 0.3% lower.\par \par \uc2 UBS\uc2  reports that the market mood remains cautious ahead of the US jobs data\par \par \uc2 Mark Haefele\uc2 , chief investment officer at UBS Global Wealth Management, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CGiven the importance of the jobs data, investors should brace for volatility in the wake of the release. Our base case remains for a soft economic landing, though recession risks have increased. We recommend focusing on long-term financial plans and ensuring adequate diversification.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.17am BST\par \par \uc2 German industrial output falls more than expected\par \par \uc2 There\u8217\'20\'19s more gloom in the German economy this morning, where factory output has fallen sharply.\par \par \uc2 German industrial production fell by 2.4% in July, statistics body Destatis reports, much worse than the 0.3% fall predicted by economists.\par \par \uc2 Production dropped by 8.1% in the automotive industry, following a 7.9% rise in June.\par \par \uc2 The declines in the manufacture of electrical equipment (-7.0%) and the manufacture of metal products (-3.8%) also had a significantly negative impact, Destatis adds.\par \par \uc2 deltrDie reale (preisbereinigte) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Produktion?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Produktion}}}\uc2  im Produzierenden Gewerbe ist im Juli 2024 gegen\u252\'00\'FCber Juni 2024 voraussichtlich um 2,4 % gesunken. Vor allem der R\u252\'00\'FCckgang in der {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Automobilindustrie?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Automobilindustrie}}}\uc2  beeinflusste das Gesamtergebnis stark (-8,1 %). Weitere Infos: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/wbaFnqVuRO"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/wbaFnqVuRO}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/MW4h1rjNqV"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/MW4h1rjNqV}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Statistisches Bundesamt (@destatis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1831938540189708436?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Carsten Brzeski\uc2 , global head of macro at ING, says:\par \par \uc2 A drop in German industrial production in July not only brings a weak start to the third quarter but is another illustration of how difficult it will be to bring the economy back to strong growth.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.59am BST\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s Halifax house price index adds to the evidence that we are in the early stages of a recovery in house prices, argues Peter Arnold, EY UK chief economist.\par \par \uc2 But, Arnold also predicts that house price growth will remain slow in the next couple of years.\par \par \uc2 He explains:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 A second successive monthly increase suggests that house prices are in the early stages of a recovery. The EY ITEM Club attributes the upturn to mortgage affordability improving due to lower quoted mortgage rates and strong nominal wage growth.\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 The EY ITEM Club expects house price growth to remain in the slow lane over the next couple of years, with transactions staying relatively low. Though mortgage affordability is better than it was last summer, it remains very stretched. Other affordability metrics also remain high, which will continue to limit the pool of potential buyers.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 UK homebuilder Berkeley says trading stable\par \par \uc2 UK house builder Berkeley has reported this morning that trading has been stable between 1 May 2024 to 31 August \u8211\'20\'13 the first four months of its current fiscal year.\par \par \uc2 Berkeley is also confident it will hit its profit targets, and is (predictably) backing the new government\u8217\'20\'19s drive to speed up houebuilding.\par \par \uc2 It told the City:\par \par \uc2 Berkeley supports the proposed changes to the planning system and the Government\u8217\'20\'19s aspiration to deliver 1.5 million new homes across this Parliament as part of its mission for growth.\par \par \uc2 Achieving this ambition requires a change of attitude and a refreshed partnership approach to allow developments, that are currently stalled, to come forward and Berkeley is committed to playing its full part in delivering the new homes the Country needs.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.43am BST\par \par \uc2 Jonathan Hopper\uc2 , CEO of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.garrington.co.uk/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Garrington Property Finders}}}\uc2 , predicts we\u8217\'20\'19ll see an \u8220\'20\'1Cautumn bounce\u8221\'20\'1D in house prices \u8211\'20\'13 with buyers benefitting from more affordable mortgages.\par \par \uc2 After Halifax reported a rise in prices in August this morning, Hopper says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith interest rates falling across the board and some lenders now offering rates not seen since the \uc2 Liz Truss\uc2  era, thousands of buyers who had been sitting on the fence have sprung into action - with many setting themselves the goal of being \u8216\'20\'18in by Christmas\u8217\'20\'19.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis upswing in demand is starting to push up prices strongly in some areas, but not all. Behind the momentum revealed by the national averages, prices are rising fastest in areas where value is strongest. In fact in the most expensive parts of London and the Southeast, we\u8217\'20\'19re still seeing prices come down.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s why the Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s data shows average prices in Wales are rising nearly four times faster than those in London, while prices in North West England are outpacing those in the capital by nearly three to one.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.30am BST\par \par \uc2 Darktrace\uc2  CEO \uc2 Poppy Gustafsson\uc2  stepping down\par \par \uc2 Poppy Gustafsson\uc2  , the co-founder and chief executive of British cybersecurity firm \uc2 Darktrace\uc2 , is to leave the company following {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/apr/26/cybersecurity-firm-darktrace-agrees-sale-to-us-private-equity-business"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  its $5.3bn (\u163\'00\'A34.2bn) sale to US private equity business Thoma Bravo}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Gustafsson, one of the most well-known figures in the UK tech industry, founded \uc2 Darktrace\uc2  in Cambridge in 2013 with the backing from the late billionaire tycoon Mike Lynch\u8217\'20\'19s Invoke Capital.\par \par \uc2 In April, \uc2 Thoma Bravo\uc2 , which walked away from previous takeover talks in 2022, agreed a deal for the London-listed company which had been considered by analysts to be undervalued by investors.\par \par \uc2 Gustafsson is to step down with immediate effect and will be replaced by \uc2 Jill Popelka\uc2 , \uc2 Darktrace\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s current chief operating officer.\par \par \uc2 Gustafsson, who was awarded an OBE for services to cyber security in 2019, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Darktrace\uc2  has been a huge part of my life and my identity for over a decade and I am immensely proud of everything we have achieved in that time.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CNow is the right time to hand over the reins so Jill can lead \uc2 Darktrace\uc2  through its transition into private ownership and beyond. I remain \uc2 Darktrace\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s number one fan.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Lynch and his wife Angela Bacares held a 6.8% stake in \uc2 Darktrace\uc2  which was worth \u163\'00\'A3300m at the time of the sale.\par \par \uc2 Lynch died last month when his superyacht sunk off the coast of Italy in a storm.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/world/article/2024/aug/22/body-found-missing-uk-tech-entrepreneur-mike-lynch-yacht-bayesian-sicily"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Mike Lynch confirmed dead after yacht sank off Sicily coast during storm}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.48am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.26am BST\par \par \uc2 Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s house price index shows the UK property market is \u8220\'20\'1Ccontinuing on its road to recovery\u8221\'20\'1D, says Liz Edwards, money expert at personal finance site {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.finder.com/uk/author/lizedwards"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 finder.com}}}\uc2  :\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CToday\u8217\'20\'19s figures indicate that the UK property market is continuing on its road to recovery. Last week, data from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  revealed that mortgage approvals have risen to the highest level since the mini-Budget in September 2022. As well as this, many are confident that there will be further cuts to the base rate before the end of the year, which should help stimulate the housing market even further. In fact, when we recently surveyed a panel of experts, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.finder.com/uk/banking#boe-panel"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 80% predicted}}}\uc2  that there will be at least one more base rate cut before the end of 2024.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThere\u8217\'20\'19s a chance that the upcoming October Budget could dampen buyer confidence slightly, as we wait to see what the new government has in store for our finances, but I think that any stall in the market is likely to be temporary.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 enltrHouse prices continued to rise during the summer albeit at a slower pace. Increasing 0.3% on July\u8217\'20\'19s efforts to \u163\'00\'A3292,505.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis Northern Ireland led the house price comeback, increasing +9.8%, followed by Wales +5.5%, the North West at +4.0%, then Scotland at +1.7% &\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/XjQpAw91jw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/XjQpAw91jw}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Emma Fildes (@emmafildes) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/emmafildes/status/1831941488755822784?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 6, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.22am BST\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland records the strongest annual house price growth\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland continues to record the strongest annual house price growth in the UK, Halifax reports.\par \par \uc2 Northern Ireland continues to record the strongest property price growth of any nation or region in the UK, rising by +9.8% on an annual basis in August. The average price of a property in Northern Ireland is now \u163\'00\'A3201,043.\par \par \uc2 The average price of a property in Northern Ireland is now \u163\'00\'A3201,043. House prices in Wales also recorded strong growth, up +5.5%, compared to the previous year, with properties now costing an average of \u163\'00\'A3224,433.\par \par \uc2 Scotland saw a more modest rise in house prices, where a typical property now costs \u163\'00\'A3205,144, +1.7% more than the year before.\par \par \uc2 The North West once again recorded the strongest house price growth of any region in England, up by +4.0% over the last year, to sit at \u163\'00\'A3232,917.\par \par \uc2 London continues to have the most expensive property prices in the UK, now averaging \u163\'00\'A3536,056, up +1.5% compared to last year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.20am BST\par \par \uc2 Looking ahead, Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s Amanda Bryden predicts that house prices will continue to rise during 2024:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith market activity picking up and the possibility of further interest rate reductions to come, we expect house prices to continue their modest growth through the remainder of this year.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: UK house prices edge up to hit two-year high\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 UK house prices have risen close to the record high set two years ago, a new survey this morning shows, as confidence in the market picks up.\par \par \uc2 Lender Halifax has just reported that the average price of a house sold in August rose to \u163\'00\'A3292,505, the highest since August 2022. That\u8217\'20\'19s only \u163\'00\'A31,000 shy of the record high set in June 2022.\par \par \uc2 Halifax\u8217\'20\'19s monthly gauge of the housing market also shows that prices rose by +0.3% in August, following a rise of +0.9% in July.\par \par \uc2 On an annual basis, prices rose by +4.3%, the strongest year-on-year rise since November 2022.\par \par \uc2 Recent cuts to mortgage rates have helped buyers afford homes; that trend could continue, with the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  expected to cut interest rates at least once more this year (after it started easing policy in August).\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/article/2024/may/16/three-uk-banks-announce-cuts-to-cost-of-fixed-rate-mortgages"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Three UK banks announce cuts to cost of fixed-rate mortgages}}}\par \par \uc2 Halifax points out that this higher annual growth largely reflects the base impact of weaker prices a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Amanda Bryden, head of mortgages at Halifax, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRecent price rises build on a largely positive summer for the UK housing market. Prospective homebuyers are feeling more confident thanks to easing interest rates. That optimism is reflected in the latest mortgage approval figures, now at their highest level in almost two years.\par \par \uc2 Such has been the resilience of house prices that the average property is now just \u163\'00\'A31,000 shy of the record high set in June 2022 (\u163\'00\'A3293,507). While this is welcome news for existing homeowners, affordability remains a significant challenge for many potential buyers still adjusting to higher mortgage costs.\par \par \uc2 Reaction to follow\u8230\'20\'26.\par \par \uc2 Also coming up today\par \par \uc2 Global investors are bracing for the latest US jobs report, which will influence how quickly, and deeply, America\u8217\'20\'19s central bank may cut interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Economists are hoping for a pick-up in hiring; expectations are for a 160,000 increase in payrolls in August, up from {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/aug/02/stock-markets-nikkei-ftse-us-gdp-growth-non-farm-payrolls-nfp-bank-of-england-andrew-bailey-business-live"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the disappointing 114,000 in July}}}\uc2 . The US unemployment rate is forecast to drop back to 4.2%, from 4.3%.\par \par \uc2 Jim Reid, strategist at \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2 , sets the scene:\par \par \uc2 After much anticipation, we have finally arrived at the latest US jobs report day, which is of crucial importance as the Fed decides how much to cut rates this month.\par \par \uc2 It was only five weeks ago that the last jobs report underwhelmed, with payrolls growth down to just +114k alongside negative revision to the previous couple of months. So the big question today is whether that disappointing report was just a blip, or was it the start of a more serious deterioration.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: Halifax house price index for August\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 7am BST: German industrial output for July\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: \uc2 UN\uc2  food price index\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 10am BST: Eurozone final GDP Q2 report\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US non-farm payroll report for August\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240906ek96000rx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
