\par\par \uc2 Law\par \b \uc2 How the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s affirmative action ruling unleashed anti-DEI cases\b0\par\par\uc2 Lauren Aratani \par \uc2 1,471 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 September 2024\par 15:34 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Activists against diversity, equity and inclusion feel the 6-3 conservative majority will aid workplace lawsuits\par \par \uc2 Days after the \uc2 US supreme court\uc2  ruled affirmative action in college admissions unconstitutional, the legal activist behind the big win for conservatives called the ruling \u8220\'20\'1Cthe end of the beginning\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThis issue of race and ethnicity in our public lives is not going to go away,\u8221\'20\'1D \uc2 Edward Blum{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.nytimes.com/2023/07/08/us/edward-blum-affirmative-action-race.html"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 told}}}\uc2  the \uc2 New York Times\uc2  in July 2023. \u8220\'20\'1CAll of these preferences, whether it\u8217\'20\'19s in the employment arena, contracting arena, internships \u8211\'20\'13 all of that I think will be energized by this supreme court opinion. And we\u8217\'20\'19re blessed to have this supreme court opinion.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 True to Blum\u8217\'20\'19s word, conservative groups have unleashed a flood of lawsuits since the ruling with the ultimate goal of dismantling {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/dec/16/diversity-dei-us-companies-backlash"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 diversity, equity and inclusion (DEI) policies in the American workplace}}}\uc2 . In the year since the supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s affirmative action decision, 68 lawsuits that call DEI into question have been filed.\par \par \uc2 This legal fight looks to be much more complicated than efforts to overturn affirmative action. DEI encompasses many different policies and practices designed to make workplaces more equitable, from hiring diverse candidates to preventing bias in the workplace. Many DEI policies started as measures to protect marginalized workers from discrimination and strengthening their positions in the workplace through initiatives like mentorship programs and de-bias training. But conservatives against DEI see this as the best moment to get DEI out of the American workplace.\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars with NYU School of Law\u8217\'20\'19s Meltzer Center for Diversity, Inclusion and Belonging have been tracking anti-DEI lawsuits and recently launched a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://advancingdei.meltzercenter.org/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 database}}}\uc2  of more than 100 lawsuits that could change DEI.\par \par \uc2 The Guardian spoke with David Glasgow, executive director of the Meltzer center, who broke down what the battle against DEI is starting to look like.\par \par \uc2 What is DEI?\par \par \uc2 Because DEI is a pretty broad term, breaking down the acronym can be helpful, Glasgow said.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDiversity\u8221\'20\'1D covers the policies that aim to diversify a workforce, like efforts in hiring and promotion. \u8220\'20\'1CEquity\u8221\'20\'1D is about creating fairness within an existing workforce, like access to mentorship or employee benefits. And \u8220\'20\'1Cinclusion\u8221\'20\'1D covers workplace culture, creating an environment where employees feel a sense of belonging at work.\par \par \uc2 DEI has been redefined by conservatives, who often use it as a way to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2024/apr/21/dei-language-conservatives-baltimore"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 mock}}}\uc2  any issue that has to deal with race or equity. But Glasgow points out DEI is not only focused on race-conscious efforts. It includes gender, sexual orientation, religion and disability, among other categories {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.eeoc.gov/employers/small-business/3-who-protected-employment-discrimination"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 protected}}}\uc2  from discrimination by federal employment law.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CDEI includes a whole bunch of different dimensions, of race, gender and the like. It just happens, though, that a lot of litigation is more focused on race,\u8221\'20\'1D Glasgow said.\par \par \uc2 From colleges to the workplace\par \par \uc2 The supreme court\u8217\'20\'19s affirmative action decision opened the floodgates for what has been a heavy stream of lawsuits attacking DEI in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 Even though the affirmative action ruling applied only to students\u8217\'20\'19 admission to colleges, the decision signaled that the supreme court, which has a 6-3 conservative majority, believes it\u8217\'20\'19s wrong to classify people by race, regardless of the reason why a classification is used.\par \par \uc2 In her scathing dissent, Justice \uc2 Sonia Sotomayor\uc2  called this view \u8220\'20\'1Ca superficial rule of colorblindness as a constitutional principle in an endemically segregated society\u8221\'20\'1D. In other words, \u8220\'20\'1Cnot seeing\u8221\'20\'1D race does not mean racism does not exist.\par \par \uc2 But the majority\u8217\'20\'19s opinion \u8220\'20\'1Cunleashed hope on the anti-DEI side that they could make significant progress through the courts to change the law\u8221\'20\'1D, Glasgow said. If conservative activists can overturn affirmative action, what can they do about the workplace?\par \par \uc2 A flood of anti-DEI lawsuits\par \par \uc2 Since the affirmative action ruling last summer, the Meltzer center has identified 68 new lawsuits that target several types of policies of DEI.\par \par \uc2 Many of the lawsuits focus on {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/article/2024/jun/04/black-women-business-grant-suspended-court"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 targeted programs}}}\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 scholarships, grants, fellowships or internships that are meant for specific groups. For example, the American Alliance for Equal Rights, headed by Blum of the affirmative-action cases, filed a lawsuit against Fearless Fund, an investment fund focused on funding Black women and other business founders from marginalized groups.\par \par \uc2 Other lawsuits target {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2024/mar/08/texas-minority-business-development-lawsuit"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 government programs}}}\uc2 , many of which have requirements specifying a number of local board members be from underrepresented groups. Many also attack required diversity training.\par \par \uc2 Another strain of lawsuits are workplace discrimination suits where a plaintiff, from a dominant or majority group, sues a company for \u8220\'20\'1Creverse discrimination\u8221\'20\'1D. In February, a white freelance writer sued \uc2 CBS\uc2  and parent company Paramount, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/tv-and-radio/2024/mar/04/diversity-lawsuit-trump-adviser-cbs-seal-team"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 arguing that he was denied}}}\uc2  a staff writer position on the Seal Team TV series because of the company\u8217\'20\'19s diversity goals.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe goal of these organizations is to file as many lawsuits as possible, get as many cases pushed through the courts \u8230\'20\'26 to try to get the supreme court to review it and reach a decision,\u8221\'20\'1D Glasgow said. \u8220\'20\'1CThey realize it\u8217\'20\'19s a 6-3 conservative supermajority supreme court right now, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t know how long they are going to have this really friendly conservative court.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Upending precedent\par \par \uc2 Many of these lawsuits are trying to give the supreme court a chance to say that DEI policies violate two civil rights laws: one made after the civil war, and the other after the civil rights movement.\par \par \uc2 The first is section 1981 of the Civil Rights Act of 1866, a broad law that prohibits racial discrimination when making and enforcing contracts. The second is Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which prohibits employers from discriminating on the basis of race, color, sex, religion or national origin.\par \par \uc2 Both laws were created as an attempt to prevent economic exclusion of Black Americans, first after the end of slavery and then after the end of racial segregation. But now, conservative legal activists are saying white Americans are the ones facing economic exclusion.\par \par \uc2 This is not the first time in history that DEI in the workplace has been challenged. Glasgow notes there are a handful of supreme court cases from the 70s and 80s in which the court upheld forms of affirmative action in the workplace.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThose are still on the books, technically,\u8221\'20\'1D he said, and the decisions offer a layer of protection to DEI policies \u8211\'20\'13 at least for now. The appointment of a conservative-majority supreme court gave groups against affirmative action an opportunity to set a new precedent that has now made affirmative action illegal.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe anti-DEI were so focused on eliminating affirmative action education, they wanted to do one thing at a time. They wanted to get that precedent on the books and then turn their attention to corporate DEI,\u8221\'20\'1D Glasgow said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-DEI activists have already seen some of their legislation get shut down by federal courts, including Florida\u8217\'20\'19s \u8220\'20\'1CStop Woke\u8221\'20\'1D law that banned companies from requiring diversity training. An appeals court {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://apnews.com/article/stop-woke-florida-race-unconstitutional-fcf6c1678bb90709f5941f89d0c193af"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 deemed}}}\uc2  the law a violation of the first amendment.\par \par \uc2 But that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t stopped companies from backtracking on their DEI programs out of fear of the legal landscape.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CMy suspicion \u8230\'20\'26 is that a lot of the organizations that are retreating from DEI right now are the same organizations that weren\u8217\'20\'19t doing anything about DEI until George Floyd and then jumped on the bandwagon and did it because they were feeling pressure from that direction,\u8221\'20\'1D Glasgow said. \u8220\'20\'1CNow that they\u8217\'20\'19re feeling pressure in the other direction, they\u8217\'20\'19re backing off.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 The end of DEI?\par \par \uc2 Glasgow and other legal scholars at the Meltzer center say that many private companies are still invested in DEI, even amid the flurry of lawsuits. But the legal landscape has changed, even if it will take years for the supreme court to take on a DEI case.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CI\u8217\'20\'19ve had general counsel of companies say directly to me that one of the biggest challenges right now is to make the kinds of adjustments to DEI programs that are necessary within the current landscape, just to protect a company from a risk standpoint,\u8221\'20\'1D Glasgow said. \u8220\'20\'1CWhile making sure that employees of the organization realize that the company is still just as strongly committed to DEI as it was before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a stark contrast to 2020, when \uc2 Black Lives Matter\uc2  protests spurred companies to promote DEI policies in the name of racial justice. But Glasgow says companies should not get rid of DEI. Doing so actually makes them more liable to discrimination claims from underrepresented groups.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIt\u8217\'20\'19s not a matter of deciding to get rid as the easiest pathway to reducing risk. It\u8217\'20\'19s more about how we thread the needle in a way that conforms with the new legal landscape we\u8217\'20\'19re all living, but that doesn\u8217\'20\'19t shoot ourselves in the foot,\u8221\'20\'1D he said.\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240906ek96001mh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
