\par\par \uc2 Business\par \b \uc2 UK housebuilding growth fastest since mini-budget; US job layoffs surge as hiring weakens \u8211\'20\'13 as it happened\b0\par\par\uc2 Graeme Wearden \par \uc2 6,915 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 September 2024\par 15:03 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 UK construction sector has \u8220\'20\'1Cturned a corner\u8221\'20\'1D after a difficult start to the year, as housing activity rises at quickest pace since September 2022\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.46pm BST\par \par \uc2 Closing post\par \par \uc2 Time for a quick recap\u8230\'20\'26\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4#block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The UK\u8217\'20\'19s housing sector has grown at the fastest rate since 2022,}}}\uc2  when the mini-budget knocked the industry almost two years ago, as the construction sector \u8216\'20\'18turned a corner\u8217\'20\'19 after a difficult start to the year.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d966088f083fc999a38f45#block-66d966088f083fc999a38f45"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 But UK car sales fell in August}}}\uc2 , for the first time in just over two years, with the industry warning that the target for electric car sales this year will be missed.\par \par \uc2 In the US, a flurry of jobs data has painted a mixed picture, ahead of tomorrow\u8217\'20\'19s official employment report, the Non-Farm Payroll.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d9a13f8f08e168cb7cfe61#block-66d9a13f8f08e168cb7cfe61"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Company payrolls rose by less than expected in August}}}\uc2  ; they increased by 99,000, the smallest gain since 2021.\par \par \uc2 Job cuts also rose in August, with US employers announcing {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee#block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the greatest number of layoffs in five months}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But in better news, the number of Americans filing new claims for unemployment support {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d9a4a78f08e168cb7cfe91#block-66d9a4a78f08e168cb7cfe91"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 fell last week}}}\uc2 , remaining at historically low levels.\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/second-class-letter-deliveries-uk-could-be-scrapped-on-saturdays"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Royal Mail could be allowed to scrap second-class letter deliveries on Saturdays}}}\uc2 , as part of a shake-up of the company\u8217\'20\'19s universal service obligation.\par \par \uc2 In the retail world, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d9551c8f081743e33b5c90#block-66d9551c8f081743e33b5c90"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Currys}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d9551c8f081743e33b5c90#block-66d9551c8f081743e33b5c90"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  has reported a jump in sales of electrical goods}}}\uc2 , thanks to the Euro 2024 mens football tournament and interest in AI.\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82#block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 But }}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82#block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Primark}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82#block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2  has suffered a drop in UK sales this summer}}}\uc2 , as bad weather kept people off the high street and dampened demand for summer clothing.\par \par \uc2 The competition watchdog has launched an investigation into the {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/oasis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oasis}}}\uc2  ticket sales fiasco.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/watchdog-to-investigate-ticketmaster-over-oasis-ticket-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Watchdog to investigate Ticketmaster over Oasis ticket sales}}}\par \par \uc2 Petrol\uc2  and diesel pump prices in the UK have fallen to their lowest levels in nearly three years.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/politics/article/2024/sep/05/uk-pump-prices-at-three-year-low-but-fuel-duty-increases-expected-in-october-budget"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK pump prices at three-year low as fuel duty hike looms}}}\par \par \uc2 John Lewis is reviving its \u8220\'20\'1Cnever knowingly undersold\u8221\'20\'1D price promise.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/john-lewis-to-bring-back-never-knowingly-undersold-price-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Lewis brings back \u8216\'20\'18never knowingly undersold\u8217\'20\'19 promise}}}\par \par \uc2 And in Germany, three executives from the defunct German payments firm have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?page=with:block-66d992e78f08db0474a66fb0#block-66d992e78f08db0474a66fb0"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ordered to pay \u8364\'20\'AC140m in damages today}}}\uc2  over loans to an Asian business partner that were never repaid.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 4.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 3.12pm BST\par \par \uc2 Economic activity in the US services sector expanded for the second consecutive month in August, a new survey from the Institute of Supply Management shows.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 ISM\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Services \uc2 PMI\uc2  for August has risen to 51.5%, up from 51.4% in July.\par \par \uc2 This is the sixth month this year that the Services \uc2 PMI\uc2  has come in over 50 points, showing an expansion.\par \par \uc2 Services companies reported that business activity, new orders and employment all rose last month.\par \par \uc2 Steve Miller\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 Institute for Supply Management\uc2  services business survey committee, says:\par \par \uc2 Slow-to-moderate growth was cited across many industries, while ongoing high costs and interest-rate pressures were often mentioned as negatively impacting business performance and driving softness in sales and traffic.\par \par \uc2 Although the Inventories Index increased by 3.1 percentage points into expansion territory in August, many respondents indicated their companies are still actively managing down their inventories.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.48pm BST\par \par \uc2 Back in the UK, there\u8217\'20\'19s been a small shareholder rebellion at electricals retailer \uc2 Currys\uc2 , over executive pay.\par \par \uc2 Almost 12% of votes at its annual general meeting were cast against approving the Directors\u8217\'20\'19 Annual Remuneration Report.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.39pm BST\par \par \uc2 Wall Street has opened cautiously, as investors digest today\u8217\'20\'19s flurry of US employment data.\par \par \uc2 The S&P 500 share index has risen by 2 points, or 0.05%, to 5,522 points at the open.\par \par \uc2 The narrower Dow Jones industrial average has dropped by 0.25%, following the slowdown in hiring and rise in layoffs in August.\par \par \uc2 Tech stocks, which do well in a low interest-rate environment, are rallying, pushing up the Nasdaq Composite index by 0.5%.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 2.28pm BST\par \par \uc2 CMA launches investigation into \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  over Oasis concert sales\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s competition watchdog has launched an investigation into \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  over the sale of tickets for Oasis concerts next year.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Competition and Markets Authority\uc2  will look into the \u8216\'20\'18dynamic pricing\u8217\'20\'19 which meant Oasis fans spent hours queuing, and were then presented with much higher ticket prices than advertised.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/music/article/2024/sep/01/oasis-tickets-dynamic-pricing-live-music"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Oasis tickets: what is dynamic pricing and why is it used for live music?}}}\par \par \uc2 The CMA will look into whether the sale of Oasis tickets by \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  may have breached consumer protection law.\par \par \uc2 It will examine whether:\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  has engaged in unfair commercial practices which are prohibited under the Consumer Protection from Unfair Trading Regulations 2008\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 People were given clear and timely information to explain that the tickets could be subject to so-called \u8216\'20\'18dynamic pricing\u8217\'20\'19 with prices changing depending on demand, and how this would operate, including the price they would pay for any tickets purchased\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 People were put under pressure to buy tickets within a short period of time \u8211\'20\'13 at a higher price than they understood they would have to pay, potentially impacting their purchasing decisions.\par \par \uc2 enltrWe\u8217\'20\'19ve launched our investigation regarding the sale of tickets for {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/OasisLive25?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #OasisLive25}}}\uc2 , including how so-called \u8216\'20\'18dynamic pricing\u8217\'20\'19 may have been used.\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19re looking at how \uc2 Ticketmaster\uc2  may have broken {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/ConsumerProtection?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #ConsumerProtection}}}\uc2  law.\par \par \uc2 Read our press release: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/bsz0W0J7cN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/bsz0W0J7cN}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/YfbBP8u4tu"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/YfbBP8u4tu}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Competition & Markets Authority\uc2  (@CMAgovUK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/CMAgovUK/status/1831680525372735896?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.37pm BST\par \par \uc2 US jobless claims fall\par \par \uc2 We are being spoiled today, with three surveys of the US jobs market.\par \par \uc2 And the third \u8211\'20\'13 the weekly tally of unemployment claims \u8211\'20\'13 is better than expected.\par \par \uc2 There were 227,000 new \u8216\'20\'18initial claims\u8217\'20\'19 for jobless support last week, a drop of 5,000 on the previous seven days, and below the 230,000 which economists expected.\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial jobless claims decline to 227K while continuing claims ease to 1.838 million from a revised 1.86 million.\par \par \uc2 We continue to make the case that the increase in the unemployment rate over the past year is not due to layoffs but rather from individuals entering the labor\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/KojVmi3vcf"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/KojVmi3vcf}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 \uc2 Joseph Brusuelas\uc2  (@joebrusuelas) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/joebrusuelas/status/1831672623262048367?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 That may calm some concerns over the US economy.\par \par \uc2 enltrInitial Jobless claims expectation: 230k\par \par \uc2 Initial Jobless claims reported: 227k\par \par \uc2 Thats good news. The market likes it. {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/94YdnuEgcq"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/94YdnuEgcq}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/VCh14Wpqn8"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/VCh14Wpqn8}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 TheChartist (@YTTheChartist) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/YTTheChartist/status/1831671283135713619?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 US private payroll growth weaker than expected in August\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: In a further sign that the US jobs market is cooling, American firms added much fewer new jobs than expected last month.\par \par \uc2 The monthly survey of private sector hiring from payrolls operator ADP has found that private employers added 99,000 jobs in August.\par \par \uc2 That\u8217\'20\'19s the smallest monthly rise since 2021, CNBC reports.\par \par \uc2 Economists had expected a rise of aroud 145,000.\par \par \uc2 This is the fifth month running that ADP has reported a slowdown in job creation among private employers.\par \par \uc2 And added to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee#block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the rise in layoffs}}}\uc2  reported by \uc2 Challenger\uc2  earlier today, it paints a worrying picture about the US jobs market.\par \par \uc2 Nela Richardson\uc2 , chief economist at ADP, says:\par \par \uc2 The job market\u8217\'20\'19s downward drift brought us to slower-than-normal hiring after two years of outsized growth. The next indicator to watch is wage growth, which is stabilizing after a dramatic post-pandemic slowdown.\par \par \uc2 enltrADP employment is the lowest since January 2021 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/keRjOg9aGX"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/keRjOg9aGX}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 ForexLive (@ForexLive) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/ForexLive/status/1831667739301224800?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Large companies, with at least 500 staff, added 42,000 workers last month, reports ADP.\par \par \uc2 Medium-sized, with between 50 and 499 staff, added a total of 68,000.\par \par \uc2 Firms with between 1 and 19 employees added 3,000 staff, but those with 2o to 49 employees laid off 12,000.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.30pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.08pm BST\par \par \uc2 John Lewis to bring back \u8216\'20\'18never knowingly undersold\u8217\'20\'19 promise\par \par \uc2 John Lewis is reviving its \u8220\'20\'1Cnever knowingly undersold\u8221\'20\'1D price promise, which it {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2022/feb/25/john-lewis-drops-never-knowingly-undersold-pledge"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 ditched just two and a half years ago}}}\uc2 , as part of a marketing blitz as it heads into the all-important Christmas selling season.\par \par \uc2 The retailer said it would use AI tools to match prices both in-store and online for 25 competitors including \uc2 Marks & Spencer\uc2 , Boots, \uc2 Currys\uc2 , House of Fraser and, on technology only \u8211\'20\'13 \uc2 Amazon\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 from Monday.\par \par \uc2 Under the new scheme, customers will be able to get their money back if they can show they have found an item cheaper at one of the 25 brands within seven days.\par \par \uc2 Peter Ruis\uc2 , head of the department store group, said {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/johnlewis"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Lewis}}}\uc2  was making a multimillion-pound investment in the relaunch of the slogan \u8211\'20\'13 which it used for nearly a century before it was dropped in February 2022 \u8211\'20\'13 including in the technology, price changes and marketing.\par \par \uc2 More {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/john-lewis-to-bring-back-never-knowingly-undersold-price-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 here}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/05/john-lewis-to-bring-back-never-knowingly-undersold-price-policy"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 John Lewis to bring back \u8216\'20\'18never knowingly undersold\u8217\'20\'19 promise}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 1.09pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.05pm BST\par \par \uc2 In another sign that the US jobs market is softening, year-to-date hiring has weakened too.\par \par \uc2 According to Challenger tracking, announced hiring plans have fallen to the lowest year-to-date total since Challenger began tracking company hiring plans in 2005.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 1.02pm BST\par \par \uc2 The Challenger job layoffs report also shows that 37,403 job cuts were attributed to \u8220\'20\'1CCost-Cutting\u8221\'20\'1D last month, while 16,439 were due to \u8220\'20\'1CMarket/Economic Conditions.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 For the first time since April, employers specified Artificial Intelligence (AI) as a reason for job cuts.\par \par \uc2 Last month, 5,943 cuts were due to AI, all of which occurred in the Technology space. So far this year, 7,126 cuts are due to AI.\par \par \uc2 enltr???? ??Just released US Challenger jobs data paints a very gloomy picture of US jobs market {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/y1fhqVsX4b"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/y1fhqVsX4b}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Trading Floor Audio (@TradeFloorAudio) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/TradeFloorAudio/status/1831659670240395587?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 Here\u8217\'20\'19s some snap reaction to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee#block-66d996688f08db0474a66fee"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the rise in US layoffs announced in August:}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr?? \uc2 Challenger\uc2  report shows biggest job cuts in tech since Q1 '23 (last big layoffs episode). Cuts in leisure/entertainment also up 85% YTD vs. last year. Lowest YTD hiring since 2005. Labor market looking fragile (at best) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/search?q=%24USD&src=ctag&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 $USD}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/Tppc6FuRZj"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/Tppc6FuRZj}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Viraj Patel (@VPatelFX) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/VPatelFX/status/1831659389507297459?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrWe just received another data point suggesting that the labor market is deteriorating faster than I expected.\par \par \uc2 The Challenger Job Cuts were almost three times higher than one month ago.\par \par \uc2 I recommend that my followers review their portfolio allocation and be confident that their\u8230\'20\'26 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/22RH6XRDvL"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/22RH6XRDvL}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Andrea Lisi, CFA (@Andrea_Texas_82) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/Andrea_Texas_82/status/1831659926181007867?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrU.S. challenger job cuts in august surged 76k, 3 times of July pretty much in line with jolts {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/SCdZGdLDA7"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/SCdZGdLDA7}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Rice cooker (@anytimeFXmetal) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/anytimeFXmetal/status/1831660072599957721?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.57pm BST\par \par \uc2 US tech companies announced 39,563 job cuts in August, today\u8217\'20\'19s Challenger report shows, out of 75,891 in total.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Challenger says the rollout of artificial intelligence systems is one factor driving layoffs:\par \par \uc2 The Tech sector is moving from a growth and innovation focus into one of profitability and efficiency. AI and automation adoption is also driving job cuts at Tech companies across roles and functions. This talent, however, is still in high demand.\par \par \uc2 Many of these professionals will land elsewhere, in and outside of the Tech industry. That said, we\u8217\'20\'19re entering a period of slower hiring, so it may take longer than it has at any point in the last decade,\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.40pm BST\par \par \uc2 Job cuts announced by US companies surge in August\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: There was a surge in layoffs at US companies in August, a new survey shows.\par \par \uc2 Firms announced 75,891 layoffs last month, roughly triple the number in July, outplacement firm \uc2 Challenger\uc2 , Gray and Christmas has reported.\par \par \uc2 enltrAugust 2024 Challenger Job Cut Report:\par \par \uc2 U.S.-based employers announced 75,891 cuts in August, a 193% increase from the 25,885 cuts announced one month prior.\par \par \uc2 It is up 1% from the 75,151 cuts announced in the same month in 2023 {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/FRWZRDCzNp"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/FRWZRDCzNp}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EvQBcTpztS"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/EvQBcTpztS}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 rich_win_again (@rich_win_again) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/rich_win_again/status/1831657839875494046?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 More than half the layoffs were led by the technology sector, where companies have been cutting their workforce as the pandemic boom in demand for IT services faded.\par \par \uc2 Challenger vice president Andrew Challenger says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CAugust\u8217\'20\'19s surge in job cuts reflects growing economic uncertainty and shifting market dynamics. Companies are facing a variety of pressures, from rising operational costs to concerns about a potential economic slowdown, leading them to make tough decisions about workforce management.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 This jump in layoffs may add to concerns that the US labor market is cooling.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 12.58pm BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.25pm BST\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace block access to \uc2 Unilever\uc2  HQ over single-use plastics row\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace UK\uc2  have blocked access to \uc2 Unilever\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s headquarters in central London on Thursday morning claiming the consumer goods company is \u8220\'20\'1Ctrashing the planet and harming communities\u8221\'20\'1D through single-use plastics.\par \par \uc2 Members of the environmental campaign group have locked themselves onto barricades made from giant Dove products, one of \uc2 Unilever\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s biggest brands, with each product\u8217\'20\'19s logo changed to a dead Dove.\par \par \uc2 Greenpeace is calling on \uc2 Unilever\uc2  to remove single-use plastic from its operations and phase it out fully within a decade, starting with plastic sachets, which they say are \u8220\'20\'1Cnear impossible to collect and recycle\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 Will McCallum, co-executive director at \uc2 Greenpeace UK\uc2 , said:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Unilever\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s plastic pollution is trashing the planet and harming communities.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey hide behind the clean, respectable face of brands like Dove but we\u8217\'20\'19re here today peeling back this facade to show the ugly truth behind it.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He added:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey must stop selling plastic sachets now, commit to phasing out single-use plastic within a decade and advocate for this same level of ambition at the final round of UN Global Plastics Treaty negotiations in November.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 12.19pm BST\par \par \uc2 Three former \uc2 Wirecard\uc2  executives ordered to pay \u8364\'20\'AC140m damages\par \par \uc2 Wirecard\uc2  ex-CEO \uc2 Markus Braun\uc2  and two other executives at the defunct German payments firm have been ordered to pay \u8364\'20\'AC140m in damages today, Reuters reports, over loans to an Asian business partner that were never repaid.\par \par \uc2 A Munich court judge upheld claims by insolvency administrator Michael Jaffe that Braun and others had violated their duties in deciding to provide the loans to a company in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 The verdict, which is not final, is the latest chapter in one of Germany\u8217\'20\'19s most egregious corporate scandals and is separate to the main trial {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2020/jun/23/former-wirecard-ceo-markus-braun-arrested-in-germany"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 against Braun}}}\uc2  and other executives. over \uc2 Wirecard\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s demise.\par \par \uc2 The company collapsed in June 2020 with a \u8364\'20\'AC1.9bn hole in its balance sheet, turning the spotlight on politicians who backed it and regulators accused of moving. too slowly to investigate allegations against it.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/mar/04/covert-cameras-alleged-hacking-how-bust-payments-company-wirecard-hired-spies-lawyers-silence-critics"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Covert cameras and alleged hacking: how bust payments company Wirecard \u8216\'20\'18hired spies and lawyers to silence critics\u8217\'20\'19}}}\par \par \uc2 Braun, deputy finance chief Stephan von Erffa and \uc2 Wirecard\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s Asia representative \uc2 Oliver Bellenhaus\uc2  are currently on trial in Munich accused of fraud and falsifying financial statements.\par \par \uc2 Braun denies all wrongdoing.\par \par \uc2 In August, German prosecutors charged another two former \uc2 Wirecard\uc2  executives with several counts of embezzlement.\par \par \uc2 Jaffe and investors are trying to sue \uc2 Wirecard\uc2  managers and auditors for damages in various civil lawsuits.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.53am BST\par \par \uc2 Unions at \uc2 Volkswagen\uc2  could propose a four-day week, to avoid factory closures and job cuts at the German carmaker.\par \par \uc2 Christiane Benner\uc2 , chair of the \uc2 IG Metall union\uc2 , has said it is \u8220\'20\'1Cconceivable\u8221\'20\'1D that it could propose a four-day week, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe will leave no idea unexplored.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 But Benner cautioned that it is impossible to lay out detailed proposals without more information on what solutions VW is proposing, saying:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe need forward-thinking ideas on where potential can be found. VW has survived difficult situations before.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Yesterday VW told staff that it is considering shutting two German factories, in what would be the carmaker\u8217\'20\'19s first closures ever in its home country.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/02/volkswagen-vw-germany-plant-closures-cars-electric-vehicles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Volkswagen considers German plant closures to save billions in costs}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 11.19am BST\par \par \uc2 Ireland on brink of technical recession\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s economy is on the brink of recession, by some measures, after activity shrank in the second quarter of this year.\par \par \uc2 New estimates from Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s Central Statistics Office show that Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s gross domestic product (GDP) fell by 1.0% in the April-June quarter. That\u8217\'20\'19s down from an initial estimate of 1.2% growth in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 GDP isn\u8217\'20\'19t a great measure of Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s economy, though, as it can be distorted by multinational firms based in the Republic.\par \par \uc2 Modified Domestic Demand (MDD), a broad measure of underlying domestic activity that covers personal, government, and investment spending, also shows a decline. It decreased by 0.5% in Q2 2024.\par \par \uc2 However, Gross national product (\uc2 GNP\uc2 ) \u8211\'20\'13 which measures the total value of goods and services produced by a country in one year, including profits made in foreign countries \u8211\'20\'13 rose by 3.3% in the quarter.\par \par \uc2 This puts Ireland on the edge of a technical recession \u8211\'20\'13 defined as two quarterly contractions in a row. In Q1, GDP rose by 0.9% while MDD inceased by 1.4%.\par \par \uc2 The CSO reports that Ireland\u8217\'20\'19s domestic sectors contracted by 1.8% in the April-June quarter with the multinational-dominated sectors declining marginally by 0.1%.\par \par \uc2 Assistant Director General with responsibility for Economic Statistics, Chris Sibley, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn today\u8217\'20\'19s results, Gross Domestic Product (GDP) is estimated to have fallen by 1.0% in April, May, and June (Q2) 2024.\par \par \uc2 The globalised Industry sector fell by 0.7% in Q2 2024 compared with Q1 2024 while the Information & Communication sector posted a decrease of 0.9% over the same period.\par \par \uc2 Overall, the combined multinational-dominated elements of the Industry and Information & Communication sectors declined by 0.1% in the quarter. These sectors accounted for 43.9% of total value added in the economy, compared with a 56.1% share for all other sectors.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.46am BST\par \par \uc2 Labour\u8217\'20\'19s election success in early July could have boosted activity in the commercial building sector last month.\par \par \uc2 Today\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 PMI\uc2  report says:\par \par \uc2 Commercial activity was the best-performing segment, despite the pace of growth slipping to its lowest since March. A number of firms noted a boost from rising sales enquiries and the release of new orders following the general election.\par \par \uc2 S&P Global adds that respondents to its \uc2 PMI\uc2  survey said improving economic conditions and greater domestic political stability had lifted customer demand, leading to an increase in orders last month.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Key event\par \par \uc2 The Labour government\u8217\'20\'19s housebuilding push should help the recovery in housebuilding to strengthen, predicts the EY ITEM Club.\par \par \uc2 EY ITEM Club say that housebuilders should feel more confident about starting projects, as housing transactions and prices have started to recover and the government has made housebuilding a key priority.\par \par \uc2 Peter Arnold, EY UK chief economist, says:\par \par \uc2 House prices and transactions have passed the bottom and are starting to recover, which will bolster firms\u8217\'20\'19 confidence in building.\par \par \uc2 The new government\u8217\'20\'19s focus on housebuilding as a key pillar of its growth plan is also likely to be supporting confidence, although capacity constraints, particularly in terms of the availability of staff with the appropriate skills, will need to be overcome.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.29am BST\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4#block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK housebuilding accelerated last month}}}\uc2  thanks to the ongoing economic recovery, and lower borrowing costs, explains Thomas Pugh, economist at RSM UK.\par \par \uc2 Pugh adds:\par \par \uc2 Mortgage approvals jumped in July and annual house price growth picked up as well, clear signs of a revival in the housing market.\par \par \uc2 Pugh points out that the future activity index and new orders measures within the \uc2 PMI\uc2  are still high indicating optimism in the industry, adding:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWhat\u8217\'20\'19s more, there was further good news for policy makers as the input prices balance dropped, suggesting that price pressures are easing in the construction sector as well as the services and manufacturing sectors. That bodes well for interest rate cuts later this year.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 10.23am BST\par \par \uc2 Although UK {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4#block-66d96e538f083fc999a38fb4"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 housebuilding activity picked up last month}}}\uc2 , hiring across the construction sector did not.\par \par \uc2 Staffing levels were broadly unchanged, according to the purchasing managers surveyed for today\u8217\'20\'19s construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  report. That ends a three-month period of expansion.\par \par \uc2 Firms also cut back on sub-contractor usage, for the first time since January.\par \par \uc2 Some firms noted that elevated wage pressures had led to delays with staff hiring, while others commented on a lack of candidates to fill vacancies. S&P Global explains.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.57am BST\par \par \uc2 UK housing activity rises at quickest pace since September 2022\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: Growth in Britain\u8217\'20\'19s housebuilding sector has hit its highest rate since the mini-budget almost two years ago.\par \par \uc2 Data provider S&P Global has reported that UK residential construction work \u8220\'20\'1Cgained momentum\u8221\'20\'1D in August, with growth accelerating to its fastest since September 2022.\par \par \uc2 This increase in housebuilding was due to \u8220\'20\'1Cimproving market conditions\u8221\'20\'1D and lower borrowing costs, S&P Global says.\par \par \uc2 However, civil engineering growth slowed last month, which pulled the overall construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  down to 53.6 in August, down from July\u8217\'20\'19s 26-month high of 55.3.\par \par \uc2 Tim Moore\uc2 , economics director at S&P Global Market Intelligence, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe UK construction sector appears to have turned a corner after a difficult start to 2024, with renewed vigour in the house building segment the most notable development in August. Residential work expanded at the fastest pace for almost two years as lower borrowing costs and a gradual recovery in market conditions helped to boost activity.\par \par \uc2 Commercial building was the best-performing part of the construction sector as the improving UK economic backdrop resulted in stronger order books, but the postelection bounce in demand faded somewhat in August.\par \par \uc2 Borrowing costs jumped after the September 2022 mini-budget, driving up mortgage rates and making it harder for buyers to get a home loan. Mortgage rates have been {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/money/article/2024/jul/23/fixed-rate-mortgages-below-4-back-on-sale"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 dropping this summer}}}\uc2 , as the City anticipates more interest rate cuts from the \uc2 Bank of England\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Labour government has pledged to \u8220\'20\'1Cget Britain building again\u8221\'20\'1D by bringing back compulsory housebuilding targets, and to build 370,000 homes a year during the course of the current parliament.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/article/2024/jul/30/the-guardian-view-on-labours-housing-proposals-revolutionary-intent"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 The Guardian view on Labour\u8217\'20\'19s housing proposals: revolutionary intent | Editorial}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.15am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Petrol\uc2  and diesel pump prices fall to lowest in nearly three years\par \par \uc2 Good news for motorists who haven\u8217\'20\'19t made the switch to electric vehicles \u8211\'20\'13 fossil fuel costs have hit a near three-year low.\par \par \uc2 Motoring body the AA reports that the average price of petrol dropped to 139.5p a litre yesterday, the lowest since October 2021.\par \par \uc2 Diesel yesterday fell to 144.2p a litre, on average, also the lowest since October 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 a few months before Russia\u8217\'20\'19s invasion of Ukraine sent energy costs soaring.\par \par \uc2 The AA says the 5p fuel duty cut made in the March 2022 budget helped push price down.\par \par \uc2 Edmund King, AA president, warned the government against removing the cut in this October\u8217\'20\'19s budget \u8211\'20\'13 something that is reportedly under consideration.\par \par \uc2 King says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CPure and simple, the only reason why pump prices are at a three-year low this week is because of the 5p fuel duty cut. Removing it threatens to send millions of low-income drivers back into the era of \u8216\'20\'18perma-high\u8217\'20\'19 road fuel prices.\par \par \uc2 However, the freeze in fuel duty does cost the Treasury billions of pounds per year, as these posts show:\par \par \uc2 enltrWhy should we stop freezing fuel duty?\par \par \uc2 1. It's a tax cut that helps wealthier households the most and poorest households the least {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/BjClqoF1Rm"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/BjClqoF1Rm}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Theo Bertram (@theobertram) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/theobertram/status/1829078437492851061?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 29, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 enltr2. It costs taxpayers a fortune - we've splurged more than \u163\'00\'A3100 billion on fuel duty freezes in the last 14 years {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/5q0hSKHmGo"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/5q0hSKHmGo}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Theo Bertram (@theobertram) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/theobertram/status/1829079006060171526?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 August 29, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.36am BST\par \par \uc2 SMMT: UK on track to miss Zero Emission Vehicle Mandate for EV sales\par \par \uc2 So far this year, the market share held by battery electric cars (BEVs) has risen to 17.2%, up from 16.5% in 2023.\par \par \uc2 The SMMT expect it to rise to 18.5% by the end of the year thanks to increasing model choice \u8211\'20\'13 with some 364,000 BEVs registrations forecast for the year.\par \par \uc2 But, that means the UK would miss the goal that 22% of each carmaker\u8217\'20\'19s sales must be pure battery cars in 2024, laid out in the Zero Emission Vehicle Mandate (as the industry {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/05/uk-carmakers-miss-government-ev-electric-target-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 warned last month}}}\uc2  ).\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/05/uk-carmakers-miss-government-ev-electric-target-sales"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 UK carmakers say they may miss government targets on EV sales}}}\par \par \uc2 The SMMT says \u8220\'20\'1Curgent action is needed\u8221\'20\'1D, including binding targets on public chargepoint provision and the reintroduction of incentives to encourage private buyers to go electric.\par \par \uc2 Mike Hawes\uc2 , SMMT chief executive, says:\par \par \uc2 August\u8217\'20\'19s EV growth is welcome, but it\u8217\'20\'19s always a very low volume month and so subject to distortions ahead of September\u8217\'20\'19s number plate change.\par \par \uc2 The introduction of the new 74 plate, together with a raft of compelling offers and discounts from manufacturers, plus growing model choice, will help increase purchase consideration and be a true barometer for market demand. Encouraging a mass market shift to EVs remains a challenge, however, and urgent action must be taken to help buyers overcome affordability issues and concerns about chargepoint provision.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.21am BST\par \par \uc2 UK car sales dip in August, BEV sales rise\par \par \uc2 Newsflash: UK car sales fell in August, ending a {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://media.smmt.co.uk/july-2024-new-car-registrations/"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 24-month run of growth}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Industry body SMMT reports that new car registrations dipped by 1.3% last month, to 84,575 units.\par \par \uc2 August is traditionally a snoozy month for car dealers, as many buyers preferring to wait for September\u8217\'20\'19s new number plate (or are busy on holiday instead!).\par \par \uc2 enltrNew car market stable in traditionally quiet month of August, dipping -1.3% to 84,575 units {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rD4waDW7aa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/rD4waDW7aa}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/aZWJ5ekzc9"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/aZWJ5ekzc9}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 SMMT (@SMMT) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SMMT/status/1831604401636516242?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 This time, fleet purchases (companies buying cars for their business) fell by 1.2% while sales to private buyers were 0.2% higher. Business registrations sank by nearly a third to just 1,136 units.\par \par \uc2 enltrContinuing the recent trend, fleet purchases drove the market, accounting for six in 10 cars registered last month, or 51,329 units, despite a -1.2% drop compared with the same month last year {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/rD4waDW7aa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/rD4waDW7aa}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/908UusO2us"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/908UusO2us}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 SMMT (@SMMT) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/SMMT/status/1831606540865823109?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 There are some interesting trends among the types of cars being bought.\par \par \uc2 Registrations of electric cars (Battery electric vehicles or BEVs) jumped by 10.8%. This lifted the BEV market share to 22.6%, the highest for a month since December 2022.\par \par \uc2 The SMMT says this is due to \u8220\'20\'1Cheavy discounting by manufacturers over the summer and a raft of new models attracting buyers.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Petrol sales fell 10.1%, while diesel dropped by 7.3% \u8211\'20\'13 but together made up 56.8% of all new registrations in August.\par \par \uc2 Plug-in hybrid (PHEV) registrations declined by 12.3%, but hybrid electric vehicle (HEV) uptake increased by 36.1%.\par \par \uc2 Yesterday, \uc2 Volvo Cars\uc2  abandoned its target of sell only electric cars by 2030, citing \u8220\'20\'1Cchanging market conditions and customer demands.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 9.21am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 9.02am BST\par \par \uc2 We\u8217\'20\'19ve covered plenty of bad economic news from Germany recently, with its economy shrinking in April-June and {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/sep/02/volkswagen-vw-germany-plant-closures-cars-electric-vehicles"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 carmaker Volkswagen considering factory closures}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 So it\u8217\'20\'19s a relief to have some good news from Europe\u8217\'20\'19s largest economy.\par \par \uc2 German factory orders rose by 2.9% month-on-month in July, statistics body Destatis reported this morning, smashing forecasts of a 1.5% fall.\par \par \uc2 This is \u8220\'20\'1Ca rare piece of good news for the country\u8217\'20\'19s important manufacturing sector,\u8221\'20\'1D say analysts at Saxo.\par \par \uc2 However\u8230\'20\'26. the figures are flattered by some large orders, such as aircraft, ships, trains, and military vehicles.\par \par \uc2 Strip those out, and industrial orders were 0.4% lower than in June.\par \par \uc2 deltrDer reale (preisbereinigte) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/hashtag/Auftragseingang?src=hash&ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 #Auftragseingang}}}\uc2  im Verarbeitenden Gewerbe ist nach vorl\u228\'00\'E4ufigen Angaben im Juli 2024 gegen\u252\'00\'FCber Juni 2024 um 2,9 % gestiegen. Ohne die Ber\u252\'00\'FCcksichtigung der Gro\u223\'00\'DFauftr\u228\'00\'E4ge war er um 0,4 % niedriger. Mehr dazu: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/poDxf6YXye"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/poDxf6YXye}}}{\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/toyy1EcYCv"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 pic.twitter.com/toyy1EcYCv}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Statistisches Bundesamt (@destatis) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/destatis/status/1831575529654260181?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 10.56am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.50am BST\par \par \uc2 Asos sells Topshop and Topman into new joint venture\par \par \uc2 We also have results from online clothing retailer ASOS this morning, but they don\u8217\'20\'19t report problems from the bad weather.\par \par \uc2 ASOS told the City that its adjusted profits for the current financial year are expected to hit the top of analyst estimates\u8230\'20\'26. but its sales will be \u8220\'20\'1Cslightly below guidance\u8221\'20\'1D.\par \par \uc2 ASOS has also struck a deal to sell a majority of its stake in Topshop and Topman brands \u8211\'20\'13 which it acquired in 2021 \u8211\'20\'13 to a new joint venture formed with Danish family office HEARTLAND, which would own 75% of the new entity.\par \par \uc2 ASOS expects to get about \u163\'00\'A3118m from the sale, and will hold the remaining 25% of the joint venture.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.41am BST\par \par \uc2 After 15 years, luxury goods brand \uc2 Burberry\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s run in the FTSE 100 index is soon to end.\par \par \uc2 Burberry\uc2  is being relegated down to the FTSE 250 index, of medium-sized companies, later this month.\par \par \uc2 FTSE Russell, the global index provider, announced last night that insurer \uc2 Hiscox\uc2  will take \uc2 Burberry\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s place in the blue-chip FTSE 100 index in the next quarterly reshuffle.\par \par \uc2 Burberry\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s relegation isn\u8217\'20\'19t a surprise; its market capitalisation has fallen to \u163\'00\'A32.23bn, meaning it was no longer valuable enough to hold a place in the FTSE 100.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/31/can-burberry-fix-its-tattered-image-after-chequered-patch"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Can Burberry fix its tattered image after chequered patch?}}}\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, UK computer firm \uc2 Raspberry Pi\uc2  is joining the FTSE 250 following its stock market float this summer.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/technology/article/2024/may/15/tech-firm-raspberry-pi-readies-for-london-stock-market-float"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Tech firm Raspberry Pi readies for London stock market float}}}\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 8.42am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.24am BST\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2 : China detains \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  staff in data, drug-import probes\par \par \uc2 Police in China have detained five current and former employees of British drugmaker {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.bloomberg.com/quote/AZN:LN"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AstraZeneca }}}\uc2  for questioning about potential illegal activities, \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  are reporting.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg\uc2  explains:\par \par \uc2 The individuals being held are all Chinese citizens who marketed cancer drugs in \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s oncology division, the people said, asking not to be identified discussing private information. The investigation is being led by police in the southern metropolis of Shenzhen, and the detentions took place earlier in the summer, the people said.\par \par \uc2 One probe is related to the company\u8217\'20\'19s collection of patient data, and whether that infringed China\u8217\'20\'19s data-privacy laws, according to the people. Authorities are also looking into some of the individuals\u8217\'20\'19 involvement in importing a liver cancer drug that hasn\u8217\'20\'19t been approved for distribution in mainland China, one of the people said.\par \par \uc2 enltrChina detains five current and former employees of British drugmaker \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2  as part of data and drug-import probes, sources say {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/EFKS25xADP"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/EFKS25xADP}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Bloomberg UK (@BloombergUK) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/BloombergUK/status/1831591435780395224?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 China made up 13% of \uc2 AstraZeneca\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s global sales last year, bringing in revenues of nearly $6bn of a total $44bn, making it the biggest overseas pharmaceuticals company by sales.\par \par \uc2 The company had considered spinning off its business in China and listing it in Hong Kong or Shanghai to shield it from geopolitical tensions between Beijing and Washington.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2023/jun/19/astrazeneca-china-spin-off"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AstraZeneca considers spinning off its China business }}}\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.11am BST\par \par \uc2 Shares in \uc2 Associated British Foods\uc2  have fallen 3.8% at the start of trading in London, as traders react to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82#block-66d94fce8f081743e33b5c82"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the drop in sales at its UK Primark stores this summer}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 They\u8217\'20\'19re the top faller on the FTSE 100 index.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.02am BST\par \par \uc2 Currys\uc2  sales boosted by Euro 2024 and AI\par \par \uc2 While the cold and wet British summer dampened demand at \uc2 Primark\uc2 , electricals retailer \uc2 Currys\uc2  was growing its sales over the summer.\par \par \uc2 Currys\uc2  grew its like-for-like sales in the UK and Ireland by 5% in the 17 weeks to 24 August.\par \par \uc2 This was partly due to England\u8217\'20\'19s performance in EURO 2024, it says, as some families bought new TVs to enjoy the Three Lions\u8217\'20\'19 occasionally choppy route to the final.\par \par \uc2 Currys\uc2  is also seeing intest in AI computing products, it says.\par \par \uc2 Alex Baldock\uc2 , \uc2 Currys\uc2  chief executive, explains:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTrading is going well, strengthening our confidence in growing profit and free cash flow again this year.\par \par \uc2 New AI-enabled computers are bringing excitement and innovation to customers, who are coming to our stores to learn more about the technology, helping us take almost 50% share of the total laptop market.\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 8.02am BST\par \par \uc2 ABF\uc2  had been expected to release its trading update this week, but has brought it forward due to the disappointing sales at \uc2 Primark\uc2  and the lower than expected sugar profits.\par \par \uc2 Retail analyst Nick Bubb says:\par \par \uc2 It\u8217\'20\'19s a bit of shock to hear that mighty \uc2 Primark\uc2  has been over 3% down LFL in the last 3 months\u8230\'20\'26\par \par \uc2 [that\u8217\'20\'19s the fall in UK like-for-like sales].\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.49am BST\par \par \uc2 Citizens Advice: \uc2 USO\uc2  must be reformed\par \par \uc2 Cutting deliveries of second-class letters to just two or three days a week would save \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  hundreds of millions of pound a year.\par \par \uc2 As my colleague Alex Lawson {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/apr/03/royal-mail-owner-proposes-second-class-post-deliveries-every-other-weekday"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 explained here in April}}}\uc2 , a postal worker could deliver on a single route on Monday, Wednesday and Friday, and on another route on Tuesday and Thursday. First class letters would then be shipped faster on \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  vans also used for parcels.\par \par \uc2 Citizens Advice say that changes to the universal service obligation are needed \u8211\'20\'13 but should benefit customers, not just save \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  money.\par \par \uc2 Tom MacInnes, Interim Director of Policy at Citizens Advice, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWith \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  failing to meet its targets for nearly half a decade, the current Universal Service Obligation (\uc2 USO\uc2 ) clearly doesn\u8217\'20\'19t protect consumers as it should.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CReforms to the \uc2 USO\uc2  need to address this. They can\u8217\'20\'19t just be a disguise for cuts that prioritise saving \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  money over providing a good standard of service.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe agree that improving reliability and affordability is essential. But cutting deliveries won\u8217\'20\'19t automatically lead to the more reliable service people need.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1C\uc2 Ofcom\uc2  has acknowledged some of \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s failings but we need to see that recognised with action. The regulator needs to make sure we have a \uc2 USO\uc2  that serves its basic purpose of protecting consumers - not \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s bottom line.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.44am BST\par \par \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 : we have to change the Universal Service\par \par \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s parent company, \uc2 International Distribution Services\uc2 , says the universal service obligation needs to change.\par \par \uc2 Responding to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/live/2024/sep/05/royal-mail-must-improve-deliveries-second-class-ofcom-primark-hit-by-bad-weather-us-jobs-payrolls-business-live?filterKeyEvents=false&page=with:block-66d946b88f08486d64f313ec#block-66d946b88f08486d64f313ec"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the news }}}\uc2  that \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  is considering its proposal to downgrade second class deliveries, \uc2 Martin Seidenberg\uc2 , Group CEO of IDS, says:\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTo save the Universal Service, we have to change the Universal Service.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CLetter volumes have fallen from their peak of 20 billion to just 6.7 billion a year today meaning the average household now receives just four letters per week. Yet whilst most countries have adapted their Universal Service requirements to reflect the new reality, in the UK the minimum requirements have not changed.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1COur proposal for the future of the Universal Service has been developed after speaking to thousands of people across the country and is designed to protect what matters most for customers. It can be achieved through regulatory change with no need for new legislation.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThe Universal Service faces a very real and urgent financial sustainability challenge. Change cannot come soon enough. We look forward to continuing to engage with all our stakeholders to secure a financially sustainable Universal Service for many years to come.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.40am BST\par \par \uc2 Primark\uc2  suffers from bad summer weather\par \par \uc2 Another British institution, \uc2 Primark\uc2 , has been hit by the grizzly weather this summer.\par \par \uc2 Primark\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s owner, \uc2 Associated British Foods\uc2 , warned shareholders this morning that like-for-like sales at the clothing chain have fallen in the last six months.\par \par \uc2 Like-for-like sales are expected to decrease by around 0.5% in the six months to 14 September, driven by a 0.9% decline in the last three months.\par \par \uc2 In the UK, alone, like-for-like sales are expected to decrease by around 2.0% in the six months to 14 September, including a 3.1% drop in the last quarter \u8211\'20\'13 with customer visits \u8220\'20\'1Cimpacted by challenging weather, particularly in April and June.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ABF\uc2  says:\par \par \uc2 This primarily reflects unfavourable weather in the UK and Ireland in H2, which resulted in lower footfall and particularly impacted sales of our seasonal lines in womenswear and footwear.\par \par \uc2 But thanks to new store openings, \uc2 Primark\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s overall revenue growth is expected to be around 4% for the last six months.\par \par \uc2 Last summer was the coolest since 2015, according to provisional \uc2 Met Office\uc2  statistics.\par \par \uc2 This made it more of a \u8216\'20\'18drat summer\u8217\'20\'19 than a \u8216\'20\'18brat summmer\u8217\'20\'19 for retailers hoping to shift summery garments.\par \par \uc2 Or, as CEO \uc2 George Weston\uc2  puts it this morning, \u8220\'20\'1Cthe British weather was not in \uc2 Primark\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s favour this summer.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 ABF\uc2  has also flagged that profits at its sugar business will be below expectations, due to \u8220\'20\'1Ca sharp fall in European sugar prices\u8221\'20\'1D this year.\par \par \uc2 block-time updated-timeUpdated at 7.58am BST\par \par \uc2 block-time published-time 7.39am BST\par \par \uc2 Introduction: \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s delivery performance "must improve"\par \par \uc2 Good morning, and welcome to our rolling coverage of business, the financial markets and the world economy.\par \par \uc2 Britain\u8217\'20\'19s \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  \u8220\'20\'1Cmust improve\u8221\'20\'1D its delivery performance, says communications regulator \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  this morning, as it proposes changes to its second-class letter service.\par \par \uc2 The proposals are meant to secure the future of the universal postal service, it says.\par \par \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  warns that \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s delivery performance has simply \u8220\'20\'1Cnot been good enough\u8221\'20\'1D in recent years. In the last five years, the regulator has found it in breach of its quality of service obligations twice and fined it both times.\par \par \uc2 Related: {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/article/2024/aug/23/royal-mail-delivers-80-first-class-post-on-time"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 Royal Mail delivers less than 80% of first-class post on time}}}\par \par \uc2 The regulator says:\par \par \uc2 We have been pressing the company on what it is doing to turn things around, and we are currently investigating its latest failure to hit its annual delivery targets. Regardless of how the universal service evolves, \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s delivery performance must improve.\par \par \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  has been examining potential changes to the universal obligation \u8211\'20\'13 that compels the postal operator to deliver letters six days a week (Monday to Saturday) and parcels five days a week (Monday to Friday) to every address in the UK.\par \par \uc2 And today, it is proposing that \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  should be allowed to downgrade its second class service, and no longer deliver letters with a 2nd class stamp on Saturdays.\par \par \uc2 The regulator argues that this would give \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  flexibility to improve its service, and is going to consult on the plan.\par \par \uc2 Ofcom\uc2  says today:\par \par \uc2 The evidence we have gathered so far also suggests people want a next-day service available six days a week for when they need to send the occasional urgent letter or card. However, people acknowledge that most letters are not urgent.\par \par \uc2 If Second Class letters continued to be delivered within three working days but not on Saturdays - and First Class remained unchanged at six days a week - it would enable \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  to improve reliability, make substantial efficiency savings, and redeploy its existing resources to growth areas such as parcels.\par \par \uc2 This proposal is likely to please \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 back in April, {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/2024/apr/03/royal-mail-owner-proposes-second-class-post-deliveries-every-other-weekday"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 it asked Ofcom to let it reduce deliveries of second-class letters to just two or three days a week}}}\par \par \uc2 enltrOfcom looks like it could well give \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  what it asked for - cutting second class deliveries to alternate days, which would allow 1,000 job cuts and \u163\'00\'A3300m in savings {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://t.co/724wRI5jBr"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://t.co/724wRI5jBr}}}\par \par \uc2 \u8212\'20\'14 Alex Lawson (@MrAlexLawson) {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://twitter.com/MrAlexLawson/status/1831576650913292517?ref_src=twsrc%5Etfw"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 September 5, 2024}}}\par \par \uc2 Lindsey Fussell\uc2 , \uc2 Ofcom\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s group director for networks and communications, says \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  must do better:\par \par \uc2 Postal users\u8217\'20\'19 needs are at the heart of our review. If we decide to propose changes to the universal service next year, we want to make sure we achieve the best outcome for consumers.\par \par \uc2 So we\u8217\'20\'19re now looking at whether we can get the universal service back on an even keel in a way that meets people\u8217\'20\'19s needs. But this won\u8217\'20\'19t be a free pass for \uc2 Royal Mail\uc2  \u8211\'20\'13 under any scenario, it must invest in its network, become more efficient and improve its service levels.\par \par \uc2 The agenda\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9am BST: UK new car sales\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: UK construction \uc2 PMI\uc2  for August\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 9.30am BST: \uc2 Bank of England\uc2  Monthly Decision Maker Panel data for August 2024\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 11am BST: Irish Q2 GDP and \uc2 GNP\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.15pm BST: ADP private US payrolls\par \par \uc2 *\par \par \uc2 1.30pm BST: US weekly jobless claims\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240905ek95000ru\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
