\par\par \uc2 US news\par \b \uc2 More than 17,000 \uc2 AT&T\uc2  workers strike over unfair labor practice by company\b0\par\par\uc2 Michael Sainato \par \uc2 774 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 September 2024\par 11:02 GMT\par \uc2 The Guardian\par \uc2 GRDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 Copyright 2024.  The Guardian.  All rights reserved. \par \par \uc2 Strike started on 16 August over firm\u8217\'20\'19s delays in bargaining a new contract since the process began in June\par \par \uc2 More than 17,000 \uc2 AT&T\uc2  workers in the US south-east remain on strike as {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://cwa-union.org/news/releases/att-uses-mediation-delay-bargaining-cwa"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 the Communications Workers of America have accused}}}\uc2  the company of unfair labor practices for attempting to further delay bargaining a new union contract.\par \par \uc2 The workers, technicians, customer service representatives and others who install, maintain and support {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://www.theguardian.com/business/at-t"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 AT&T}}}\uc2  \u8217\'20\'19s network have been on strike since 16 August in Alabama, Florida, Georgia, Kentucky, Louisiana, Mississippi, North Carolina, South Carolina and Tennessee.\par \par \uc2 Bargaining began in June, ahead of the 3 August expiration date for the workers\u8217\'20\'19 five-year contract.\par \par \uc2 Richard Honeycutt\uc2 , the \uc2 CWA\uc2  district 3 vice-president, explained the union is seeking improvements to wages that take into account the increases to the cost of living and high inflation, maintaining affordable healthcare and to secure protections for a second tier of workers to improve their work-life balance. The employees in question are subjected to forced overtime without notification, making it difficult for them to plan out their lives and spend time with their families.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CThey\u8217\'20\'19ve had to raise their families through FaceTime because they don\u8217\'20\'19t know if they\u8217\'20\'19re going to be working till 10 or 11 o\u8217\'20\'19clock at night, and so you can forget about T-ball games and the typical stuff that you try to do with your family, because there\u8217\'20\'19s no certainty,\u8221\'20\'1D Honeycutt said. \u8220\'20\'1CThat\u8217\'20\'19s what we went into bargaining with trying to get for our membership. But as we got closer to the expiration it just seemed like \uc2 AT&T\uc2  was not bargaining.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He noted that the union filed unfair labor practice charges over the company\u8217\'20\'19s delays in bargaining a new contract in 2019 negotiations, which resulted in managers from \uc2 AT&T\uc2  corporate attending bargaining sessions, with a tentative agreement reached shortly thereafter. Despite this, the same delays and issues have repeated and remain.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CTheir movement was very, very minimal and so that\u8217\'20\'19s what led to us filing unfair labor practices,\u8221\'20\'1D Honeycutt added. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we were approaching, and even since the contract has expired, it\u8217\'20\'19s like they\u8217\'20\'19re just shuffling stuff around. There\u8217\'20\'19s no real substance to what they\u8217\'20\'19re proposing to try to get to a tentative agreement that can be voted on by the membership.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 He explained that \uc2 AT&T\uc2 \u8217\'20\'19s representatives have not been able to explain their proposals or make decisions in bargaining at the negotiation table. And the union is pushing for \uc2 AT&T\uc2  to have the company representatives who are making decisions on proposals to attend bargaining sessions.\par \par \uc2 The union recently pulled out of federal mediation with \uc2 AT&T\uc2  with the \uc2 Federal Mediation and Conciliation Service\uc2  (\uc2 FMCS\uc2 ) as they went into talks believing \uc2 AT&T\uc2  would improve how they have been bargaining. But the union viewed \uc2 AT&T\uc2  postures in mediation as just another delay tactic to reach an agreement.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CBasically they continued to bargain the same way. And so we felt like, for whatever reason, they were hiding behind the process of mediation,\u8221\'20\'1D Honeycutt concluded. \u8220\'20\'1CWe know it\u8217\'20\'19s having an impact on the customers and I think our members and me, included in the leadership, can say that\u8217\'20\'19s not a good thing.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CWe\u8217\'20\'19re not happy to have to do that. Our members do want to be out there doing their job, working for the customers, keeping their internet up. It\u8217\'20\'19s not just your home phone and your home internet. We\u8217\'20\'19re talking about circuits for 911 \u8211\'20\'13 you\u8217\'20\'19ve got circuits that feed airports. You\u8217\'20\'19ve got circuits that feed various government agencies and businesses. We\u8217\'20\'19re talking about some major stuff that could go wrong. We\u8217\'20\'19re ready to go back if we just see the right movement as far as trying to address the charges.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 A spokesperson for \uc2 AT&T\uc2  criticized the union for withdrawing from federal mediation. They said doing that is inconsistent with the unfair labor practice charges that \uc2 CWA\uc2  district 3 filed with the \uc2 National Labor Relations Board\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CRegardless of whether a neutral third-party is present, progress will not be made without a willingness to compromise,\u8221\'20\'1D the spokesperson said in an email. \u8220\'20\'1CAs we have said from day one, we are focused on reaching a fair and competitive agreement that benefits our hard-working employees as quickly as possible and this won\u8217\'20\'19t change.\par \par \uc2 \u8220\'20\'1CIn the meantime, we will remain prepared for all contingencies to ensure our customers receive the excellent service they deserve.\u8221\'20\'1D\par \par \uc2 Document GRDN000020240904ek94001jl\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw127\pich40\picscaley313\picscalex313\picwgoal610\pichgoal192
